window.AKK_MATRIARCH_MANUAL_CHAPTERS=(window.AKK_MATRIARCH_MANUAL_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":101,"title":"Chapter 101: Radiant Glow","paragraphs":["It was now the twenty-fifth of the third month, the day of the Zhou family's Taoist purification rites.","The weather that day was not good. A rain had fallen the night before, leaving the sky overcast and low; it seemed the rain had not yet fully spent, and the air was more stifling than in previous days.","The members of the Duke of Chen's manor rose early and set off for Chongyang Temple.","As they departed, it began to rain again—a fine rain, like a thin mist through the forest.","Cheng Zhao wore a sky-blue embroidered jacket and a white silk pleated skirt. Her thick, dark hair was pinned in the most inconspicuous of low buns, adorned with a few pieces of tourmaline jewelry—simple yet elegant.","Zhou Yuanshen was dressed in a pale blue robe, his hair fastened with a jade hairpin. His expression was cool and distant, as if lost in thought or deep contemplation.","The husband and wife rode together in a small oiled-cloth carriage.","When they reached the main gate, carriages were lined up one after another. The matrons accompanying the carriages were dressed in straw rain capes and conical hats, while the maids held umbrellas, having waited respectfully for some time.","\"They haven't arrived yet,\" Cheng Zhao said to Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, \"You get in the carriage first, so the rain doesn't dampen your skirt.\"","\"It's fine. The rain is not heavy. Grandmother hasn't come yet—how could I board first?\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Waiting here is the same,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao still refused.","She had no idea what tricks the Dowager Madame might play on her. If Cheng Zhao dared to think boldly, perhaps this purification ceremony was aimed at her.","Though she was quick-witted, she had little experience. Who knew if she could avoid whatever trap was laid?","She wanted to wait for the Dowager Madame herself.","\"I'll wait a little longer,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen did not press further.","The next to emerge were still not the grandmother and the eldest branch's mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, but rather Cheng Zhao's mother-in-law, Second Madam, and her younger brother-in-law, Zhou Yuanqi.","Only the Second Master did not go; he had left early for the morning court session. Zhou Yuanshen had specifically requested leave to stay home.","Second Madam wore a lake-blue robe with a round collar and a front-opening jacket embroidered with a floral pattern, paired with a magnolia-colored skirt. She adorned herself with a few pieces of sapphire jewelry, combining dignity with a touch of brisk efficiency. Zhou Yuanqi, meanwhile, wore a sapphire-blue robe of fresh, bright fabric that made his fair and refined features appear even more lovely.","\"Mother,\" Cheng Zhao greeted, then addressed Zhou Yuanqi, \"Fifth Brother.\"","Zhou Yuanqi said to Cheng Zhao, \"Why are you dressed so lavishly? You'll only catch someone's eye again and cause trouble for yourself.\"","Second Madam said, \"Zhaozhao does look lovely in that outfit today.\"","Looking more closely, the clothes—from the color and embroidery to the silk fabric—were all quite ordinary.","It was simply that the person herself was beautiful.","Cheng Zhao had been blessed with natural good looks. Her face was not large, but her cheeks were full. She had almond-shaped eyes that were slightly elongated and alluring, yet not coquettish.","Fine features, beautiful skin—she was beautiful everywhere, radiating an air of nobility.","Looking at Cheng Zhao, one always felt that only a household of immense wealth, with every item of food, clothing, and daily use exquisitely fine and costly, could produce such a beauty.","\"...I deliberately dressed modestly,\" Cheng Zhao said to Zhou Yuanqi with a smile. \"Fifth Brother, you've misjudged this time.\"","Zhou Yuanqi ignored her, muttering, \"Show-off.\"","Even a brute loved to show off. Whenever something fell into the brute's hands, it was easily solved, and he would act as if his tail were brushing the sky. Zhou Yuanqi couldn't stand it.","\"Zhaozhao, you've been looking well lately—full and radiant. Is it because you've been eating more bird's nest porridge?\" Second Madam asked.","It wasn't that she had gained weight; rather, she glowed with vitality and energy. Her whole person seemed fresh and lush, from her complexion to her spirit—full to the brim.","\"I have been eating quite a bit,\" Cheng Zhao said.","And then she remembered. Lately, Zhou Yuanshen had been staying overnight in Nonghua Courtyard, and the two of them would always be lively until late into the night.","Every morning when she woke, Mama Li would have bird's nest porridge ready, telling her to drink a bowl first, saying it nourished the qi.","Fearing that Cheng Zhao might overexert herself and damage her vitality, which would be bad for bearing children.","Cheng Zhao was well-nourished, her mood was good, and she felt that these past few days had been very pleasant indeed.","It was nothing, really, yet from Second Madam's mention of bird's nest porridge, Cheng Zhao's mind wandered to Zhou Yuanshen behind the bed curtains at night...","She glanced at him.","Zhou Yuanshen looked back at her.","At the very moment the young couple's eyes met, the Dowager Madame and the others emerged.","Behind the Dowager Madame, besides the eldest branch's mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, were Mu Jiang and another unfamiliar young girl.","Mu Jiang saw this scene and immediately darkened her face.","She was pregnant with a child, yet Cheng Zhao was exchanging flirtatious glances with her Third Brother at the front gate.","What merit did Cheng Zhao have? She was merely pretty.","Mu Jiang was also very beautiful, and her devotion to her Third Brother was more sincere. She had set her heart on him since she was old enough to understand. She had been confident that they would be the most loving couple in the world.","Later, things did not go as she had hoped, but Mu Jiang was not discouraged.","What infuriated her, though, was that she didn't know where her Third Brother had been spending his nights lately.","The main kitchen was a wellspring of information. Ever since Cheng Zhao had taken over the main kitchen, Mu Jiang's sources of news had dried up.","Even the Dowager Madame's trusted stewards didn't know where the Duke's evening meal was served; they only knew that the young page Nanfeng had personally taken it away.","It was all Cheng Zhao's doing!","Cheng Zhao was wealthy. She forwent the perks that the main kitchen could distribute to the stewards and instead subsidized other managers. She was also very calculating and quickly won over a group of people, splitting the main kitchen into two camps.","One camp was loyal to the Dowager Madame; the other was loyal to Cheng Zhao.","And neither side dared to cause trouble. They balanced each other out, managing the main kitchen properly with no mishaps in the meals.","The Dowager Madame had no opportunity to find an excuse to express her dissatisfaction.","So Mu Jiang heard the Dowager Madame say of Cheng Zhao, smiling, \"Naughty girl.\"","\"Third Brother,\" Mu Jiang stepped forward.","Beside her, Cheng Zhao's face changed slightly, as if she had thought of something.","Seeing Cheng Zhao's expression shift made Mu Jiang happy. She moved closer, trying to tug Zhou Yuanshen's sleeve: \"Third Brother, did you also take leave today to accompany Grandmother?\"","Zhou Yuanshen acknowledged her with a sound and, together with Cheng Zhao, bowed to their grandmother.","\"It's only drizzling. Why are you standing out here?\" the Dowager Madame said with a smile.","She then extended her hand toward Zhou Yuanshen, signaling for him to help her.","Her other hand was supported by Huan Qingtang.","If Zhou Yuanshen stepped forward, he and Huan Qingtang would look like a young couple, helping the Dowager Madame into the carriage.","Seeing this, Mu Jiang's dissatisfaction nearly overflowed, but she still feared the Dowager Madame and dared not show it. She only looked at Zhou Yuanshen with a pitiful expression.","\"Eldest Aunt, I'll trouble you to look after Grandmother. Grandmother, my carriage will go ahead first to receive you at Chongyang Temple,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He bowed again.","He looked at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao also curtsied: \"Grandmother, I'll go with the Duke. Though everything has been arranged in advance, we still need to check it over once more to ensure all goes smoothly. The Duke is being filial, and I'll go to lend a hand.\"","She was very good at elevating herself. Mu Jiang curled her lip.","Seeing Mu Jiang staring intently, Cheng Zhao even asked her, \"Concubine Mu, would you like to come with us?\"","Zhou Yuanshen turned back to her: \"Our carriage goes too fast; it might jolt the pregnant woman. Concubine Mu's pregnancy is very important. Any mishap would be on our heads, wouldn't it, Grandmother?\"","Mu Jiang's heart leaped with joy, and a smile unconsciously appeared on her face.","Her child was far more important than Cheng Zhao, the Duchess.","The Dowager Madame remained kind and gentle: \"You go on ahead.\"","She took Huan Qingtang's arm, then boarded the carriage.","Standing nearby all along was a young woman in a bright red dress, a stranger who had remained silent, not uttering a single word."]},{"number":102,"title":"Chapter 102: Making a Grand Entrance in Costume","paragraphs":["On the carriage, Cheng Zhao turned her head away.","So, Zhou Yuanshen really liked being called “Third Brother” by others.","To him, Cheng Zhao and Mu Jiang were no different.","Mu Jiang’s repeated calls of “Third Brother” made Cheng Zhao feel as if the entire front gate of the Duke’s residence were their bedchamber, as if she had blundered into a private space meant for just the two of them.","They seemed oblivious, but in that instant, Cheng Zhao felt acutely awkward.","Yet she quickly composed herself.","Mu Jiang had always been Zhou Yuanshen’s woman—this was not something that had only just happened today. And her own hopes for bearing an heir still depended on him.","She decided to pretend she had gone deaf for a moment and heard nothing.","There were many pressing matters today; these trivial annoyances could wait.","“...Isn’t that young lady in the crimson dress Hu Zhiwei, Eldest Aunt’s niece?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanshen’s face was expressionless. “Yes.”","Cheng Zhao thought to herself, That is the future secondary concubine.","If the Dowager Madame had brought her along to the temple service, it meant she already had intentions. Without her approval, First Madam, née Song, would never have dared to bring Hu Zhiwei.","Cheng Zhao said nothing more.","Zhou Yuanshen also remained silent, even closing his eyes to doze off, his brow furrowed in deep thought.","Soon, their carriage arrived at the Chongyang Temple.","The rain seemed to have stopped, yet not quite; it lingered in the air like a thin mist, threatening to fall but never quite doing so.","Zhou Yuanshen jumped off the carriage. Without waiting for the driver to set down the mounting stool, he called out to Cheng Zhao, “Come down.”","As soon as Cheng Zhao leaned out, he wrapped his arm around her waist and lifted her down.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","The Chongyang Temple was not on a mountain, but still had over a hundred stone steps leading up to the vermilion platform before its gate.","The weather and the path were both damp. Zhou Yuanshen extended his hand toward Cheng Zhao. “Come.”","Their carriage was at the very front, followed by those of the Dowager Madame and the others; the maids’ carriages were at the rear.","The Dowager Madame’s carriage had not yet arrived, let alone the maids’.","Only a few guards were nearby; there was no one to assist Cheng Zhao.","The steps were indeed slippery, making it easy to lose one’s footing.","A few young Daoist priests had already come down to pay their respects.","The entire Chongyang Temple was solemn and orderly. No idlers loitered at the gate, and the steps had been scrubbed clean, not a single fallen leaf in sight.","The deferential attitude of everyone toward the Zhou family spoke volumes about their elevated status.","“It’s not proper,” Cheng Zhao hesitated.","She was the Duchess.","Under the watchful eyes of so many, how could she behave like a young girl and let him hold her hand up the steps? She was hardly a maiden just past her coming-of-age ceremony.","As she thought this, Zhou Yuanshen had already taken firm hold of her hand.","He had just lifted her down from the carriage in front of the young priests and the guards. Cheng Zhao kept reminding herself: Focus on the important matters; do not let your attention waver.","She did not pull her hand back but ascended the steps alongside Zhou Yuanshen.","Greeting them at the entrance was none other than the temple’s master, Grandmaster Yang.","“Old Immortal,”","the young couple addressed him respectfully, performing the customary greeting.","Grandmaster Yang smiled. “Lord Duke, Duchess, you are too kind. My Daoist name is Zhichang. Merely calling me Zhichang would be more than I deserve.”","“Old Immortal, you are far too modest,” Cheng Zhao replied with a smile. “Grandmother should be arriving soon. We’ll go inside first and check that everything is in perfect order.”","Grandmaster Yang nodded in agreement.","He instructed his two senior disciples to wait properly and report immediately when the Dowager Madame arrived. Then he accompanied Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen to inspect the venue where the ritual would be held today.","The entire temple was impeccably clean. Everything that could be washed had been washed; everything that could be repainted had been given a fresh coat of paint.","Even if the Empress Dowager herself were holding a Daoist service here, the standard of preparation could hardly have been better, Cheng Zhao mused.","She felt she had gained a new layer of understanding about the Dowager Madame.","The aristocratic clans treated the Dowager Madame with respect, but nowhere near as keenly as these temples and monasteries, which were truly masters at reading the minds of the powerful.","“Lord Duke, Duchess, there is something this old Daoist must report: Half an hour ago, a eunuch arrived from the palace, saying that Consort Dou will also be attending today to show her support. Is your esteemed household already aware of this?” Grandmaster Yang asked with a smile.","Zhou Yuanshen remained impassive.","Cheng Zhao glanced at him, then answered honestly, “We were in such a rush this morning, afraid of missing the auspicious hour, that we did not hear Grandmother mention it.”","She added with a smile to Grandmaster Yang, “Thank you, Old Immortal, for telling us. Now we are prepared.”","Grandmaster Yang chuckled. “You are too kind, Duchess. The Consort is most gracious and deeply favored by His Majesty.”","Of course she was favored.","Being able to leave the palace so freely to attend the Dowager Madame’s service was a clear sign of immense imperial grace—it was a rare privilege for any palace consort to leave the palace, often recorded by court historians.","“Consort Dou assists the Empress in managing the Six Palaces and is indeed deeply trusted by Their Majesties. Her presence today is also a mark of Their Majesties’ respect for Grandmother,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Then Zhou Yuanshen and Grandmaster Yang engaged in idle conversation.","Behind them, Cheng Zhao thought to herself: Three variables.","There were three unexpected factors in today’s ritual.","First, Grandmaster Yang was probing their relationship with the Dowager Madame. By deliberately mentioning Consort Dou, he wanted to confirm whether Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen had known about her arrival.","Second, Consort Dou’s attendance was the biggest variable, completely catching Cheng Zhao off guard. She had expected other noble ladies to send gifts, but never an imperial consort.","This even bent the rules of propriety.","Mu Jiang was far from being treated like a princess; it was the Dowager Madame who truly seemed like an “Empress Dowager in all but name.”","The third variable was Hu Zhiwei. Having her come along to the temple was also surprising.","Cheng Zhao wondered, Which of these three is putting on a performance, and which are merely innocent bystanders being used by the Dowager Madame to divert attention? Surely not all three are going to perform on the same stage?","By now, she firmly believed that the Dowager Madame planned to make an example of her today.","At the birthday banquet, Cheng Zhao had bested the eldest branch’s matriarch and daughter-in-law, and she held a noble title in her own right. In the Dowager Madame’s eyes, that was a complete imbalance of power.","Anyone who slipped out of control and threatened the Dowager Madame’s “rule” was guilty of a capital offense. If the household were compared to a court, Cheng Zhao was the rebellious minister plotting a coup.","She would either die or at least suffer grievously.","Cheng Zhao was reminded of her sea voyage.","She had always heard tales of great waves powerful enough to shatter a ship, and she had worried and feared.","But when the great wave truly came, the deathly dread and danger had instead stirred something in her—she even felt eager to see where fate would push her.","She had faced the great wave.","And she had won; she had survived.","Every crisis, she felt, was a trial before a great burden.","Just like now.","She was curious to see what the Dowager Madame would do to her.","While Cheng Zhao was lost in thought, a young Daoist priest came hurrying to report that the Dowager Madame’s carriage was approaching and would soon arrive.","They had actually sent someone ahead to wait halfway along the road.","Grandmaster Yang smiled and said to Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen, “Lord Duke, Duchess, please wait here a moment. This old Daoist will go greet our guest.”","“We’ll go together,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","The three of them returned to the main gate of Chongyang Temple.","No sooner had they arrived than the Dowager Madame’s carriage pulled up just in front.","This time, Grandmaster Yang did not wait on the vermilion platform. Instead, he personally descended the steps to help the Dowager Madame down from her carriage, as dutifully as one of her own household servants.","Zhou Yuanshen and Cheng Zhao also descended the steps. He still held her hand, afraid she might slip.","Everyone who alighted from their carriages—including the Dowager Madame herself—looked at their clasped hands.","Mu Jiang’s expression turned sour again in an instant.","As for Huan Qingtang, her gaze flickered over them briefly and then quickly withdrew.","Second Madam couldn’t help but laugh; Zhou Yuanqi muttered under his breath, “How improper, a brute with no manners.”","“Stop your nonsense. It’s you who has no manners. If you keep being so disrespectful, I’ll have to hit you,” Second Madam said through clenched teeth.","“You’re taking his side,” Zhou Yuanqi criticized both sides at once, scolding his elder brother and then his mother, not letting anyone silence him.","Second Madam: “...”"]},{"number":103,"title":"Chapter 103: Surrender, or Pacification","paragraphs":["The Zhou family had just settled into their seats at the Chongyang Temple when a servant came to announce that Consort Dou had arrived.","Everyone, who had barely sat down, rose again to greet her.","Cheng Zhao overheard Mu Jiang complain, “My hem dragged through the water, soaking my shoes and stockings. What a nuisance.”","She seemed to have said it deliberately for Zhou Yuanshen to hear.","Whether he heard or not was uncertain, but the Dowager Madame did.","The Dowager Madame smiled and said, “If that’s the case, let Ah Jiang be sent back first. I told you last time to rest more and stop running around before the birth...”","Mu Jiang shuddered.","Thus, Cheng Zhao understood that last time, after Mu Jiang had killed the crane, the Dowager Madame had intended to confine her to her quarters. But for some reason, she had been let out again, and on an important day like this, she had to be brought along.","It was because she carried the next generation of the Zhou family in her womb, and the Dowager Madame pinned her hopes on her.","Mu Jiang understood the Dowager Madame’s warning and stopped muttering.","Everyone waited on the stone steps at the entrance of the Chongyang Temple.","Before long, a group of about thirty people slowly ascended the steps.","Every one of them was dressed in plain clothes. The guards were disguised as retainers, and the eunuchs wore stewards’ robes.","A woman in an apricot-colored silk gown embroidered with a pattern of crabapple blossoms, her head adorned with gold-inlaid jade jewelry, appeared to be about the same age as the Second Madam. She was helped up the steps by a young gentleman.","“Who is he?” Zhou Yuanqi suddenly asked.","Though Cheng Zhao did not recognize him personally, she knew the relationships among the group.","The young gentleman who could assist Consort Dou was undoubtedly the Fifth Prince, Helian Hong.","He was betrothed to Sun Zhiya of the Marquis Qingyuan’s household, which was the Dowager Madame’s natal family.","Thus, Consort Dou and the Dowager Madame shared a deeper connection.","“First, rule out the Consort,” Cheng Zhao thought to herself.","There was definitely no plan involving Consort Dou in the Dowager Madame’s scheme today. The reason was simple: the Dowager Madame could not afford any mishap.","If anything went wrong, it would damage her relationship with the Consort, thereby jeopardizing the marriage alliance between her Sun family and the Fifth Prince.","The Dowager Madame must have known that Cheng Zhao was not easy to deal with.","Yet, by chance, the Consort’s presence, being so exalted, would naturally draw everyone’s attention, diverting their focus.","As this thought crossed her mind, Cheng Zhao joined the Zhou family in bowing to the Consort and the Fifth Prince.","“Dowager Madame, you must not,” Consort Dou said softly, stepping forward to support the Dowager Madame’s hand, her smile gentle. “You are holding a sacrificial ceremony; I have only come to join in the fun. I’d be troubling you if I made you bow to me.”","She then turned to the Fifth Prince and said, “You still haven’t offered your birthday congratulations to the Dowager Madame. Make up for it now.”","The Fifth Prince was sixteen this year. Unlike the Crown Prince’s pale complexion, he had a healthy, ruddy look.","His features closely resembled the Consort’s, not the Emperor’s at all, which made him very handsome without any hint of gloom.","He stepped forward, paid his respects sincerely, and offered belated congratulations to the Dowager Madame before lifting his eyes.","He also bowed to the Zhou family’s First Madam and Second Madam.","Then, turning his gaze, his eyes fell on Cheng Zhao.","To her surprise, he seemed to be stunned for a moment, staring at her without shifting his gaze.","Cheng Zhao had already lowered her eyes, suppressing the urge to frown.","Nothing was said, for at that moment, the Marquis Qingyuan’s household—the Dowager Madame’s natal family—arrived.","This time, not only the Marchioness came, but also Marquis Qingyuan himself, along with his two children, Sun Zhiya and Sun Zhisheng.","They exchanged greetings.","When Sun Zhiya saw her betrothed, the Fifth Prince, she blushed shyly.","“Your Highness,” she greeted him in a delicate voice.","The Fifth Prince replied, “Rise.”","His tone was gentle.","The Dowager Madame took Consort Dou’s arm and led her into the Chongyang Temple. Then, Nanny Sun, the Dowager Madame’s trusted aide, approached and instructed, “If any more guests arrive, send them back.”","The steward acknowledged the order.","When the time came, the ritual began.","People arrived one after another to offer gifts, all sacrifices.","Managing the gifts was Cheng Zhao’s responsibility at home, so it was the same here.","Every time someone came, she had to rise to receive them, then convey the Dowager Madame’s message: “Today, the Zhou family is performing a peace ritual; it’s not convenient to entertain guests.”","Thus, she courteously sent them on their way.","Many households only sent stewards bearing gifts. Those who came in person without being close relatives were eager to curry favor and were turned away at the gate.","After bustling about for a while, Cheng Zhao found a side room to rest. There, she heard someone speaking, “I’ve come to deliver tea to the Duchess.”","It was a strange, young, soft female voice.","Suyue replied, “The Young Madam is resting. Miss Hu, it’s best you return to the Dowager Madame’s side.”","Cheng Zhao called from inside, “Suyue, let Miss Hu come in.”","Though Suyue was reluctant, she obeyed her mistress without question and opened the door.","The resting room was simple. Cheng Zhao sat at the table, resting her head on her hand. When she saw the visitor enter, she sat up straight.","Hu Zhiwei carried a tray with a teapot and a cup.","She set down the tray and knelt before Cheng Zhao, performing a grand bow. “Duchess, this humble girl begs your forgiveness.”","Cheng Zhao’s heart stirred slightly.","“Miss Hu, please rise. I am not worthy of such a grand courtesy,” Cheng Zhao said, remaining seated without reaching out to help her up, her expression somewhat cold.","“Duchess, please grant me a chance to speak a few heartfelt words,” Hu Zhiwei said. “Last time...”","She paused, glancing tentatively at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao did not interrupt, merely watched her quietly.","After a beat, Hu Zhiwei resumed, “Last time, when my aunt ordered me to storm the Morning Glow Courtyard, I had no choice. I’m an orphan living under someone else’s roof. If I don’t obey, I’ll be kicked out.","It wasn’t just me—I also rely on my mother. Her health is poor; she can’t bear any turmoil.”","As she spoke, her eyes reddened, and tears streamed down her face.","Cheng Zhao paused briefly before saying, “Still, you shouldn’t have broken into the duke’s private study. When your elders do wrong, you should advise them against it.”","“Yes, it’s all my weakness and cowardice. Duchess, my aunt also wanted me to put forth effort, trying to push me onto the duke.","I swear to you, I will never do anything so shameful again. I beg you to think of a way for me, to find a suitable marriage. My aunt is determined to use me.” Hu Zhiwei choked back sobs.","Cheng Zhao watched her wordlessly.","After a long while, she finally said, “Rise. You truly are pitiable.”","Hu Zhiwei broke into a smile through her tears and quickly stood up.","Cheng Zhao said, “Go about your business. Don’t be seen. From now on, stay by the side of the First Madam. If you have any news, bring it to me. Whether you want a future or wealth, exchange it for news.”","Hu Zhiwei replied, “Yes, thank you for showing me a way to live, Duchess! I won’t let you down!”","With that, she left.","After she departed, Suyue and Qiubai came in.","Suyue looked worried. “Young Madam, what did she say?”","“Some words of self-defense. She wants to pledge allegiance to me,” Cheng Zhao said.","Qiubai asked, “Do you think she is trustworthy?”","“Of course not,” Cheng Zhao said with a light laugh. “Do you know how she addressed me? Over and over, she called me ‘Duchess.’”","“What’s wrong with that?”","“No one in the family or among close acquaintances calls me ‘Duchess’ as a flattery.","Such groveling is meant to win my favor and my trust, clearly knowing what I value most,” Cheng Zhao explained.","She added, \"Such scheming—she's not First Madam's person, but the Dowager Madame's.\"","Suyue shuddered. \"The Dowager Madame? What does she intend to do?\""]},{"number":104,"title":"Chapter 104: Cheng Zhao's Sharp Tongue","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao mused.","At this point, she had mostly figured out the situation.","Suyue and Qiubai were watching her, a bit tense, but Cheng Zhao comforted them in turn. \"Don't be afraid.\"","She smiled calmly.","That smile gave Qiubai and Suyue confidence, and the two of them promptly steadied themselves, their spirits revived.","\"Go ahead and get busy,\" Cheng Zhao said as she stood up.","As they walked out of the side chambers in the rear courtyard of the Taoist temple, they unexpectedly encountered a young nobleman.","He stood tall and graceful, elegant as a bamboo. Young in years, the youth was slender yet handsome, his gaze especially intense.","He stood there looking at Cheng Zhao but did not approach.","Cheng Zhao pretended not to notice and quickly walked away.","\"That's the Fifth Prince,\" Suyue said in a low voice. \"What is he staring at?\"","\"It seems he's staring at the Young Madam,\" Qiubai replied. \"Could he have lingered here on purpose just to get a look?\"","Cheng Zhao's brows were already furrowed.","Up to now, the only members of the imperial family she found pleasant were those close to the Rong Prince's household—likely because the Rong Prince's heir, Helian Jian, was a friend of Fan Xiao and Zhou Yuanshen. Birds of a feather flock together.","Apart from that, the Emperor, the Crown Prince, the Andong Commandery Prince Helian Xuan, and the Fifth Prince Helian Hong who had come today all seemed to clash with Cheng Zhao's nature. An inexplicable wave of irritation rose within her.","Before she could say anything, Suyue had already grown angry. \"Cad!\"","She added, \"He looks quite young, yet he stoops to such low behavior. His mother and betrothed are both here, yet he still runs off to stare at the Young Madam.\"","Qiubai quickly told her to keep her voice down. \"The Young Madam is the Duchess of the Zhou family. Even a prince wouldn't dare to act rashly. Don't make a scene and give them a handle to use against us.\"","Cheng Zhao nodded. \"Right now, anyone and anything could be a distraction. Don't let yourself be diverted.\"","Dealing with a prince was actually quite difficult.","Their status and position weighed down on you; you couldn't make a move. A minor slip could cost you your life, or at worst, bring ruin upon your entire clan. It wasn't that he was formidable, but that his identity and rank were.","A father could kill his son, but a son who killed his father would be sentenced to death; the imperial clan could be executed by the Emperor, but no one else could touch them.","For now, Cheng Zhao had no way to handle a prince. She could only sidestep him and focus on resolving the matters at hand.","She returned to the bell tower. The first session of the fasting rites had ended, and then came the drama selection: the play titles were announced before the shrine, and a few were chosen to be performed, meant as an offering to the gods. This too was part of the Daoist rites.","When Cheng Zhao returned, the Dowager Madame beckoned her over.","\"Have all the monetary gifts been distributed?\" the Dowager Madame asked her.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"Yes, they've all been given out.\"","She also told the Dowager Madame which households had sent managing servants and which had sent their madams or wives in person.","The Dowager Madame smiled slightly. \"You handle things thoroughly. Go sit down and listen to the opera.\"","When the morning \"fasting rites\" concluded and everyone took a short break, Grandmaster Yang arrived again.","He carried a tray lined with crimson satin, on which lay wooden hairpins.","\"This is a small token from the temple. I made them myself, steeped in elixir water. They can clear the mind and calm the spirit and also accumulate blessings,\" Grandmaster Yang said.","\"How could we put you to such trouble?\" the Dowager Madame said with a smile.","\"The Dowager Madame is too kind. I am an old Daoist of modest means, and can only offer these humble items as a tribute.\"","The Dowager Madame thanked him again. She deferred to Consort Dou, inviting her to choose first, but the Consort insisted on taking after the Dowager Madame.","After a round of polite refusals, the Dowager Madame took one and had her maid pin it up for her.","Everyone else then helped themselves.","Cheng Zhao also took one.","She sniffed it and caught a faint scent of sandalwood, along with some other medicinal fragrances. It smelled very pleasant.","Just as she was about to put it in her hair, Hu Zhiwei, who was standing beside her, suddenly said, \"Duchess, let me put it in for you.\"","She happened to be standing directly behind Suyue.","Her voice was not very low, and many people heard her. Sun Zhiya even cast a curious glance.","The First Madam was a bit surprised and somewhat displeased; she shot Hu Zhiwei a meaningful look.","With so many eyes on her, refusing would be a direct slight to the First Madam and a show of disrespect toward an elder—especially with Consort Dou present.","Cheng Zhao had no choice but to smile. \"Thank you.\"","The Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi looked over; Zhou Yuanshen remained expressionless, not glancing that way, taking a wooden hairpin himself.","Mu Jiang walked over to him, wanting him to help pin it on, but he avoided her.","The temple had prepared a midday meal. The party followed the Dowager Madame down the tower and through the second mountain gate to eat.","During the meal, Cheng Zhao exchanged hushed words with the Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi. The three of them talked for a good while.","The Second Madam looked around several times.","When Zhou Yuanshen glanced over, he saw his mother and Zhou Yuanqi blocking Cheng Zhao, who was doing something out of sight behind them.","After the meal, everyone moved toward the main hall at the front.","The morning rain had stopped, but the sun had not yet come out and there was no wind. The mountain paths were still damp. Supported by maids and serving women, everyone walked slowly.","Sun Zhiya of the Qingyuan Hou Mansion leaned close to the Dowager Madame. \"Great-Aunt, how about we stroll around level by level and walk off our meal?\"","Clearing-up Hou's wife quickly said, \"Ya'er, don't be willful. The mountain road is slippery.\"","\"It's already cleared up. This is such a rare outing, and the Chongyang Temple is the foremost temple in the capital. I've longed to visit it,\" Sun Zhiya said.","The Dowager Madame chuckled. \"Ya'er's sincere devotion will surely bring merit to today's rites. Well then, let's take a stroll.\"","Everyone agreed.","As they went up the steps, Sun Zhiya deliberately nudged Cheng Zhao, trying to push her to the back so she would be with the Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi.","She did not want Cheng Zhao getting close to Zhou Yuanshen and Huan Qingtang.","But Cheng Zhao seemed to lose her footing. Flailing, she grabbed Sun Zhiya's hair, loosening her chignon and pulling painfully at her scalp.","Sun Zhiya cried out in alarm.","Everyone turned to look at them, including the Fifth Prince, Helian Hong, and Consort Dou—the one her future husband, the other her future mother-in-law. Sun Zhiya dared not show an ugly expression.","Eyes brimming with tears, she looked at the Dowager Madame and then at the Fifth Prince. \"Third Sister-in-Law couldn't keep her balance. When she slipped, she yanked my hair and even shoved me.\"","The Dowager Madame said, \"That's nonsense...\"","Consort Dou laughed lightly. \"Don't be upset, Dowager Madame. Children just playing. Ya'er is refined and proper, born into a capital family. Her ancestors for three or five generations are not some rural upstarts from some province.\"","She added with a smirk, \"You wouldn't know, Dowager Madame—what with the instability in the court in earlier years, some backwater wealthy folk now call themselves local aristocratic houses. How they puff themselves up.\"","Among those present, only Cheng Zhao's family was from a commandery clan. The rest were native to the capital.","This was a dig at Cheng Zhao.","Consort Dou was from the Piguo Duke's Mansion.","Cheng Zhao remembered that years earlier, there had been a case involving the Piguo Duke. The Grand Court had been unable to reach a verdict, and in the end, it was her grandfather who submitted a memorial to the Emperor, requesting a special decree to settle it.","As a result, the Piguo Duke was penalized with a two-year salary forfeiture and ten strokes of the rod.","A punishment harsh enough for a duke meant it was a case involving a death.","Cheng Zhao was not completely certain of the details; she had been young at the time, and her grandfather had likely withheld them, thinking she was too young to hear such things.","Now Consort Dou still held a grudge and was publicly stepping on Cheng Zhao.","No wonder she had felt hostile from the start—Cheng Zhao had assumed it was because the Fifth Prince had cast too many glances her way.","\"The Consort speaks truly,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Wild swans are born on the border, fine birds raised in ornate halls. We have indeed lived too comfortably and can no longer even walk steadily. It has indeed been a lapse in dignified upbringing. Please forgive our poor manners.\"","Second Madam: “...”","This daughter-in-law’s tongue spared neither the senior branch’s mother-in-law nor daughter-in-law—she wouldn’t even let the Consort off.","The Fifth Prince glanced at her again.","Zhou Yuanshen stepped forward without a change in expression, shielding Cheng Zhao.","The Dowager Madame smiled. “Let’s continue our stroll. Someone, take Zhiya to have her hair tidied.”","She brushed the matter aside as if nothing had happened.","But Consort Dou was furious.","She had only called the Cheng family “country bumpkins,” and Cheng Zhao had compared her own family to wild geese and Consort Dou to a caged canary.","Consort Dou held power in the palace, and the Dou family wielded influence at court. She had even dared to bully the Empress—when had she ever swallowed such humiliation?","Such blatant disrespect toward a noble—she truly deserved to be dragged away and beaten to death.","And Cheng Zhao had been the Empress’s choice for marriage, too. Consort Dou’s gaze turned cold, a glint of murderous intent flickering in her eyes."]},{"number":105,"title":"Chapter 105: Is Cheng Zhao in Trouble","paragraphs":["The Dowager Madame led the group as they strolled through the layers of the Double Ninth Temple.","They arrived at a small shrine, where clay statues of Thousand-Mile Eyes and Wind-Following Ears stood at the entrance, with the Heavenly Empress enshrined inside; behind it stretched a vast bamboo grove.","The rain had stopped, but a faint mist still lingered among the bamboo. The colors within the grove were lush, emerald green interspersed with golden yellow.","Sun Zhiya remarked in surprise, “Is this bamboo grove dying? Why are there so many withered leaves?”","The Dowager Madame chuckled and said, “In spring, new bamboo shoots sprout and leaves renew, so the old leaves turn yellow at this time.”","Eldest Madam Song smiled, “I never noticed that. How observant of Zhiya.”","Mu Jiang sniffed lightly, “That’s just ignorance.”","Consort Dou grew even more displeased.","After all, Sun Zhiya was her future daughter-in-law; even if she had some flaws, it was not for others to criticize her.","“A-Jiang has a sharp tongue, and that temper of hers has been spoiled by me,” the Dowager Madame said with a laugh.","The Marchioness of Qingyuan quickly chimed in, adding a few words of praise for Mu Jiang.","Grandmaster Yang arrived again.","The Dowager Madame smiled and asked, “Is it about to begin?”","“It has already begun, Dowager Madame. You needn’t sit and wait; let the junior disciples conduct the rites themselves,” Grandmaster Yang said with a smile. “Have you enjoyed your stroll? Are your feet tired?”","“The Double Ninth Temple has excellent feng shui; it nourishes one’s vitality. I don’t feel tired at all while walking,” the Dowager Madame replied.","She then added, “Grandmaster, since we’re at leisure, how about giving A-Jiang’s unborn child a childhood name and writing a name-talisman to be placed before the gods? When the child is born, we can come back to retrieve it.”","Grandmaster Yang eagerly agreed, saying it was an honor.","He suggested “Minxing,” which the Dowager Madame found suitable. Mu Jiang thought it lacked refinement and backbone, but she dared not act out.","Taking this opportunity, Grandmaster Yang began fortune-telling for everyone.","According to his readings, each person’s fate was auspicious; he could explain precisely why it was good, never repeating himself, and striking a chord in everyone’s heart.","But when it came to Cheng Zhao, Grandmaster Yang praised her as “blessed by Heaven,” drawing the attention of Consort Dou and the Fifth Prince.","This praise seemed excessive.","Second Madam, who had been silent all along, could no longer hold back. “Old Immortal, please don’t overpraise her. She’s just a child and cannot bear such flattery.”","What was deemed great fortune might instead invite misfortune—this was clearly a veiled attempt to exalt only to harm Cheng Zhao.","Grandmaster Yang smiled and said, “Third Young Madam indeed has a noble fate, which is why she faces many trials. Recently, she is due for a calamity. If she cannot avoid it, she may suffer bloodshed—at worst, her life could be at risk.”","Second Madam’s expression shifted again.","The brothers Zhou Yuanshen and Zhou Yuanqi both turned to look at Cheng Zhao, their gazes probing.","Cheng Zhao seemed flustered and quickly asked, “Old Immortal, what should I do?”","“Boundless longevity. Do not panic, Young Madam. This old Taoist will devise a solution for you. Ahead lies the bamboo grove. I will set up an array there, and if you enter and meditate for half a day, it may ward off the disaster,” Grandmaster Yang said.","Cheng Zhao asked, “Is it that simple?”","Everyone’s thoughts were mixed.","Second Madam held her breath, her expression very grim. “Grandmaster, I fear you’ve misjudged?”","“Mother, please don’t question the Old Immortal,” Cheng Zhao said. “Old Immortal, please dispel my misfortune!”","Second Madam called out urgently, “Zhaozhao!”","She desperately tried to signal her.","What calamity was there to dispel? This was clearly slander. First, he praised her, then sought to undermine her.","Yet Cheng Zhao seemed bewitched, trusting the words completely. Could everyone have a flaw—was Cheng Zhao’s simply a superstitious devotion to Taoists?","Second Madam grew frantic.","Zhou Yuanshen spoke from the side, “Since that’s the case, I request the Old Immortal to set up the formation for my wife.”","The onlookers grew even more curious.","Consort Dou wondered if there was a way to have her die within that array, given that Grandmaster Yang did possess real skill.","Or perhaps...","She signaled her attendant eunuch with a glance.","The Dowager Madame gazed at Cheng Zhao with a kind expression.","Earlier, a Daoist ritual was ongoing, so the crowd waited briefly before returning to the main hall to sit, leaving Cheng Zhao alone in the bamboo grove.","Several members of the Second Branch accompanied her for a while.","Then, they went upstairs.","The Dowager Madame asked with concern, “How is Cheng?”","“She is meditating,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “The Old Immortal said an hour would suffice to dispel the misfortune. He is highly accomplished, and I trust his words.”","The Dowager Madame nodded slightly. “It’s fortunate we encountered the Grandmaster today to ward off Cheng’s disaster.”","Huan Qingtang sat quietly beside them, her gaze unflinching.","Eldest Madam Song silently speculated, though unsure of what was truly happening.","Zhou Yuanshen had returned, but Zhou Yuanqi had not.","After a long while, Zhou Yuanqi finally came upstairs. As a child, he moved stealthily, unnoticed by most.","He whispered something to Zhou Yuanshen, and Mu Jiang overheard, “There’s no compass...”","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “Don’t worry about it. Just sit still.”","“Don’t be smug. Without a compass, you can’t...”","Noticing Mu Jiang eavesdropping, Zhou Yuanqi fell silent and even shot her a sharp glare. Mu Jiang found the little brat annoying, but she couldn’t stoop to his level, as he was her brother-in-law after all.","She asked ingratiatingly, “Yuanqi, what are you two discussing?”","“Naturally, we’re talking about economics and scholarship. Do you understand that?” Zhou Yuanqi retorted.","Mu Jiang: “...”","Zhou Yuanshen said flatly, “Don’t be impertinent.”","A warmth filled Mu Jiang’s heart.","Third Brother always stood up for her.","Zhou Yuanqi snorted and sat upright.","When the Fifth Prince, Helian Hong, quietly slipped downstairs, Mu Jiang noticed him; Sun Zhiya immediately followed.","Mu Jiang curled her lip in disdain. “Running after the Fifth Prince like that—how pathetic is it?”","She held nearly everyone in contempt.","The eunuch personally attending Consort Dou came upstairs and whispered something in her ear; she simply nodded slightly.","Second Madam sat restlessly, several times on the verge of rising, only to be held back by Zhou Yuanqi.","Zhou Yuanshen soon also went downstairs.","Mu Jiang couldn’t resist wanting to follow and take a look, but was stopped by Nanny Sun, who served the Dowager Madame.","“Secondary Wife, with child, it’s best not to wander around,” Nanny Sun said.","Mu Jiang replied, “I just need to get some air.”","“Please sit down. Don’t worry the Dowager Madame,” Nanny Sun said coldly.","Mu Jiang had no choice but to stay seated.","She knew something was afoot, yet didn’t know exactly what was being orchestrated.","Cheng Zhao had gone to meditate. Without her, shouldn’t everyone be happier and more at ease? Why did everyone seem unable to sit still?","Were they trying to help her, or to harm her?","Mu Jiang had always detested Cheng Zhao, and if something befell her, Mu Jiang would be delighted.","Unable to leave, she had to sit there patiently.","Fortunately, Zhou Yuanshen returned soon, as if he had merely taken a brief stroll downstairs.","This time, he didn’t move again.","Mu Jiang looked at him, feeling reassured and calm. The Dowager Madame and Huan Qingtang sat together, both composed and unruffled, eyes fixed straight ahead.","Mu Jiang felt as if she had been sitting forever; the Taoist rites made her drowsy, and when the theatrical performance was about to begin, she almost fell asleep.","She was indeed nodding off when a piercing, abrupt cry burst into her ears, jolting her awake.","“Disaster! Disaster, Grandmaster!”","The young acolyte’s voice was tinged with tears and terror.","Mu Jiang’s drowsiness vanished completely, a surge of joy rising in her: Could Cheng Zhao have run into trouble?","Everyone present reacted differently. Second Madam sprang to her feet, panic-stricken and pale, while the rest remained remarkably calm.","Grandmaster Yang said with a smile, “My little disciple, don’t panic. What could have happened?”","Without waiting for the young acolyte to speak, he turned to apologize to the Dowager Madame and Consort Dou. “Forgive me for disturbing Your Graces.”","“What trouble could there be, Old Immortal?” Consort Dou replied with a smile, unconcerned. “Children are always excitable.”","The Dowager Madame also smiled. \"Quickly, ask what is wrong with the child. Don't let him be too anxious.\"","Only then did Grandmaster Yang turn to the young acolyte. \"What has happened?\"","\"Grandmaster, someone has died. Someone has died!\" the young acolyte said, barely suppressing the tremor in his voice."]},{"number":106,"title":"Chapter 106: Madam's Crushing Defeat","paragraphs":["\"A death!\"","These words fell like thunderclaps on everyone's eardrums. Whether genuinely terrified or merely feigning it, all present abruptly rose to their feet, faces etched with shock.","\"Who died?\" The first to speak was Second Madam.","Second Madam could not stop trembling. She fixed her gaze on the young Daoist acolyte, desperately trying to read the answer on his face. She struggled to compose herself, her nails digging into her palm.","\"Speak!\" As if unable to wait for the acolyte to open his mouth, she urged him harshly again, even threatening to charge down the stairs.","Though only a few seconds had passed, she felt as though the acolyte was deliberately stalling.","\"It is the Fifth Prince.\" The acolyte knelt on the ground, weeping.","His voice was choked with sobs.","But the scene fell silent.","Second Madam stood frozen in place.","Everyone present had initially assumed the deceased was Cheng Zhao, hence their earlier expressions—whether shock, surprise, or schadenfreude—had all been performed accordingly. Now, each person seemed to lose their bearings for a moment.","Then, the reaction came flooding in like a tidal wave.","Consort Dou's face drained of color, her voice even more shrill than Second Madam's: \"Who?\"","Grandmaster Yang's immortal and detached demeanor vanished. His cheek seemed to twitch violently: \"The old Daoist... the old Daoist will go see what happened. Where?\"","He yanked the young acolyte to his feet and strode downstairs in long, hurried steps.","He wanted to get ahead and inspect the matter with his own eyes. If there was anything to intercept or control, he would act first to prevent it.","Consort Dou shrieked frantically behind him: \"Impossible! It can't be Hong'er! He was clearly...\"","Grandmaster Yang quickened his pace, and the crowd stumbled and staggered after him, all rushing toward the bamboo grove.","Second Madam was about to hurry after them, but Zhou Yuan Shen pulled her back: \"Mother.\"","\"Ah Shen, what happened?\" Second Madam asked him urgently, her tone frantic. \"What happened? Where is Zhaozhao?\"","\"Mother, just let things unfold as they will. But speak as little as possible,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He released Second Madam's hand. \"Walk slowly.\"","Zhou Yuanqi then took Second Madam's arm: \"Mother, let's go together.\"","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at his younger brother, gave him a nod, and strode off to catch up with the Dowager Madame. Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi were left at the rear, with even the maids, servants, and stewards rushing ahead of them.","They had barely entered the bamboo grove when they saw a thin mist, and a person was screaming hysterically.","Daoists and guards tried to restrain her, but she raved like a madwoman: \"Stay away! I'm not afraid of evil spirits! You won't kill me!\"","She brandished a short dagger, swinging it wildly in all directions, her hand dripping with blood. No one could get near her.","The right side of her face was covered in blood—whether her own or someone else's, no one could tell.","It was Sun Zhiya, the daughter of the Qingyuan Hou Mansion, and the Fifth Prince's betrothed.","Everyone felt dizzy at the sight.","And the Fifth Prince, Helian Hong, lay collapsed on the ground nearby, blood gushing continuously from his neck, soaking his clothes completely.","His eyes were wide open. He did not move.","Grandmaster Yang disregarded the blade in Sun Zhiya's hand, took a cut from her knife, and moved forward to pin her down.","In the struggle, his movements were perhaps too forceful, and her hair came loose.","Huan Qingtang saw Grandmaster Yang remove a wooden hairpin from Sun Zhiya's head. But she quickly averted her gaze.","Consort Dou knelt before her son, gently touching his face. She felt only stiffness under her hand, and then her eyes rolled back as she fainted.","Chaos erupted in full force.","Another young Daoist ran over and said to Zhou Yuanshen: \"Lord Duke, something has happened to your wife.\"","Zhou Yuanshen hurried forward with the Daoist.","Second Madam wanted to follow, but Zhou Yuanqi held her back, preventing her from moving.","It was utter pandemonium.","This Daoist ritual became the talk of the capital for a long time.","The Fifth Prince was dead, Sun Zhiya had gone mad, and the Duchess, Cheng, had been bitten by a venomous snake.","The Dowager Madame sat in Shou'an Courtyard, her face ashen. Nanny Sun, afraid she might faint, kept soothing her and patting her back.","She could not speak.","The Dowager Madame had arranged this ritual. She had enlisted Grandmaster Yang's help to make Cheng Zhao lose face in front of Consort Dou.","Then, the Dowager Madame would put on an act and go weep before the Empress, complaining that Cheng Zhao had disgraced the Zhou family and even implicated the household. The Emperor, hoping to placate her, would order Zhou Yuanshen to take a secondary wife, thereby continuing the family line through a dual marriage arrangement.","By then, neither the Cheng family nor the Guo family would have a right to object.","As long as they did not oppose the dual-marriage arrangement, opposition from other court officials would be far weaker, and the Emperor could override everyone and grant the marriage by imperial decree.","Grandmaster Yang was skilled in arranging formations.","In the bamboo grove formation, if someone wore his talisman while within the array, the concentrated malevolent energy within the formation would afflict them within an hour, clouding their mind.","It might cause confusion for a day or two.","\"At most two days. Usually, they can wake up and slowly recover, becoming no different from normal,\" Grandmaster Yang had said.","The Dowager Madame had thought: \"As long as her mind is clouded, she must take medicine.\"","And what medicine entered the Zhou household was hardly beyond the Dowager Madame's control.","Whether Cheng Zhao stayed confused for a day or two, or dragged on for a month or two, all depended on the Dowager Madame's progress with the dual-marriage arrangement.","She had full faith in Grandmaster Yang.","Nanny Sun was not as convinced: \"Is he truly that powerful? If so, how could His Majesty the Emperor tolerate his presence?\"","The Dowager Madame replied: \"He is a transcendent being beyond the mortal world; the Emperor admires him rather than opposes him. Since he says it can be done, it must be true.\"","Nanny Sun then said: \"Since she can be confused for a day or two, why not make her completely insane? Let her die from madness in two years, and that would save you trouble.\"","The Dowager Madame responded: \"That is possible, but I fear the Cheng family would not let the matter rest. If she falls ill for half a month, the Chengs will surely find a way to intervene. She is already inside the household, and making her suffer is not difficult. First, secure the imperial decree for the dual marriage. Later, we can still toy with her like a little monkey, just for amusement.\"","The Dowager Madame even thought that having Cheng Zhao mad for a while would work in her favor; killing her outright was disadvantageous, as they needed someone to restrain Huan Qingtang.","Look at the Eldest Madam Song—lacking competition, she had regressed in her ways, which disappointed the Dowager Madame deeply.","To make Huan Qingtang excellent and obedient, Cheng Zhao was the leash to restrain her. The Dowager Madame saw no need for Cheng Zhao's death.","But at this critical juncture, she needed her to contribute to the dual-marriage scheme.","Inviting Consort Dou was a suggestion she had made to the Emperor, and the Emperor had helped make it happen.","Consort Dou's presence at the Chongyang Temple was merely to bear witness: the Duchess had gone mad, and the Zhou family needed a new Duchess.","What no one expected was that the Fifth Prince would die.","And that he would be killed by a maddened Sun Zhiya.","The dagger had belonged to the Fifth Prince himself; he normally wore it as an ornament.","Why had Sun Zhiya gone mad?","The Dowager Madame recollected the earlier incident: Sun Zhiya had bumped into Cheng Zhao, and Cheng Zhao seemed to have tugged at her hair bun. In that single motion, had she switched the hairpin?","Or had Grandmaster Yang's \"talisman\" been mistakenly handed out from the start?","The Dowager Madame did not know.","Sun Zhiya had killed a prince. How this crime would be sentenced, the Dowager Madame could not say. But she knew this matter would not be easily settled.","Regardless of the law or imperial authority, the Sun family was about to face ruin.","The Sun family was her maternal family, the family she had always championed. Every woman, beyond her husband's household, was also defined by the excellence of her birth.","The \"birth family\" was part of her social standing, and a crucial part.","\"Dowager Madame, please don't be too angry. You must recover quickly and make plans for the Qingyuan Hou Mansion,\" Nanny Sun said in a low voice.","Sun Zhiya was likely beyond saving.","The Chongyang Temple would also be implicated.","For murdering a prince, the entire Qingyuan Hou Mansion would be...","To save himself, Grandmaster Yang might even reveal what the Dowager Madame had confided in him.","The Dowager Madame became so agitated that she choked and coughed up blood.","Nanny Sun’s face turned deathly pale: “Dowager Madame, Dowager Madame, please don’t frighten this old servant. Lie down quickly and ease yourself.”"]},{"number":107,"title":"Chapter 107: The Marquis's Final Move","paragraphs":["One night, the Emperor changed into civilian clothes and quietly arrived at the Duke of Cheng's mansion.","Seeing the Dowager Madame's face ashen gray, looking as if she were at death's door, the Emperor's heart felt as if it were being cut with a knife.","\"Mother-in-law, please don't be too grieved,\" the Emperor comforted her. \"I will grant Lady Huan and Yuanshen a marriage. As for Hong'er's death, it will all be blamed on the Chongyang Temple—it won't implicate the Sun family.\"","The Dowager Madame was extremely weak.","\"Zhuo'er,\" she called the Emperor softly by his name.","The Emperor's heart warmed, and he nearly shed tears. In the days of his own anxiety and unease, he had been only sixteen or seventeen—young and confused.","He was born of the previous Emperor's empress, but he had several full brothers and even more half-brothers. He was mediocre, and no one paid him any attention.","He suffered the contempt of his father, the Emperor, and his mother, the Empress. The palace staff fawned on the powerful and trampled on the weak—everyone looked down on him. Even his wife treated him with little regard.","The only one who had cherished him and trusted him was his mother-in-law.","She was his true mother.","She knew his fears, understood his forbearance, and comprehended his strange interests. In those most helpless days, she was like a bright lantern, illuminating his pitch-black world.","A kind mother—that was the Dowager Madame.","\"Zhuo'er, I understand your heart. You always want the Duke of Cheng's mansion to prosper—you granted it the title of dukedom after your ascension, and it is most precious to you,\" the Dowager Madame said.","The Emperor nodded deeply. \"Yes, Mother-in-law.\"","\"But the children of the Zhou family are not ambitious,\" the Dowager Madame said, gently closing her eyes. When she opened them again after a moment, there were tears in her gaze.","\"I understand all of it,\" the Emperor said.","\"For the lineage to thrive, the Zhou family must enjoy lasting prosperity,\" the Dowager Madame said. \"To grant a marriage under the dual-heir system—those old wretches at court will never agree. They will oppose you.\"","The words \"old wretches\" echoed the Emperor's own sentiments.","He had been on the throne for over a decade, yet the senior ministers still did not respect him.","Even when he unleashed bloody purges, he could not intimidate them.","They did not fear him; instead, they turned around and terrorized him.","And he—he was truly frightened by them.","He wanted the Duke of Cheng's mansion to thrive; he wanted to bestow another wife upon the Duke of Cheng, but he could not achieve it. The old ministers would always fabricate arguments to oppose him.","\"This time, I intended to make Cheng Shi stumble, to cloud her judgment for a few days, and clear the obstacles for you. But I never imagined the Fifth Prince would be so ill-fated as to die in the midst of it all,\" the Dowager Madame said.","The Emperor sighed. \"He was born under a bad star. Dying at the hands of a woman shows his incompetence. How could he let himself be stabbed to death by a powerless young girl? He is nothing like me.\"","Tears rolled down the Dowager Madame's cheeks.","\"Do not grieve for him,\" the Emperor said. \"His maternal grandfather is the Duke of Pi. Who knows if he was coveting my throne? A good riddance.\"","The Dowager Madame wiped away her tears and sighed gently again.","\"I am afraid the Duke of Pi's mansion will not let this rest, and there will be more trouble at court. The Sun family of Marquis Qingyuan's mansion is innocent. That rogue Grandmaster Yang has no skill; that is why the matter ended up like this,\" the Dowager Madame said.","For the moment, Consort Dou, who had lost a son, and her natal family, the Duke of Pi's mansion, were the enemies; Grandmaster Yang was the villain.","Only the Dowager Madame and the Sun family were innocent.","\"I will find a way to handle everything,\" the Emperor said.","The Dowager Madame gently took his hand and patted it, just like a mother would.","It was late when the Emperor finally left.","As he walked out of Shou'an Courtyard, Zhou Yuanshen was waiting at the gate.","He bowed respectfully. \"Your Majesty.\"","\"Say nothing,\" the Emperor said coolly. \"How is the Duke of Cheng's wife?\"","He had heard that Cheng Shi was bitten by a venomous snake.","At this time of year, snakes had just awakened from hibernation—their venom was at its deadliest after a winter's sleep. Perhaps Cheng Shi would die from it.","The Emperor certainly hoped so, as it would resolve many troubles.","The Empress’s act of betrothal was truly a way to vex the Zhou family. Whenever the Emperor thought of it, he wished he could kill both her and her father, Grand Preceptor Guo.","These people—all of them—were his enemies.","“My wife is currently taking antidotes for the poison and will recover soon,” Zhou Yuanshen said, bowing deeply. “Your Majesty’s concern weighs heavily on me. I am undeserving of your grace.”","“Rise,” the Emperor said.","Zhou Yuanshen escorted him out, continuing to speak of the events at the Double Ninth Temple.","“...Your Majesty, only if Yang Zhichang dies can this matter be resolved at its root,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","This matched the Emperor’s own plans. But how to carry it out—that, the Emperor had yet to decide.","Once Grandmaster Yang was dead, everything could be pinned on him, absolving Sun Zhiya and the Sun family of the Marquis Qingyuan of their crimes.","Otherwise, the Sun family would be stripped of their title, and Sun Zhiya would be executed.","“But he is skilled in esoteric arts and divination, and he has already fled,” Zhou Yuanshen added. “Without his head, it will be hard to settle the accounts.”","“Do you have a plan?” the Emperor asked.","“Your Majesty, I am willing to serve you, to cleanse the remnants of this trouble,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Once it is done, I wish to take on a burden for you.”","“What burden?”","“Your Majesty, the Capital Garrison cannot fall into another’s hands. If some crisis should arise, the Capital Garrison can control the capital, and the Imperial Guard would be powerless against it. I am willing to lead the Capital Garrison, to hold this shield for Your Majesty,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","The Emperor glanced at him.","He had removed Zhou Yuanshen from the Capital Garrison before, wary of the Zhou family’s military prestige and fearing Yuanshen might not be obedient.","But Zhou Yuanshen had performed exceptionally well over the past year.","He had done much for the Emperor.","Even if the Dowager Madame still held some discontent toward Zhou Yuanshen, the Emperor now felt that Zhou Yuanshen’s loyalty was trustworthy.","“Very well, I grant your request. If you resolve this matter properly, I will place the Capital Garrison under your command,” the Emperor said.","Zhou Yuanshen knelt on one knee: “I thank Your Majesty for your immense favor!”","The Emperor helped him rise.","“Not only will I give you the Capital Garrison, but I will also reward you with a wife. Lady Huan comes from a good family and is quite beautiful,” the Emperor added.","He continued, “The Dowager Madame told me something that Lady Huan should have told you herself but never found the chance. When Lady Huan married Zhou Yuancheng, Zhou Yuancheng was in poor health and could not consummate the marriage. She remains untouched to this day. My bestowing her upon you, alongside the Dowager Madame’s wishes, is no insult to you.”","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “Your Majesty, the matter of the Double Ninth Temple is pressing. I wish first to serve you in resolving it.”","Seeing that Zhou Yuanshen’s expression never flickered, the Emperor thought to himself that the man had considerable composure.","He truly was a fine blade to wield.","After seeing the Emperor off, Zhou Yuanshen returned to the Nonghua Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao was already asleep.","She had not actually been bitten by the viper. She had noticed someone throwing the snake, dodged nimbly, and then feigned being bitten, falling to the ground.","Thus, word spread that she had been poisoned by the snake.","Zhou Yuanshen had also told her to pretend to be ill and rest for a few days, so as not to become a target again.","The next morning, Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi came to see Cheng Zhao.","Though they knew the truth, they feigned great concern, putting on a show for outsiders.","“The Emperor came last night,” Zhou Yuanqi said in a childish voice.","Zhou Yuanshen replied with a hum.","“What did he ask you to do?”","“Kill Grandmaster Yang,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Zhou Yuanqi immediately bared his teeth in anger: “That cursed Daoist deserves death—he schemed against my sister-in-law!”","He added, \"Unexpectedly, you actually have some skill. You found the life gate of the old Taoist’s formation without using a compass to deduce the direction.\"","Zhou Yuanqi had also studied a little, but he was only half-taught.","He had examined the unusual gate formation and saw that the life gate was in the Li position. But locating where the formation’s core actually was and how to deduce the Li position left him completely baffled.","Zhou Yuanshen took a turn around the area and immediately moved the ritual implement at the Li position, shifting the formation’s eye. The Fifth Prince and Sun Zhiya happened to fall right into it."]},{"number":108,"title":"Chapter 108: Who struck the first killing blow","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen saw his mother off back to Jiangyun Courtyard, then went out with his younger brother.","Their father sent someone to summon him to the study.","“I want to go too!” Zhou Yuanqi, always eager to join in, piped up.","Second Master’s face was grave.","“Father, don’t ask Third Brother. I’ll explain everything!” Zhou Yuanqi was raring to go.","Second Master sighed helplessly. “Alright, alright, you speak.”","Zhou Yuanqi cleared his throat and began chattering away.","“Grandmother wanted Grandmaster Yang to spout nonsense, insisting that Third Sister-in-Law would suffer a blood-related calamity. If Third Sister-in-Law didn’t enter the formation, then if anything happened to her inside the mansion, Grandmother would have an excuse to blame it on fate. That would put Third Sister-in-Law in serious danger.","Third Sister-in-Law realized this immediately, but my mother didn’t think that far,” Zhou Yuanqi said, sneering spitefully.","Second Master clicked his tongue. “Be careful or you’ll get a whipping.”","Zhou Yuanqi stuck out his tongue and went on.","In short, after Grandmaster Yang’s ominous words, Cheng Zhao was forced into the formation.","Everyone present didn’t understand the mysterious formation and thought it was just like a temple ritual—a way to seek comfort, nothing more.","So no one paid it much mind.","But Zhou Yuanshen, his brother, and Cheng Zhao were very alert.","When the wooden hairpins were handed out, Cheng Zhao felt something was off; Hu Zhiwei took the initiative to put one on her, replacing her original hairpin.","Grandmaster Yang gave everyone ordinary wooden hairpins, identical for all; Hu Zhiwei’s was a magical tool crafted by Grandmaster Yang himself.","Cheng Zhao later secretly took it off and switched it with Zhou Yuanqi’s; Zhou Yuanqi hadn’t worn his, so no one noticed the boy.","“Third Sister-in-Law hid the magical tool up her sleeve, always wanting to find a place to discard it. Unexpectedly, Sun Zhiya deliberately bumped into her,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Fifth Prince coveted Cheng Zhao’s beauty; Consort Dou insulted Cheng Zhao’s family; Sun Zhiya provoked her again. So Cheng Zhao swapped the magical tool with the plain hairpin on Sun Zhiya’s head.","“At the time, no one knew what use it had,” Zhou Yuanqi said, “She just switched it for peace of mind.”","Second Master’s expression grew even more serious.","“Third Brother immediately spotted the formation’s core—it’s an Eight-Star Formation. I saw it too, and I even told Third Brother that the ‘life gate’ was at the ‘Li’ position,” Zhou Yuanqi was eager to show off.","“...You’d better spend more time on your proper studies. How did you manage to learn Qimen Dunjia?” Second Master sighed.","“Third Brother learned it too. Why don’t you say that to him?”","“Qimen Dunjia is a military strategy technique; it must be learned. Are you planning to join the army as well?” Second Master asked.","Zhou Yuanqi snorted.","He was dissatisfied. “If you don’t want to hear it, fine, I won’t say anymore. You keep interrupting.”","Second Master: “...”","Zhou Yuanqi couldn’t hold back. How could he just stop mid-sentence?","He went on: “Third Brother found the ‘Li’ position of the Eight-Star Formation without using a compass.”","Second Master glanced at Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen said, “I only know the basics—I can disrupt a formation, but I can’t set one up myself.”","Being able to set up a formation is the true mark of skill.","Grandmaster Yang is an expert in Qimen Dunjia. If he were to set up military formations in the army, he might win every battle.","Zhou Yuanshen still hoped to learn from him, but since Grandmaster Yang planned to scheme against Cheng Zhao—","Then he was doomed.","Second Master turned his gaze back to Zhou Yuanqi, signaling him to continue.","“The formations and magical instruments aren’t bad. Sun Zhiya must have been guilty as a thief—he didn’t wander around long before his mind went fuzzy,” Zhou Yuanqi said again.","The Second Master was slightly shocked. “Is it really that powerful?”","“True power belongs to sorcerers who can call the wind and summon the rain. These are just petty tricks. Father, have you ever seen miasma? The old Taoist set up the formation in the bamboo grove, where the air was already stale. Being in there makes people uncomfortable.","Add a few subtle moves, and it’s easy to make someone lose their senses,” Zhou Yuanqi said boldly.","The Second Master was speechless.","“Sun Zhiya got confused and was killed with the Fifth Prince’s dagger,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Second Master sighed.","“Father, what people call ‘evil energy’ is just a common phenomenon in the world. When they say it invades the brain and causes confusion, it’s like being drunk—exposing one’s true nature.","Miss Sun probably had a violent temper since childhood—spoiled and willful, often beating and scolding the servants, though outsiders didn’t know. Once she was ‘drunk,’ she went mad and tried to kill someone.","As for the Fifth Prince, why he didn’t fight back or how the dagger was taken from him, we don’t know. Who’s to say he wasn’t the one who first drew the dagger to attack Miss Sun?” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Hearing this, Zhou Yuanqi nodded in approval. “You have insight. That’s a very good analysis. Neither of them were innocent; both had secrets in their hearts, which led to one dead and one injured.”","The Second Master was speechless again.","They also mentioned the matter of the poisonous snakes appearing in the formation.","“Those snakes definitely didn’t just suddenly appear. To get poisonous snakes within half an hour, it had to be the Consort—she had over thirty people with her,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Zhou Yuanshen: “Indeed, the snakes didn’t just wander in by chance; they were deliberately released.”","“Third Sister-in-Law has no grudge with Consort Dou, yet she tried to kill her. These royal family members don’t care about anyone else’s life,” Zhou Yuanqi said bitterly.","He had no sympathy for Helian Hong, the Fifth Prince.","Especially since Helian Hong looked at Third Sister-in-Law with such longing, completely indifferent to the others present.","If Third Sister-in-Law had to attend social events in the future and met him at a banquet, and he kept staring like that again and again, her reputation would be ruined.","Destroying the reputation of a noble lady, a titled madam, is like killing her socially.","He couldn’t control his greed and didn’t care about Third Sister-in-Law’s life or death. He deserved to die.","The Second Master sighed deeply after listening.","“...Since I can remember, there has never been a time of clarity in the court. The glory of ‘a wise ruler and virtuous ministers’ I’ve only read about in history books,” the Second Master said.","Zhou Yuanqi: “Father, every dynasty has its filth. You don’t need to be saddened by this. True purity is always just what the history books gloss over and cover up.”","The Second Master smiled good-naturedly. “You’re still so young, yet you see things more clearly than I do.”","“Because I read more, Father,” Zhou Yuanqi said. “You read less and see the world from the bottom of a well, always thinking everything is narrow and petty.”","The Second Master, in a good mood, smiled and patted Zhou Yuanqi’s head.","He praised his son for being diligent.","It was getting late, and Zhou Yuanqi had to go to the clan academy. He was speaking so passionately that he left with a hint of reluctance.","When Zhou Yuanshen was about to leave, the Second Master called after him.","“Ah Shen, you...”","“Father, like I said, if I don’t do it, someone else will. You don’t need to try to stop me,” Zhou Yuanshen said without breaking his stride.","He strode away.","Second Master sat alone, a wave of despair and hopelessness washing over him once again. He desperately wanted to help the children, but his thoughts inevitably drifted to his sister, his eldest brother, and his nephew.","He closed his eyes tightly, swallowing back the tears that threatened to spill.","In early April, Grandmaster Yang died.","He was captured by Zhou Yuanshen and his men and “committed suicide out of guilt.”","With Grandmaster Yang’s death, there was no one left to testify, and the blame for the Fifth Prince’s death was pinned squarely on him. The Chongyang Temple was sealed off, and while the Taoist priests were not implicated, they all fled for their lives.","Sun Zhiya went mad and was left disfigured, a grotesque scar marking her right cheek.","The court ministers sought to vent their anger on the Qingyuan Marquis’s household. The Fifth Prince’s maternal grandfather, the Duke of Pi, fiercely demanded that both the Qingyuan Marquis’s estate and the Zhou family be held accountable, but the emperor rebuked him in anger."]},{"number":109,"title":"Chapter 109: A False Alarm","paragraphs":["Public outcry was immense, and popular sentiment seethed.","The Marquis of Qingyuan’s residence, the Sun family, became the target of public wrath, scorned by everyone. Sun Zhiya was vilified as a “poisonous woman.”","The Dowager Madame’s natal family had retained their hereditary title, and no deaths had occurred, but their reputation was utterly destroyed. A stain had been added to her wealth and honor, one that not even twenty years could wash away. Whenever the opportunity arose, Consort Dou’s natal family would certainly fight tooth and nail against the Suns and even the Zhou family.","She had even developed resentment toward the Emperor.","The Dowager Madame had no choice but to spread the word: “The Duchess was bitten by a venomous snake. The snake was arranged by Consort Dou, who couldn’t stand Lady Zhou.”","The implication was that Consort Dou was also at fault, that she was not merely a victim.","This only added more fuel to the gossip.","Even the Cheng family was dragged into the midst of it.","“Grandmother is furious to the point of death,” Cheng Zhao said to Zhou Yuanshen as they played chess by the open window on the kang bed.","In early summer, it was neither hot nor cold. A night breeze drifted in through the window, carrying the faint, sweet fragrance of flowers, a pleasant and intoxicating aroma.","Zhou Yuanshen placed a piece and said indifferently, “She’s always been invincible. This time, she capsized in a ditch and took a major blow.”","She would probably quiet down for a while.","“How did you manage to capture Grandmaster Yang?” Cheng Zhao asked again.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “Young Uncle provided the secret intelligence. You mentioned last time that taverns like Drunken Immortal Pavilion are places for gathering information. That was spot on.”","“Young Uncle can be relied upon after all.”","“He’s only playful with trivial matters; he’s never slipped up on important things. Previously, at the border, he was in charge of these matters for Grandfather,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Fan Xiao had returned to the capital in his teens and established roots there. After a decade of laying his networks, he now had some achievements. Even if the Emperor wanted to reclaim the Fan family’s military power, the Fans wouldn’t be utterly passive.","The Zhou family, however, had no such option.","There were too few people who could take charge, and the Dowager Madame detested military officers. She had forced the Zhou family to surrender all their military power, take up civil offices, and befriend literary officials, becoming true nobility.","Military officers were considered inferior.","“... Fortunately, you understood the art of stratagems. Otherwise, even if we found him, we couldn’t have caught him,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen reminded her, “You’ve won this move.”","Cheng Zhao withdrew her piece and casually placed another, her mind entirely absent from the chessboard. “Lord Duke, will the Emperor really let you command the Capital Garrison?”","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at her.","He was silent for a moment, then wiped out a large cluster of Cheng Zhao’s pieces.","This time, Cheng Zhao didn’t care about winning or losing. She only urged him to answer quickly.","“Grandmother might oppose it, but what use would that be? Unless the Emperor decides he no longer wishes to use me,” Zhou Yuanshen said after a long pause.","“Then your odds of victory increase by one more point,” Cheng Zhao said.","She also asked Zhou Yuanshen, “This time, aside from getting rid of me, this ‘rebel’, wasn’t it also about the concurrent marriage system? What do you think?”","“That won’t happen,” Zhou Yuanshen said firmly.","Cheng Zhao nodded. “Lady Huan schemed against you so much. If she really married you, setting aside how disgusted you’d be, I’d feel terrible for you too.”","Zhou Yuanshen hummed in agreement.","He placed another piece.","Since Cheng Zhao’s mind wasn’t on the game, he won with ease.","“I win, Cheng Zhao,” he said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Oh...”","Win or lose, they hadn’t placed any bet.","Zhou Yuanshen walked over and lifted her horizontally into his arms. The weather had turned warm, so the bed curtains weren’t let down. Instead, a screen was placed beside the bed.","He easily maneuvered around the screen, laid her down on the bed, and leaned in to kiss her.","Cheng Zhao turned her head aside. “...Aren’t I supposed to be in recovery from poison?”","“Don’t you want to?” he actually asked.","Cheng Zhao gritted her teeth inwardly. “Time to sleep.”","Wait—her period hadn’t arrived.","Before her marriage, Cheng Zhao’s monthly cycle was fairly regular. Now, it was always delayed.","Thanks to Zhou Yuanshen’s relentless attentions.","“... Could I be pregnant?” she said. “It’s been delayed by four or five days.”","She quickly pushed Zhou Yuanshen away, got up, and called for the maid on night duty.","It happened to be Suyue.","“What day was my last period?” she asked.","She had a vague memory but couldn’t recall clearly.","Suyue told her.","It was indeed five days late.","“... You’re recovering from a venomous snake bite. Can we have the doctor take your pulse?” Suyue asked.","Cheng Zhao said, “Send someone to the Cheng residence. Have my mother bring a doctor over.”","For the past few days, she had been feigning illness, acting as if she were very sick. Her family members had sent in visiting cards to see her, but Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen felt that walls have ears. They needed to keep this matter confidential.","They would wait until most of the problems were resolved before inviting her family to come.","Now, with public opinion heavily against the Dowager Madame and her in deep trouble—the concurrent marriage plan becoming a fantasy—it was time to let her family know.","“... Let’s wait a bit longer. Once Lord Duke’s official appointment is settled, then we can invite my family over,” Cheng Zhao said.","That night, she refused Zhou Yuanshen.","If she really were pregnant, the embryo might still be unstable and easy to harm. And Zhou Yuanshen—though he appeared cool and detached—never went easy on her when he had his way.","Cheng Zhao was always left utterly exhausted without exception.","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing.","His personality was cold; he might be secretly annoyed when rejected, but he never made a fuss.","A few more days passed. The court was still wrangling over Sun Zhiya’s killing of the Fifth Prince when Zhou Yuanshen obtained the position overseeing the Capital Garrison.","He became its true commander, and the Capital Garrison fell into his hands.","Naturally, some found this inappropriate and tried to oppose it, sparking another round of heated debate.","On the eighth day of her period’s delay, Cheng Zhao finally got it.","She wasn’t pregnant, after all.","She sent people to invite her mother and sisters to visit, and let out a discouraged sigh.","“Time after time, why is it so hard for me? Madame Concubine got pregnant so easily,” Cheng Zhao said.","The doctor said there was nothing wrong with her, and she had prayed to the Child-Giving Goddess. Why was there still no sign?","That day, feeling unwell, Cheng Zhao sat quietly reading a book.","Mid-morning, Cheng Zhao’s mother, eldest sister, second sister-in-law, and third sister all arrived.","Second Madam had sent someone to fetch them.","After dismissing the servants, the eldest sister took Cheng Zhao’s hand and choked up. “How are you? Where does it hurt?”","“I’m on my period, Eldest Sister,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile. “The snake didn’t bite me at all.”","Everyone was stunned.","The eldest sister’s tears burst forth. “So it was a false alarm all along.”","In life, nothing was more joyous than a false alarm. She wept with happiness.","Cheng Zhao leaned into her embrace.","Madam Cheng repeatedly inquired about the details.","The rumors outside were outrageous, but within the wild tales, there was some truth.","“... They argued a few words, and she set a venomous snake to bite you? If something really happened and it was traced back to her, would she have gotten off so easily?” Second sister-in-law asked with indignation.","Cheng Zhao’s mother said, “The Duke of Pi had some old grudge with your grandfather. His punishment back then was due to your grandfather. They’ve always held a grudge.”","“Those who do many unrighteous things will bring about their own doom,” the third sister said. “She didn’t care about other people’s lives, so her son easily lost his—karma.”","“But the real culprit has...” Second sister-in-law trailed off mid-sentence, swallowing her words.","After all, they were in the Zhou residence. She feared the walls had ears and might implicate Cheng Zhao.","Her mother-in-law, Second Madam, let out a deep sigh. “The Dowager Madame is truly an overbearing woman. I still can’t believe she orchestrated this entire scheme.”","Madam Cheng said, “Perhaps there’s some misunderstanding.”","“There’s no misunderstanding. It was all her,” Second Madam said."]},{"number":110,"title":"Chapter 110: Cheng Zhao’s joy was written all over his face.","paragraphs":["The room was quiet.","Cheng Zhao smiled to reassure everyone: \"It's all over. Our group came out unscathed, while the Dowager Madame's side suffered losses.\"","Second Madam couldn't help but let out a laugh.","Once she laughed, the faces of the Cheng family members brightened a little.","When the discussion turned to the current situation, Second Madam said, \"The Emperor grows more irrational by the day. He's always been a bit unhinged.\"","The women of the Cheng family fell silent again.","Cheng Zhao sighed softly.","The Emperor was like a runaway horse, treating everyone badly. Everyone lived on a knife's edge, never knowing whether the next moment would see them soar to the heavens or plunge into the sea.","For the senior ministers, it was a test of wits, but even more so of luck.","The great aristocratic families were all on edge.","The topic was too heavy, so after a few remarks, they changed the subject.","Second Madam turned to Madam Cheng and praised Cheng Zhao lavishly for her cleverness.","\"She outfoxed the Dowager Madame. My dear in-laws, I always thought no one could ever beat the Dowager Madame,\" Second Madam said, swelling with pride.","Madam Cheng smiled. \"Zhaozhao has a bit of a cunning streak.\"","\"All thanks to your excellent teaching,\" Second Madam replied.","Madam Cheng responded, \"It's your affection for her that gives her the nerve to 'run wild.' As a daughter-in-law, gentleness and filial piety should come first.\"","They exchanged compliments for a good while.","The younger generation stood nearby, smiling.","For a time, the Nonghua Courtyard was filled with laughter and cheerful voices.","After the midday meal, Cheng Zhao's sisters-in-law, sisters, and Second Madam chatted in the eastern side room. Madam Cheng deliberately called Cheng Zhao into the inner bedchamber.","\"Zhaozhao, you still need to be cautious in this household,\" Madam Cheng said. \"But don't be afraid, either. The Lord Duke has already taken command of the Capital Guard.\"","The Dowager Madame should understand that Zhou Yuanshen, by having the Celestial Master Yang killed, had once again won the Emperor's trust.","In fact, the Dowager Madame had handed Zhou Yuanshen that opportunity herself.","Zhou Yuanshen was no longer just a family head that the Dowager Madame and the Duke of Chen's mansion had attached to the Emperor. He now had his own value in the Emperor's eyes.","If the Dowager Madame insisted on fighting him to the bitter end, she would only end up hurting herself.","She wouldn't make a move against Cheng Zhao openly.","This time, Cheng Zhao had won a step, and Zhou Yuanshen had gained ten steps. The couple had taken the initiative on the chessboard into their own hands.","Madam Cheng warned her to be careful, but she also told her not to live in fear and lose that spirit.","A person who lives constantly in worry not only suffers pain but also loses their judgment, leaving only suspicion and paranoia.","\"Mother, I understand it all,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile. \"I've triumphed again this time. Heaven favors me.\"","\"You once said that a fortune-teller told you your destiny is noble. 'Noble destiny' doesn't mean you'll hold a high position; it just means you have good fortune and will always turn danger into safety,\" Madam Cheng said.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","Madam Cheng gently touched her face. \"Zhaozhao, I heard you were poisoned. Your father couldn't sleep for several nights.\"","Cheng Zhao's feelings for her father were somewhat distant, because he was always busy and very serious.","When Cheng Zhao and Cheng Chao made mistakes, they would both kneel in the scorching sun as punishment.","Under normal circumstances, if both siblings erred, their mother would only physically punish Cheng Chao; for Cheng Zhao, she would use methods like \"confinement, copying books, recitation\" rather than rough punishment for a girl.","But their father treated them equally.","Kneeling for an hour had sunburned Cheng Zhao's face, and it hurt for a long time. When she cried from the pain, she told her mother, 'I'll never speak to Father again!'","For all these years, she had indeed barely interacted with him.","Many people doted on her—her grandfather, mother, elder brother, sister-in-law, and sisters. She was the family's treasure, and she could be very willful.","\"...Tell Father I'm sorry for worrying him,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Zhaozhao, your father does love you,\" her mother said.","Cheng Zhao: \"I've always known that. I'm not angry with him. It's just that I truly have nothing to say to him, and he doesn't like casual conversation.\"","She added with a smile, \"Actually, I don't know what he enjoys talking about. Mother, he's a bit like Zhou Yuanshen.\"","A sudden inspiration struck her. \"Next time, I can play chess with Father.\"","\"That's not a bad idea. Your father does enjoy playing chess,\" her mother said.","As the Cheng family members were about to leave, Zhou Yuanshen hurried back.","He had been exceptionally busy lately.","In fact, from now on, he wouldn't be able to come home every day.","He greeted everyone.","Cheng Zhao's mother exchanged a few more words with him privately.","After seeing off the women of the Cheng family, the couple went to the Jiangyun Courtyard for dinner.","Second Master examined Cheng Zhao: \"Zhaozhao looks a bit thinner. This ordeal frightened you.\"","\"I'm not afraid, Father,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Second Master nodded slightly.","That evening, Second Madam spoke again: \"We really should move out.\"","Second Master was silent.","Now they could leave, but Zhou Yuanshen certainly couldn't, since he was the Duke of Chen. And Cheng Zhao probably wouldn't leave either.","Between Zhou Yuanshen and Cheng Zhao, there seemed to be a bond now. To let the eldest branch take Zhou Yuanshen and have him serve as heir to both houses—Second Master could no longer bring himself to suggest it.","Simply put, the young couple were no longer strangers to each other as they had been at the start.","If Cheng Zhao left with them, she would be living as a widow in all but name. Yet she was doing an excellent job as the Duchess, even capable of defeating the Dowager Madame.","\"Let's sleep,\" Second Master said in a low voice.","\"She actually plotted against a child!\" Second Madam sneered in the darkness.","She was even too tired to be angry.","After this incident, the Dowager Madame's standing in her heart had completely collapsed. All her prestige as an honorary first-rank lady and mother-in-law was gone.","She was just an annoying old woman past her prime.","Second Madam suddenly thought, she didn't want to play this game anymore.","Leave her behind to enjoy her honors alone, and stop riding on her coattails.","\"It's like a cat chasing a mouse. The children seem to be having a lot of fun,\" Second Master said.","Second Madam: \"...\"","It seemed to be true.","Whether it was Cheng Zhao, Zhou Yuanshen, or Zhou Yuanqi, none of them felt \"aggrieved.\" They were all savoring their victories.","Even if Zhou Yuanshen and Zhou Yuanqi never said so out loud.","But Second Madam often felt aggrieved.","Upon reflection, only those in an inferior position, looking up at those on high and feeling slighted, would feel aggrieved.","It was a form of self-disparagement.","Second Madam suddenly realized that for over twenty years, she had always lowered herself when facing the Dowager Madame, which only invited more disdain.","Some people bully the weak.","\"So that's how it is,\" Second Madam had another moment of enlightenment.","Her talent for understanding was truly poor. The children grasped these truths, but it took her until her forties.","How had she lived the first forty years?","The elderly couple chatted a little more, and gradually Second Madam fell asleep.","Over at the Nonghua Courtyard, Cheng Zhao was still awake.","Zhou Yuanshen told her to instruct the maids to pack his spare clothes.","Starting next month, he would spend fifteen days each month at the Capital Guard camp, so he needed some clothes and shoes placed there.","Cheng Zhao immediately gave the orders.","She thought to herself: \"He'll be away for half a month.\"","How wonderful. She could sleep in peace at night. And when he came back, Mu Jiang's pregnancy would be more stable, and he could go to the Courtyard of Beautiful Scenery.","Cheng Zhao had previously thought she could manage to have him stay overnight at Nonghua Courtyard only twice a month. Now that might finally be achievable.","\"Cheng Zhao.\"","He suddenly called her name.","Cheng Zhao looked up to meet his eyes.","\"You had a happy look on your face,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao was startled and hurriedly tried to hide her expression.","Before she could say anything, Zhou Yuanshen turned and went to the washroom, slamming the bead curtain so hard that the beads clattered noisily.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","She didn't.","He was bluffing.","And he had scared her into revealing her guilt. Truly infuriating."]},{"number":111,"title":"Chapter 111: Who has received your gift, Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen went to wash up, changed into his underclothes, and returned to the bedroom.","Cheng Zhao finished a bowl of ginger and brown sugar tea and was rinsing her mouth. She glanced at him.","He wore a blank expression as he picked up a book to read in bed.","Cheng Zhao brought out her sewing basket again. She lit another horn lantern to make the area brighter.","It had been a while since she’d done any needlework.","But she couldn’t help thinking of something from before the New Year.","It had also been when she was sewing...","She cast a look toward Zhou Yuanshen.","At that same moment, he lifted his eyes to meet hers.","The married couple’s gazes locked. Cheng Zhao looked away as if nothing had happened.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t move, but he spoke: “Cheng Zhao.”","Cheng Zhao’s nerves tightened.","Annoying—he wasn’t her father, yet when he called her like that, her skin prickled. Even her father had never carried such authority.","She raised her eyes and glared right back at him.","“You promised to make me a pouch, but you never did,” he said. “You broke your word.”","“Did I?”","“Think carefully,” he said. “If you won’t admit it, fine.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","She remembered.","That time, she had snatched back an embroidered handkerchief she’d made for her niece. To get it returned, she’d promised to sew him a pouch.","But then one thing after another happened, and they’d both been too busy to even catch their breath.","Cheng Zhao refused to let him see her guilt, though.","She turned the blame around: “You didn’t remind me. Since it’s been so long, the deal is off. You forgot first.”","Zhou Yuanshen put down his book.","Seeing that, Cheng Zhao immediately swung her legs off the bed, slipped on her shoes, and turned to bolt.","But he was faster. Before she could reach the door, he caught her around the waist.","He pressed her against the door, using considerable force, and her body thudded softly against the wood.","Footsteps from a maid in the outer room quickly followed.","Before Cheng Zhao could tell the maid on night duty to withdraw, Zhou Yuanshen kissed her.","Their tongues tangled, and he kissed her until she almost couldn’t breathe, then gently nibbled her lips.","Cheng Zhao gasped for air: “I still have my period.”","“Hm.” Zhou Yuanshen hummed in acknowledgment and kept kissing her.","She tried to pull away.","She pushed at him: “Let’s go to the bed...”","Zhou Yuanshen finally drew back, his gaze dropping: “Are you willing?”","Cheng Zhao understood. Last time, he’d done the same, guiding her hand...","“I’m not willing!” Cheng Zhao’s eyes went wide. “Don’t even think about it!”","“How will you make it up to me, then?” he asked. “My pouch...”","“You haven’t made me anything either,” Cheng Zhao retorted. “Why should I make you a pouch?”","Zhou Yuanshen considered this seriously and nodded. “You’re right. What do you want?”","“A kite,” she said. “While the weather isn’t too hot or cold, I want to take my niece out to fly one.”","As soon as she said it, she recalled when she’d first moved in—she’d seen him and Mu Jiang flying a kite in the courtyard. She’d remarked then that the weather wasn’t right for it, that they were being odd.","A flicker of regret crossed Cheng Zhao’s face. She frowned slightly. “Did you ever make a kite for anyone else?”","“No. I don’t know how yet,” he said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","“And you? How many people have you given pouches to?” he asked.","“Oh, far too many to count,” she said.","He leaned in, gently kissing her snowy neck, even lightly scraping her skin with his teeth. “Who, for example?”","Cheng Zhao couldn’t withstand such teasing; her body trembled. “I can’t remember all of them at once. My needlework is pretty good, you know. Last time I even made one for the Empress.”","“Think harder...”","Without her noticing, his pulling had loosened her collar. He lifted her up.","Back on the bed, he didn’t set her down. Instead, he leaned against the headboard and held her in his arms, an arm around her waist.","Cheng Zhao felt herself breathing hard.","A warmth spread up her spine, almost fogging her mind.","He kissed her again.","“Cheng Zhao,” he murmured. This time there was no authority in his voice—it was low, coaxing, like sweet, sticky syrup.","Cheng Zhao mustered a fierce impulse to break free, but he anticipated it and held her tighter.","Her collar had slipped nearly to her navel, leaving her exposed before him. She tried to cover herself, but she couldn’t escape, so she bit him in a moment of recklessness. “Zhou Yuanshen!”","He drew in a breath. “I have to go to the camp tomorrow...”","Cheng Zhao immediately loosened her bite.","If he had a teeth mark on his lip, it would embarrass him, and she’d rather not be alive either.","“What are you even trying to do? You know tonight isn’t possible,” Cheng Zhao said, her breath scalding.","Tormenting each other like this was uncomfortable for him—and unbearable for her too.","When two people caught fire like this, they felt ravenous.","He couldn’t have the meal, yet he kept whetting his appetite till the urge burned like wildfire.","“I just want to know who you gave those pouches to,” he said.","“You’re being impossible!” She cupped his face.","It was like asking her how many pastries she’d eaten growing up.","How could she possibly remember?","She’d been forced to take up embroidery at eight, only to discover she didn’t hate it and could sit patiently with the work.","Pouches and handkerchiefs were small items she made on a whim.","And she worked fast—she wasn’t one of those delicate young ladies who spent half a month on a single pouch. When she focused, she could finish one in two days.","She truly couldn’t recall, so instead she pressed her lips to his and kissed him first.","Zhou Yuanshen peeled off her upper garment and took her hand.","The night grew deep.","Cheng Zhao was too exhausted to argue. She fell asleep pressed against him—it was so late, she was nearly unconscious with drowsiness.","Thinking he wouldn’t be back for half a month, she slept even more soundly.","The next day, when she woke, Zhou Yuanshen was already gone.","“His Lordship went to the Jingji Camp today. He said he won’t return tonight,” Nanny Li reported.","“Wasn’t he supposed to be at camp for half a month?” Cheng Zhao asked.","“I asked him. He said once things are settled, he can still come home. On horseback, it’s only an hour and a half each way,” Nanny Li said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Three hours for a round trip every day, and he actually planned to come home every single night?","Was he insane?","With that much energy, why didn’t he go plow the fields?","“Nanny, next time you see him, tell him not to come back. Running himself ragged—who’s going to run the Duke’s mansion?” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li gave a wry smile. “How could I say that?”","“Then I’ll tell Mother and ask her to speak to him,” Cheng Zhao said.","She was at a loss.","Nanny Li laughed. “Don’t worry. It’s hard for him to get away anyway. Duties are exhausting; things don’t always go as planned.”","Cheng Zhao felt relieved. “You’re right. He’s just daydreaming. He probably can’t actually make it home. His plan was to come back once every half month.”","The two of them chatted for a long while before Cheng Zhao went for breakfast.","Qiubai helped Cheng Zhao dress and whispered, “Young Madam, there’s something you might find interesting.”","It was a little secret.","“The boy Nanfeng told me—maybe His Lordship sent him to say it,” Qiubai said.","“What is it?”"]},{"number":112,"title":"Chapter 112: The Duke's Tactics","paragraphs":["Qiubai told Cheng Zhao that Hu Zhiwei and her mother had disappeared.","The Song family had taken the mother and daughter away.","\"...They were taken away the evening before last,\" Qiubai said.","Upon hearing this, Cheng Zhao frowned slightly. \"Who ordered this? The Dowager Madame?\"","Qiubai replied, \"I do not know. If it was the Dowager Madame, then this mother and daughter are in grave danger.\"","In the Chongyang Temple, Hu Zhiwei had been the Dowager Madame's assistant.","Originally, it was only a minor matter, but the death of the Fifth Prince had turned it into a major case overnight. Grandmaster Yang, who was involved, had died, let alone a minor figure like Hu Zhiwei.","Cheng Zhao had remained inactive because she could not move against Hu Zhiwei without going over the Dowager Madame and Eldest Madam Song—it would alert the enemy.","The Dowager Madame was now enraged and might not lack means to counter Cheng Zhao.","Provoking a wounded beast was extremely dangerous, and Cheng Zhao dared not act rashly.","Moreover, Zhou Yuanshen had been busy these days establishing himself at court and throwing off the Dowager Madame's grip on him. He had no time to enter the inner quarters to help Cheng Zhao.","The matter was left in limbo. Cheng Zhao had already abandoned hope; Hu Zhiwei had become a dead piece on the board.","But her sudden disappearance still felt regrettable to Cheng Zhao, as though she had foolishly lost a chess piece.","\"...It doesn’t matter anyway. The Dowager Madame’s backing is the Emperor; the truth holds no real significance. Whether this person lives or dies cannot shake the Dowager Madame,\" Cheng Zhao thought again.","So Zhou Yuanshen had not focused his attention on Hu Zhiwei.","Even if she were made to speak the truth, what use would it be?","Proving the Dowager Madame guilty—what good would that do Cheng Zhao? She was the wife of a duke, her status tied to the Chen Duke Manor. If the Dowager Madame fell into disgrace, Cheng Zhao, as the Duke’s wife, would also be besmirched.","Hadn’t Zhou Yuanshen known that Grandmaster Yang had learned too much, yet still ordered his death in exchange for real power over the Capital Garrison?","\"The Dowager Madame has grown weak,\" Cheng Zhao said suddenly with a faint smile.","Qiubai asked, \"Why?\"","\"If she still had her former strength and authority, with everything under control, she wouldn’t have disposed of Hu Zhiwei. She’s nothing but an ant; Hu Zhiwei could not affect anyone. Yet the Dowager Madame treated her as a loose thread and found a way to eliminate her,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Hu Zhiwei had sought the help of a tiger. She acted as the Dowager Madame’s blade to scheme against Cheng Zhao, and she should have known the consequences of failure.","She claimed she had no choice.","But having no choice was not an excuse for scheming against Cheng Zhao.","If she had no ambition and only wanted to live peacefully, she would never have stepped forward, and the Dowager Madame would not have used her.","Cheng Zhao had some authority over household management, especially over the main kitchen. She knew very well that in the servants’ quarters at the back of the Chen Duke Manor, many impoverished relatives who had come seeking shelter were being fed.","Their meals were not especially refined, but they were filling, with some meat every day, and they received a few hundred coppers each month.","If a family is poor and must rely on relatives, yet can still eat fully and dress warmly without hard labor—isn’t that a good life?","But Hu Zhiwei was not content with that.","From the very beginning, she was used by Eldest Madam Song because she considered herself beautiful and ambitious enough to seduce the Lord Duke. Later, she caught the Dowager Madame’s eye.","She participated willingly.","Take the incident of throwing herself at the Lord Duke—could Eldest Madam Song have forced her to barge into Morning Glory Courtyard?","She was never coerced; she lied to Cheng Zhao.","She wanted the wealth and glory of the Zhou family too much and was willing to be a pawn.","Once she started down that path, she should have known that whether she succeeded or failed, she might very well be silenced.","Her end was brought on by her own actions.","Cheng Zhao was the truly innocent one.","\"Qiubai, do you truly think the Fifth Prince’s death was just an accident?\" Cheng Zhao suddenly asked.","Qiubai was taken aback. \"The Dowager Madame had no reason to kill him.\"","Precisely. Because the Fifth Prince died, the Dowager Madame’s scheme spiraled out of control. Otherwise, even if her plan had failed, it would have been nothing more than a squabble among family.","If it had succeeded, it would have achieved the Dowager Madame’s goal at a very small cost.","She had looked down on Cheng Zhao so much that even in dealing with her, she was unwilling to pay a heavy price.","She tried to catch a whale with an earthworm.","\"But someone benefited from this incident,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She stared intently at Qiubai.","Qiubai’s face changed, and she grasped Cheng Zhao’s hand tightly. \"Young Madam, do not say it!\"","Who benefited?","The Lord Duke, of course.","He gained real authority over the Capital Garrison instead of an empty title—this was what he wanted most. Moreover, the dual inheritance arrangement with Huan Qingtang was postponed, because the Fifth Prince’s death caused such a stir that the Emperor was too distracted to see to that matter.","Hadn’t the Fifth Prince’s blatant staring at the Duke of Chen’s wife openly shown contempt for Zhou Yuanshen?","His wife being so openly coveted was also a challenge to his prestige.","Thus, the Fifth Prince died at the hands of Sun Zhiya.","In this matter, Zhou Yuanshen bore not a shred of suspicion.","\"Young Madam, this matter must remain buried within your heart,\" Qiubai said.","Cheng Zhao nodded slightly and said no more.","Zhou Yunqi had gone to the Capital Garrison and indeed did not return for seven or eight days.","Cheng Zhao’s days returned to a peaceful routine, much like the early days after she had first married in—she kept to a regular schedule.","She slept early and rose early, went to pay respects to her mother-in-law after breakfast, and was never late. Then she made her way unhurriedly to Chenning Hall, without any delay.","Unlike those days when Zhou Yuanshen was around, when they would argue until late at night, forcing her to rush through her morning salutations when she barely woke in time.","Fortunately, her mother-in-law was fond of her and did not hold it against her, nor did she expose her, so Cheng Zhao could smooth things over.","\"Is Zhou Yuanshen still in the Capital Garrison these days?\" Second Madam asked.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"Presumably.\"","\"We have not been summoned to greet the Dowager Madame at Shouan Courtyard,\" Second Madam said in a low voice.","\"Grandmother is probably observing her devotions,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam snorted contemptuously. \"More likely she’s feeling guilty. The court officials are still pressing against the Sun family. The death of a prince is not so easily tidied up.\"","Cheng Zhao lowered her voice. \"Mother, please be careful. The walls have ears. With the Lord Duke away from home, Grandmother might take her anger out on us, and no one would stop her.\"","Second Madam fell silent.","She then asked Cheng Zhao, \"How is Chenning Hall these days?\"","\"Eldest Madam is handling household affairs, though she seems somewhat timid in her speech and actions, being overly cautious. The Elder Young Lady has not stepped forward and even claims to be ill and refuses to help,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam replied, \"She brought it upon herself.\"","Then she added, \"The elders of the main branch are too greedy. Since the title has fallen to us, they should cede Chenning Hall. Do they think I would treat them poorly?\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"Mother, your words are just.\"","\"Don’t mock me for being petty. I have never fought for what is not mine, but when what is rightfully mine is withheld from me, others mock me behind my back and treat me like a fool,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"Mother, this is not being petty. They are trampling over you; of course, you would feel aggrieved.\"","The two women spoke heartfelt words for a long while.","Cheng Zhao comforted and understood her, and Second Madam felt somewhat better.","That day, in the late afternoon, Cheng Zhao and Second Madam strolled through the back garden to relax and pick some fresh flowers for decoration.","They ran into the Dowager Madame there.","The Dowager Madame was leaning on Hu Yanqing’s arm and accompanied by Mu Yan. Her spirits seemed well, and she was smiling as she spoke about the peonies being near the end of their bloom and wanting to pick some for Shouan Pavilion.","Meeting face to face, both sides were startled.","\"Grandmother,\" Cheng Zhao greeted her, then exchanged greetings with Huan Qingtang and Mu Jiang. \"Elder Sister-in-Law, Secondary Wife.\""]},{"number":113,"title":"Chapter 113: Cheng Zhao Did Not Recognize Her Own Husband","paragraphs":["They ran into the Dowager Madame’s party, and everyone’s expressions were varied.","Cheng Zhao and the Dowager Madame acted as if nothing was amiss; Huan Qingtang smiled gently and returned the greeting warmly: “Sister-in-law.”","Second Madam’s face was stiff.","If the Dowager Madame chose to make things difficult, accusing her of disrespect toward her mother-in-law, Second Madam would be put on the defensive, and might even get her daughter-in-law scolded.","But she simply couldn’t bring herself to put on a false front.","Mu Jiang glanced disdainfully at Cheng Zhao, then looked at Cheng Zhao’s flat stomach, deliberately puffing out her own.","She was nearly five months pregnant; her belly wasn’t very large, but she stuck it out constantly, making it quite conspicuous.","“...This is the last season of peonies,” the Dowager Madame said cheerfully. “I’ll cut some to arrange in vases at home. You should cut some too—don’t let them wither away in the fields, what a waste.”","Cheng Zhao replied, “Yes. Mother and I were just thinking of picking some peonies and also gathering some lotus leaves.”","She added, “Grandmother, please continue your stroll. We’ll go see how the lotus leaves in the pond by the river are doing.”","The Dowager Madame nodded slightly. “Go on, then.”","Second Madam bowed stiffly and left with Cheng Zhao.","Along the way, Second Madam didn’t want to speak anymore.","Later, the gardener sent a good number of peonies and lotus leaves to the Courtyard of Luxuriant Grace and the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","Second Madam refused them: “Just looking at them irritates me.”","Because they reminded her of the encounter with the Dowager Madame in the back garden.","Now, whenever she looked at her, she found her even more ugly and could hardly bear to meet her eyes. Second Madam felt both disgusted and pitiful for her; it was a tangle of complicated emotions.","Cheng Zhao tried to soothe her.","Seeing that she was still unhappy, Cheng Zhao smiled and said, “Mother, how about we go to the Pillar of the State General’s mansion?”","That was the Fan family, Second Madam’s maiden home.","“What for?”","“The training ground in the General’s mansion is so big. Let’s go horseback riding,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam’s eyes lit up slightly.","“Alright,” she said.","She finally managed a hint of a smile.","All the peonies and lotus leaves were delivered to the Courtyard of Luxuriant Grace.","“The peonies go in vases; put a lotus leaf in a vase for the inner chamber too—it has a fresh scent,” Cheng Zhao said.","The maids acknowledged her orders.","The next day, Cheng Zhao requested leave from the Hall of Reverence and went with Second Madam to the Fan household.","At the breakfast table, Zhou Yuanqi immediately said, “I want to go too!”","“You again? To ride horses?” Second Madam refused to take him. “Have you forgotten your lesson last time? Your Third Brother isn’t home. If anything happens again, you’ll frighten me to death!”","“I’ll choose a gentle horse this time,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Then he added, “Last time I went shopping, I bought gifts for you and Third Sister-in-Law. If you take me, I’ll give you the gifts.”","Second Madam: “Do I really care?”","The mother and son bickered for a while before finally reaching an agreement.","Second Madam took Zhou Yuanqi along, and Zhou Yuanqi brought his gifts.","In the carriage, Cheng Zhao received the gift from Zhou Yuanqi—a box of face powder imported from overseas.","“They say it’s very fine, and it makes your complexion look good,” Zhou Yuanqi explained earnestly. “The shopkeeper charged me a hundred taels of silver per box.”","Cheng Zhao: “Fifth Brother, you alone could support an entire street.”","When Second Madam heard the price, she nearly fainted: “You’re throwing money away again! What face powder costs a hundred taels a box? A hundred taels could buy a cartload.”","Cheng Zhao opened it to have a look.","As the carriage jolted along, the moment she opened the small box, powder flew up and dusted her face.","Cheng Zhao sneezed, and couldn’t help bending over. The powder nearly covered her face. It was too heavy, and the scent was overwhelming; her eyes stung sharply.","Second Madam quickly closed the lid.","“What kind of devilish thing is this?” Second Madam wiped Cheng Zhao’s face while scolding Zhou Yuanqi. “Are you trying to harm someone?”","Cheng Zhao’s eyes were still watering: “Mother, don’t blame Fifth Brother. I wasn’t holding it steady.”","“That’s right. The powder is excellent, and especially fragrant. We can’t make a scent like this ourselves,” Zhou Yuanqi defended himself.","Second Madam: “This fragrance is practically poisonous.”","She hastily turned her head away, sneezed loudly, and shifted to the other side.","By the time the carriage arrived at the Fan mansion, all three who got out were a bit disheveled.","Cheng Zhao’s vision was slightly blurry. She still felt like something was in her eye—maybe the powder hadn’t come out yet. Second Madam sneezed again.","Zhou Yuanqi, having been thoroughly scolded by his mother, was also downcast.","Once they were in front of the Old Dowager, however, they all perked up again.","The Old Dowager had also heard about what happened at the Zhou household.","The Fifth Prince had died; the public announcement would spread across the realm, and the details of his death were already widely known.","Zhou Yuanshen had told Fan Xiao the inside story, and Fan Xiao in turn informed the Old Dowager, so she knew Cheng Zhao hadn’t been bitten by a poisonous snake.","But she still asked with concern: “Were you very frightened, Zhao Zhao?”","Cheng Zhao smiled. “Everything was under control at the time. There was no real surprise, so I wasn’t scared, Grandmother.”","“Truly bold,” the Old Dowager said with a laugh.","She sent someone to summon Fan Xiao and also asked an aunt-in-law to keep them company.","Second Madam didn’t need company; she only wanted to go to the training ground and ride.","Cheng Zhao still felt something in her eye—not pain or itch, just a sense that some powder was still there, making everything she saw seem as if through a thin mist.","She picked a tall, brown horse with a glossy coat and was delighted with it. Fan Xiao praised her good eye.","They went to the training ground to ride, round after round.","After ten laps, Cheng Zhao stopped and sat to the side for tea. Second Madam was still riding, with Zhou Yuanqi following behind her.","Fan Xiao also spurred his horse to accompany Zhou Yuanqi.","After a while, they slowed down and let the horses amble, chatting idly. Fan Xiao spoke now with Second Madam about serious matters, and now teased Zhou Yuanqi.","Cheng Zhao felt someone watching them from not far away, with a focused gaze.","She turned and saw a person wearing a dark, soft armor outfit—somewhat familiar, like one of the Fan family’s deputy generals.","And because of the lingering irritation in her eyes, she had to strain to see his face clearly.","Since she was a guest and also an honored duchess, staring intently at someone who was clearly a man would have been highly improper.","So she turned her face back and continued drinking her tea.","The person didn’t approach or move—he just stood there, still looking at her.","Cheng Zhao noticed it from the corner of her eye and glanced again.","More familiar now.","Just then, Zhou Yuanqi’s horse came closer, and he called out, “Third Brother is here.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","No wonder he looked familiar.","Zhou Yuanshen rarely wore soft armor; she had only seen him in it twice, so she had some memory of it—but the way he looked and carried himself in soft armor was completely different from usual.","The aristocratic refinement of a noble lineage dissipated entirely, leaving only a rugged composure.","He seemed to prefer wearing the soft armor.","Cheng Zhao did a quick calculation: “Why is he back? It hasn’t even been half a month yet.”","Still thinking this, she rose to her feet.","Zhou Yuanshen also heard Zhou Yuanqi’s shout and walked in their direction.","Cheng Zhao took a couple of steps to meet him.","As the distance closed, looking again, it was indeed him.","She greeted him with a bow: “Lord Duke.”","But Zhou Yuanshen ignored her and walked past her toward the training ground.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","It seemed he didn’t recognize her.","But that wasn’t unusual—she thought he looked very unfamiliar too.","They were, after all, no more than vaguely familiar strangers, and after nearly half a month apart, they had grown even more distant."]},{"number":114,"title":"Chapter 114: The Duke's Punishment","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen was grim and stern.","Even though he was usually expressionless, others could tell he was displeased.","Fan Xiao asked him, \"Did you come back from the Jingyi Camp? Why are you still in armor?\"","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t answer him, only said, \"Lend me some of your clothes.\"","Fan Xiao led him to his own courtyard.","Second Madam watched their retreating figures.","Zhou Yuanqi was quite annoyed. \"What’s the Brute acting out for? We haven’t done anything to him. Who’s he giving the cold shoulder to?\"","Cheng Zhao replied, \"It doesn’t seem that way. Lord Duke is always like this.\"","Second Madam said, \"He’s not usually like this. Maybe something unpleasant happened at the camp. After all... forget it, the situation at Jingyi Camp is complicated.\"","Zhou Yuanqi grew even more dissatisfied. \"If he’s unhappy, he has places to go. Why must he come here to make us feel down?\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"I think he came to see Uncle. Maybe he was wronged at Jingyi Camp and wanted to drink with Uncle to vent. He didn’t know we’d be here. Look, he didn’t even change out of his light armor.\"","Second Madam nodded. \"He came straight from Jingyi Camp.\"","Then she looked at Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi. \"Alright, let’s stop worrying about this. Zhaozhao, do you still want to go galloping?\"","\"No more galloping,\" said Cheng Zhao. \"Mother, are you feeling a bit better?\"","\"The pent-up feelings in my heart have all eased,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao smiled. \"In the future, if you’re unhappy, just go gallop. Don’t bottle it up inside.\"","Zhou Yuanqi chimed in, nodding. \"Right, Mother. You’re at such an age. If you keep getting unhappy all the time, you’ll soon grow white hair.\"","Second Madam let out a cold snort. \"How old do you think I am?\"","She still felt quite young.","After all, she wasn’t yet a grandmother and was just over forty. Her health was good, her complexion fine, and with a little grooming, she still had charm.","In truth, at this age, she ought to be a grandmother by now. After all, Zhou Yuanshen was so grown up. If he’d married early, children would be running all over the place.","But it was coming soon.","In another four months, Mu Jiang would give birth, and Second Madam would truly become an old lady—she couldn’t deny aging.","Thinking this, Second Madam felt even more vexed. She didn’t know whether she was annoyed with Mu Jiang or with time itself for making her older.","\"...With a single word, you’ve undone all the good Mother got from galloping all morning. A son like you must be collecting a debt—did Mother owe you in a past life?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanqi retorted, \"One must be able to hear the truth. The truth is always hard to hear.\"","\"You don’t go along with Mother, yet you dare call yourself ‘filial’? If you’re unfilial, how can you stand among the scholars?\" Cheng Zhao questioned.","Zhou Yuanqi was left speechless.","His young face turned red, whether from the galloping or from being angered by Cheng Zhao.","Second Madam couldn’t help but laugh.","\"See, I’m the one who can cheer Mother up. There’s no one more filial than me in the whole world,\" said Cheng Zhao.","Second Madam agreed. \"That’s true. Among my children, Zhaozhao is the most filial.\"","Zhou Yuanqi, having been teased by his mother and sister-in-law together, was quite unhappy but had to grit his teeth and praise his mother a few words.","Before the midday meal, the main hall was lively.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi occasionally bickered; Fan Xiao cracked jokes, siding with whoever was winning, and Zhou Yuanqi called him a fence-sitter.","Zhou Yuanshen did not come.","Second Madam asked once, \"Where’s Yuanshen? Did he go to talk with his grandmother?\"","\"He just came back from the camp; he needs to bathe,\" Fan Xiao said.","Waiting for his hair to dry would take some time.","Just before the meal was served, the hall filled with people, and Zhou Yuanshen arrived late.","He was wearing one of Fan Xiao’s sapphire-blue silk-woven robes, the hem embroidered with cloud patterns. The clothes were brand-new and made of expensive fabric, softening his warrior’s ruggedness and giving him the elegance of a noble son.","Zhou Yuanqi noticed that the Brute was naturally handsome; with a little grooming, he could easily attract attention.","Second Madam praised, \"This robe looks good.\"","\"The color is bright,\" said the Old Dowager. \"Yuanshen usually doesn’t wear such striking shades. This looks much better.\"","Second Madam added, \"We had one made for him, but he wouldn’t take it.\"","They chattered away, but Zhou Yuanshen remained silent, cold as ice.","Second Madam’s heart started to race, wondering what had happened. His expression seemed as if he was holding back a seething rage.","But he rarely lost his temper, and it was impossible to tell whether he was sad or angry.","Zhou Yuanqi mentally cursed Zhou Yuanshen again for spoiling the mood.","Fortunately, Fan Xiao was there, so everyone was still quite cheerful, simply ignoring Zhou Yuanshen.","After the meal, they chatted for a long time before heading home.","The Fan family arranged a horse-drawn carriage for Zhou Yuanshen.","Cheng Zhao was about to leave with Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi when she heard Zhou Yuanshen call out, \"Duchess of Chen.\"","Second Madam looked at him in surprise. \"Who are you calling?\"","She thought, What kind of strange address is that?","Cheng Zhao couldn’t help but laugh. \"He’s calling me.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","Why are you laughing? A young couple, and he calls you Duchess of Chen—what’s so happy about that?","You should be hitting him, daughter-in-law.","Zhou Yuanshen’s gaze landed on Cheng Zhao’s face. \"Get into my carriage.\"","Cheng Zhao agreed.","She left Zhou Yuanqi and Second Madam behind and stepped into Zhou Yuanshen’s carriage.","The curtain fell, and the interior of the carriage was dim.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t speak.","Not until the carriage left the entrance of the Grand General’s mansion did he move.","He leaned over directly, pulling Cheng Zhao into his arms.","His force was great, his movements swift.","Cheng Zhao hadn’t expected him to act this way and was momentarily caught off guard. Before she could react, she was carried and made to straddle his lap.","This posture reminded her of that night he went to Jingyi Camp, behind the tent screen...","She quickly tried to pull away, but Zhou Yuanshen’s hand held her firmly.","\"Duchess of Chen, almost half a month gone, and you don’t recognize me anymore?\" he asked.","His voice was muffled, as if hiding thunderous rage, his breath hot against her cheek.","Cheng Zhao stammered, \"No...\"","As she tried to explain, Zhou Yuanshen tore open her collar with one hand.","Half her shoulder was exposed to the cool air.","Cheng Zhao was shocked and, disregarding any explanation, hurried to cover herself. \"Have you gone mad?\"","Zhou Yuanshen kept one arm around her while his other hand had already pulled loose the sash of her skirt.","Cheng Zhao hadn’t yet fixed her top when her skirt came undone. \"Zhou Yuanshen, you’re outrageous! If you keep this up...\"","Zhou Yuanshen twisted her hands behind her back, binding them securely with her own sash in a swift knot.","He seemed to move with casual ease, and then Cheng Zhao realized her hands were tied tightly—the more she struggled, the tauter they became.","She tried to move, but in her struggle, her collar slipped off again.","Her chest was nearly pressed against his lips.","She tried to dodge again, and her skirt fell open even more.","Cheng Zhao had never been so flustered. \"Zhou Yuanshen, if you dare...\"","Her lips were sealed by his.","Cheng Zhao gradually heard the noisy sounds of people around—the carriage had reached a bustling street.","She froze, her movements stopping.","Zhou Yuanshen nibbled at her lips, then kissed her collarbone. \"Duchess of Chen, be a little quieter. Don’t let anyone hear.\"","Cheng Zhao glared at him, panicked and furious. \"Zhou Yuanshen, if you humiliate me like this, I will never...\"","\"Never what? Not want your title of Duchess of Chen?\" His face was cold. \"Would you be willing to give that up, Duchess of Chen?\"","Cheng Zhao was stunned, caught between shock and anger.","\"Go around half the city, take the long way back,\" he said to the driver.","Before Cheng Zhao could speak, Zhou Yuanshen kissed her again."]},{"number":115,"title":"Chapter 115: Tell Her to Get Lost","paragraphs":["When the carriage returned to the entrance of the Duke of Chen’s mansion, it encountered Mu Jiang and Huan Qingtang.","Mu Jiang was heading out to buy some things. She could have ordered the steward to purchase them, but because she was truly bored and wanted to go out for a stroll, no one dared stop her.","Huan Qingtang was going to check on her dowry silk estates. On the street, she had run into Mu Jiang. At the time, she had addressed her as “Secondary Wife,” but Mu Jiang ignored her.","Now, as the two returned and stepped down from their separate carriages, they happened to meet again at the gate.","Mu Jiang was fuming, feeling that today was extremely unlucky—like running into a ghost in broad daylight.","Huan Qingtang’s expression was gentle, impossible to read as happy or angry. However, the senior maidservant beside her kept staring at Mu Jiang.","Mu Jiang interpreted this as the maidservant thinking that it might lower the Eldest Madam’s status to be seen leaving and returning with the Lord Duke’s concubine.","Mu Jiang was dressed in a bright red gown, vivid as fire, and her temper was just as explosive. She immediately snapped at the maidservant, “What are you staring at?”","Huan Qingtang stepped in front of her. “Secondary Wife, please don’t argue with a servant. She wasn’t looking at you—it was just a misunderstanding.”","“Do you think I’m blind?”","Just as the two were about to quarrel, a carriage pulled up.","It was an unfamiliar carriage, not as luxurious as those of the Duke of Chen’s mansion, but the driver was a young man with a round, dark face.","Both Huan Qingtang and Mu Jiang recognized this driver—he was one of Zhou Yuanshen’s deputy generals.","One of his trusted men, always by his side.","Without a doubt, this was Zhou Yuanshen’s carriage.","“Someone, lower the threshold! The Lord Duke has returned to the mansion,” the deputy general announced loudly.","Before the gateman’s servants could respond, Mu Jiang stepped forward. “Third Brother.”","As she spoke, she tried to lift the carriage curtain.","She vaguely sensed someone moving inside the carriage, thinking Zhou Yuanshen was about to push the curtain aside and emerge. But instead, the deputy general stepped forward to block her. “Secondary Wife, the Lord Duke is resting.”","Mu Jiang frowned. “I’m not going to disturb him.”","Then she raised her voice. “Third Brother, my feet are sore and my legs are weak—I want to ride into the mansion, too.”","There was some kind of rustling noise from inside the carriage, but no one answered her.","Mu Jiang reached again to lift the curtain, but suddenly something struck the back of her hand.","It was a hidden weapon, though it seemed to be an earring made of gold inlaid with pearls. It hit her hand so hard that it almost drew blood.","She cried out sharply in shock, utterly incredulous.","Without meaning to, she stumbled back and fell to the ground. Her maidservant hurried over to help her up.","Then Zhou Yuanshen’s voice came, carrying a unique depth and cruelty: “Get lost!”","By then, the servants had already laid down the threshold and set up the wooden ramp. The deputy general drove the carriage through the gates of the mansion.","As the carriage passed, something shifted by the window, lifting the curtain slightly.","Huan Qingtang happened to look over and caught a glimpse of skin as pale as snow.","For a moment, she thought it might be her imagination.","Her maidservant, seeing Mu Jiang sitting dazed on the ground, couldn’t hide her glee. She whispered to Huan Qingtang, “Some ‘Secondary Wife’ she is. In the Lord Duke’s eyes, even if she’s pregnant, she’s nothing but a concubine.”","If he didn’t hold her in contempt, why would he refuse to let her into the carriage?","This was a direct defiance of the Dowager.","Under normal circumstances, even the head of the family would hardly have the threshold lowered—they would walk in themselves. Since the Lord Duke was making an exception, taking Mu Jiang into the mansion with him wouldn’t have been an inconvenience.","Yet he absolutely refused to let her aboard, even using a hidden weapon to strike her.","Mu Jiang was so furious that tears filled her eyes.","She looked utterly humiliated.","And with Huan Qingtang there to witness the scene, Mu Jiang was even more beside herself with anger. She strode quickly through the gates of the Duke’s mansion.","Her maidservant supported her, cautioning, “Secondary Wife, please slow down. You are carrying twins—you absolutely must take care.”","Mu Jiang wished she could throw a tantrum: He had humiliated her like this, why should she bear his child? If something happened to this child, would he feel any remorse?","But without this child, she would be trampled even deeper into the dirt, which would be even worse for her. The first one to suffer would be herself.","She raised her hand to hit a maidservant to vent her anger, but spotted a male servant standing at the door. She suppressed the impulse with great effort, the veins on her temples bulging.","Huan Qingtang took a few steps and picked up a gold-and-pearl earring.","She examined it closely.","Her maidservant said, \"This must belong to the Third Young Madam, right? She loves wearing gold jewelry the most.\"","Cheng Zhao was naturally beautiful, her skin as fair as frost and snow. When she wore gold-and-pearl headpieces, the pearlescent sheen reflected off her face, as if adding a soft glow to her.","\"Keep it for now. Return it to her tomorrow,\" Huan Qingtang said.","She could also inform Mu Jiang, letting her know why the Lord Duke had told her to get out today. This might serve as an opportunity to stir up conflict between the two.","When the carriage arrived at the entrance of Ronghua Courtyard, Cheng Zhao was already somewhat faint.","The sash binding her hands had long been loosened by Zhou Yuanshen.","A general's armor and lance were heavy, so Zhou Yuanshen possessed astonishing arm strength. His hands gripped Cheng Zhao's waist tightly, the jostling causing all her hair to come loose.","She broke out in a light sweat.","She clung tightly to Zhou Yuanshen, lowering her head to bite down on the fabric of his shoulder, hearing a deep, low sigh escape his throat.","He was usually so silent and unnoticed.","That moment's sensation, Cheng Zhao felt she might never taste again in her lifetime; Zhou Yuanshen seemed to feel the same, as he broke his usual restraint, letting her hear his voice.","Cheng Zhao felt very warm.","Zhou Yuanshen's sapphire-blue kesi robe was still on him. As Cheng Zhao pressed against him, she realized that his collar was already soaked with sweat, and the fabric was scorching hot from his body heat.","At the entrance, she encountered Mu Jiang, and Cheng Zhao was fiercely startled. Subconsciously, she looked for her clothes.","She sobered up a little.","By the time they reached the entrance of Ronghua Courtyard, she had barely managed to wrap herself in her garments, her skirt in disarray; her hair was also loose and scattered.","Zhou Yuanshen adjusted himself casually, his lapel already wrinkled. He jumped down from the carriage first, then carried Cheng Zhao in with broad strides.","Nanny Li was momentarily stunned.","Suyue, a senior maidservant who had never experienced such matters but often kept night watch, reacted even faster than Nanny Li, ordering everyone to step back.","Zhou Yuanshen carried Cheng Zhao back to the inner sleeping chamber.","Nanny Li had already instructed the rough maids to prepare hot water.","There was a small tea room nearby, with two stoves that could both simmer soup and boil water.","They happened to have two pots of hot water on hand; the weather wasn't cold at that moment, so it was barely sufficient.","Suyue asked at the doorway, \"Third Young Madam, should we bring water in?\"","Zhou Yuanshen's voice came from inside: \"Bring it in.\"","After Cheng Zhao had tidied herself up and changed into an under robe and trousers, she lay on the bed, drowsy, and drifted into a deep sleep without realizing it.","When she woke up in the latter part of the night, she found Zhou Yuanshen was not by her side.","Cheng Zhao called for the maidservant on night duty.","Qiubai came in: \"Are you hungry? There's bird's nest congee on the small stove.\"","\"Bring me a bowl,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Qiubai acknowledged the order.","Cheng Zhao draped a short jacket over herself, got up, and walked around the screen to sit at the table and drink the bird's nest congee.","\"Where is the Lord Duke?\" she asked.","\"The Lord Duke saw that you were sound asleep, changed his clothes, and went out. He didn't give any instructions or say whether he would return,\" Qiubai said.","The spoon in Cheng Zhao's hand paused slightly.","Qiubai asked softly, \"...Did he mistreat you?\"","\"No,\" Cheng Zhao said.","At the time, she was absolutely furious.","But in the moment when her soul was utterly intoxicated, she simply couldn’t stay angry.","Inside the carriage, hidden by the walls and curtains, no one could see them.","In the mortal world, pleasures were always scarce. To have a fleeting moment of exquisite rapture was exceedingly rare.","Cheng Zhao did not chase after happiness—she could endure a plain and uneventful life just as well. She was not the sort to indulge in delight. Yet, when genuine pleasure came to her, she was also willing to savor it.","Thus, she chose not to be angry.","She even asked after Zhou Yuanshen’s whereabouts."]},{"number":116,"title":"Chapter 116: The Duke of the State Ignores Her","paragraphs":["Late at night, a crescent moon hung faintly on the distant treetops, its thin silvery light streaming through the window.","Cheng Zhao had finished her bird’s nest porridge when her maid Suyue entered and placed a lamp on the kang table.","“...The Lord Duke sent it in.” Qiubai handed a kite to Cheng Zhao.","It was a butterfly kite.","The butterfly closely resembled the one Cheng Zhao had embroidered on a pouch last time—plump, endearingly naive, and full of childlike charm.","“The painting is well done. The kite itself is a bit roughly made, but the artwork is exquisite,” Cheng Zhao said. “Did he claim to have made it himself?”","“He didn’t say,” Suyue replied. “Soon after you left, the Lord Duke arrived.”","Qiubai added, “He was wearing soft armor, and his hair was damp, as if wet by dew. Arriving in the city so early, he must have ridden back from the camp late at night and waited at the city gate. The moment the gates opened, he returned.”","Suyue nodded. “The Lord Duke set down the kite and was about to leave when Mingyu, the maid from the outer study, came looking for him.”","Cheng Zhao pieced the events together.","Zhou Yuanshen had likely taken a night ride back to the city, waited at the city gate for it to open, and then rode slowly home because galloping was prohibited inside the city walls.","By the time he reached the mansion, Cheng Zhao and Second Madam had already left for the Fan residence.","After delivering the kite, he had intended to go to the Fan residence to find her. But during his absence from home, numerous matters must have piled up.","Mingyu, the maid from the outer study, had probably spotted his adjutant, learned that he had returned to the mansion, and sought him out for urgent business.","By the time he arrived at the Fan residence, Cheng Zhao and the others had already been riding for quite a while.","Thinking about how hectic his day must have been, Cheng Zhao no longer felt angry.","She said, “This kite is nice. When things are less busy in a few days, I’ll take Heng’er to fly it.”","Heng’er was her niece, the daughter of her eldest sister.","Unable to sleep, Cheng Zhao took out her sewing basket and asked Suyue to bring out some fabric. She chose a piece of sapphire blue, intending to make a pouch for Zhou Yuanshen.","That sapphire blue outfit Fan Xiao had lent him today really suited him well.","Qiubai was getting drowsy, so Cheng Zhao told her to go to bed first.","Suyue stayed with her, helping to arrange the threads.","“Young Mistress, please don’t quarrel with the Lord Duke,” Suyue said. “Given the current situation, I’m truly a bit frightened.”","She was referring to the Dowager Madame’s faction.","What had started as minor skirmishes had turned bloody since the death of the Fifth Prince.","A domestic matter had become a blood feud between two great clans. Consort Dou and the Duke of Piguo’s household would not forgive the Dowager Madame, and the Dowager Madame would undoubtedly blame Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen.","“I don’t intend to fight with him,” Cheng Zhao said. “Didn’t you see I’m preparing to make him a pouch?”","“That’s good,” Suyue said. “But you shouldn’t let him push you around either. I was terrified when we returned yesterday.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","She recalled everything that had happened in the carriage—anger mixed with a trace of uncontrollable arousal. She was becoming addicted to it.","After all, Zhou Yuanshen was a military officer, and in that regard, he was truly formidable.","“I’ll talk to him when he gets back tomorrow,” Cheng Zhao said.","They sat for a while, and she yawned; Suyue was also a bit sleepy.","The mistress and maid rested and slept until daybreak.","As usual, Cheng Zhao went to Crimson Cloud Courtyard first thing in the morning. Second Madam asked her what she and Zhou Yuanshen had discussed the previous day, and Cheng Zhao only said “trivial matters.”","“What is Yuanshen busy with today? If he’s free, tell him to come here for dinner. He hasn’t been home for some time,” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “He went to the early court session this morning, I think. I didn’t see him. I’ll pass the word.”","Second Madam nodded.","That day, while handling duties at the Hall of Brightness, Huan Qingtang arrived.","She handed a gold-and-pearl earring to Cheng Zhao. “I picked this up yesterday. Could it have fallen off you, sister-in-law?”","Cheng Zhao took it. “Thank you, Elder Sister-in-law.”","“Be more careful in the future, sister-in-law. If things are lost, they may be gone for good,” Huan Qingtang said with a smile.","Cheng Zhao nodded. “You’re right. But such small things—if lost, I can simply replace them. It’s no loss.”","Huan Qingtang inclined her head slightly. “Well said, sister-in-law.”","They exchanged a few more words, all seeming quite amicable, until First Madam Madam Song emerged.","Madam Song’s expression was none too pleasant.","She looked at Cheng Zhao, then at Huan Qingtang, as if she wanted to say something.","“Mother, what’s wrong?” Huan Qingtang asked.","Madam Song said, “I only just found out that my relative has left the Duke’s mansion. Were either of you aware?”","She was referring to Hu Zhiwei.","Her gaze turned to Cheng Zhao.","That day at the Double Ninth Temple, Hu Zhiwei had stepped forward to help Cheng Zhao put on a wooden hairpin; she might have been involved in the matter, though Madam Song wasn’t sure whose orders she had followed. Had Cheng Zhao used her, or was it the Dowager Madame?","“Where did she go?” Huan Qingtang asked, sounding slightly surprised.","“They said she went to the Song family. I sent someone to inquire, but the Song family said the Hu clan had come and taken her back to the Hu ancestral home,” First Madam said.","“That might be for the best,” Huan Qingtang said. “Going back to her own clan is safer, after all. And our younger cousin has reached the age for marriage.”","“If her clan were good to her, why would she have run away in the first place?” First Madam said bitterly.","Her anger seemed directed at someone, but it wasn’t clear whom.","“After all, she bears the Hu name. If her clan wants to take charge, there’s nothing we can do. You’ve already done your best,” Huan Qingtang said.","First Madam’s wrath spilled out toward Huan Qingtang. “You speak so easily!”","Their mutual discord was now out in the open.","Cheng Zhao, uninvolved, merely listened silently as they exchanged words. Even if First Madam had questioned her directly, she wouldn’t have intervened.","At noon, Cheng Zhao rested at the Morning Light Pavilion.","She asked her servant Nanfeng, “What time will the Lord Duke return this afternoon?”","Nanfeng said, “That’s uncertain.”","In the late afternoon, when her duties at the Hall of Brightness were finished, Zhou Yuanshen still hadn’t returned to the Morning Light Pavilion.","Cheng Zhao told Nanfeng, “Second Madam has requested that the Lord Duke go to Crimson Cloud Courtyard for dinner. If he returns before dinner, be sure to inform him.”","Nanfeng acknowledged the order.","Cheng Zhao went back to Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","She didn’t mention what had happened at the Hall of Brightness.","She felt that, compared to when she had first married into the family, Great Uncle’s wife and Huan Qingtang had both changed a lot. Their usual calm composure was cracking, revealing their flustered inner selves.","Zhou Yuanqi returned from the clan school.","“How are your eyes?” he asked Cheng Zhao.","Her eyes had been uncomfortable the day before and still blurry while riding, but after a night’s sleep, they were fine.","“That powder was just heavily scented, and it wasn’t poisonous. I’m better now. Thank you for your concern, Fifth Brother,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi asked, “Did you put that powder on? Is the color light on your face?”","Cheng Zhao replied, “Not yet. I still don’t have the courage...”","“What courage does it take?”","“It’s so fragrant that my nose would be overwhelmed in a day. Anyone walking past me would think I’m trying to attract bees and butterflies. I’m not ready to endure the mockery,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi: “...”","“Would you like it back?” Cheng Zhao asked with a smile.","Zhou Yuanqi said, “It was so expensive. What use would it be to me?”","Second Madam scolded him, “How many times must street vendors swindle you before you learn your lesson?”","Cheng Zhao, teasing him but fond of her younger brother-in-law, defended him. “Mother, even if he does get cheated, it’s not like he can’t afford it. He’s lucky.”","“You’ve learned this from your grandmother!” Second Madam said. “You two spoil him.”","Zhou Yuanqi snorted.","That evening, Second Master returned to Crimson Cloud Courtyard, but Zhou Yuanshen didn’t come.","Nanfeng said he hadn’t returned to the Duke’s mansion at all.","Three days later, Cheng Zhao had finished making the pouch but still hadn’t seen Zhou Yuanshen.","She asked Nanfeng.","“The Lord Duke still hasn’t returned, so I’ve been eating his evening meal myself. Young Madam, thank goodness you’re in charge of the main kitchen; we can bend the rules.” Nanfeng said cheerfully.","The child ate well, in high spirits, and recounted this to Cheng Zhao with great animation.","But Cheng Zhao frowned.","She was still waiting for Zhou Yuanshen to apologize to her.","He had actually been avoiding her. Could it be that he was angry because she hadn’t recognized him at the time?","Did she have to go and apologize to him?","Utterly senseless.","Cheng Zhao had originally planned not to be angry, to let it go this time as long as he said a few kind words; but now, mulling it over, she felt a surge of indignation.","She returned to Ronghua Courtyard, and the first thing she did was cut that embroidered pouch to pieces.","Nanny Li saw it and said with regret, “Why cut up something so nice? Making one is such a bother.”","Cheng Zhao didn’t reply, but turned and went to the washroom to freshen up."]},{"number":117,"title":"Chapter 117: Pretending to Be Ill to Force Him to Show Up","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen had vanished without a trace.","No one knew where he lived.","His manservant, Nanfeng, went to the main kitchen every day to fetch his evening meal, leading everyone to believe he was resting in the Morning Glory Courtyard or his outer study.","Mu Jiang sobbed to the Dowager Madame, “Third Brother never comes to the Scenic View Courtyard. Cheng Shi is going too far—she’s monopolizing him.”","The Dowager Madame had no heart for such matters.","She merely said, “Cheng Shi is the Duchess. Whatever quarrel you have with her must be settled between yourselves. You can also ask the Lord Duke directly.”","Mu Jiang was displeased.","She wanted to wheedle, but the Dowager Madame was clearly out of sorts.","Mu Jiang had seen the Dowager Madame lose her temper before, though only twice. It was terrifying, and she was afraid.","As she left the Longevity Courtyard, her maidservant had picked up a little secret and whispered it to her.","“...I heard from the carriage house that on the day of the Fan family’s carriage, Cheng Shi was also inside,” the maidservant murmured.","At first, Mu Jiang didn’t recall.","Then, as she slowly pieced it together, rage flared within her.","Third Brother hadn’t let her board the carriage. To stop her, he hadn’t even hesitated to strike her with an earring—because Cheng Zhao was in the carriage.","Those earrings belonged to Cheng Zhao.","But as Cheng Zhao was the Duchess, what was she afraid of in seeing Mu Jiang?","Unless...","Unless the two of them were in no state to be seen at that moment.","Third Brother had always taken care of her, yet that day, in front of the servants and Huan Qingtang, he told her to go away—just to cover up Cheng Zhao’s disgrace?","That woman stopped at nothing to monopolize Third Brother and secure an heir.","Mu Jiang’s chest tightened with a dull ache.","After brooding half the night, Mu Jiang decided to “feign illness.” She was desperate to see Zhou Yuanshen, but the Dowager Madame was unwilling to help her.","“Ever since the Fifth Prince died and Third Brother got the post at the Capital Garrison, Grandmother has been wary of him,” Mu Jiang said to those around her.","The most loyal maidservant in the Scenic View Courtyard had been sent by her to kill the crane and was subsequently dealt with.","The current servants were mostly terrified of Mu Jiang.","But they feared the Dowager Madame even more.","Mu Jiang dared to say such things, but the maidservants dared not listen—each kept her head low.","The palace-attended governess assigned to care for Mu Jiang’s pregnancy grew worried when she heard about the feigned illness and said, “Secondary Wife must be careful not to tempt fate.”","A pregnancy was always fraught with uncertainties, prone to accident. Newborns, too, could easily be lost.","Frail as glass—how could one use a child to claim illness?","Even the palace ladies, with imperial physicians at hand, dared not do such a thing.","If the worst came true, there would be no time for tears.","Ever since these governesses arrived at the Scenic View Courtyard, the Lord Duke had hardly set foot there. If Mu Jiang made trouble and lost the child, the Lord Duke would be even angrier, and he might cut ties with the courtyard for good.","They had been sent to look after Mu Jiang; if harm came to her unborn child, they would be blamed.","“My child is fine. He is blessed. How could the future Duke of Chen be harmed by a little wind or rain?” Mu Jiang said.","The governess fell silent.","She wanted to argue further, but another governess shook her head, silently telling her to save her breath. If she couldn’t persuade Mu Jiang, she should leave no evidence of their conversation, lest the Secondary Wife later turn around and accuse them of cursing the child.","Both governesses held their tongues.","The next day, when Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang arrived at the Hall of Inheritance to attend to their duties, they saw a maidservant from the Scenic View Courtyard hurry over to say to the First Madam, “The Secondary Wife says her stomach hurts.”","The First Madam Song’s first reaction was not worry but smugness. She turned her gaze to Cheng Zhao, waiting to see how she would react.","Cheng Zhao put on a perfectly standard look of concern.","Madam Song was at a loss for words.","Cheng Zhao was truly skilled at putting on an act.","The maidservant had spoken, but no one answered her.","After a few moments of silence, Huan Qingtang picked up the thread: “What happened?”","“She said she felt unwell yesterday. Last night she had a nightmare—dreamt the Lord Duke was injured. She barely slept, and this morning she woke up feeling unwell,” the maidservant explained.","Again, silence.","The First Madam and Huan Qingtang both looked at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao looked back at them with an almost girlish, panicked expression, as if lost—as if to say, What should I do, Eldest Aunt-in-law, Sister-in-law?","The First Madam’s heart seized up.","Cheng Zhao was too cunning, too troublesome.","When there was benefit to be had, she lunged forward like a beast, refusing to let go until she had bitten into her prize. But the moment trouble arose, she played the fool.","To saddle her with a burden or responsibility was an almost impossible task.","Even Huan Qingtang didn’t have that kind of sharpness.","The First Madam suddenly felt her own daughter-in-law was somewhat inferior to Cheng Zhao.","Only half a year ago, she had thought Cheng Zhao had no way forward, that the Duke’s mansion had no place for her, that she was utterly unremarkable.","Huan Qingtang had been the one to say they should scrutinize Cheng Zhao more closely, learn her background.","In less than half a year, they still hadn’t uncovered her background, but the mansion had already made room for her.","“...Mother, shall we inform Grandmother, or send directly for a physician?” Huan Qingtang broke the silence once more.","The one ruling the Hall of Inheritance was the mistress of the Duke’s mansion; the two women, mother and daughter-in-law, had to decide.","“Go tell the Dowager Madame and ask which imperial physician she wishes to summon. Use her name card to request the visit,” the First Madam said.","Only titled ladies had the right to summon imperial physicians. The one who could get the best was no doubt the Dowager Madame of the Zhou family.","Huan Qingtang said, “I’ll go.”","Without waiting for the First Madam’s reply, she rose and left.","The First Madam felt an urge to call her back and rebuke her, an instinct to keep Huan Qingtang in line.","But she hadn’t lost her mind.","If anything happened to Mu Jiang, the Dowager Madame would flay her alive. With Mu Jiang involved, this was a hot potato—let Lady Huan handle it.","The First Madam said to Mu Jiang’s maidservant, “Go to the Longevity Courtyard and see what happens.”","The maidservant answered in the affirmative.","After they left, the First Madam got up and headed to the western side-room to rest a little, leaving Cheng Zhao and the stewards in the main hall.","The stewards hardly dared discuss the Secondary Wife. They chatted idly; Cheng Zhao answered in a leisurely manner, her demeanor unchanged.","“...Why do I find Lady Huan so unbearable these days?” the First Madam wondered as she sipped her tea.","Shouldn’t she hate Cheng Zhao more?","In a short time, her distaste for Huan Qingtang had grown intense. Every time Huan Qingtang spoke, she felt like she was about to explode with rage.","Had Cheng Zhao poisoned the well?","Or was it Huan Qingtang’s attitude that was off?","The First Madam couldn’t think straight; she was simply irritable.","Soon, someone from the Longevity Courtyard arrived and said to Cheng Zhao, “Third Young Madam, the Dowager Madame requests your presence.”","Cheng Zhao stood up. “I’ll inform the First Madam and then go.”","She stood at the entrance of the western side-room, exchanged a few words with the First Madam, and then turned to leave.","As she stepped out of the Hall of Inheritance, she glanced at her maidservant, Suyue, and said to her, “I’m going to the Longevity Courtyard.”","Suyue softly assented.","Watching her walk away, Suyue hurried to the Morning Glory Courtyard and found the manservant Nanfeng.","“Where is the Lord Duke? The Third Young Madam has been summoned to the Longevity Courtyard by the Dowager Madame,” Suyue said. “If anything happens, call the Lord Duke to save her.”","Nanfeng said, “I don’t know.”","“Tell the truth. Otherwise, you won’t get to eat the Lord Duke’s evening meal anymore.”","“The Lord Duke is at the General’s Mansion. He has been staying there these days.”","Suyue was speechless."]},{"number":118,"title":"Chapter 118: A Houseful of Women Belonging to Him","paragraphs":["The Dowager Madame was not seriously ill.","She had simply given her name card to Cheng Zhao, instructing her to tell the head steward at the gate to arrange for a carriage to fetch Doctor Wang.","Doctor Wang specialized in women’s health.","“There’s also Doctor Feng, who is just as skilled in women’s health, but he is close with the Dou family,” the Dowager Madame said.","The Dou family was the Duke of Piguo’s household, Consort Dou’s maternal relatives. Since Consort Dou’s son, the Fifth Prince, had just died, summoning Doctor Feng at this time would be highly unwise.","No one knew if Consort Dou had lost her mind and might do something rash.","“Doctor Wang is a fine physician, and he has some connections with our estate. When you reward him later, be generous,” the Dowager Madame added.","Cheng Zhao acknowledged the order.","Cheng Zhao understood the Dowager Madame’s meaning at once: Doctor Feng was the best at women’s health, and he had previously been the one to diagnose Mu Jiang. Doctor Wang was second only to Doctor Feng, but due to the matter with Consort Dou, they had to switch to him on short notice.","Since this was likely Doctor Wang’s first visit, the gatekeeper would need to prepare a gift—explaining this task to anyone else would take ages. But telling Cheng Zhao required only a single sentence; she instantly knew what to do.","The Dowager Madame’s demeanor held no particular displeasure. She simply directed Cheng Zhao to handle the matter, and once done, they both left together.","Cheng Zhao went to the gatehouse to delegate the task and appoint a capable steward. Meanwhile, the Dowager Madame and Huan Qingtang took a small oiled cart to Lijing Court to visit Mu Jiang.","At the entrance, after finishing her errands and preparing to return, Cheng Zhao spotted the young servant Nanfeng heading out.","“...Where are you off to?” Cheng Zhao asked as she looked at Nanfeng, who was leading a horse.","Nanfeng was only twelve, barely taller than the horse, yet he was leading a fine steed; Cheng Zhao worried he might not be able to control it.","“I’m going to the General’s Mansion, Young Madam,” Nanfeng said with a grin.","He waited for Cheng Zhao to ask further.","Cheng Zhao only warned him, “Take it slow. Don’t gallop through the streets—if the authorities catch you, no one will save you. Remember?”","Riding a horse recklessly through a bustling market was a crime, and getting caught would even implicate the master’s household.","“Yes, Young Madam. But the Lord Duke, he...”","Cheng Zhao turned and went back inside.","She didn’t wait for Nanfeng to finish.","Nanfeng: “...”","Hadn’t the Young Madam gotten into trouble with the Lord Duke? How could she be even angrier than he was?","Nanfeng scratched his head. Thinking of the generous piece of meat the head cook in the main kitchen had slipped into his bowl, he knew better than to offend the Young Madam. Without her special favor, why would the head cook give him extra food? And without her approval, how would he dare take the portion of the evening meal his master had refused?","In a grand estate, the most lucrative places had the most tangled human relationships and the most sycophantic, snobbish behavior. The main kitchen was one such “fat-cat post.”","Nanfeng was only twelve, and the two most important things in his life were sleeping well and eating well.","Food was paramount.","He had to go to the General’s Mansion.","After giving her orders, Cheng Zhao sat quietly in a small room of the gatehouse.","Two stewards chatted idly with her.","Their words were all flattery and ingratiation.","“If you have other matters to attend to, feel free to go. I’ll personally escort Doctor Wang inside when he arrives,” one steward said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “It’s fine. Today, this is my only task; I’ll wait here.”","She couldn’t afford to give the Dowager Madame any handle.","The Dowager Madame had specifically called her to Shou’an Court and solemnly entrusted her with this duty.","She also inquired about a social matter: “For Grand Princess Fukang’s recent banquet, what gift did our estate send?”","From Grand Princess Fukang, the talk turned to the estate’s interactions with the Prince of Jingnan’s mansion and the Princess Qing’an’s residence. Some matters would never be recorded in ledgers.","Cheng Zhao’s questions were purposeful; the stewards were tight-lipped and answered selectively.","Although the gate was under Cheng Zhao’s supervision, she hadn’t managed to win over many loyal followers. Her people were in the inner courtyard, and she couldn’t easily extend her reach outward.","The gate was too crucial—it governed the family’s social connections—and the stewards there were all confidants of the Dowager Madame.","Back when Zhou Yuanshen had contested the Dowager Madame, she had compromised and given Cheng Zhao authority over the gate, but the stewards had always been wary of her.","Cheng Zhao had no intention of gobbling everything up at once. She made inquiries slowly, probed indirectly, without fanfare.","The April weather was warm, and a thin sheen of sweat formed on the steward’s forehead.","This told Cheng Zhao she had gained some prestige. Both the inner and outer courtyards knew of her competence, and the stewards were so on edge when speaking to her that they sweated in nervousness.","“Manager Sun, your daughter married the head steward’s youngest son, didn’t she?” Cheng Zhao asked abruptly. “And Nanny Sun, who serves the Dowager Madame, seems to be your relative by clan?”","“Yes, Young Madam’s intelligence and connections are keen,” Manager Sun replied with a smile.","“Are you all originally from the Marquis of Qingyuan’s lineage? A house-born servant family of the Marquis?” Cheng Zhao pressed.","Manager Sun: “Our family has been with the Zhous for decades. My parents were originally the Dowager Madame’s dowry servants. Strictly speaking, we’re house-born servants of the Zhou clan, not the Sun family.”","“So you do share ties of long-standing loyalty with the Dowager Madame,” Cheng Zhao remarked with a smile.","She affected an air of deep respect and high regard for him.","But it startled Manager Sun, who broke out in a cold sweat: “I dare not claim that. We’re all merely servants.”","“You are indeed loyal and steadfast, Manager Sun,” Cheng Zhao said. “The ledgers you’ve given me have always been very clear and very clean.”","“Clean” meant they hadn’t disclosed the full truth.","Sweat trickled down Manager Sun’s nose.","Cheng Zhao took a sip of tea and asked no further.","A servant boy came to announce that Doctor Wang had arrived.","Cheng Zhao rose leisurely, handed Manager Sun a red packet containing a fifty-tael banknote, and said, “Give this to Doctor Wang discreetly when you see him. It’s his first visit, and the Dowager Madame holds him in high regard.”","Manager Sun accepted the packet.","Cheng Zhao walked along the covered corridor to the main gate, but before she stepped over the threshold, she came face-to-face with Zhou Yuanshen.","He was dressed in his court robes, neat and formal, with dark, quiet eyes that held a detached and stern coldness.","“Lord Duke.” Cheng Zhao performed a curtsy.","“Has the physician arrived?” he asked.","Cheng Zhao looked behind him. A carriage had just stopped, and a doctor in official robes was descending: “He has arrived.”","Zhou Yuanshen turned.","Seeing him, Doctor Wang respectfully bowed: “The Grand Tutor.”","Zhou Yuanshen responded, “We are in your debt, Doctor.”","Cheng Zhao mused that in the official world, they addressed each other by rank. Of Zhou Yuanshen’s three posts, two were military; “the Grand Tutor” was his highest and most prestigious title.","His peers called him “Grand Tutor Zhou.”","They went together to Lijing Court.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t enter the inner bedchamber, but a servant girl informed Mu Jiang inside.","She wanted to come out, but didn’t dare act rashly.","Zhou Yuanshen and Cheng Zhao waited in the eastern side room. The Dowager Madame stayed in the inner room; once the physician went in, Huan Qingtang emerged.","She greeted Zhou Yuanshen: “Lord Duke.”","“Elder Sister-in-law.” Zhou Yuanshen’s face grew even colder. “How is Concubine Mu?”","In his mouth, it was always “Concubine Mu” — never an intimate term, and certainly no one might imagine he would call her “Lady Consort.”","Those two words, “Concubine Mu,” must have grated on the ear, hadn’t they?","Huan Qingtang thought this, glanced at Cheng Zhao, then quickly looked away: “Her complexion is fair. It may be due to excessive worry. The physician is still taking her pulse.”","Zhou Yuanshen gave a slight nod.","He said nothing more.","Someone brought in tea.","Then someone else brought in refreshments.","They remained standing on either side, serving, and didn’t leave.","These were two young, beautiful women, dressed in finery and adorned with pearls and jade—far from ordinary servant girls’ attire.","They were presumably bestowed by the Dowager Madame and placed in Lijing Courtyard as bedchamber maids to help Mu Jiang win over the Lord Duke.","\"All of them are quite attractive—the Dowager Madame has good taste,\" Cheng Zhao couldn't help thinking."]},{"number":119,"title":"Chapter 119: Zhou Yuanshen Physically Assaults Someone","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao didn't look around.","She slowly drank her tea, her gaze never wandering recklessly.","She neither looked at Zhou Yuanshen's current expression, wondering if he was smug, nor did she glance at the two maidservants, though her peripheral vision caught them sneaking peeks at her and him.","She also didn't look at Huan Qingtang. From any angle, with the Lord Duke, his wife, and his concubine sitting there, she shouldn't have stayed—yet she acted as if nothing was amiss, remaining with them.","As if this large bed had a place reserved for her, too.","Cheng Zhao found it inexplicably amusing.","Her marriage couldn't bear deep thought; the moment she dwelled on it, it became absurd.","Still, it was rather interesting.","Life within high-ranking households was often dull and insipid, with conflicts that grew tiresome. But the Zhou family added a few extra laughs.","If the Zhou family ever fell from power, these matters would be gossiped about freely, providing fodder for mockery for over a century.","Cheng Zhao sat up straight, expressionless, until a maidservant entered and said: \"Doctor Wang has finished taking the pulse; the Dowager Madame requests the Lord Duke.\"","Zhou Yuanshen turned to Cheng Zhao instead: \"Go and ask Grandmother and the doctor to come out here for a chat. If we all crowd in there, it won't be good for Concubine Mu.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Either he didn't return to the residence at all, or he'd toss a hot potato to her the moment he came back.","But thinking it over, with all these women in the room, could he order his elder brother's widow around, or command the two maidservant-concubines?","Only Cheng Zhao was available.","Grumbling inwardly, Cheng Zhao wasted no time on her face. The moment his words fell, she performed a crisp bow: \"As you command, Lord Duke.\"","She left.","The inner bedchamber of Mu Jiang was particularly ornate. Between the bed and the door stood a boxwood screen—costly material, with a painting on it by a master's hand.","Cheng Zhao still remembered how Zhou Yuanshen had once spent a night in the Nonghua Courtyard; afterward, to \"appease\" Mu Jiang, he had specially given her an expensive screen, something Mu Jiang bragged about to the entire household.","This was probably it.","Truly a fine piece, certainly worth boasting about.","Circling around the screen, she saw Mu Jiang sitting on the bed, the Dowager Madame seated beside her on the edge; half a roomful of maidservants and matrons stood around. She was no gentle-born lady—during the pulse diagnosis, the bed curtain wasn't lowered, as the doctor needed to observe and listen.","Doctor Wang had already packed his medical bag, standing respectfully to the side, waiting.","Cheng Zhao caught a faint, familiar fragrance.","It seemed recognizable, but she couldn't recall where she'd smelled it before.","\"Cheng Shi, where is the Lord Duke?\" the Dowager Madame asked with a smile.","Cheng Zhao: \"Grandmother, the Lord Duke is very worried about the Secondary Wife. He came straight from court in his official robes. Since he's been running around the court and government offices all day, he was afraid the smell on his clothes would bother the Secondary Wife. He asks Doctor Wang to come out for a word.\"","The Dowager Madame smiled: \"The Lord Duke is always so thoughtful and considerate.\"","As she said this, she looked at Mu Jiang.","Mu Jiang could have replied, uttering a few polite and graceful words, but her expression changed instead.","She stood up abruptly: \"I want to see Third Brother.\"","Two matrons quickly moved to hold her down: \"Secondary Wife, please don't get agitated. Doctor Wang said you must rest quietly.\"","Even Doctor Wang was startled: \"Secondary Wife, you need to take it slowly.\"","\"I want to see Third Brother!\" Mu Jiang raised her voice.","In front of Cheng Zhao, the Dowager Madame, and—an outsider—Doctor Wang, she blatantly disregarded all propriety, shouting loudly: \"Third Brother! Third Brother!\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Sometimes she thought, to live as freely as Mu Jiang, doing everything without overthinking, must be very easy.","She called out several times in a row.","What was surprising was that no footsteps came from beyond the screen.","Zhou Yuanshen, in the Eastern Side Room, seemed not to have heard.","He should have heard.","Mu Jiang seemed stunned, tilting her ear to listen for movement outside.","Dead silence.","After several breaths, she stood there dazed.","Doctor Wang wished he could disappear; the Dowager Madame's smile didn't fade in the slightest.","\"Grandmother, did Third Brother...did he go out?\" Mu Jiang asked, shock giving way to tears brimming in her eyes, before she called out again with a sob, \"Third Brother!\"","This time, crying and shouting, she called out four or five times in a row.","Again, silence outside.","No one spoke.","The maidservants and attendants held their breath, barely daring to breathe.","The Dowager Madame's face turned pale.","Doctor Wang looked at Cheng Zhao, wanting to retreat but feeling it would be too obvious.","Still, silence.","When Mu Jiang rushed out again, no one tried to stop her; instead, they all followed her.","Wearing only her inner garments and trousers, without shoes, she charged straight into the Eastern Side Room.","Zhou Yuanshen sat on the heated brick bed by the window in the Eastern Side Room; Huan Qingtang sat on a nearby armchair, while the two maidservant-concubines stood quietly to the side.","Seeing Mu Jiang burst in, Huan Qingtang rose slowly.","Without a word, she gestured to the two maidservant-concubines, and the three of them withdrew into the main hall.","The Dowager Madame, supported by the supervising Nanny Sun, also came out, followed by Cheng Zhao and the two matrons.","Doctor Wang lagged behind, standing among the maidservants.","The Eastern Side Room and the main hall were separated by a single wall, with no door shut—only a half-length curtain hanging in the doorway.","\"Third Brother, why did you ignore me?\" Mu Jiang's voice, crying and accusing, carried clearly to everyone: \"Why do you only favor Cheng Shi and neglect me? I'm already able to serve you.\"","Zhou Yuanshen's voice was low and slow: \"Concubine Mu, not only do you disrespect me, but you also fail to show respect to the lady with a super-rank honorary title?\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Very clever—he shifted the blame onto her. The men of the Zhou family were indeed all masters at this.","\"And you still want to defend her?\"","\"It's not because you're delicate or because you're pregnant that I won't strike you. It's because that slap wouldn't land on your face—it would land on Grandmother,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He seemed to have turned his head: \"Grandmother, am I right with those words?\"","The Dowager Madame could no longer stand idly by: \"Go and bring Concubine Mu out.\"","Concubine Mu...","Those two words struck like a thunderclap in the room. Not only Cheng Zhao, but even the unflappable Huan Qingtang raised her eyes to look at the Dowager Madame.","In front of everyone, the Dowager Madame was also calling Mu Jiang \"Concubine Mu\"?","If one didn't know Zhou Yuanshen's cold nature, one might think he deliberately provoked Mu Jiang just to challenge the Dowager Madame.","And clearly, he had won.","Mu Jiang had trampled both her own face and the Dowager Madame's.","Nanny Sun and another matron entered the Eastern Side Room.","Mu Jiang wept and screamed: \"Third Brother, why are you treating me like this? I'm carrying your child!\"","Nanny Sun pressed a hand over her mouth.","\"Secondary Wife, if you move again and the child is harmed, you'll have even more to cry about in the days to come,\" Nanny Sun threatened her in a low voice.","Mu Jiang's struggles instantly stopped.","She was led out of the Eastern Side Room, and upon seeing the Dowager Madame, tears streamed down her face: \"Grandmother, you must do right by me.\"","Then she saw Cheng Zhao: \"It's all your fault! You've stirred up chaos in our household, poisoning Third Brother with your wicked ways... Ah!\"","Her knees buckled. Nanny Sun and the matron didn't steady her in time, and she stumbled, kneeling before Cheng Zhao.","Her lower abdomen nearly hit the ground.","\"Secondary Wife!\" The matron feared she'd accidentally dropped her, panicking.","But everyone could see that Zhou Yuanshen, standing at the doorway, had pulled one of the beaded strings from the room's curtain and struck Mu Jiang's knees with the beads.","The beads on the curtain snapped at that very moment, scattering with a series of sharp cracks across the floor. Plump and round, they bounced vigorously against the blue bricks.","For a long time, the room remained utterly silent, the only sound the rolling of beads.","Zhou Yuanshen stepped forward, bypassing Mu Jiang, bypassing the Dowager Madame, and clasped Cheng Zhao's hand.","\"Grandmother, you have seen it for yourself. Concubine Mu is not ill; she is merely acting spoiled and throwing this tantrum,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He fixed his gaze on the Dowager Madame. \"If you refuse to handle it, let Cheng Shi take charge. She is my secondary wife, and disciplining my concubines is her rightful duty, is it not, Grandmother?\"","The Dowager Madame’s cheek twitched sharply.","She was losing control.","She stared back at Zhou Yuanshen, her gaze fierce yet quiet: \"That will not be necessary. I will continue to teach Ah Jiang myself. Cheng Shi is young; what does she know of managing others?\""]},{"number":120,"title":"Chapter 120: The Duke Ignores Cheng Zhao Again","paragraphs":["The confrontation between the Dowager Madame and Zhou Yuanshen was something none of the onlookers could quite comprehend.","But one thing was clear: the Dowager Madame cared more about the child in Mu Jiang’s womb.","Zhou Yuanshen, on the other hand, represented the complete opposite—he didn’t care at all.","If he had the slightest regard for it, he would never have snapped the prayer beads and used them as hidden projectiles to strike her knees when Mu Jiang cursed Cheng Zhao.","It was a blatant act.","Beads scattered across the floor, and rumors spread like wildfire—none of it could be contained or hidden.","Mu Jiang was completely dazed.","Cheng Zhao remained silent, letting Zhou Yuanshen’s large hand firmly hold hers.","The grandmother and grandson stared at each other for a long moment before Zhou Yuanshen spoke first: “Since Grandmother has no use for us, we’ll take our leave now.”","He led Cheng Zhao away, carefully stepping around the beads that had rolled all over the floor and left.","Mu Jiang stared blankly.","She was utterly stunned.","Even after such a major scene, the Dowager Madame hadn’t said a single word in her defense.","Mu Jiang’s tears streamed down like the broken beads from before. She wasn’t just in pain; she was lost.","The Dowager Madame swayed slightly, struggling to stay steady.","Huan Qingtang supported her: “Grandmother.”","The Dowager Madame looked defeated—not from mental fatigue, but from physical exhaustion. The fury had drained her, even though it was a restrained anger.","Huan Qingtang’s heart clenched.","She had never realized before that her grandmother was old, far weaker than the young.","Mu Jiang was still crying.","“Someone, help the Secondary Wife back to her bed to rest,” Huan Qingtang ordered.","Turning to Doctor Wang, who looked both horrified and awkward, she said, “Doctor, I’d appreciate it if you could take the Secondary Wife’s pulse again.”","She handled everything calmly.","When they left Lijing Courtyard, Zhou Yuanshen let go of Cheng Zhao’s hand.","He strode ahead.","But the direction was wrong—not toward Nonghua Courtyard, but the opposite way toward Chenhui Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao stood there, momentarily unsure: “Should I follow him?”","Today’s events had caught her off guard too.","Zhou Yuanshen refused to be manipulated by the Dowager Madame or Mu Jiang, even when they used the unborn child as leverage against him.","So he didn’t long for this child?","Were men’s thoughts so different from women’s?","The Dowager Madame, Mu Jiang, and even Cheng Zhao herself had assumed he wanted an heir—the Duke’s estate had few sons, and this pregnancy was the Emperor’s “request,” beneficial to his career and the family.","But judging by his behavior today, he was willing to lose everything. If the Dowager Madame insisted on clashing with him, he’d accept mutual destruction without hesitation.","Cheng Zhao pondered for a moment, then instead of returning to Nonghua Courtyard, she headed for Chengming Hall.","Doctor Wang hadn’t left yet.","Besides, the Dowager Madame had instructed Cheng Zhao to entertain Doctor Wang.","“What’s all the fuss about?” Eldest Madam Song asked when she saw her.","News from Lijing Courtyard had reached her quickly.","“Just a small matter.” Cheng Zhao forced a faint smile. “Aunt, I still have some duties to attend to.”","Her maidservant Suyue was waiting at Lijing Courtyard’s gate. Seeing Nanny Sun escort Doctor Wang out, she rushed to inform Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao went to the main entrance.","“How is the Secondary Wife’s condition?” she asked.","Doctor Wang gave a vague reply.","Cheng Zhao understood: Mu Jiang had been feigning illness earlier. This huge row might have actually upset her pregnancy, and she’d need monitoring for a few more days.","She handed Doctor Wang another red envelope, urging him to keep it confidential.","“... As you know, His Majesty dislikes anyone gossiping about the Zhou family,” she said.","Doctor Wang accepted the envelope with repeated nods: “Rest assured, I dislike idle talk myself. You can ask around about that.”","Cheng Zhao nodded.","She headed back to the inner courtyard.","Passing the entrance of Chenhui Courtyard, she spotted Nanfeng standing at the gate.","He saw her and seemed about to greet her, but Cheng Zhao waved him off and returned to Nonghua Courtyard.","When she finally arrived, she was utterly exhausted. She instructed her maids to help her change clothes and comb her hair. After slipping into her everyday attire and letting her hair down, she leaned against a bolster, finally feeling some relief.","Nanny Li brought her a cup of tea.","“... Someone probably provoked her,” Cheng Zhao said. “The Secondary Wife went mad just to see the Lord Duke.”","It was likely linked to the time at the main gate, when the Lord Duke had told her to “get lost.”","“It’s probably Huan Qingtang,” she continued. “She found my earring and guessed what happened behind the scenes.","Naturally, she wants a fight between the wife and the concubine here so she can reap the benefits. And the Secondary Wife has no brains—a little provocation is all it takes.”","Nanny Li’s face darkened: “How vicious. You should tell the Dowager Madame that Huan Qingtang stirred up the Secondary Wife’s tantrum and see how she handles it.”","“We don’t have proof. And even if we did, what then? The Dowager Madame might just see it as clever scheming. She’s not exactly looking for a kind and righteous granddaughter-in-law,” Cheng Zhao replied.","Nanny Li shuddered: “At this rate, something terrible will happen sooner or later.”","“But nothing terrible has happened so far, and the Zhou family thrives in prosperity. She’s deeply convinced her methods work,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li shuddered again.","Suyue and Qiubai returned as well.","“The main kitchen has already heard the news. It’ll probably spread through the whole mansion soon,” Suyue said.","“Too many eyes and ears on the scene.” Qiubai added.","Cheng Zhao said nothing.","She sat in deep thought for some time.","“Is there something bothering you?” Nanny Li asked.","Cheng Zhao answered, “I’m thinking about the Lord Duke. If he believes he’s right, he’s stubborn. For instance, with the Secondary Wife’s matter, he hasn’t relented once.”","Back at Chenhui Courtyard, when he had tormented Cheng Zhao, he admitted he was wrong, so he went out of his way to apologize.","But this time, in the carriage, he didn’t think he was at fault at all.","Even though he had publicly stepped in to defend her, once they left Lijing Courtyard, he simply ignored her.","Cheng Zhao felt wronged too.","She barely knew him, and he wasn’t in his usual clothes; her eyes weren’t feeling great, so failing to recognize him—was that such a grave crime?","He had already made a scene in the carriage and gotten his revenge.","Anyway, Cheng Zhao wasn’t about to apologize. She wouldn’t indulge his temper.","“Ah, right!” She suddenly sat up. “Where did I put the perfume powder Yuan Qi gave me last time?”","“In the cosmetics cabinet,” Suyue said.","“Find it,” Cheng Zhao instructed.","Suyue went to look for it.","Cheng Zhao opened it carefully, wary of the powder flying into her eyes like last time.","There was still some dust—its craftsmanship wasn’t great.","But the scent was lovely.","“Smell this. What does it remind you of?” She passed it to them.","Nanny Li and the others all took a whiff.","“A very fresh fragrance.”","“Sweet-smelling,” Qiubai said. “Pleasant.”","“I only recognize this kind of scent from cosmetic powder,” Cheng Zhao noted. “I caught it in the Secondary Wife’s bedroom, but she doesn’t wear it herself.”","“Maybe she bought the same powder and keeps it on her dressing table,” Nanny Li suggested.","Cheng Zhao handed the box to Qiubai: “Go out discreetly and find out exactly what this scent is.”","Then she added, “I need to go to Jiangyun Courtyard. Mother will have heard the news soon. And I must ask Yuan Qi where he bought this powder.”","Seeing that she had thought of everything except how to handle the tension with the Lord Duke, Nanny Li asked, “Should we call the Lord Duke?”","“No need,” Cheng Zhao replied.","Nanny Li: “...”"]},{"number":121,"title":"Chapter 121: Cheng Zhao Accuses Face-to-Face","paragraphs":["Within Crimson Cloud Courtyard, Second Madam had just heard the news.","At dinnertime, when all quarters were getting their meals from the main kitchen, news spread quickly.","And with plenty of embellishment.","Second Madam was speaking softly to her trusted steward, Nanny Fan: \"She might not be able to keep this pregnancy.\"","She didn't want to curse Mu Jiang, but Mu Jiang was simply too tumultuous. Second Madam had a bad feeling.","And the Dowager Madame never punished Mu Jiang.","Last time, even when Mu Jiang killed the crane, the Dowager Madame let it off lightly with only a servant girl's punishment.","The crane had been the centerpiece of the birthday feast, and guests had been told in advance to expect a crane dance performance, but it never happened. It was terrible—both the ill omen for the Dowager Madame's birthday and the feast's reputation. Mu Jiang's crime was unforgivable, yet she received no punishment at all.","She would grow bolder, more greedy, because there were no constraints on her.","Second Madam felt it was only a matter of time before she lost this child. Even if Heaven blessed her and she gave birth, she would likely use the child constantly to stir up trouble. Whether the child would even survive to grow up was another question.","Just as they were talking, Cheng Zhao arrived, followed by Second Master and Zhou Yuanqi returning.","Second Madam ordered the maids to help Second Master change into his everyday clothes, had water brought for Zhou Yuanqi to wash his hands, and called for tea to be served to Cheng Zhao.","It was a flurry of activity.","Nanny Fan lowered her voice: \"The Lord Duke is also in the residence.\"","Second Madam: \"Is that so?\"","Cheng Zhao hadn't mentioned it to her.","\"The Secondary Wife making such a scene—she did it so the Lord Duke would see it,\" Nanny Fan said.","Second Madam: \"Send someone to invite him here for dinner.\"","Nanny Fan acknowledged the order.","Second Madam entered the eastern side room and saw Cheng Zhao sitting on the large heated kang by the window, drinking tea. She asked, \"Did you see Ah Shen earlier?\"","\"Yes, the Lord Duke has gone to the Morning Light Courtyard,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam said no more.","She refrained from asking Cheng Zhao about the matters of the Beautiful Scenery Courtyard, deciding to wait until Zhou Yuanqi would surely bring it up later, so Cheng Zhao wouldn't have to tell the story twice.","\"...Did you have to endure any harsh words?\" Second Madam only asked.","Cheng Zhao smiled: \"No.\"","She briefly explained what happened.","Once Second Master and Zhou Yuanqi were properly dressed, they too came into the eastern side room to sit.","\"Let's wait for Ah Shen,\" Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanqi, seeing there was still time, quickly asked Cheng Zhao: \"What happened at the Beautiful Scenery Courtyard?\"","Cheng Zhao recounted everything that had happened that day, starting with the servant girl coming to report at the Glorious Hall.","It wasn't anything major, not complicated.","\"...So it's just faking illness?\" Zhou Yuanqi asked.","Cheng Zhao: \"Doctor Wang seems to think so. Still, she fell—both knees hitting the ground at the same time. I heard a loud thud, her knees cracking sharply against the floor tiles.\"","Second Madam sucked in a breath through her teeth, like a toothache.","Second Master remained silent, sipping his tea without comment.","Zhou Yuanqi gave a scornful half-smile: \"A boor, indeed a crude man. Our grandmother will seize this wrongdoing and force him to kneel in the ancestral hall.\"","Cheng Zhao laughed: \"She wouldn't dare now. He's Grand Tutor Zhou.\"","She added, \"The title 'Grand Tutor Zhou' does sound very impressive.\"","\"She can't handle him, so she'll take it out on us instead, won't she?\" Zhou Yuanqi said. \"You, Mother, and I—we're all vulnerable.\"","\"I'm not afraid. If you're scared, you can sever ties with him,\" Cheng Zhao said.","After all, she wouldn't despise Zhou Yuanshen.","Despising him would mean despising her own status as Lady of the first-rank noble title.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"Whose side are you on, exactly?\"","Second Madam: \"What kind of question is that?\"","As the three of them were trading these quips, Zhou Yuanshen arrived.","The meal from the main kitchen also arrived.","The maids and servant women set the table. Cheng Zhao went out to help arrange the chopsticks while Zhou Yuanshen entered the eastern side room to share a few words with his parents and younger brother.","The meal itself was eaten quietly.","After eating, everyone rinsed their mouths and gathered in the side room for conversation.","\"...How is the Secondary Wife? Has she started making trouble again?\" Second Madam asked him.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"Mother, these matters are for Grandmother to handle. She handles things properly. If Concubine Mu has some issue, it's only because Grandmother thought something ought to happen. It has nothing to do with us.\"","\"How is it none of our concern? She carries your child. That's our child too,\" Second Madam said.","She added, \"I can't help but think about it—our first grandson. How could your father and I not love him?\"","Then she glanced at Cheng Zhao: \"Zhaozhao, when you have children, they will certainly be the most beloved.\"","Cheng Zhao saw that her mother-in-law always considered her feelings even in casual remarks, and couldn't help laughing: \"Naturally, they will be.\"","Her smile was quite lovely.","Zhou Yuanshen's gaze didn't linger on her, but his peripheral vision caught it, and he deliberately turned his head even further away, completely avoiding looking at her.","Second Madam immediately asked: \"Have you two been quarreling?\"","Cheng Zhao declared: \"The Lord Duke is in a snit with me.\"","Second Master: \"...\"","His daughter-in-law never revealed anything in private conversations, yet she was bold enough to tell on him to his face. She was indeed straightforward and brave.","With her around, it was as if a ray of sunshine had poured in, brightening even their gloomiest days in the second branch of the household.","Zhou Yuanqi, his eyes lighting up, asked: \"A snit? About what?\"","\"The scented powder you gave me got all over my face and got into my eyes. My vision was blurry for a good while, so I didn't see the Lord Duke clearly when he was standing near the training ground, and he got upset,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi scoffed and shot his brother a dirty look: \"You're pretty petty.\"","Zhou Yuanshen's expression was blank. He showed neither annoyance nor did he argue.","He might as well have not even heard.","Second Madam couldn't help chuckling: \"Since when did you become so particular about little things? Especially with your own wife.\"","Zhou Yuanshen remained silent.","He was completely unresponsive.","That, too, was one of his talents.","Seeing him sit there wordless and expressionless, everyone knew Cheng Zhao wasn't lying.","Second Master said, \"You need to make allowances for Zhaozhao. She's a new bride. If you don't treat her well, others will take it as permission to bully her.\"","\"Yes,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Finally, he spoke, though his voice was cold as ice.","Second Madam laughed heartily: \"The two of you, usually acting so mature and serious, quarreling over such a trivial matter. You're really just a couple of children!\"","Zhou Yuanshen had always played the grown-up since he was young, so it was rare to see this childish side of him.","It was indeed amusing.","Second Madam kept laughing to herself for a good while.","Zhou Yuanqi, for his part, only grew more contemptuous of his brother, feeling that the brute was, after all, not as impressive as he seemed.","Cheng Zhao, having made her complaint openly before her in-laws and brother-in-law, filled the side room with laughter and lively chatter. Second Madam felt the family had grown a bit closer.","\"Yuanqi, where did you buy that scented powder?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"The House of Wonders. They sell real curiosities from overseas and the Western Regions. Not fakes this time.\"","He then asked, \"Do you like that powder?\"","\"I like it very much,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"I was thinking of checking if they have more in stock, to buy some as gifts.\"","\"Don't bother sending any to Third Miss. Perfume that's too strong is too common and unworthy of her,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: \"What, so Mother and I are just worthy of common things? We're the common ones?\"","\"Exactly,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao reached for him.","Even though it was quite improper—since her brother-in-law was already eight, old enough that there should be some distance between them—she couldn't help herself. She firmly kneaded and rubbed his face with both hands.","Zhou Yuanqi's eyes went wide.","He was so startled and furious that he wanted to snap at Cheng Zhao, but his voice got there before his better judgment: \"Help! Save me!\"","Realizing what he'd said, he immediately clamped his little mouth tightly shut.","Second Madam nearly laughed herself off her seat and onto the floor; Second Master, too, let out a laugh.","The trusted maidservants and old nannies attending by the side all burst into laughter.","Zhou Yuanshen’s gaze finally fell upon Cheng Zhao’s face.","Zhou Yuanqi, however, announced that he would never make peace with Cheng Zhao again and would be irreconcilable with her from now on!"]},{"number":122,"title":"Chapter 122: The Little Couple Makes Up","paragraphs":["In April, the night breeze was warm, blending the fragrance of flowers and grass into an intoxicating mix.","Cheng Zhao walked out of Jiangyun Courtyard alongside Zhou Yuanshen and Zhou Yuanqi.","Zhou Yuanqi had stormed off first in a huff.","Cheng Zhao stopped, lifting her gaze to study Zhou Yuanshen. Moonlight bathed his profile like silver, adding an extra touch of handsomeness to his features.","\"Lord Duke, I'll be heading back now,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen hummed in acknowledgment.","He turned to stride toward Chenhui Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao didn't move, watching his broad-shouldered retreating figure with a slight frown.","She had already explained herself.","So, did he still...","Cheng Zhao thought it over and decided not to dwell on it for now. Mu Jiang had just caused a scene, and there was no telling what would happen next.","She needed to send someone to the Rare Treasures Pavilion to investigate what exactly had been mixed into that face powder.","Cheng Zhao mulled this over slowly as she turned to leave, only to suddenly hear footsteps.","Zhou Yuanshen had wheeled around and was striding back.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","What was the point of him leaving just now? Was he playing games?","Before she could react, he had taken her hand and pulled her a few steps forward.","Cheng Zhao stopped and refused to move. \"Where are we going?\"","This wasn't the way to Nonghua Courtyard.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"Chenhui Courtyard.\"","Cheng Zhao tried to shake off his hand. \"It's too far. If you have something to say, say it here, or we can go to Nonghua Courtyard.\"","She didn't want to go.","Not only was it far, but it was also uncomfortable to stay there.","Chenhui Courtyard was essentially an inner study, with small rooms and incomplete furnishings. There was only one bed, simple and basic, with pillows and blankets that didn't suit Cheng Zhao's preferences at all.","There was also no one to wait on her.","Zhou Yuanshen tugged harder, causing Cheng Zhao to stumble a couple of steps. The couple now stood beneath the shadows of the trees.","The moonlight and the lights from Jiangyun Courtyard were both blocked from view, leaving them in a hazy, dim obscurity.","She could only make out Zhou Yuanshen's silhouette.","\"Cheng Zhao, shouldn't you apologize to me?\" he said.","He wasn't calling her \"Duchess of Chen\" anymore.","Nor was he being sarcastic.","Cheng Zhao, recalling his earlier behavior, felt a surge of anger.","\"Apologize for what?\" Cheng Zhao asked. \"I thought that your being absent all these days was because you felt guilty and didn't dare face me.\"","\"Guilty?\" He lowered his head slightly, bringing his face close to hers. \"Guilty about what, Cheng Zhao? Because that day I made you feel so pleased?\"","Cheng Zhao, enraged, exclaimed, \"You...\"","The man, usually cold as ice, could spout such shameless words.","She hadn't expected this, caught off guard and furious, and could barely speak.","\"Cheng Zhao, can you deny it?\" His lips grazed her cheek, barely touching.","Cheng Zhao couldn't help but think of that day.","The carriage curtain swayed slightly, letting in a sliver of sunlight. She'd looked down to see the hand gripping her waist, the faintly bulging veins.","Her body seemed to tremble, just like the bumpy carriage ride that day.","In a flash, she was furious beyond measure. She turned to leave, but Zhou Yuanshen wrapped an arm around her waist, pulling her into a tight embrace. She crashed into his chest.","He lowered his head and kissed her.","A tiny pebble dropped into the lake of Cheng Zhao's heart, sending ripples spreading through her.","Only a moment later did she remember that this was the path outside Jiangyun Courtyard. Even in the shadow of the trees, if Second Madam's people were watching from the doorway, they would be able to see them.","She pushed against him. \"Zhou Yuanshen, you lecher!\"","She couldn't push him away. As she shoved him back a few steps, his arm around her waist pulled her forward, and she ended up pressed solidly against his chest.","They bumped into a tree trunk, leaves rustling. A faint breeze brushed her cheek, and she realized her face was burning hot, making the wind feel cool by contrast.","Cheng Zhao's breathing pattern was completely off.","\"Cheng Zhao, I apologize. I shouldn't have fooled around like that in the carriage that day. Next time, we'll save some energy,\" Zhou Yuanshen said, his face seemingly expressionless, but his hand never let her go. \"You should apologize to me too, and promise to recognize your husband next time, no matter where you encounter him.\"","Cheng Zhao shot back, \"Don't even think about it!\"","She hadn't done anything wrong; there were reasons for it.","Besides, what grave sin was it to not recognize one's own husband? No law dictated that.","\"If you won't let me think about that, then I'll think about other things,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. His hand actually reached for the ties of her skirt.","Cheng Zhao's eyes went wide with disbelief.","Would he really dare?","No, he wouldn't. He was just threatening her.","They were at the entrance to Jiangyun Courtyard, with his parents just a wall away.","But then Cheng Zhao remembered the study in Chenhui Courtyard, the maid who had answered from behind the screen, and the carriage that had slowly crept through the crowded market...","Maybe he would dare!","Outwardly, the man seemed perfectly proper, but on the inside, he was simply appalling.","If he actually did it, he'd turn it around and claim Cheng Zhao had goaded him into it.","Maybe next time, he'd even say Cheng Zhao had enjoyed it and needed to thank him.","Cheng Zhao felt that her first real clash with Zhou Yuanshen had been in the study of Chenhui Courtyard. She should never have let her defenses down and forgiven him.","By reconciling with him instead of stopping his \"misdeeds,\" she had only unleashed the beast within him, and he was becoming more and more audacious.","He had even learned to argue his way out of it.","\"Zhou Yuanshen! Zhou Yuanshen!\" she called out urgently.","Zhou Yuanshen let go. \"Mm. Call me that again later.\"","He took her hand and strode toward Nonghua Courtyard.","The two places weren't far apart, but Cheng Zhao didn't have time to say anything, nor did she dare make a scene that might be heard by passing maids and servants.","She found herself half-jogging back to Nonghua Courtyard, pulled along by him.","When they arrived, she was out of breath.","Pinned down on the bed by him, Cheng Zhao felt like she couldn't breathe. Her heart was pounding wildly in her chest, a result of their mad dash.","She tried to turn her head and push him away, but he held both her hands above her head with one hand.","Cheng Zhao thought the bed would collapse. The hanging canopy came loose in one corner, nearly falling to envelop the two of them.","She bit down hard on his shoulder.","Only when her legs were trembling and weak from the exertion did she realize how much she was sweating.","The weather was getting warmer, and being this active at night left both of them drenched.","The warm water in the bathtub flowed over her body, along with his scorching hot hand.","Cheng Zhao was too tired, so she just leaned against him, too lazy to struggle.","He turned her face towards his and kissed her lips again. \"How are your eyes feeling?\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Right now, she was so sleepy and so exhausted she couldn't even speak.","She'd settle the score with him tomorrow morning, after she'd had her fill of sleep.","She gave a soft, vague hum.","\"Maybe we should say you're ill, so you don't get dragged into the Lijing Courtyard matter,\" he said again.","Cheng Zhao thought, oh, the Lijing Courtyard business... well, she had no energy for that now either. She'd deal with it tomorrow morning, too.","She hummed vaguely again.","Zhou Yuanshen dried her off and helped her into her underclothes. She was aware of it, but she just stayed sprawled in his arms.","She had no memory of when she finally fell asleep the rest of the way.","When she woke the next morning, he wasn't in bed, as usual.","Suyue said he had gotten up early to go to the morning court session. Nanny Li brought her ginseng soup and bird's nest congee, telling her to take them both as a tonic.","\"One supplement is enough. I can't drink this much,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She still preferred the sweet rock sugar bird's nest congee. The ginseng soup was good for energy, but it had a strange taste that Cheng Zhao didn't like.","She wasn't one to endure unnecessary hardship.","After the morning meal, Cheng Zhao didn't have time to go to Jiangyun Courtyard to pay her respects. She asked Nanny Li to inform them, while she rushed to Chengming Hall instead.","Second Madam was no longer surprised.","Every time Cheng Zhao missed the morning greetings, it was because Zhou Yuanshen had stayed overnight at Nonghua Courtyard.","Second Madam had been young once herself. In her youth, she and the Second Master had been devoted to each other as husband and wife, with a deep, extraordinary love that few could match—she understood such matters all too well."]},{"number":123,"title":"Chapter 123: Cheng Zhao Hao Ruan","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao hurried to Chengming Hall, where First Madam Song and all the stewards had already gathered.","“Where is the eldest sister-in-law?” Cheng Zhao asked.","First Madam said, “The Dowager Madame sent her to see the Secondary Wife.”","She wanted to say that the matter should have been handled by Cheng Zhao, not Huan Qingtang.","But the Dowager Madame had sent Huan Qingtang for two reasons: Huan Qingtang would eventually become the mistress of Chengming Hall, entrusted with major affairs; she would also become Zhou Yuanshen’s wife, and Mu Jiang was connected to this.","Both points irritated First Madam.","She recalled how she had previously advised Huan Qingtang and now felt like a laughingstock, her mood dejected.","“Is the Secondary Wife alright?” Cheng Zhao asked.","First Madam replied, “They won’t let you see her, nor me. Even if something is wrong, would they tell us?”","She said this to complain about the Dowager Madame, but the entire hall of stewards was present.","Cheng Zhao fell silent immediately.","First Madam also regretted her careless words. “Let’s begin. No need to wait.”","By the time the morning’s work ended, First Madam was summoned to Shou’an Courtyard.","When she returned for the afternoon affairs, her face looked even more worn, clearly having been scolded.","Cheng Zhao pretended not to notice, silently sitting beside her.","Huan Qingtang came back and, in front of several stewards reporting their tasks, said to Cheng Zhao and First Madam, “The Secondary Wife is in low spirits. I called Doctor Feng again and had him prescribe some calming powders.”","“Doctor Feng is a skilled physician,” Cheng Zhao commented.","Just yesterday, they had said Doctor Feng was too close to Imperial Consort Dou’s family and didn’t dare use him. Yet today they summoned him, meaning Mu Jiang was likely not well.","She was five months pregnant.","If she stayed calm and didn’t stir trouble, the pregnancy should have stabilized by now. But after Mu Jiang’s fuss yesterday, even a healthy person might fall ill from anger.","“Sister-in-law, please urge the Secondary Wife to rest and recover well. I’ve recently caught a mild chill and fear I might pass miasma to her, so I won’t visit her,” Cheng Zhao said.","With that, she deliberately coughed twice.","Song thought to herself that Cheng Zhao was clever and good at evading trouble, never letting any bothersome matters touch her.","“Send an experienced matron to see her. You’ve never given birth, so you don’t understand women’s matters of childbirth,” First Madam said to Huan Qingtang.","The stewards lowered their heads.","Huan Qingtang acknowledged the comment, her face betraying no sign of embarrassment. Everyday jokes played out in Chengming Hall.","That evening, Zhou Yuanshen did not return for dinner. Cheng Zhao assumed he was again at the General’s Mansion.","Unexpectedly, just as she was about to rest, he came back.","“... Had some drinks with colleagues during the evening engagements,” he said. “I’ll bathe first. You go to bed, no need to wait.”","But how could Cheng Zhao sleep now?","She sat by the lamplight, picking out a lake-blue fabric to make a pouch.","She had only embroidered a few patterns when Zhou Yuanshen emerged from the bath.","He walked over. “Did you like the kite I gave you last time?”","Cheng Zhao: “The butterfly was too plump.”","“Don’t you like plump butterflies? We’ll fly kites together with her next time,” he said.","Cheng Zhao: “Am I supposed to spend all my time playing with children?”","He said nothing, just reached out and touched her face.","The palm of his hand carried a hint of alcohol, seeming to burn her cheek. Cheng Zhao wanted to dodge.","“Is this pouch for me?” he asked.","Cheng Zhao looked up.","He wore plain undergarments. Perhaps due to the wine, his eyes were unusually bright, staring at her intently, but his lips were flat, without a trace of a smile.","“No.”","“Didn’t we agree you’d make me one?” He sat down across from her, deliberately bargaining.","Cheng Zhao felt provoked again. This man was pushing his boundaries, increasingly disregarding her importance. He even dared to sulk with her now.","Cheng Zhao’s face darkened. “Yours is already done.”","“Really?” There was a note of surprise in his voice. “Sent it to Chenhui Courtyard?”","“I cut it up,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen stared at her steadily.","“You didn’t come home, so you didn’t care about my gift. I worked hard on it; why should I beg you to accept it? So I cut it up.” Cheng Zhao continued, “I made one. There won’t be any more.”","Zhou Yuanshen walked behind her and pulled her into his embrace.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","He still dared to lay his hands on her.","Zhou Yuanshen held her tightly from behind, burying his face in her hair. “Cheng Zhao, how old are you?”","His tone carried a hint of helplessness, like when he teased Zhou Yuanqi.","The implication was, “How old are you to throw such a childish tantrum?”","“Lord Duke, please be proper,” Cheng Zhao pushed him. “I want to sleep.”","He grabbed her chin.","His fingers exerted slight pressure, tilting her head back, and he kissed her stubbornly.","His other hand had already loosened the tie of her under robe.","“Cheng Zhao, you’re so soft.”","Half-dazed, Cheng Zhao heard him say this.","His voice was very light.","As he spoke, he caught her lips again, gently sucking on them.","Cheng Zhao thought of the elderly matriarchs, each dignified and commanding. They must have been decisive and tough in their youth, wielding authority with skill.","She believed she was doing well, fated to be like them one day.","Yet he called her “soft.”","He had stripped away all her authority.","“Bodhisattva, bless me that I quickly become pregnant!” This time, Cheng Zhao kowtowed three times more devoutly. Fearing it wasn’t enough, she added three more.","She was truly fed up with his lack of respect.","Once she was pregnant, he wouldn’t be able to get near her. Distance would bring authority, and she would never let him kiss her again.","The next day, Cheng Zhao didn’t go to Chengming Hall; she took leave.","“... This incense is called ‘Fragrant Shadow,’ also known as ‘Sweet Cherry,’ often used in rouges and powders. It’s made from bitter almonds and sweet gardenia,” said the maid Qiubai.","Cheng Zhao was surprised. “The shopkeeper told you?”","“A merchant wouldn’t reveal that,” Qiubai laughed. “I didn’t go to Yizhen Tower. Instead, I took it to Fourth Young Master and asked him to find a perfume maker to inquire.”","Cheng Zhao praised her for her cleverness.","“And Fourth Brother has some use after all, knowing all sorts of people from different walks,” she added.","Qiubai lowered her voice: “Young Madam, this incense is toxic.”","Cheng Zhao was stunned.","“Bitter almonds and sweet gardenia are both poisonous if consumed,” Qiubai said. “The scent is nice, but you can’t inhale too much either.”","“But Yizhen Tower still sells cosmetics with it!” Cheng Zhao exclaimed.","“As long as it’s not eaten, it won’t poison you,” Qiubai said. “Didn’t you mention that this same fragrance is in the Secondary Wife’s inner chamber?”","“Yes.”","“If it’s in her lip balm, she’s in some danger. She still applies lip balm,” Qiubai noted.","Cheng Zhao frowned.","“However, you can’t warn her. She’s already on guard against you. You can tell the Lord Duke and have him speak to the Secondary Wife,” Qiubai suggested.","Cheng Zhao pondered.","“Huan Qingtang has been visiting Lijing Courtyard often these days. Could she know about this?” Cheng Zhao suddenly asked.","Qiubai couldn’t guess.","“The Secondary Wife’s pregnancy cannot risk any mishap. The Emperor highly favors the Lord Duke because he is obedient in every matter and carries out his orders well. He commanded the Lord Duke and the Secondary Wife to conceive, and she did. If anything goes wrong, the Emperor will hold the Lord Duke accountable,” Cheng Zhao said.","She called Suyue and told her to go to Chenhui Courtyard.","“Tell Nanfeng that when the Lord Duke returns, send him back to Nonghua Courtyard. I have urgent matters to discuss,” Cheng Zhao instructed.","Suyue acknowledged and left.","Cheng Zhao herself went to find Zhou Yuanqi.","Little did she expect that at the entrance of Zhou Yuanqi’s courtyard, she would catch sight of Huan Qingtang, who had only just left."]},{"number":124,"title":"Chapter 124: Toxic","paragraphs":["Huan Qingtang walked away without noticing Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao was silent for a moment, then, instead of entering Zhou Yuanqi’s courtyard, she turned and headed toward the gatekeeper’s lodge, pretending that had been her destination all along.","There were many matters to attend to at the gatekeeper’s lodge. Cheng Zhao made up an excuse to linger briefly before turning back toward the inner courtyard.","“...Go back home and give this to Fourth Brother. Tell him to make inquiries for me,” Cheng Zhao said to her maid Qiubai.","Qiubai acknowledged the order, nodded obediently, and left the courtyard.","Unlike Suyue, Qiubai could blend into the shadows wherever she went, rarely drawing anyone’s attention.","Cheng Zhao repeated her thoughts to Nanny Li and Suyue.","The two of them came up with many suggestions for her.","Before Qiubai returned, the young servant Nanfeng entered Qinghua Courtyard.","“Young Madam, the Lord Duke has gone to the Beijing Garrison Camp. The vice-commander came home to inform the family—he said it’s a temporary matter, and His Lordship should return in three to five days,” Nanfeng said. “Perhaps he might even return tonight.”","Cheng Zhao did some mental calculations.","He had mentioned before that he might spend half the month at the garrison camp. Quite some time had passed since his last return, the time he had been angry with Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao remained silent.","Nanfeng, quick-witted as always, added, “If there is a need, I can ride to the garrison camp and inform His Lordship. He might be able to free himself. Young Madam, would you like me to make the trip for you?”","Cheng Zhao came back to her senses and smiled, shaking her head. “No need for that.”","She then asked, “What happened at the garrison camp?”","“It seems someone died,” Nanfeng said. “The garrison camp is very important, and various factions have their hands in it. When His Lordship suddenly took command, some people naturally weren’t happy. They don’t want to let go, and they’re trying to cause trouble.”","Cheng Zhao was speechless.","Zhou Yuanshen’s situation was truly difficult.","What Cheng Zhao had to face were the scheming members of the household. But Zhou Yuanshen had to deal not only with family affairs but also with the knives and swords of the court.","His position as Grand Tutor and his command of the garrison camp—both were extremely advantageous for gaining real power. Many people coveted these roles.","The struggles of the court were truly matters of life and death.","Despite all this, Zhou Yuanshen rarely lost his temper or appeared flustered.","He handled everything with ease.","“Nanfeng, you really do know a little bit about everything,” Cheng Zhao remarked.","She instructed Qiubai to give him some sweets and also rewarded him with a handful of copper coins.","Nanfeng replied, “His Lordship and Mingyu both teach me.”","Cheng Zhao smiled. “Go play now.”","Nanfeng’s mention of Mingyu reminded Cheng Zhao of the senior maid who was in charge of the outer study. Last time, Cheng Zhao had heard her refer to herself as “an agent.”","Mingyu occasionally came to Qinghua Courtyard. She was respectful, efficient in her work, and both Qiubai and Nanny Li thought highly of her.","However, Zhou Yuanshen had never revealed his people or his network to Cheng Zhao.","Even someone as clever as Nanfeng, Zhou Yuanshen had hardly ever mentioned to her.","Cheng Zhao had always felt that Zhou Yuanshen was a general.","Everything around a general was classified. Matters that needed to remain secret were known only to him alone.","Advisors and subordinates could discuss the battle situation and offer opinions, but in the end, the one who made decisions and gave orders had to be the general alone.","Lonely and powerful.","No one could walk alongside him.","Cheng Zhao didn’t mind. Her own family was good. Her parents-in-law and her younger brother-in-law were also quite decent. She had a crowd of people around her.","As long as Zhou Yuanshen didn’t help Mu Jiang and Huan Qingtang or act as the Old Madam’s puppet, his being a lone wolf wouldn’t hinder Cheng Zhao’s future in the slightest.","After all, in the bedroom, he did what he was supposed to do as a husband.","That evening, when they went to Crimson Cloud Courtyard for dinner, Cheng Zhao told Second Madam that Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the Beijing Garrison Camp.","Second Madam and Second Master took it in stride.","“Fifth Brother, what did Eldest Sister-in-law want with you today?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanqi replied, “I heard the maid say she was just passing by. I was at the clan school at the time, so I don’t know what she wanted.”","Second Madam frowned. “Fetch Fifth Young Master’s maid over here.”","Soon, a maid with slightly plump cheeks and a gentle, docile expression appeared.","Second Madam asked her why Huan Qingtang had come.","“...Eldest Young Madame said she was just passing by and showed some concern. She asked Fifth Young Master what he had been busy with lately, whether he had been going out, and mentioned that her cousin had run into Fifth Young Master at Yizhen Pavilion. Eldest Young Madame said the owner of Yizhen Pavilion was very unscrupulous, always passing off imitations as genuine foreign goods to cheat people who didn’t know better. She said she was worried Fifth Young Master might be deceived,” the maid reported.","Second Madam frowned deeply.","Second Master pondered for a moment and asked Zhou Yuanqi, “What did you buy?”","“Just two boxes of face powder. I gave them to Mother and Third Sister-in-law,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Second Madam remarked, “A hundred taels per box—we really should go and return them.”","Cheng Zhao quickly laughed and interjected, “Mother, we are the Chen Duke Manor. If we went to return something just because it might be fake, it would make us seem small-minded. Consider it Fifth Brother donating to the temple and performing a good deed.”","Second Master glanced at Cheng Zhao, then naturally shifted his gaze to Zhou Yuanqi and said with a casual chuckle, “Zhao Zhao is right. Forget it. Anyway, there are people willing to spend money on him.”","He instantly understood what Cheng Zhao meant.","The powder had already been bought. If they tried to deny it or cause a scene, they could perhaps raise a ruckus. But returning it would only confirm that a purchase had been made.","However, causing a scene would be useless. Huan Qingtang already knew that the Second Branch possessed face powder scented with Sweet Cherry spices.","And coincidentally, Mu Jiang also had this kind of spice.","If Mu Jiang’s pregnancy...","Second Master might not have known exactly what was wrong with that specific face powder, but he quickly grasped the implication of Cheng Zhao’s words. Moreover, he was adept at smoothing things over without drawing attention.","Someone less observant would never notice Second Master’s subtle mediation, thinking he was just being a peacemaker again.","Second Madam, for instance, completely missed what Cheng Zhao and Second Master were really saying. Displeased, she scolded Second Master, “You always indulge him too much.”","Zhou Yuanqi’s eyes darted around.","He tried to speak, but Cheng Zhao shot him a pointed look. He fell silent.","The three of them had exchanged subtle glances and silently understood the situation. Only Second Madam, who couldn’t read their undertones, instructed them to eat.","After the meal, Cheng Zhao returned to Qinghua Courtyard.","She had barely sat down when the maid Qiubai returned.","“...Third Sister was at home today,” Qiubai told Cheng Zhao.","“Why did Third Sister go back? Did something happen?” Cheng Zhao had always been worried about her third sister. Any slight disturbance regarding her made Cheng Zhao anxious.","The Prince Jingnan’s mansion was a place with high walls and deep inner workings; Zhang Yunqi was unreliable, too.","“Third Sister is fine. The Second Young Madam invited her home for some fun. I happened to be in Fourth Young Master’s quarters. He called me in to talk, dismissed the servants, and asked after Third Sister. Then Third Sister told me a secret. The Prince Jingnan’s wife knows about it, but probably not many others do,” Qiubai said.","Cheng Zhao called for Suyue and Nanny Li, instructing them to keep watch in front and back, ensuring no one came near.","Once they were certain there were no eavesdroppers, she asked Qiubai to slowly recount the secret she had uncovered from the Cheng family.","Qiubai explained everything in detail.","After hearing it, Cheng Zhao gave a faint smile.","Things were becoming quite interesting. Much of the worry in Cheng Zhao’s heart faded.","Three days passed.","Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the garrison camp and hadn’t returned in three days. It seemed the matter couldn’t be resolved quickly.","Five days had passed since Mu Jiang’s scene.","During the day, Mu Jiang saw blood and was terrified, crying and screaming incessantly.","By evening, news came that she had miscarried."]},{"number":125,"title":"Chapter 125: The Emperor in Command","paragraphs":["The entire Duke of Chen's mansion was in chaos.","The atmosphere was tense, but no one could enter the Courtyard of Beautiful Views, not even Cheng Zhao.","The Second Madam sent for Cheng Zhao to come to the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","Zhou Yuanqi and the Second Master were already there.","\"There are armed guards stationed outside the Courtyard of Beautiful Views,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","He couldn't help himself and had gone to take a look, only to be turned back at a rockery.","Those guards weren't from the Zhou family.","Zhou Yuanqi knew everyone in the household and what they looked like; he was just as clever and inquisitive as Nanfeng.","Those were imperial guards—the Emperor's own guards.","The Second Madam's face shifted colors several times.","The Second Master pondered for a moment and asked Cheng Zhao, \"What about that child from the Morning Light Courtyard? Send someone to fetch him; I have questions for him.\"","He meant Nanfeng.","The Second Madam said, \"What would a child like him know?\"","\"He knows quite a bit,\" the Second Master said meaningfully.","Before the Second Madam could grasp the implication of his words, Zhou Yuanqi explained, \"Nanfeng is the eyes and ears that brute placed in the household.\"","\"Him?\"","A child, not yet old enough to have his hair bound, thin and small—what could he possibly do?","Though he had grown a bit taller lately.","Cheng Zhao said, \"I'll send Qiubai.\"","Zhou Yuanqi added, \"Among your maids, Qiubai is the most skilled in martial arts.\"","The Second Madam was surprised again.","Of the maids around Cheng Zhao, Suyue was the most capable and efficient, while Qiubai was quiet and unremarkable.","Zhou Yuanqi had first exposed his third brother's secret, then revealed his sister-in-law's hidden talent. The Second Master felt that if he got beaten, every beating would be well deserved.","\"Shh, keep it secret!\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam: \"...\"","One of them was like a little oriole, chirping all day and unable to keep any secret—not that she could blame anyone, since the child took after her. The other got caught and still tried to cover her ears while stealing a bell.","Hopeless. The Dowager Madame was going to have them all killed.","Cheng Zhao instructed Qiubai to handle it.","Qiubai was efficient and returned quickly, reporting in a low voice: \"I didn't see Nanfeng. I checked the stables; he took a horse and left half an hour ago.\"","Cheng Zhao understood and dismissed her, then said to her in-laws, \"Nanfeng has probably left the city to deliver a message. The question is whether he can get out before the city gates close.\"","She added, \"If he can't get out, he'll likely turn back. If he does get out, once the city gates open tomorrow morning, the Lord Duke will be home. Whatever happens, if we hold out until the Lord Duke returns, our chances are better.\"","The Second Madam nodded obediently.","Listening to her daughter-in-law speak brought her a sense of calm and reassurance, even though Cheng Zhao could sometimes be childish herself.","The Second Master stood up: \"I'll go outside and take a look.\"","He left.","Zhou Yuanqi didn't want to sit around waiting either, so he ran out as well.","Cheng Zhao instructed Qiubai: \"Follow the Fifth Young Master.\"","Only the two women remained in the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao said, \"Mother, please eat something.\"","The Second Madam's lips were dry: \"How can I eat at a time like this?\"","Her mind was in turmoil.","Her emotions were a tangled mess, and she couldn't process them, so she just sat there in a daze.","She pitied Mu Jiang because, as a mother herself, she knew exactly how much pain Mu Jiang was suffering right now. But she was also afraid—if the Dowager Madame was angry enough, she might take it out on many people, including their branch of the family.","\"...Zhaozhao, she must have been five months along, right?\" the Second Madam suddenly asked.","Cheng Zhao: \"Yes.\"","\"At five months, the fetus would have taken shape. They could tell if it was a boy or a girl,\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao: \"Mother, it's already lost. The gender doesn't matter.\"","\"You're right.\" The Second Madam sighed deeply again.","She was too overwhelmed to think strategically, so she just chatted randomly with Cheng Zhao to ease her mind.","\"I kept thinking, wouldn't it be nice if Mu Jiang had a daughter? I have no daughter of my own, and I was hoping for a granddaughter. If it were a girl, Mu Jiang might cause less trouble—she wouldn't be able to compete with you as much,\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao held her hand: \"Mother, Secondary Wife conceived this pregnancy quickly; she's fertile. After resting for half a year or so, she'll have another child.\"","The Second Madam: \"Who knows what the future holds? The Dowager Madame is probably furious.\"","That was certain.","Not only was the Dowager Madame angry, but Zhou Yuanshen was likely furious too.","With armed guards at the Courtyard of Beautiful Views, it meant the Emperor was there.","In Cheng Zhao's mind, she thought, The Emperor actually came.","He was truly unhinged—the affairs of the Zhou family had practically become his personal business. He had been crazy enough in the Imperial Study when he said Zhou Yuanshen and Mu Jiang should have a child to become his heir.","And now, this same insane thread of logic seemed even more frightening.","A man whose madness followed a pattern was far more terrifying than one who was chaotic without rhyme or reason.","What would become of the Duke of Chen's mansion?","\"As long as the Dowager Madame is here, the Zhou family won't fall. But what about us?\" Cheng Zhao couldn't help shivering. \"Especially me.\"","If the Emperor wanted to vent his fury by killing her, the Zhou family could even cover it up.","Cheng Zhao's heart tightened, but her face betrayed nothing. At this moment, she remained remarkably composed, thinking through her options step by step.","She also comforted her mother-in-law.","Nanny Fan had also gone out.","Night had fallen. It was dark now. The Duke of Chen's mansion lit its lanterns, and the floor-level horn lanterns illuminated the paths brightly.","No gates had been locked, yet there wasn't a single sound of commotion. The maids and servants all tiptoed, afraid to make even the slightest noise.","The Second Master returned, and so did Nanny Fan.","\"...I heard a fully formed fetus was expelled, the size of a palm, like a little mouse. But it should have been the size of a kitten by five months; Secondary Wife's pregnancy was particularly small,\" Nanny Fan whispered.","Being too small meant it was inherently weak.","So even without all the upheaval, that fetus might never have come to term.","But she had pretended to be ill and then had her knees struck when the Lord Duke punished her.","\"Could they tell if it was a boy or a girl?\" the Second Madam asked.","Nanny Fan nodded: \"Yes, it was a girl.\"","The Second Madam felt a pang of sorrow.","If it had been a boy, the Dowager Madame would have a \"hostage\" to control Zhou Yuanshen, and the Second Madam might not have cared for the child.","But a granddaughter—it gave the Dowager Madame less leverage and posed no threat to her daughter-in-law's position. And the Second Madam had longed for a granddaughter, so now she felt a deep regret.","Cheng Zhao held her hand: \"Mother, please calm down.\"","Then she ordered, \"Bring some bird's nest porridge.\"","A maid went to pass the order.","Soon, three bowls of bird's nest porridge were brought in. No one had the appetite for a meal, but the Second Master joined the two women in having some porridge.","Having an empty stomach was truly hard to bear.","The Second Madam sighed deeply again.","Zhou Yuanqi returned from his outing and also told the Second Madam it had been a girl.","The Emperor was really there.","The Second Madam frowned: \"This Emperor...\"","Cheng Zhao covered her mouth.","The Second Madam: \"...\"","After midnight, when it was deep into the night, the Dowager Madame's people arrived at the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","It seemed they assumed no one had slept.","\"The Dowager Madame summons everyone to the Hall of Inherited Light,\" the stewardess said. Noticing Zhou Yuanqi trying to sneak away, she added, \"Fifth Young Master, you are also required to go.\"","The Second Madam was about to speak, but Cheng Zhao firmly held her hand.","\"Mother, we will win this. Please trust me. No matter what you hear, don't panic. Say as little as possible,\" Cheng Zhao said solemnly.","The Second Master straightened his collar and led the way: \"All of you, follow me.\"","The group took small oiled carriages and headed toward the Hall of Inherited Light."]},{"number":126,"title":"Chapter 126: Defaming the Second Branch","paragraphs":["The Chengming Hall was brightly lit.","Guards stood solemnly from the doorway all the way to the main hall, their posture impeccable and imposing.","The Emperor wore a simple sky-blue robe, sitting upright in the main seat; the Dowager Madame sat beside him.","Huan Qingtang and Madam Song stood quietly by the side, heads bowed low; on the floor knelt two wet nurses and two maidservants.","The members of the Second Madam’s household entered, first kneeling to perform the grand salute.","Yet the Emperor and the Dowager Madame remained silent, no one beckoning them to rise.","After a long moment, the Dowager Madame finally spoke, still not calling them to stand, but asking, “Have you heard that the Secondary Wife’s child was lost?”","The Second Master responded promptly, “This is indeed a grave matter for the Duke’s household. We have heard, Mother.”","Not “Mom,” but a very formal and respectful “Mother.”","“The Secondary Wife’s miscarriage was unusual—it was caused by someone,” the Dowager Madame said.","The Second Madam already knew about the incident a few days ago at Lijing Courtyard, where Zhou Yuanshen had struck Mu Jiang with a bead, forcing her to kneel.","Could the Dowager Madame be attributing Mu Jiang’s miscarriage to that event?","Before she could say anything, the Dowager Madame continued, “There is a box of lip balm in the Secondary Wife’s chamber, containing bitter apricot kernels and sweet gardenia.","Doctor Feng said that although it looks fresh and fragrant, it is poisonous. The Secondary Wife may have mistakenly ingested it, which caused the miscarriage.”","The Second Madam looked up in surprise.","The Dowager Madame asked, “Fan, do you know the truth behind this?”","She was actually asking her.","The Second Master quickly answered, “Mother, Fan does not know. She never meddles in Lijing Courtyard affairs—you may investigate this.”","“I’m giving her a chance now!” The Dowager Madame’s face showed no outward rage, but her eyes were dark and full of murderous intent. “If she confesses honestly, I will ask His Majesty to be lenient.”","She added, “But if I send someone to investigate and find evidence, the law of the land and the family code will show no mercy!”","“Mother...” The Second Madam could not help but speak.","Cheng Zhao, standing behind her, subtly tugged at her sleeve.","Startled, the Second Madam recalled how her daughter-in-law had told her not to panic or speak recklessly; she also remembered how, despite being clearly in the right before, a few words from her mother-in-law and elder sister-in-law always found fault with her, ultimately slapping a heavy accusation on her.","After over twenty years in the Zhou family, she still hadn’t learned their way of speaking.","The Second Madam wanted to ask the Dowager Madame: Mu Jiang is a concubine, Zhou Yuanshen’s aunt by marriage; no matter who found her or raised her, is she still not a concubine?","Mu Jiang is a junior, a concubine. No matter how precious her unborn child was, how could family law be applied to the Second Madam herself, the mother-in-law?","At this moment, has the Zhou family abandoned filial piety?","Filial piety is always spoken of so highly.","And the law of the land!","What law? A concubine’s unborn child—no matter if the Emperor is sitting here—how could the law apply?","All these questions the Second Madam wanted to ask, but Cheng Zhao gripped her sleeve tightly.","Without turning around, she could imagine the worried frown on her daughter-in-law’s face—so breathtakingly beautiful.","Seeing that worry, the Second Madam couldn’t help but feel a pang of sympathy.","She simply kowtowed, feigning tears and said, “Mother, your daughter-in-law is confused and knows nothing.”","The Second Master added, “Mother, you may investigate carefully. Fan truly does not know about the affairs of Lijing Courtyard; she’s always been a scatterbrain.”","The Dowager Madame glared coldly at him.","Her gaze was like a sharp sword, aiming to pierce through the Second Master: “Second Master, are you defying me?”","The Second Master lowered his body submissively: “Your son dares not. Mother, if you wish to punish the daughter-in-law and granddaughter-in-law, it is entirely your prerogative. Your son dares not utter a single word of complaint.”","By retreating to advance, the Dowager Madame’s cheek twitched slightly.","The Emperor sat silently, his expression dark and unsettled, like the sky before a storm. It seemed calm now, but the next moment a tempest would break loose.","After the Second Master finished speaking, the Second Madam said nothing, and the room fell into silence.","The stillness was terrifying.","Everyone felt as if they were trapped in a steam-filled chamber, stifling and suffocating.","Suddenly, someone announced, “Lord Duke has returned!”","The Dowager Madame lifted her eyes.","But the Emperor frowned. “Isn’t Yuanshen supposed to be at the Jingji Camp?”","How could he be returning home at this late hour? By this time, all the city gates had long been closed.","The Emperor recalled that there had been a mutiny at the Jingji Camp—a faction loyal to the previous commander was trying to send Zhou Yuanshen a warning.","Reports about the incident had flooded the imperial study over the past two days like snowflakes. The Emperor believed Zhou Yuanshen should still be at the camp.","No one could open the city gates at night without special orders.","The Emperor’s eyes darkened even further.","As Zhou Yuanshen just stepped inside, everyone caught the scent of makeup and alcohol; his steps were unsteady.","The Emperor furrowed his brow.","The Dowager Madame’s face grew even more displeased.","“Your Majesty, I apologize for my late return,” he said, bowing to the Emperor.","He continued, “After finishing matters this afternoon, I was pulled into drinking by some people. When my household sent someone to look for me, they found out that Concubine Mu had suffered an accident.”","Huan Qingtang, who had been silently standing to the side, looked up at him.","So, even in front of the Emperor, he refused to address her as “Secondary Wife”?","He was reckless, yet remarkably resolute, always standing firm in his views.","Huan Qingtang felt as if she was seeing him anew.","“You were out enjoying yourself while Ah Jiang nearly died!” the Dowager Madame snapped, tears welling in her eyes. “You truly deserve to die!”","Those words were heavy with accusation.","The Emperor sighed.","He said, “Mother-in-Law, Yuanshen going out drinking isn’t a grave offense. Even if he had stayed home, it wouldn’t have stopped someone from deliberately causing harm.”","He was defending Zhou Yuanshen.","He did not follow the Dowager Madame’s wishes to punish him.","The Dowager Madame’s grip tightened.","“Your Majesty, Grandmother, I want to know exactly what happened,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Once the truth is uncovered, I will make sure Concubine Mu receives justice.”","“You explain it!” The Dowager Madame pointed at a nanny kneeling on the floor.","It must have been her.","“Lord Duke, the rouge and cosmetics supplied to Lijing Courtyard have always come from the official household. Someone mixed poison into the lip rouge. Because it’s a sea product, none of us knew. That’s why Secondary Wife suffered a miscarriage,” the nanny said.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t ask about the “sea product,” but instead asked, “Who said it was poisonous? And who confirmed that this was the cause of Concubine Mu’s miscarriage? Since the miscarriage already happened, it’s unlikely anyone would immediately suspect the lip rouge.”","The nanny hesitated for a moment: “...It was the Eldest Young Madame who discovered something was wrong with the rouge. She took it to Doctor Feng, who confirmed there was a problem.”","“So, after Concubine Mu’s miscarriage, you investigated the cause. Lady Huan pointed out the lip rouge, and Doctor Feng confirmed it as the cause. Is that correct?” Zhou Yuanshen asked.","The Emperor frowned.","The Dowager Madame’s face turned pale.","Huan Qingtang stood quietly by the side. Some glanced at her, but she said nothing rashly. If no one asked, she would remain silent.","Zhou Yuanshen reeked of alcohol, his cheeks flushed as if drunk, yet his mind was clear and his speech precise, without a hint of confusion.","“I want to confront Doctor Feng,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Ask him directly whether he is certain that Concubine Mu’s miscarriage was caused by this jar of ointment.”","The Emperor nodded. “Summon Doctor Feng.”","Soon, Doctor Feng entered the Chengming Hall, carrying his medical bag and dressed in his official physician’s robe. He knelt and kowtowed.","“There are indeed signs of poisoning in the Secondary Wife,” Doctor Feng confirmed.","“Where did this ointment come from?” Zhou Yuanshen pressed.","A nanny spoke up, “I don’t know. I heard someone say it was bought by the Fifth Young Master.”","Like a thunderclap, the Second Master and Second Madam suddenly looked up sharply at the nanny.","“Nonsense! What are you talking about?” the Second Madam snapped angrily."]},{"number":127,"title":"Chapter 127: The Emperor Kills a Man","paragraphs":["The dispute in Chengming Hall had been sorted out between the Second Master and his wife.","Mu Jiang’s miscarriage required someone to take the blame—even Mu Jiang herself had no say in the matter.","Huan Qingtang had pointed out the problem with the lip balm, which was not a lie on her part.","This was her merit.","But where the lip balm had come from was not something she had stated; it had been investigated by the household stewards.","The investigation led to Zhou Yuanqi, who had purchased such an item at the Yizhen Pavilion.","\"I did not buy lip balm!\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Second Madam also said, \"Even if he bought it, he would not have given it to the concubine!\"","\"Mother and son, coordinate your stories—did he buy it or not?\" the Dowager Madame asked coldly.","The Second Madam was startled.","She never imagined that such a remark could be seized upon by the Dowager Madame.","She once again recalled her daughter-in-law’s advice: do not speak.","\"Summon the owner, manager, and shop assistants of the Yizhen Pavilion for questioning. I only bought two boxes of face powder, not lip balm,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Dowager Madame snorted coldly, saying, \"The Yizhen Pavilion has countless customers—how could they remember clearly? Even if they were called in to check the accounts, what could they clarify?\"","\"Is there a ledger?\" Zhou Yuanqi asked.","If they had planned to frame him, there would certainly be a ledger.","The accounts would record two boxes of face powder and one box of lip balm.","What Zhou Yuanqi bought, and how it ended up in Mu Jiang's quarters—someone would then come forward to say a few words, and it would be settled.","As a child from the Zhou family, what did Zhou Yuanqi understand?","Cheng Zhao, who was on good terms with him, was likely the mastermind behind it all.","Zhou Yuanshen, the Second Madam, and the Second Master all understood.","As long as the evidence pointed to Cheng Zhao, she would have no chance to defend herself.","\"Your Majesty, may this subject’s wife speak?\" Cheng Zhao suddenly interjected.","The Emperor frowned. \"Step back; there are others who may speak.\"","\"It concerns Consort Dou,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Everyone was taken aback.","The Dowager Madame also looked at her in surprise.","Hearing the words \"Consort Dou,\" the Emperor’s mood grew more irritable, with a flicker of anger pulsing in his brow.","\"Your Majesty, the one who harmed the concubine’s unborn child is Consort Dou. Consort Dou holds a grudge against the Dowager Madame and the Zhou family. She lost her own son and wanted us to taste the same piercing heartache, so she used Doctor Feng’s hand to kill the concubine’s child,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She spoke with absolute certainty.","The Emperor frowned at her.","The Dowager Madame’s expression shifted several times, but she did not command her to be silent.","Doctor Feng was horrified. \"Your Majesty, your subject is wronged! The Duchess is shifting blame and slandering me!\"","\"Whether it is slander or not, investigate Doctor Feng. A small lip balm, not even ingested—how could it cause harm to the fetus? If Doctor Feng had not been bribed by Consort Dou, the concubine would never have met with trouble,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Your Majesty, please issue an order to thoroughly investigate Doctor Feng.\"","The Emperor hesitated.","\"Send someone to search his residence,\" he commanded nonchalantly.","Doctor Feng’s face lost all color. \"Your Majesty...\"","\"Silence!\" The Emperor rubbed his tight temples. \"If you are innocent, I will naturally grant you justice—and at that point, I will cut off the Duchess’s head for you.\"","Everyone trembled again.","The Second Madam wanted to speak again, but this time Zhou Yuanqi firmly pressed down her other sleeve to remind her to stay silent.","\"Duchess, are you certain this is not a false accusation?\" the Emperor asked again.","Cheng Zhao shook her head firmly. \"Your subject’s wife’s reasoning is all true. Your Majesty, think: how could the concubine and her child have come to harm? It was impossible!\"","\"Then search,\" the Emperor ordered.","His bodyguards took men and left.","Doctor Feng’s mansion was some distance from the Chenguo Mansion, and during the wait, everyone—kneeling, sitting, or standing—felt immensely agonizing.","Torture seemed to weigh on every person.","Some were deep in thought, some were frantically plotting countermeasures, and others were already drenched in cold sweat.","An hour later, the guards returned with the items they had seized.","Jewels and gold aside, there was also a phoenix hairpin inlaid with a sapphire—an item from Consort Dou’s palace.","The Emperor immediately recognized it as a court artifact and knew it belonged to the Consort. He sneered, \"What is this?\"","\"Your subject—your subject—\" Doctor Feng’s official robes were already soaked with sweat as he kowtowed repeatedly but could offer no defense.","Cheng Zhao’s third sister, Cheng Ying, said that there was a secret which outsiders might not know, but the Princess of Jingnan was aware: Doctor Feng was the deputy director of the Imperial Hospital, and he and Consort Dou had been childhood sweethearts.","Their statuses were too far apart for any illicit affair, and the Dou family had previously objected. But Doctor Feng had always been loyal to the Consort.","Later, when the Consort entered the palace, bore a son, and secured the Emperor’s favor, Doctor Feng had assisted her countless times, as was known among the meritorious families.","Yet, out of fear of the Duke of Piguo’s influence, the Consort’s authority, and Doctor Feng’s current position and connections, no one wished to court this trouble.","Of the many consorts in the palace, only four princes remained—so many sins had Doctor Feng helped the Consort commit that he had likely lost count.","The Dowager Madame had even heard of it, which was why she had hesitated to summon Doctor Feng initially.","Mu Jiang had made such a fuss that the Dowager Madame feared something might happen to the child, yet she thought Doctor Feng would not dare act so blatantly.","When Doctor Feng arrived, the Dowager Madame had already warned him that if he played tricks, both he and the Consort would face dire consequences.","This time, Doctor Feng had indeed done nothing.","But his residence was not clean: besides bribes and gifts exchanged in secret with the Consort, there was also a ledger detailing the crimes he had committed on the Consort’s behalf.","The Emperor looked at the phoenix hairpin and said coldly, \"This was the one I personally placed on the Consort’s head on the day Hong’er was born. Now it seems—whose son is Hong’er, after all?\"","Doctor Feng nearly collapsed to the ground. \"Your Majesty, Your subject and the Consort are innocent...\"","The Emperor drew the longsword at his waist, walked over slowly, and pointed it at Doctor Feng.","Doctor Feng did not dare to dodge.","Everyone thought the Emperor was just trying to intimidate him, perhaps even strike him—when the tip of the blade plunged into Doctor Feng’s chest.","Blood gushed from Doctor Feng’s mouth and nose.","His eyes wide open, he convulsed on the ground for a moment, then gradually stilled.","\"Ah!\" First Madam Song, standing nearby, let out an involuntary gasp.","She covered her mouth and nose tightly, glancing fearfully at the Emperor and the Dowager Madame, terrified that her outburst might bring death upon her.","Huan Qingtang was rigid, her face ashen.","At that moment, Zhou Yuanshen swiftly moved to stand in front of the Second House’s people. The Second Master rose promptly, and together father and son shielded the two women and the child behind them.","The Emperor watched this scene as if he found it amusing, and a faint smile curled at the corner of his mouth.","Blood still dripped from his longsword.","Huan Qingtang could no longer stand; her knees buckled, and she knelt on the ground. First Madam Song, mistaking this for a gesture of respect, quickly knelt as well.","All the serving maids and servants in the hall prostrated themselves.","The Second Master glanced at Zhou Yuanshen, and Zhou Yuanshen dropped to one knee: \"Your Majesty, please calm your wrath.\"","\"Your Majesty, please calm your wrath,\" the Second Master also knelt again.","The Dowager Madame watched it all, her chest heaving.","\"Enough.\" The Emperor discarded his bloody sword. \"My head aches. Mother-in-law, what do you say?\"","The Dowager Madame said, \"It is late, Your Majesty. Return to the palace and rest. Today’s farce has disturbed Your Majesty.\"","\"It is no matter. The affairs of the Zhou family are all my household matters,\" the Emperor said.","The listeners’ hearts trembled.","And thus the Emperor left.","Doctor Feng’s corpse lay limply on the brick floor of Chengming Hall, gradually stiffening."]},{"number":128,"title":"Chapter 128: Breaking the Deadlock","paragraphs":["The people of the Second Branch returned to the Crimson Clouds Courtyard.","Everyone was silent.","“...It’s late. You should go rest. Yuan Qi, you’ll sleep in the inner warming chamber,” said Second Master.","But Second Madam said, “Don’t go, don’t go!”","The fear she had been holding back suddenly surged up, and she began to tremble.","Cheng Zhao stepped forward and firmly wrapped an arm around her shoulders. “Mother, it’s over. We’ve escaped this calamity.”","Second Madam looked at her in panic. “He killed that imperial physician without a second thought! If—if there hadn’t been this man, he would have killed Yuan Qi, or you!”","Whether it was hatred or lingering terror, tears welled in Second Madam’s eyes.","She knew nothing of Doctor Feng’s secret.","In that moment, she had been utterly at a loss for how to untangle the situation, convinced that Zhou Yuanqi was about to be framed for plotting against Mu Jiang’s unborn child.","And Cheng Zhao would have been implicated as well.","But she hadn’t expected Cheng Zhao to simply ignore the “lip balm” and instead insist that Doctor Feng was the culprit, using his connection to Consort Dou and his past misdeeds to shift the blame for the incident.","Thank heaven for her quick wit.","“Thank goodness we brought in this Doctor Feng; he bore the brunt of the Emperor’s wrath. Otherwise, what would you have done?” Second Madam said, inhaling deeply.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Mother, Grandmother knew full well that this man had improper ties to the Consort, yet she insisted on summoning him. Perhaps he was the backup plan she had prepared all along.”","The Dowager Madame had also feared losing control of the situation.","After the Fifth Prince’s death, Consort Dou had become the Zhou family’s formidable enemy. Naturally, her connections within the Imperial Medical Institute had to be eliminated.","Thinking more deeply, every imperial physician who took Mu Jiang’s pulse had reported her condition to the Dowager Madame. And skilled physicians might well have discerned the gender of the child in Mu Jiang’s womb.","Perhaps the Dowager Madame had long known that Mu Jiang was carrying a girl, and that the fetus was weak.","So then...","—Where, then, was the true stage for this play?","As Cheng Zhao delved deeper, she felt as though she had plunged into total darkness.","Could people truly be so wicked?","Or was she merely projecting her own suspicions onto others?","Second Madam leaned heavily on Cheng Zhao, unable to calm herself for a long while.","Zhou Yuanqi remained uncharacteristically silent.","Blood makes one grow up fast—faster than any lecture ever could.","It was his first time being caught up in schemes and conspiracies, and he understood that “silver-tongued eloquence” was useless; he needed to be like his sister-in-law—planning ahead and staying composed in a crisis.","“Mother, sometimes during midsummer, there are thunderstorms with fierce winds and lightning. I find them frightening too. But I can’t stop them, and they always pass quickly,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at her.","Second Madam took her words to heart. She seemed to gather all her strength and cast aside her lingering fears.","She was the elder. It was her duty to spread her wings and shelter her children. How could she afford to be weak at a time like this?","What was there to fear?","What use was regret?","As Cheng Zhao said, the most critical moment had passed, and the Emperor was not coming back to kill anyone.","Second Madam straightened up and smoothed Cheng Zhao’s collar. “It’s late. You’re tired, too. Go rest. We’ll talk after we’ve had some sleep.”","Then she looked at Zhou Yuanshen. “Go on.”","The young couple left. The servants laid out bedding in the warming chamber, and Zhou Yuanqi went to sleep. Only then did Second Master and his wife lie down.","Neither felt the least bit sleepy, but neither spoke.","In the darkness, Second Madam thought, “If the Emperor ever dared to point a sword at my children, I would kill him!”","And after killing him?","Would her entire clan have their heads chopped off?","At that thought, Second Madam turned over, her back to Second Master.","Then she turned again.","By the time she had turned for the fourth time, Second Master gently took her hand. “Don’t blame yourself. You did well.”","He understood her.","She didn’t need to say a word; he already knew.","“Not well at all!”","“You didn’t say a single unnecessary word. You kept your temper under control, and you even managed a few fake tears. Being able to swallow your pride for the sake of your children—that takes true courage,” Second Master said.","Second Madam gave a bitter laugh.","From the warming chamber, Zhou Yuanqi’s voice chimed in: “‘Conquering others takes strength; conquering oneself takes true might.’ Mother, overcoming your impulsive nature and staying silent was indeed an act of extraordinary courage.”","Second Madam chuckled despite herself. “You really do know how to read and how to talk.”","Her mood lightened considerably.","And so, she drifted off to sleep.","In the Verdant Blossom Courtyard, the young couple lay awake, but they had changed into nightclothes and were resting to conserve their energy.","In the darkness, Cheng Zhao spoke. “Lord Duke, was it discreet of you to leave the Capital Garrison?”","If the Dowager Madame caught hold of evidence that he had slipped through the city gates in the middle of the night, she would not let him off easily.","The Emperor valued Zhou Yuanshen—on the condition that he acted prudently.","If he violated the law, the Emperor would abandon him without hesitation. After all, Zhou Yuanshen was merely a blade; even if the Emperor regretted it, he would still discard him.","“I never went to the Capital Garrison,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao turned her head toward him. “Then you left the estate because of the concubine’s matter?”","“I prepared for both scenarios. I had someone cause trouble at the garrison and expelled two troublesome commanders—one was a member of the Guo family, the other belonged to the Piguo Duke’s estate,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Then he added, “I’ve been putting it about that I was in the camp’s headquarters, but I never showed myself; I let my deputy commander handle everything. In reality, I’ve been in the city this whole time, with disinterested witnesses to corroborate it.”","He paused briefly before continuing, “As for what happened at home, I wanted to see how it would play out. When I struck in the Splendid View Courtyard, I was slapping Grandmother in the face. Naturally, she had to retaliate.”","Cheng Zhao fell silent.","There were times when the ugliness of human hearts was so unbearable that she wished she could cover it with a veil. She couldn’t bring herself to look directly at it.","So she didn’t press him further.","“Cheng Zhao, I obtained some evidence of crimes committed by the Changling Marquis’s Song family. At the critical moment, I could drag the main branch into this mess.","That way, Grandmother wouldn’t dare insist that the Second Branch was responsible for harming Mu Jiang. She would have to make a choice. I wouldn’t have thrown you all into danger without a safety net,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Still, Cheng Zhao’s method had worked well.","She had shoved forward the scapegoat the Dowager Madame had prepared, rendering the “lip balm” matter entirely irrelevant.","Even if the Emperor hadn’t killed Doctor Feng on the spot, the man’s guilt would have been impossible to explain. He had harmed more than one palace consort before.","With such precedent, if the Zhou family insisted that he was the one who harmed Mu Jiang, he wouldn’t have been able to prove his innocence.","As for whether the lip balm containing bitter almond and sweet gardenia had actually caused Mu Jiang’s miscarriage, no one could confirm it.","If no other cause could be found, then that accusation would have had to stick—and that was when Zhou Yuanqi and Cheng Zhao would have been made to bear the blame.","“Lord Duke, are you saying that Grandmother might have known all along that the child in the concubine’s womb was weak, and thus decided to sacrifice it? And using this incident, she intended to bring disaster upon us?” Cheng Zhao asked.","After a pause, she added, “The eldest sister-in-law also did her part. She wanted to take the opportunity to push me into the abyss.”","Zhou Yuanshen was silent for a moment before saying, “Another soul enslaved to evil.”","Cheng Zhao replied, “I admit that the eldest aunt-in-law is a pitiable, hateful victim consumed by Grandmother. But the eldest sister-in-law is not the same.”","Huan Qingtang’s actions had nothing to do with the behavioral patterns forged by the Dowager Madame’s oppression. From the very beginning, she had carried her own open ambition.","Those of the same kind could most keenly sense each other’s presence.","It was Cheng Zhao’s own fierce competitiveness that allowed her to so sharply detect Huan Qingtang’s thriving ambition.","“The truly innocent one was the child of the concubine,” Cheng Zhao said.","She wanted very much to say, “Lord Duke, please accept my condolences,” but so much had happened tonight, and countless details had pieced together a horrifying truth—one that was almost fully exposed before Cheng Zhao. She simply could no longer pretend to be blind.","Of course, as long as Zhou Yuanshen chose not to speak of it, she would never bring it up."]},{"number":129,"title":"Chapter 129: Zhou Yuanshen's Manipulation","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao barely managed a doze just before dawn.","Zhou Yuanshen lay down briefly, then said to Cheng Zhao, \"I need to go out. There’s much to handle. Don’t be afraid. Don’t go to the Hall of Morning Brightness or the Garden of Longevity today, no matter who calls you.\"","Cheng Zhao acknowledged his instructions.","It wasn’t just the second branch of the family who had spent the night sleepless.","Almost everyone in the Duke’s estate had barely slept.","Huan Qingtang huddled in the Courtyard of Concentrated Fragrance, drenched in cold sweat. The image of Doctor Feng’s corpse haunted her vision.","Yet she couldn’t let this disturb her.","She got up and went to the Hall of Morning Brightness. She saw a few rough servants crouching under the eaves, but no one else. Doctor Feng still lay on the floor tiles.","“...Why hasn’t he been dealt with?” Huan Qingtang asked, her hands trembling within her sleeves, though her voice remained calm.","The servant woman replied, “No one has given orders, Eldest Young Madame.”","The Dowager Madame had returned to the Garden of Longevity; the First Madam, frightened, had hidden in a side chamber to rest.","“Summon someone. Have the men outside wrap this man in a straw mat and place him in a vacant side room in the rear,” Huan Qingtang ordered.","Set him aside for now, but what then?","Her mind raced.","An old servant woman immediately went to relay the order.","Soon, Steward Sun arrived. He brought two manservants and an oxcart, and they moved Doctor Feng’s body away.","“...Eldest Young Madame, how should we explain this to Doctor Feng’s family?” Steward Sun asked for guidance.","Doctor Feng was an official. There were a thousand ways to execute him, but none as simple as the Emperor running him through with a sword.","Huan Qingtang thought for a moment, then said, “He took his own life out of guilt, defiling the Hall of Morning Brightness. Tomorrow at first light, send his body to the Court of Great Justice, along with the evidence of his crimes.”","Steward Sun acknowledged her orders.","At the very rear of the Duke of Chen’s estate, beyond the back garden, stood several clusters of courtyards and rows of low houses. These were quarters for stewards’ families, servants, and poor relatives seeking shelter.","Since there were many empty rooms, one could be chosen to hold the corpse temporarily for the night without issue.","At dawn, Zhou Yuanshen arrived at the Hall of Morning Brightness, and Nanny Sun, who served the Dowager Madame, also came.","“The Eldest Young Madame has already ordered the body placed in a rear side room,” said a servant woman from the Hall of Morning Brightness. “She also instructed it to be sent to the Court of Great Justice.”","Nanny Sun nodded.","After greeting Zhou Yuanshen, she turned and returned to the Garden of Longevity.","Zhou Yuanshen then left.","Before the morning court session, he rushed to the Imperial Study to see the Emperor.","“...Your Majesty, I have reorganized the evidence of Doctor Feng’s crimes. At dawn, the Zhou family will deliver both his suicide note and his body to the Court of Great Justice,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","The Emperor had, in fact, slept.","After waking, he recalled killing the imperial physician the day before at the Duke of Chen’s estate and felt deep regret.","But at that moment, his mind had been consumed by that one thing—he had to kill someone. He always carried a sharp sword on his person; he had used it to wound a minister before.","Those damned ministers would now make a fuss over this minor matter, buzzing like flies.","They were utterly detestable—untouchable by threats, unbreakable by intimidation.","If he tried to kill them, they would think of “dying by remonstration” to secure their place in history, and they would be more thrilled than he was!","What the Emperor enjoyed was his ministers’ fear, not their eager expressions in the face of death. That infuriated him.","He could neither strike nor kill them; he had to follow their rules.","Only his mother-in-law understood his torment.","Now, one more person did.","Zhou Yuanshen not only understood him but also helped him clean up unpleasant matters.","“If only you were my son,” the Emperor sighed. “None of my sons has your resolve.”","He added, “If your aunt hadn’t died, she would have borne me a Crown Prince. That prince would naturally possess the shrewdness and decisiveness of the Zhou family—just like you.”","As he spoke, tears flowed from his eyes.","Zhou Yuanshen lowered his head and spoke earnestly, “Your Majesty, please restrain your grief and take care of your dragon body.”","The Emperor wiped his tears. “Forget it.”","Then he said, “You handled Doctor Feng’s matter well. His crime is unforgivable. I will exterminate his three clans.”","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “Your Majesty is most wise. If you order the extermination of his three clans, the court officials will surely plead for leniency. That way, no one will question why he took his own life.","Then, Your Majesty can relent and spare his clansmen. All the officials will be grateful for Your Majesty’s magnanimity and will believe it was their words that swayed your judgment.”","The Emperor laughed. “This idea suits me well. We shall do that.”","In that brief moment, he wept with genuine sorrow and then laughed with unrestrained delight.","He thought to himself: on the throne, he would announce the extermination of Doctor Feng’s three clans, watching the officials panic and beg for mercy. Once he agreed to spare the Feng clan, they would think they had won again.","They were nothing but rats for him to toy with.","Zhou Yuanshen, expressionless, bowed respectfully and took his leave.","He still attended the morning court that day.","The Court of Great Justice had not yet received the case, so the court discussed many matters, including the \"mutiny\" in the Capital Garrison.","The Duke of Pi was furious, because one of his men had been killed.","He argued fiercely with Zhou Yuanshen, insisting that Zhou Yuanshen had orchestrated the mutiny and was pulling the strings.","Zhou Yuanshen did not refute much; he simply presented his memorial, laying out everything clearly.","After court, Zhou Yuanshen walked over to the Duke of Pi and spoke quietly to him:","“Perhaps you should exert some influence in the Court of Great Justice to finalize Doctor Feng’s charges. If you look closely, Your Majesty intends to exterminate not just the Feng clan, but also the Dou clan.”","“What charges? What clans?” The Duke of Pi was baffled.","Zhou Yuanshen walked away.","When he returned to the estate, he went first to the Garden of Longevity.","The Dowager Madame had already risen and was taking some nourishing tonics.","She had Zhou Yuanqi go to the eastern side room, put down her spoon, and asked, “How did things go?”","“Consort Dou and her family are even more afraid of the Feng matter escalating. They will bribe the coroner to confirm that Doctor Feng took his own life,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","The Dowager Madame nodded slightly. “You handled it well.”","Then she added, “A pity...”","If the Emperor hadn’t killed Doctor Feng on the spot but had turned him over to the Court of Great Justice, perhaps they could have eliminated Consort Dou today.","But dealing with her natal family, the Duke of Pi’s estate, was still difficult, since many of these matters involved the Consort’s interests. The Dou family could always disclaim responsibility.","But the Emperor...","The Dowager Madame felt regret.","Two pawns had been wasted this time—Doctor Feng and the child that Mu Jiang had failed to carry.","Both had died for nothing.","None of the goals the Dowager Madame had wanted to achieve were accomplished.","The only thing gained was a test of Huan Qingtang.","“...Your eldest aunt hid herself away, but Huan Qingtang handled everything calmly and properly. She is truly a noble lady of a high family, steady and reliable in her actions,” the Dowager Madame said to Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen met her gaze evenly, his dark eyes showing no emotion. “Grandmother, Cheng Shi will bear many children. Our sons will inherit the incense of the Duke of Chen’s estate. I will not become a second husband to a widow and ruin my reputation.”","The Dowager Madame’s heart seized.","This was the first time Zhou Yuanshen had stated his intentions so clearly.","He was defying her.","She slowly sat up straighter. “Yuanshen, do you think you can go against my will now?”","“Grandmother, the force behind your will is nothing more than the Emperor’s support,” Zhou Yuanshen said, each word measured. “Grandmother, open your eyes and look at the Emperor. How much longer will he remain clear-minded? A mad emperor may be manipulated by you, but can he not be manipulated by others?”","The Dowager Madame was left speechless.","The eastern side room was silent for a long while, when suddenly the Dowager Madame hurled both the bowl and spoon at Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen dodged.","The spoon and bowl crashed to the floor, sending shards of porcelain scattering in all directions."]},{"number":130,"title":"Chapter 130: She is not as good as Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["The Dowager Madame had not been this furious in a long time.","Her hand gripped the edge of the heated brick bed so hard that her knuckles stood out white.","Nanny Sun hurried in and said, “Lord Duke, do you wish for the Dowager Madame to accuse you of unfilial conduct before the court? That would truly ruin your reputation!”","A man lacking filial piety was deemed disloyal and was deeply scorned in the court.","Filial piety was written into the law.","Zhou Yuanshen spoke: “Grandmother, calm your anger and rest well.”","He did not bow, but turned and left.","Nanny Sun patted the Dowager Madame’s back, gently persuading her for a long time, almost weeping with worry.","“I misjudged him. He is so cruel and ruthless,” the Dowager Madame finally managed to say after a long pause.","“Dowager Madame, please calm your anger. The heavy mountains of ‘loyalty’ and ‘filial piety’ press down on him—he cannot stir up much trouble,” said Nanny Sun.","The Dowager Madame’s status embodied filial piety; before the emperor, loyalty was paramount, and the emperor himself could be influenced by the Dowager Madame.","As a man, he might disregard everything, but as one who aspired to be a powerful minister, he could slaughter the innocent yet dare not be unfilial or disloyal.","Her very identity was enough to suppress him.","The Dowager Madame had no need to be so enraged.","“...If he refuses to continue the Hedong Huan lineage through plural marriage, find an excuse to divorce Cheng Shi, or even divorce Lady Fan, and drive out his mother and wife. Within the inner quarters, you, honored elder, are the heavens. Who can defy heaven’s will?” Nanny Sun continued.","There were plenty of ways to deal with Zhou Yuanshen.","The Second Branch had so many people—if the Dowager Madame casually seized upon a mistake, they would be unable to bear the consequences.","Zhou Yuanshen dared to provoke her—it was as if he did not know his place.","“I was truly muddled by his anger. Fortunately, you are by my side,” the Dowager Madame said, her spirits much relieved.","Once her strength gave way, her shoulders and back hunched slightly—Nanny Sun noticed she had aged.","At only sixty, to show such signs of age was startling.","“Dowager Madame, you are angered. Lord Duke deliberately provokes you to make you act rashly, so he can seize upon your missteps,” Nanny Sun added.","The Dowager Madame nodded. “Do you think I don’t know his intent?”","Knowing it was one thing, but every time Zhou Yuanshen struck at her weaknesses, she could hardly keep from being overcome by rage.","“He says nothing, appearing like a dull, unresponsive block of wood, yet the emperor trusts him greatly,” the Dowager Madame said.","Last night in the Hall of Grand Brightness, the Dowager Madame had seen the emperor’s partiality toward Zhou Yuanshen.","The emperor had not rebuked Zhou Yuanshen a single time.","In the Dowager Madame’s view, Zhou Yuanshen had many faults; with a few words of instigation, the emperor would have turned on him.","Unexpectedly, the emperor instinctively protected him.","Zhou Yuanshen killed on behalf of the emperor, truly striking at what the emperor cherished.","The Dowager Madame suddenly realized that Zhou Yuanshen seemed to have won the imperial favor through a method she did not understand.","“Dowager Madame, he is your grandson,” Nanny Sun said. “One identity, one heavy mountain—he cannot escape your palm.”","The Dowager Madame fell silent for a moment.","Nanny Sun, fearing she would sink into brooding, spoke many words to comfort her.","“The child of Lady Mu Jiang is lost; she can conceive again in the future,” Nanny Sun said. “Next time, she will surely carry a prince.”","The doctor had told the Dowager Madame that the child in Mu Jiang’s womb was a girl, and the Dowager Madame had been deliberating.","“The Eldest Young Madame is wise and clever, steadfastly loyal. With her here, you can rest assured,” Nanny Sun added.","She also said, “The Second Branch is a bunch of blockheads. That Cheng Shi only has a sharp tongue and a few petty tricks.”","The Dowager Madame’s thoughts shifted from Huan Qingtang to Cheng Zhao.","If she set aside personal likes and dislikes, Cheng Zhao suited the Dowager Madame’s wishes better. She was not only intelligent but also well-connected.","Cheng Zhao had managed to uncover the secret of Physician Feng.","That was no small feat, given that the Dowager Madame herself had investigated it to deal with the Honored Consort, yet had barely heard anything.","Consort Dou had risen to her current position, and her deceased son had nearly become the heir apparent—a testament to her craftiness.","The situation back then had been so dire; Huan Qingtang was too terrified to speak, but Cheng Zhao had turned the tide, wresting life from a dead end.","This capability, Huan Qingtang lacked, and the Dowager Madame had to acknowledge it.","Huan Qingtang could not be said to lack wisdom, but her spirit and methods were inferior, especially compared to Cheng Zhao.","Pity Cheng Zhao was not the Dowager Madame’s choice, so she simply disliked her. Coupled with several plans that failed because of her, the Dowager Madame was almost resentful.","Whenever she looked at Cheng Zhao, she saw only a cunning poison; she could not appreciate her talents and only wished to trample her into the dirt.","Thinking of Cheng Zhao, then of Zhou Yuanshen, the Dowager Madame felt a pang in her heart.","She had not been angered to such a degree in many years.","Cheng Zhao rose early, washed and dressed, then went to Crimson Cloud Court.","Second Master had already gone to court, but Zhou Yuanqi had not yet left for his studies.","Her mother-in-law was somewhat silent.","They did not mention the Hall of Grand Brightness incident, only chatting idly about small matters.","Until the head steward of the main kitchen came to find Cheng Zhao.","“...No council today, but there are several matters in the main kitchen that require your direction,” the steward said.","Cheng Zhao went with her to Dawn Radiance Court.","She handled the matters under her charge concerning the main kitchen and the gatehouse.","“Is no business being conducted at the Hall of Grand Brightness?” Cheng Zhao asked.","The steward replied, “First Madam is feeling unwell. The Hall of Grand Brightness also has to hold a ritual, so they likely won’t handle affairs for about half a month.”","“Then how are other matters to be reported?” Cheng Zhao asked.","“Head Steward Sun said everyone should go to the Eldest Young Madame’s Joyful Elixir Court. Your stewards come to you here,” the steward replied.","Cheng Zhao understood.","Apart from the gatehouse and the main kitchen, all other duties in the ducal mansion were now under Huan Qingtang’s management.","Huan Qingtang had repeatedly schemed against Cheng Zhao and had indeed won the Dowager Madame’s trust and favor.","Once Cheng Zhao finished her tasks, she returned to Crimson Cloud Court.","“Is there anything wrong in the main kitchen?” Second Madam asked her.","Cheng Zhao: “Just the usual trivial matters. Since the Hall of Grand Brightness is not holding council, they needed to find someone to make decisions.”","She then told Second Madam about the Hall of Grand Brightness matter.","“Zhao Zhao, let me give you a word of advice,” Second Madam said after listening.","Cheng Zhao nodded: “I’m listening, Mother, please speak.”","“You and I are alike—not chosen by the Dowager Madame. You must understand her temper. If she didn’t order that dish, no matter how delicious others find it, she won’t touch it,” Second Madam said.","She added, “No matter how good you are, you’ll never win her favor. Do not waste your efforts.”","“I understand, Mother. Grandmother is used to controlling everything. Anything outside her palm, she will sweep away,” Cheng Zhao said.","She had long been an eyesore to the Dowager Madame.","Second Madam nodded.","As she spoke, she reached out and gently stroked Cheng Zhao’s head, as a mother would her own daughter. “It’s hard on you. A fine girl like you, married into any family, would be cherished like a treasure. But for us...”","“Mother, our family is good too,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile. “You and Father treat me well, and so does Yuanqi. Lord Duke’s rules are strict, but dealing with him is easy. I think I am very fortunate.”","Second Madam was stunned.","Fortunate?","She had just passed through the gates of life and death.","In any other family, there would not be such chaos.","If it had been Second Madam, she would have considered divorce and escape by now. Cheng Zhao had no children; she could have left everything behind.","Yet she called herself fortunate.","“You child!” Second Madam held her hand.","“Mother, we will be fine,” Cheng Zhao said. “You and I are still young. The Dowager Madame is old.”","Second Madam: “...”"]},{"number":131,"title":"Chapter 131: The Prestige of the Sister-in-Law Is Damaged","paragraphs":["It was already May, and the back garden of the Duke of Chen’s mansion was a riot of colorful blossoms.","Compared to the fiery pomegranate flowers in full bloom, the atmosphere within the mansion was stagnant and oppressive; everyone moved with caution.","The only light and unrestrained things were the sparrows flitting and chirping among the branches.","“They really are holding a rites ceremony at the Hall of Reverence!” Second Madam heard the news, went to see for herself, and returned to tell Cheng Zhao.","Seeing that a small Daoist ritual was set up inside the Hall of Reverence, she was somewhat surprised.","She hadn’t expected Eldest Madam to be so fearful, nor had she thought the Dowager Madame would agree to it.","“If she’s that scared, she might as well give up the Hall of Reverence and let me take it. I’m not afraid,” Second Madam added.","Cheng Zhao had been working at the Dawn Courtyard these days, which was quite close to the Hall of Reverence, so she had long been aware of the commotion there.","“This is to win sympathy,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam thought it over and immediately understood: “Is that really the case?”","Doctor Feng had died at the Zhou residence.","The Court of Judicial Review had ruled it a suicide, with various pieces of evidence pointing to him having harmed Mu Jiang’s child, fearing the Zhou family’s retaliation.","The Zhou family had also uncovered other evidence of his crimes.","Consort Dou and the Duke of Pi’s mansion were busy covering things up; no one stepped forward at this time to seek justice for Doctor Feng.","But there were many holes in this story.","For instance, the Imperial Guard had ransacked Doctor Feng’s home in the dead of night.","Was the Emperor spending the night at the Zhou mansion?","Even if they guessed the truth, out of deference to the sovereign, no one would speak of it openly.","The Dowager Madame had agreed to the rites ceremony to garner a bit of sympathy for the Zhou family—to show that the main courtyard of the mansion had seen a death, the womenfolk were terrified, and the Zhou family had also lost an unborn child. The Zhous were pitiful.","At the same time, it diverted the curious eyes away from the Emperor.","“Zhaozhao, this household is getting messier. It used to be not so harmonious, but at least we had peace day to day, with only one or two incidents a year,” Second Madam said.","She then quickly added, afraid Cheng Zhao might misunderstand, “It’s not because of you. None of this is your fault.”","Ever since Cheng Zhao married into the family, conflicts had been constant. The timing lined up, but the responsibility wasn’t hers. Second Madam feared that Cheng Zhao might take her words to heart.","Cheng Zhao smiled. “Mother, I understand.”","She added, “It’s because the title fell to the wrong branch, and the burden and power became misaligned, that calamities erupted.”","With no man left in the senior branch, under the laws of inheritance and custom of this age, that branch had lost its authority.","This wasn’t a rule Cheng Zhao had set; it was the law, the way of the world.","The senior branch was unwilling to accept it, and the Dowager Madame refused to let go, turning the household of the Duke—who held the title—into a place where a wife was no proper wife and a concubine no proper concubine.","When the joints and mortises don’t fit, the wheels of the cart can never roll smoothly forward.","Cheng Zhao had suffered much injustice.","She had never received the treatment and authority befitting her role as the Duke’s wife and a lady of the highest rank. And she had never been the one to stir up trouble.","She was very careful. Stirring trouble could lead to failure, ruining her reputation; and as one who would play the role of the honored elder matriarch, her lifelong standing was vital.","So no matter how you looked at it, it made no sense to blame Cheng Zhao. She had never been the source of these disasters.","On the contrary, she had been the one enduring their impact.","Cheng Zhao did not blame herself, nor did she feel that her mother-in-law blamed her for anything.","Her mother-in-law might not be able to articulate the logic, but her sense of right and wrong was razor-sharp; she was never confused. Cheng Zhao understood her intentions and did not take offence at her choice of words.","The truly confused were those who thought themselves wise.","“...Eldest Aunt has been resting at the Jade Embroidery Courtyard these days,” Cheng Zhao told her mother-in-law.","The Jade Embroidery Courtyard was close to the Hall of Reverence and also connected to Mu Jiang’s Beautiful View Courtyard.","It was a residence traditionally given to the favored concubines of the dukes.","Hearing this, Second Madam sneered again. “She doesn’t want to be too far from the Hall of Reverence. The Jade Embroidery Courtyard was built as a residence for concubines, yet she willingly lives there just to stay close to the Hall of Reverence.”","Around the Hall of Reverence, there weren’t many habitable courtyards to begin with; everything was built around the “needs” of the Duke as head of the family.","For instance, the Alchemy Fragrance Courtyard was the wedding home of the eldest son. As the heir, the eldest son could not be too far from the Hall of Reverence.","There were also several courtyards for banquets and entertaining.","The Dawn Courtyard, which served as the inner study, was also nearby.","The rest consisted of two connected courtyards set aside for the Duke’s favored concubines, making it convenient for him to visit them overnight.","“Zhaozhao, look at this household—everyone is a mess, like ghosts with disheveled hair. No wonder it’s such chaos,” Second Madam went on.","For the second time, she thought of moving out and setting up her own household.","“Mother, every storm eventually calms,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam considered this and thought, yes, how could she act like a deserter?","As the mother-in-law and daughter-in-law were talking, Nanny Fan entered: “Third Young Madam, the steward at the gate is looking for you.”","Before Cheng Zhao could ask, Nanny Fan had already gathered the details. “The Secondary Wife is not doing well. The Dowager Madame has instructed you to summon a physician.”","Second Madam asked, “What happened to her?”","Losing a five-month-old fetus was a brutal blow to any woman, both physically and mentally. Second Madam did not much care for Mu Jiang, but she pitied the pregnant woman.","Without seeing Mu Jiang face to face, she was just a pitiable, unrelated pregnant woman, and Second Madam couldn’t help worrying that something had gone wrong.","“She’s bleeding again, and they can’t stop it,” Nanny Fan said.","Nanny Fan was efficient; she didn’t wait for the mistress to ask but learned all the particulars before reporting.","Cheng Zhao stood up: “I’ll fetch the token and tell the steward to call for a physician.”","She left.","A few days ago, Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the Capital Garrison camp. He had specially urged Cheng Zhao to be cautious in all matters and, for the first time, told her: “If anything goes wrong, go to the outer study to seek help.”","He added, “Mingyu can be trusted.”","Cheng Zhao sent someone to call the physician and then went to the Longevity Rest Courtyard.","The Dowager Madame did not receive her.","Nanny Sun came out and said to Cheng Zhao, “Third Young Madam, you should focus on your own affairs. The Dowager Madame knows you are filial.”","“I came to tell Grandmother that I won’t visit the Secondary Wife at the Beautiful View Courtyard. She’s in low spirits, and seeing me will only make her feel worse,” Cheng Zhao said.","She had come specifically to say this.","She did not flaunt her status as the victor by going to provoke Mu Jiang. Instead, she yielded and kept her distance.","In every aspect, she displayed her magnanimity—and she made a point of saying so, so that the servants also heard.","Cheng Zhao’s character was such that while she was sharp and formidable, she also had a certain compassionate discernment; yet if you called her pure-hearted, her thirst for authority was extremely strong.","Given her demeanor, even someone as worldly as Nanny Sun couldn’t help but take a second look at her.","In every way, she was a qualified matron of the household.","“The Secondary Wife wouldn’t be so petty,” Nanny Sun said flatly.","Cheng Zhao replied, “That may be, but perhaps I simply don’t please her. Even a beautiful flower can be disliked by some—it has nothing to do with being petty or generous.”","Nanny Sun was taken aback for a moment.","She said nothing more.","Time passed.","After the rites ceremony at the Hall of Reverence ended, First Madam Song still did not move back, but Huan Qingtang and Cheng Zhao began conducting business at the Hall of Reverence again.","As the Dragon Boat Festival approached, the steward of the storage asked, “Should the painted pleasure boat be launched? We need to prepare early.”","“Not this year,” Huan Qingtang said.","But the steward glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Huan Qingtang froze.","The steward, noticing her expression, smiled and explained, “I was wondering if the Dowager Madame might want to go for an outing.”","A knot formed in Huan Qingtang’s chest.","Questioning her words was a denial of her authority; looking to Cheng Zhao instead, as if seeking her opinion, showed that Cheng Zhao’s standing among the servants had already surpassed Huan Qingtang’s.","But Cheng Zhao did not say a word, as if she hadn’t even noticed the steward’s look.","We can discuss this matter another day, said Huan Qingtang."]},{"number":132,"title":"Chapter 132: Hello, Good Boy, Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["In the mid-afternoon, Cheng Zhao went to the Scarlet Cloud Court to wait for the evening meal.","Soon, her father-in-law and young brother-in-law Zhou Yuanqi returned.","The four of them chatted about trivial matters.","Zhou Yuanqi, still a child at heart, had recovered from his fright and reverted to his usual nature—pretending to be mature but unable to keep his mouth shut.","“...Can we go to Grandmother’s place to celebrate the Dragon Boat Festival this year?” he asked Second Madam.","On the Dragon Boat Festival, married daughters could return to their maternal homes to avoid the midday heat.","Second Madam didn’t go every year.","Sometimes the Dowager Madame was in high spirits and wanted to watch the dragon boat races, so everyone had to accompany her.","Every year, the dragon boat races on the Grand Canal were attended by the Emperor and Empress, making it the grandest event of the year, comparable to the Lantern Festival.","Second Madam usually returned to her maternal home for a while in the late afternoon and occasionally stayed overnight.","This year, the Emperor and Empress would certainly watch the races again; it was a perfect opportunity for the Emperor to share joy with the people. But the Dowager Madame might not be in the mood.","“Wait and see what the Longevity Court decides,” Second Madam said, to avoid being caught in a trap.","The Dowager Madame was simmering with anger, and anyone who fell into her hands would come to no good.","“Has the Longevity Court mentioned it yet?” Zhou Yuanqi asked Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao: “No. Today, the steward even asked about the pleasure boat.”","“Our family’s pleasure boat is especially large—three decks high, half a deck taller than Princess Fukangchang’s,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Such extravagance.","A picture of prosperity burning like oil.","She just smiled: “I haven’t seen it yet.”","“What were you doing before?” Zhou Yuanqi asked curiously. “Everyone in the capital knows about the Zhou family’s pleasure boat, and you’ve never seen it?”","The Zhou boat was launched on the river every year.","Second Madam also chuckled: “Yes, Zhao Zhao, you rarely socialize. People outside are very curious about you.”","Cheng Zhao then told them about her own situation.","She had lived with her father in Fengzhou for several years and had been forbidden by her mother to socialize, so during the time she should have been building her reputation, she remained obscure.","Then, because of her fourth brother Cheng Chao’s marriage complications, her mother planned to wait until she turned eighteen to arrange a match for her.","Just as they were preparing to build her up, the Empress granted her marriage to Zhou Yuanshen.","“...That’s what they call ‘a pearl hidden in the dust.’ Fortunately, our family was lucky enough to find you,” Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanqi nodded.","Though his tongue was sharp, he had a high opinion of his sister-in-law, and calling her a “pearl” was fitting.","Cheng Zhao couldn’t help but smile.","Second Master asked Cheng Zhao: “Is Ah Shen still at the Capital Garrison Camp?”","“Yes.”","“Did he say he’d come back for the festival?” Second Master asked again.","Cheng Zhao: “He didn’t mention it.”","Zhou Yuanqi started showing off his “knowledge” again: “Festivals are the best time to share joy with the troops. He’s sure to celebrate at the Capital Garrison Camp.”","“But he’s also the Grand Tutor to the Crown Prince. The Crown Prince will attend the dragon boat races, so he should be by his side like other Eastern Palace officials,” Second Master said.","Cheng Zhao responded: “The Lord Duke is never at a loss in official matters. He will have considered everything.”","After they said this, the next day—the first day of the fifth month—Zhou Yuanshen returned from the Capital Garrison Camp.","Early in the morning, he first came home to see his parents, met briefly with Cheng Zhao, and then hurried off to the government office.","“So busy—he’ll wear himself out,” Second Madam lamented about her son.","Cheng Zhao: “The Lord Duke looks quite energetic.”","Zhou Yuanshen was still young, and even after riding through the night to return to the city, his face showed no signs of fatigue.","Cheng Zhao went to the Bright Mirror Hall as usual to handle affairs.","First Madam, Lady Song, had already moved back. However, four maidservants and four sturdy serving women had been added to her courtyard.","The duties that Huan Qingtang had been managing were still handed over to First Madam.","The steward in charge of the storeroom asked again about the pleasure boat, because if no decision were made soon, it would be too late.","Launching a pleasure boat couldn’t be decided at the last minute; it required reporting to the authorities several days in advance and reserving a spot.","First Madam: “No pleasure boat this year.”","The steward agreed without glancing at anyone.","The atmosphere in the parlor was a bit tense, and First Madam noticed it.","During the midday meal, she kept a trusted stewardess behind and asked what was going on.","“...Is there something wrong with the pleasure boat?”","The stewardess repeated what had been said that day to First Madam.","After hearing it, First Madam gave a cold laugh and dismissed the stewardess.","She discussed it with her confidante.","“Lady Huan, during the concubine’s miscarriage, tried to ruin the Second Branch by kicking someone when they were down. That’s already known to everyone.”","When someone had a fault and had lost real power, their prestige took a major hit.","If Lady Huan had held seventy percent authority in the stewards’ eyes before, now it was only fifty percent.","She couldn’t suppress Cheng Zhao.","First Madam now found Lady Huan more annoying than Cheng Zhao.","“Madam, if you and the eldest daughter-in-law keep fighting like this, Lady Cheng will be the one to reap the benefits,” her trusted stewardess said.","“Don’t lose the big picture over a small matter.”","First Madam understood this well, but she had already made up her mind in her heart.","Hatred and favor are very intense emotions. In public, they can be hidden; in private, how can they be controlled?","Cheng Zhao was an outsider, and First Madam had never liked her.","But Lady Huan was originally one of her own, yet she had betrayed her. An ally turned enemy—that hatred burns much fiercer than hatred for a simple foe.","First Madam even thought that she would rather benefit Cheng Zhao than hand the Bright Mirror Hall over to Lady Huan.","After the midday meal, First Madam took a short nap and returned to manage affairs in the afternoon. Then Cheng Zhao’s maidservant arrived.","“...Third Young Mistress is feeling a bit unwell. She said that the day’s tasks at the main kitchen and the gatehouse are done, and she would like to have the afternoon off,” the maidservant said.","First Madam frowned.","She said nothing.","She didn’t even offer a perfunctory “Should we call a physician?”","Cheng Zhao returned to the Silk Clouds Court, because Zhou Yuanshen had come back to the mansion after lunch.","He said he hadn’t bathed in days and that the camp was inconvenient, so Cheng Zhao ordered the maids to prepare hot water.","In the bath chamber, he called Cheng Zhao in to help him, asking her to scrub his back and wash his hair.","Cheng Zhao was wary that he might get up to mischief, but he seemed tired and quietly closed his eyes to doze off.","After washing his hair, Cheng Zhao noticed the scar on his shoulder.","It looked like a wolf’s head without eyes.","She couldn’t help her curiosity and reached out to touch it, but Zhou Yuanshen caught her hand.","“It’s a bit ticklish,” he said in a slow, deep voice.","Cheng Zhao immediately pulled her hand back.","But she forgot he might strike unexpectedly. Before she could dodge, his long arm wrapped around her waist.","Cheng Zhao fell into the water, still fully clothed and wearing her shoes.","Warm water, a searing-hot man—she couldn’t help shivering.","“Zhou Yuanshen...”","Before she could finish, his lips covered hers.","Her wet clothes and shoes were thrown aside, and Cheng Zhao ended up sitting in his lap.","Zhou Yuanshen held her waist with one hand and cupped the back of her head with the other as he kissed her, nearly suffocating her.","Later, more than half the water in the wooden tub had splashed out.","Cheng Zhao lay limply in his arms, too exhausted to lift her head, leaning against his shoulder.","He turned his face slightly and kissed her lips again.","“You’re being so good, Cheng Zhao,” he murmured softly.","Cheng Zhao was so angry she wanted to jump up, but he held her fast.","It was only because, in the heat of passion, he had whispered to her, “Put in some effort too,” and she had complied, using the opportunity.","Yet this man wasn’t grateful and instead called her “good.”","He was taking advantage, pushing his luck!"]},{"number":133,"title":"Chapter 133: Peeping","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao was exhausted.","Zhou Yuanshen held her in his arms, dressed her, and dried her hair.","“... Bring the hair-drying hood in,” he instructed the maid.","Cheng Zhao wanted to refuse. It was May, the weather was warm, and drying hair required a brazier, which would be too hot and dry.","It had been a while since their indulgence earlier, yet Cheng Zhao’s body was still burning; she couldn’t bear the heat of the brazier.","“Don’t bother. I’ll sleep for a while first,” she said.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “You’ll get a headache.”","Cheng Zhao was speechless.","Now you know it’ll cause a headache—why didn’t you think of that when you were messing around in the bathtub?","You were practically done washing, couldn’t you have waited to leave the tub and go back to the room?","Cheng Zhao shot him a sideways glare.","His eyes were bright, and he lightly brushed her brow with his fingers before kissing her cheek.","Cheng Zhao felt the futility of throwing sharp glances at a blind man—he wasn’t intimidated by her, so she simply closed her eyes.","After a brief rest, she dozed off in his arms for a while, then woke up clear-headed.","Her hair was still half-dry.","The sunset had slipped past the treetops, and night had slowly blackened the sky. The bedroom was unlit, the light dim and shadowy.","Cheng Zhao rose from Zhou Yuanshen’s embrace.","“... Shall we go to Crimson Cloud Courtyard for dinner?” she asked.","Her in-laws surely had a thousand questions for Zhou Yuanshen.","Ever since Concubine Mu’s miscarriage, there had been upheavals at home and in the court. Her mother-in-law, in particular, was full of doubts.","“We’ll go tomorrow evening,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","“It’s still early,” Cheng Zhao said. “No need to put it off until tomorrow night.”","“Cheng Zhao.”","She looked at him, puzzled.","His dark eyes sparkled, bright with suppressed laughter. “In your current state, my parents will notice. Don’t get mad later.”","Cheng Zhao was stunned.","She shot him a resentful glare, got up, and looked at herself in the mirror.","She seemed to have a languid, rosy glow—a kind of contentment and satisfaction after a good meal. In simple terms, her face was radiant with the flush of romance.","Cheng Zhao remembered him calling her “good girl” earlier.","She flicked the curtain heavily and went to change clothes.","They had dinner just the two of them.","Cheng Zhao had nearly forgotten in her exhaustion, but now she recalled being called “good girl,” so she put on a stern face, trying to reclaim her dignity.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t laugh, but his brows were relaxed, and his eyes stayed overly bright.","Behind his calm exterior, Cheng Zhao felt he was laughing to himself.","This only irritated her more.","This man was even more infuriating than her fourth brother, Cheng Chao, and even more eager to take charge of her—though their personalities were worlds apart.","During dinner, Zhou Yuanshen was hungry. He didn’t wolf down his food, but he ate with relish, which also whetted Cheng Zhao’s appetite.","She ended up overeating again.","Strolling in the courtyard, Zhou Yuanshen walked beside her. Dismissing the maids, he carried a small horn lantern for Cheng Zhao.","“... This year, the duke’s mansion won’t launch the painted boat for the Dragon Boat Festival,” Cheng Zhao said, bringing him up to date on family matters, her tone not particularly good.","Whether noble clans or commoners, everyone would be watching the Zhou family.","Even small changes could spark endless speculation.","Perhaps this was exactly what the Dowager Madame wanted: first, playing the victim by holding a memorial ceremony, and then, with the unusual behavior at the Dragon Boat Festival, airing the duke’s mansion’s grievances.","To salvage their reputation. To keep the nobility and commoners from branding them as “tyrants who forced a physician to death.”","For a high-ranking house to be linked with “oppressive power” would not last long.","They needed to spread an image of noble integrity, charitable deeds, and scholarly virtue to gain a good name and ensure the family’s longevity.","This wasn’t something Cheng Zhao just made up; it was recorded in historical texts.","The Dowager Madame had surely read many histories and received a traditional, scholarly upbringing. She was particularly infatuated with the concept of “pure nobility.”","“A truly wise decision,” Cheng Zhao remarked.","Even in her madness, the grandmother still had the wits to orchestrate things—that was the mark of a true old matriarch. If she were a little more fair-minded, she would be an elder worthy of admiration.","“These are all empty,” Zhou Yuanshen said lightly. “As long as she refuses to settle, the duke’s mansion will eventually collapse.”","He added, “The next dynasty’s landscape is something she cannot dictate.”","Cheng Zhao felt that the turmoil of the late emperor’s final years had seeded too much fear in the Dowager Madame.","She had been forced to leave the capital’s prosperity and endure eight bitter years on the frontier.","Eight years—long enough to grow thorns in a person’s heart. Cheng Zhao couldn’t fathom it.","She was only eighteen. She had lived too short a life; eight years seemed like an eternity, like a long “half-life.”","The Dowager Madame’s sons, because of those eight years, had taken the path of military officers, defying her expectations for them.","All her life’s plans had been disrupted.","Fortunately, she later managed to “correct the course,” and many things turned out as she wished.","Her only misfortune, perhaps, was having too few descendants. When the time came to rely on someone, only that stubborn Zhou Yuanshen could inherit the title.","Cheng Zhao also wanted to hold tight to what she had now. She could understand the Dowager Madame; she also saw herself reflected in the old woman and used that to reflect on her own actions.","But could holding tight truly secure it?","At this thought, Cheng Zhao shuddered.","She recalled the physician from Wu Commandery who had taken her pulse and said to her, “Young Miss, let water carry the floating log—just let yourself be carried.”","Perhaps life was like that.","What you tried to grasp would ultimately prove futile...","“Who’s there?” Zhou Yuanshen suddenly spoke, cutting off Cheng Zhao’s thoughts.","He strode toward the bushes nearby, and soon a woman’s sharp, fearful cry rang out: “Lord Duke, spare me!”","The voice was very young, like a little maid’s.","Cheng Zhao walked over and saw a young maid pinned to the ground by Zhou Yuanshen’s foot.","She had been skulking, following them all the way.","Zhou Yuanshen shined the horn lantern on her: “You’re from Glorious View Courtyard.”","His tone was certain.","Cheng Zhao remembered that he spent more nights at Glorious View Courtyard and knew it better—despite all the mysteries surrounding Concubine Mu’s pregnancy.","“Lord Duke, I was sent on orders. The Secondary Wife wants to see you. She says she’s dying,” the little maid said, terrified.","She seemed about to be crushed by the weight on her, gasping for air as she spoke.","“After the physician took the Secondary Wife’s pulse, did you fill the prescription for her?” Cheng Zhao asked.","The maid pleaded, “Young Madam, please save me!”","Cheng Zhao said, “Let her go first.”","Zhou Yuanshen lifted his foot, and the maid scrambled to her knees, kowtowed in gratitude to Cheng Zhao, and waited for her questions, crouching low.","“... What medicine was prescribed?” Cheng Zhao asked.","“I don’t know, miss. Nanny An is the one serving the Secondary Wife.”","Cheng Zhao recalled that Glorious View Courtyard had two palace matrons assigned there.","Back when Concubine Mu was pregnant, the Dowager Madame was overjoyed and immediately reported it to the emperor. The emperor was delighted, showered Concubine Mu with gifts, and ordered the empress to send two matrons to attend to her.","Imperial rewards and the empress’s appointees...","At the time, everyone in the duke’s mansion said Concubine Mu was being treated like a “princess.”","Cheng Zhao was young and had seen little of the ugliness in human affairs. Limited by her experience, she hadn’t thought much of it.","Now, looking back, her heart chilled as she glanced at Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen’s face was expressionless.","“What about those two palace matrons? Aren’t they attending to the Secondary Wife?” Cheng Zhao asked further.","The maid said, “They went back to the palace to report and never returned.”","Then, as if trying to earn favor, she added to Cheng Zhao, “I was gathering the Secondary Wife’s clothes for a change. Her bleeding still hasn’t stopped.”","Cheng Zhao frowned."]},{"number":134,"title":"Chapter 134: Great Uproar in Nonghua Court","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen let the maid go.","He gave no instructions.","The maid scrambled to her feet and fled. When she returned to Lijing Courtyard, Mu Jiang had been waiting for her report and immediately summoned her inside.","\"Did you see the Lord Duke?\" Mu Jiang asked.","Her complexion was terrible.","Not gaunt, but swollen.","She couldn’t eat well, couldn’t sleep well, and would periodically break into loud crying and tantrums. The almost-stopped vaginal bleeding had somehow increased again.","Her temper was utterly foul.","The day before, when the maid on duty served her tea, she insisted it was too hot, grabbed the horsewhip by her bedside, and struck the maid, leaving her bleeding profusely.","Today, that maid was nowhere to be seen, her fate unknown.","The young maid was terrified of Mu Jiang. Trembling, she knelt and truthfully told her what had just happened.","When Mu Jiang heard that Zhou Yuanshen and Cheng Zhao had been walking together to aid digestion and that he refused to see her, rage consumed her. Snatching up the whip, she lashed the maid: \"Useless thing! I might as well kill you!\"","The maid crumpled on the ground, not daring to dodge.","Even Nanny An, the steward, dared not intervene; she held little authority over Mu Jiang.","After a few lashes, Mu Jiang burst into tears, drowning in self-pity. She felt utterly pitiful, utterly miserable.","She wanted to see Zhou Yuanshen.","Throwing down the blood-streaked whip, she ran out, losing one of her shoes.","The maids and Nanny An scrambled after her, yet they dared not restrain her. The previous day’s dead maid had been covered up.","Lijing Courtyard was the residence of the \"Secondary Wife,\" and the household’s masters would never antagonize the Dowager Madame over a servant. A dead maid was a dead maid, no consequence.","Thus, when Mu Jiang went into her frenzy, no one dared risk their lives to stop her.","\"Secondary Wife, your body is frail; you shouldn’t run like this. It’s already night,\" Nanny An said, almost weeping. \"The inner courtyards are all locked.\"","Mu Jiang paid no heed.","She charged to Nonghua Courtyard, only to find its gate bolted shut. The lanterns at the entrance cast a faint, dismal glow.","The May night breeze was not cold, yet Mu Jiang felt chilled to the bone, as if frozen through.","She pounded on the gate: \"Third Brother! Third Brother!\"","Inside Nonghua Courtyard, the maids had been washing up and tidying. Though not rowdy, some noise had lingered.","At the pounding, all sound ceased. The courtyard fell into a deathly hush.","Cheng Zhao had already changed into her undergarments and was sitting on the heated brick bed by the window, combing her hair, ready to sleep. Hearing the commotion, she glanced at Zhou Yuanshen, who sat nearby perusing a chess manual.","Zhou Yuanshen’s brow did not stir, his face utterly impassive.","\"...I’ll go see,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"Let her call.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"After all, it concerns our household. It’s too unseemly and won’t do either of us any good. Servants love to gossip.\"","Secrets within the Duke’s mansion could even leak beyond its walls.","\"As long as they call her ‘Secondary Wife,’ she has nothing to do with us,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","You even recognize the maids of Lijing Courtyard and share a past with her. How lightly you speak of ‘nothing to do with us’?","Is it because of that child, which made you feel betrayed?","Cheng Zhao wished to stay out of it.","But Mu Jiang was pounding on Nonghua Courtyard’s gate—Cheng Zhao’s courtyard. Any gossip would inevitably drag Cheng Zhao in.","\"Let your maid handle it,\" Zhou Yuanshen said again.","Cheng Zhao also didn’t want to see Mu Jiang.","She knew Mu Jiang was now unhinged.","Losing a five-month fetus had ravaged Mu Jiang’s body and mind, making her temper explosively volatile.","In her agony, she didn’t just torment herself; she lashed out at everyone around her, including Cheng Zhao.","If Mu Jiang turned violent, Cheng Zhao would not yield.","A fight between wife and concubine would be like fine jade striking a rough stone. Win or lose, Cheng Zhao’s prestige would be damaged.","And the only one to benefit would be Huan Qingtang.","Cheng Zhao could contend with machinations and schemes, but she wouldn’t court a berserk madwoman.","\"Suyue,\" Cheng Zhao called to her maid.","Suyue stood at the door: \"Young Madam, I’ll go have a look.\"","\"Don’t let her in,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Don’t let yourself be hurt, and don’t touch her.\"","If she accidentally touched Mu Jiang and the Dowager Madame was angered, Cheng Zhao’s maids’ lives would be forfeit.","Suyue acknowledged the order.","\"Qiubai,\" Cheng Zhao called again. \"Come in.\"","The bedroom door opened, and Qiubai entered noiselessly.","Cheng Zhao spoke in a low voice, instructing her to slip out over the wall.","Qiubai acknowledged.","The two maids went about their tasks.","Nanny Li stood at the door and asked Cheng Zhao: \"Young Madam, shall I bring more tea?\"","\"Come in,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li entered with tea and whispered: \"The Secondary Wife’s bleeding hasn’t stopped. If she makes such a scene and something goes wrong, we can’t bear the responsibility.\"","Her implication was that Cheng Zhao should persuade Zhou Yuanshen to go out and calm Mu Jiang.","A concubine was hardly that important, but the Dowager Madame was.","Cheng Zhao understood Nanny Li’s meaning but shook her head: \"It’s fine. She started this trouble, not us. I’ve kept my distance from her.\"","Since Mu Jiang’s miscarriage, Cheng Zhao had avoided meeting her, precisely to prevent the Dowager Madame from blaming her in case Mu Jiang lost control.","Nanny Li said no more and withdrew.","Cheng Zhao listened to the noise outside.","Zhou Yuanshen slowly turned a page of his chess manual, studying it intently, as if deaf to the uproar.","Outside, Mu Jiang’s cries grew more piercing and anguished, punctuated by the maids and servants’ frantic pleas.","Suyue blocked Mu Jiang from entering. When she went out, she had ordered two rough-service matrons to stand behind the courtyard gate, ready to slam it shut once she stepped out.","When Mu Jiang tried to push through, Suyue blocked her, and Mu Jiang scratched at Suyue.","Suyue couldn’t evade properly and took a stinging scratch on her cheek.","Soon, more people gathered.","It was early evening; many in the inner courtyards were still awake. The crowd served as witnesses.","Mu Jiang desperately tried to grab Suyue; Suyue dodged behind the maids and servants of Lijing Courtyard, shielding herself.","Several maids were pinched by Mu Jiang.","\"How dare you cause such chaos!\" came the voice of Second Madam.","Cheng Zhao shot to her feet.","Zhou Yuanshen’s eyes finally lifted from the chess manual.","The couple walked out of the courtyard.","Cheng Zhao approached Second Madam: \"Mother, what brings you here so late?\"","\"With her screaming like this, the whole household will know by tomorrow, and then we’ll be blamed!\" Second Madam fumed. \"Send people to tie her up and take her back!\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"Men, tie her up.\"","Nanny An knelt before Zhou Yuanshen: \"Lord Duke, you mustn’t! The Secondary Wife’s body can’t endure such treatment!\"","\"If you don’t stop her and let her rage, that’s how she ends up like this. It seems she’s clouded by phlegm delusion,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"Tomorrow, call a physician to give her medicine.\"","\"I’m not mad!\" Mu Jiang wailed. \"Third Brother, our son is gone—Cheng Zhao harmed me!\"","Second Madam’s flicker of pity vanished without a trace. Pitying Mu Jiang was truly a pointless sentimentality.","\"She who harmed you has long been determined. Don’t slander the Duke’s wife!\" Second Madam snapped.","\"It was her! I know it was her! She didn’t want me to bear the eldest son!\" Mu Jiang cried.","Second Madam was sorely tempted to slap her.","\"Someone, tie her up and send her to Shou’an Courtyard,\" Zhou Yuanshen ordered.","The two coarse serving women of Nonghua Courtyard immediately brought out ropes and firmly pressed Mu Jiang down, binding her tightly.","She was still struggling.","She hurled curses at Cheng Zhao with all her might.","Cheng Zhao thought, \"She is certain it was me.\"","Mu Jiang's hatred required a clear, specific target, and someone had confirmed her suspicions, so Cheng Zhao became that scapegoat."]},{"number":135,"title":"Chapter 135: Punishing the Concubine","paragraphs":["Mu Jiang caused a tremendous uproar in Nonghua Courtyard in the middle of the night.","She hurled curses at Cheng Zhao.","When Zhou Yuanshen reached out to steady her, she suddenly lost her voice. She struggled violently, her hair completely disheveled, her face flushed red, yet she could not utter a single word.","Her eyes were fixed on Zhou Yuanshen.","In that moment, the hatred she felt for Zhou Yuanshen matched almost equally the depth and intensity of her hatred for Cheng Zhao.","Old Madam Li came out of the house and stuffed a cloth into Mu Jiang's mouth.","A small carriage arrived swiftly.","The First Madam and her daughter-in-law had arrived.","\"Eldest Aunt, you have finally come,\" Cheng Zhao stepped forward as if with great warmth. \"I truly did not know what to do with such a disturbance from the Secondary Wife.\"","Second Madam paused.","Her daughter-in-law was quite clever, immediately shifting the responsibility upon arrival. Such matters ought to be handled by the head of the household. Her daughter-in-law had performed admirably.","Qiu Bai, Cheng Zhao's maidservant, had gone to Chengming Hall to wake the First Madam, who had already retired for the night. The First Madam then sent for Huan Qingtang.","Thanks to Qiu Bai's swift actions, the First Madam was delayed only briefly.","\"The Lord Duke is here, so we shall follow the Lord Duke's orders,\" said First Madam, née Song.","\"Send her to the family temple,\" Zhou Yuanshen said coldly. \"Creating a disturbance in the dead of night, utterly without discipline. Let her rest for half a year. If she still shows no remorse, she need not return.\"","Mu Jiang, who had inexplicably lost her voice moments ago, erupted in rage again upon hearing this. She struggled with all her might against the old women restraining her, wanting to argue with Zhou Yuanshen.","How utterly muddled he was!","How could he be so completely swayed by Cheng Zhao, to strike her down like this?","Even ignoring the child she had carried for him, a child that was now gone, did the authority of the Dowager Madame and the Emperor mean nothing to him?","Did he want to throw away his own future?","To vent Cheng Zhao's anger, was he willing to abandon not only her but also himself?","Mu Jiang was in agony.","He was the one whose mind was clouded with phlegm!","In pain and desperation, Mu Jiang wept. \"Third Brother!\"","A faint sound escaped her throat, slightly better than before.","But the First Madam spoke, drowning out Mu Jiang's cry. \"Yuanshen, this matter should be raised with your grandmother first.\"","Second Madam immediately countered. \"Is the Lord Duke not authorized to decide? Eldest Sister-in-law, is that your attitude toward the head of the household?\"","The First Madam sneered. \"Sister-in-law, there is no need to split hairs. I know your son is the Lord Duke, and from now on we must all kneel when speaking to him.\"","She turned back to Zhou Yuanshen. \"Am I not still your elder?\"","Zhou Yuanshen replied, \"You are an elder. But elders must act with dignity to commands respect, Eldest Aunt. You disregard my orders and show contempt for my mother. What aspect of that is worthy of respect?\"","The First Madam was left speechless.","She was stunned for a moment, her face turning ugly from embarrassment. She turned her anger on Cheng Zhao. \"Since this is a matter within your chambers, why did you, as the Duchess, summon me?\"","Everyone chose the softest target to bully.","Second Madam moved a step forward, shielding Cheng Zhao behind her. \"Calling Eldest Sister-in-law here is naturally to obtain the authorization records for this matter. Are you suggesting the Lord Duke himself must escort the Secondary Wife to the family temple?\"","The First Madam nearly choked with fury.","She had been rebutted twice in a row, each time leaving her speechless. And one of those rebuttals came from a sister-in-law she had long dominated.","Huan Qingtang stepped forward and spoke. \"Since the Lord Duke has made his decision, let it be done. We can inform Grandmother about it later.\"","The First Madam turned her full wrath toward her. \"What business is this of yours? Are you also part of the Lord Duke's household?\"","Huan Qingtang slowly raised her eyes to meet her mother-in-law's gaze. Her expression did not waver, nor did she flinch. \"Mother, you are the one who presides over Chengming Hall.\"","\"Then you intend to replace me? Did I authorize you to replace me?\" the First Madam demanded angrily.","The maidservants and older serving women gathered—not only those from Nonghua Courtyard or Jiangyun Courtyard but also others who had arrived from elsewhere—all stared at the First Madam in surprise.","She was publicly humiliating her own daughter-in-law.","And the Eldest Young Madame seemed to have no grounds for defense. She was neither the titled lady of Chengming Hall nor was she the Lord Duke's legal wife. She held no position of authority in this household.","Everyone felt embarrassed for her.","Huan Qingtang merely said, \"Mother, please calm down. Do not harm your health over this.\"","She showed no sign of offense, only concern for her mother-in-law.","It appeared as though the First Madam, having been cornered and humiliated herself, had simply taken out her anger on an innocent. The onlookers abandoned any thought of mocking Huan Qingtang. Instead, they felt the First Madam had lost her temper for no reason.","The First Madam grew even more furious.","In the midst of this chaotic scene, another small carriage arrived.","Cheng Zhao saw it. She gently patted Second Madam's hand, then let go and walked toward the carriage.","Old Madam Sun descended first.","Cheng Zhao helped her escort the Dowager Madame out of the carriage.","Huan Qingtang quickly came over to pay her respects to the Dowager Madame. \"Why have we disturbed you at such a late hour, Grandmother?\"","As Huan Qingtang approached, the Dowager Madame seemed about to shake free from Cheng Zhao's supporting hand.","Before she could act, Cheng Zhao released her and moved to stand behind Second Madam.","\"What is going on?\" The Dowager Madame raised her hand, pointing at Mu Jiang.","Mu Jiang wanted to call out to her, but she was tied up, pinned down, and still gagged.","Even with the Dowager Madame's arrival, the old women from the Nonghua Courtyard did not release Mu Jiang.","These two women were part of Cheng Zhao's dowry retinue.","Previously, the Nonghua Courtyard had been staffed with a few members of the Zhou household's servants, but Cheng Zhao had found excuses to send them all away. Only a few were left for rough work, all of whom had to return to the servants' quarters in the back courtyard by nightfall. The maids and older women who could spend the night in the Nonghua Courtyard were all Cheng Zhao's trusted confidantes.","It was a tightly controlled fortress here.","Seeing that they treated Mu Jiang with such contempt, their hands not loosening even in her presence, the Dowager Madame's brow furrowed almost imperceptibly.","\"Grandmother, the Secondary Wife suddenly came here,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"She reviled both me and the Lord Duke. I was worried something might happen to her, as she had just lost a baby girl. Her health was fragile, which is why the child was born with deficiencies.","If she continues this way, something awful could happen. Since she was raised by you, Grandmother, we dared not make decisions for her. That is why I called for Eldest Aunt.\"","She continued, \"Eldest Aunt asked the Lord Duke for a decision. The Lord Duke ordered her sent to the family temple. Then Eldest Aunt objected.","She insisted we first ask for your opinion. All of this descended into chaos. I am sorry for troubling you, Grandmother.\"","Mu Jiang struggled with all her might to make a sound.","Cheng Zhao's words reached not only her own ears but those of everyone present.","The Dowager Madame gave Cheng Zhao a long, hard look. \"Very well. Withdraw.\"","Cheng Zhao complied, her manner utterly respectful. She did not take the Dowager Madame's attitude to heart; she had said all she needed to say.","The Dowager Madame fell silent.","The courtyard grew quiet.","Even Mu Jiang ceased her struggles, looking at the Dowager Madame with tear-filled eyes.","\"...Yuanshen?\" the Dowager Madame finally spoke.","She did not press further, merely calling his name.","\"Grandmother,\" Zhou Yuanshen said, his voice cold and distant. \"If Concubine Mu remains in this household, she will be constantly incited and manipulated by others. Her life will be in danger. Sending her away might make her see reason.\"","He paused then added, \"This agitation would take a toll on even a strong person. If anything truly happens...\"","He looked at the Dowager Madame, his words trailing off as if unfinished.","The Dowager Madame understood him.","Another long silence followed.","Everyone waited for the Dowager Madame's final decision.","\"Send her to the family temple,\" the Dowager Madame said. She turned her gaze to the dazed Mu Jiang. \"Her mind is indeed clouded. If she cannot learn to think clearly, then let her not return.\""]},{"number":136,"title":"Chapter 136: The Mistress of the Duke's Mansion Can Only Be Her","paragraphs":["Mu Jiang wept tears like rain, quietly gazing at the Dowager Madame.","In that fleeting look, anyone could see the thick hatred; hatred mixed with tears, both heart-wrenchingly painful and venomously malicious.","In the depth of the night, it sent chills down one’s spine.","She no longer struggled as she was dragged away.","There were dark stains on her dress, as if blood had seeped through from beneath.","The Dowager Madame boarded a small oiled cart, supported by Zhou Yuanshen.","“Yuanshen, come here,” the Dowager Madame beckoned to him.","He followed her to Shou’an Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao said to Second Madam, “It’s late, Mother. You should go back and rest.”","Second Madam held her hand. “Don’t be afraid.”","“Yes,” Cheng Zhao replied.","Only then did Second Madam turn and leave.","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law of the eldest branch took a small oiled cart and returned to Chengming Hall.","Huan Qingtang helped the First Madam inside, and the First Madam did not push her away, though she certainly showed no kindness.","“The Concubine is gone. How will Lijing Courtyard be arranged?” the First Madam seemed to ponder, as if asking Huan Qingtang.","Huan Qingtang replied, “The Concubine will surely return, Mother. If Grandmother and the Lord Duke wanted her dead, they wouldn’t keep reminding her of things.”","She also recalled the Emperor killing someone in Chengming Hall...","Huan Qingtang felt that Mu Jiang still had plenty of life left. She wouldn’t die until the very last moment, unless the Dowager Madame found her worthless.","If Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen wanted to get rid of Mu Jiang, they’d have to rely on their own abilities.","The First Madam saw none of this, and Huan Qingtang wouldn’t say more.","“It won’t be easy for her to return. Cheng Shi is no pushover,” the First Madam said.","Saying this, she looked at Huan Qingtang with a half-smile. “Cheng Shi holds the title of Duchess of the Duke’s residence. She’s not easy to deal with. Fortunately, I have it too.”","Huan Qingtang did not.","Cheng Zhao could easily target Huan Qingtang; but the First Madam, being both a titled lady and an elder, had the advantage when facing Cheng Zhao.","First Madam Song’s words didn’t seem intended to win Huan Qingtang over—they were purely meant to provoke her.","She used words to needle and belittle her.","Huan Qingtang looked up at her. “Mother, you are the mistress of Chengming Hall...”","“I think you’re the one who wants to be the mistress of Chengming Hall! You pride yourself on your youth and beauty, and your petty cleverness. You think the Lord Duke will be charmed by you and disregard me,” First Madam said coldly.","Huan Qingtang had been treated poorly everywhere that night.","She looked at the First Madam without changing her expression, her cheeks trembling slightly, but her words came slowly and respectfully: “Mother, I have no such intention. You will understand my heart in time.”","“In time?” the First Madam sneered. “In time, who will be sitting here is still uncertain. Don’t think that just because you’re young, you’ll outlast me. You’re still too green.”","Having vented her anger from Second Madam and Cheng Zhao all on Huan Qingtang, she felt much better.","Huan Qingtang walked out of Chengming Hall, giving only a helpless smile.","She turned and glanced back at the gate of Chengming Hall, taking in its solemn grandeur. How could the mistress of this place lack tolerance? The Duchess of the Duke’s residence would only be her.","She could endure these slanders and cold stares.","In the future, children, grandchildren, servants, and clan members who had branched out would all surround her, flattering her just as they did the Dowager Madame.","As her mother-in-law had said, she was young, beautiful, and brilliant.","She walked away with light steps.","Mu Jiang had lost her child and been sent to the family shrine. The power struggles in this residence were now clearer and more defined.","From now on, she was the only blade in the Dowager Madame’s hand.","Just as Zhou Yuanshen was the Emperor’s blade.","She would gain more from this.","—Would the blade ever turn on its master?","The one most like Zhou Yuanshen should be her; and with Mu Jiang’s miscarriage, she finally had some respect for Zhou Yuanshen.","He truly was unlike ordinary people.","She returned to Cuiyun Courtyard.","The lights in Nonghua Courtyard blazed brightly.","Cheng Zhao called Suyue to her side and carefully applied medicinal powder to her injured hand.","There was a break on Suyue’s hand.","“It doesn’t hurt, just a small wound,” Suyue insisted.","Cheng Zhao said, “Rest for a few days. Don’t work until the wound scabs over.”","Suyue assented.","Nanny Li, Qiubai, and Suyue gathered around Cheng Zhao, discussing the commotion from earlier.","They wondered, “Will the Concubine ever return?”","Cheng Zhao thought she would. Neither the Dowager Madame nor Zhou Yuanshen had intended for her to die.","But these were not things to share with anyone, not even her confidants.","An hour later, Zhou Yuanshen returned from Shou’an Courtyard.","The attendants withdrew. Cheng Zhao helped him remove his outer clothes, and the couple went to bed.","“What did Grandmother say?” Cheng Zhao asked him quickly.","“She told me not to touch Concubine Mu, to spare her life,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Basically, it was a threat.”","There were too many things the Dowager Madame could use to threaten Zhou Yuanshen.","“The greater situation matters most,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing.","He lay down and covered the bedside lamp. “This Dragon Boat Festival, we’ll go to the maternal family’s home. Too many things have happened lately. You and Mother need some peace.”","“What about you?”","“I’ll watch the dragon boat races with the Emperor and the Crown Prince in the morning, and then head to my maternal family’s home in the afternoon. We’ll stay overnight,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","He added, “You all can go watch the dragon boat races too.”","“I’ll ask Mother,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen grunted in the dark.","Then he asked, “Cheng Zhao, would you like to visit the General of the Western Pacification’s mansion?”","Cheng Zhao thought it was neither here nor there.","But since he brought it up, it wouldn’t hurt to go see.","“When?”","“After the Dragon Boat Festival,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “There are several pomegranate trees, and the pomegranate flowers are blooming beautifully.”","“I really like pomegranate flowers,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen hummed in acknowledgment.","Early the next morning, Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen got up and went to Second Madam’s Jiangyun Courtyard.","Zhou Yuanqi had arrived earlier and was asking about the previous night’s events.","He had missed all the excitement.","“...She is utterly despicable,” Second Madam said, gnashing her teeth about Mu Jiang.","When she saw Cheng Zhao, her anger immediately subsided. “It’s still Zhaozhao who is clever. By talking to the Dowager Madame, you made her realize the truth about the Concubine’s miscarriage.”","Zhou Yuanqi curled his lip. “She might not believe it. She’ll surely think Third Sister-in-law is lying on purpose.”","“Who cares whether she believes it? All that matters is that the servants do. No one harmed her—she got rid of the child herself,” Second Madam said.","She added, “How much trouble has she stirred up since getting pregnant?”","She had never let up.","Second Master listened quietly, then looked at Zhou Yuanshen, who was sitting nearby. “What did your grandmother say?”","“Grandmother said the environment at the family shrine is peaceful, which will do Concubine Mu good. She’s told her to stay there for a while,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Master said, “That’s not a good thing.”","He gave Zhou Yuanshen a meaningful look. “Lijing Courtyard is empty now.”","The Dowager Madame had many ways to keep Zhou Yuanshen in check.","Once, she had rewarded him with many beauties and sent him soup regularly to maintain her image as a kind matriarch.","That “soup-delivering” had been forced to stop after First Madam Song drugged it; Lijing Courtyard then became the Dowager Madame’s sacred ground for displaying her affection for her grandson.","But with Mu Jiang gone, Zhou Yuanshen had an excuse not to go to Lijing Courtyard. How could the Dowager Madame accept that?","Thus, Lijing Courtyard might soon host someone new.","Next to Lijing Courtyard was Yujin Courtyard, which could also accommodate a concubine.","“Father, I have it under control,” Zhou Yuanshen said blandly.","Cheng Zhao looked up at the father and son, then lowered her gaze."]},{"number":137,"title":"Chapter 137: Encountering the Prince","paragraphs":["The weather was pleasant just before the Dragon Boat Festival.","Both sides of the South Gate River were lined with streets, usually bustling with activity, and during festivals they were thronged with tourists.","Two or three months earlier, the best restaurant seats on the west bank had all been booked; Fan Xiao had long since secured the finest vantage spots for everyone.","There was much to do at the Duke of Chen’s mansion.","Gift-giving for the festival, social engagements everywhere—the gatekeepers and the main kitchen were all very busy.","The relationship between the mother- and daughter-in-law in the Hall of Bright Heritage was tense, making many tasks difficult, and Cheng Zhao and the stewards found themselves caught in the middle.","“Third Young Madam, why don’t you speak to the Dowager Madame and tell her not to let the Eldest Young Madam come to the Hall of Bright Heritage?” one steward suggested privately to Cheng Zhao.","His meaning was clear: The Hall of Bright Heritage had nothing to do with Huan Qingtang, and Eldest Madam Song disliked her.","With her around, Eldest Madam Song was always losing her temper, delaying several matters.","If things weren’t done properly later, the steward would be blamed. He might not only be scolded but also beaten or even dismissed.","Cheng Zhao glanced at the steward. “Steward Sheng, your words are rather pointless.”","Then she added, “Go about your business now.”","The steward wanted to say more, but meeting Cheng Zhao’s gaze, he bowed. “Yes, Third Young Madam.”","Cheng Zhao, Suyue, and Qiubai headed back.","Suyue asked quietly, “Was he sent by the Dowager Madame to test you?”","“Probably not. He’s always been straightforward.”","“Around the New Year, the Eldest Young Madam’s authority outweighed yours. In just a few months, the situation has shifted dramatically,” Suyue said.","She wanted to be happy for Cheng Zhao but dared not show it.","Cheng Zhao, however, was less optimistic than Suyue. “Elder Sister-in-law still has her uses. Now that Eldest Aunt is trampling on her like this, it will likely anger the Dowager Madame soon.”","This had happened before.","On the third day of the fifth month, Cheng Zhao was having dinner at the Crimson Cloud Courtyard when Second Madam mentioned the festival.","“Zhao Zhao, are you going back to avoid the midday heat?” Second Madam asked her.","Cheng Zhao replied, “I’ll return tomorrow to deliver festival gifts and pay respects to Grandfather and my parents; on the day itself, I’ll go to the Fan family.”","She added with a smile, “My maternal family’s home is fun and lively, and I like it very much. My aunts treat me well, too.”","The Cheng family had many members; one person’s absence during such a festive and bustling time meant little. Besides, Cheng Zhao hadn’t married far away and could go back anytime; one day didn’t matter.","But her mother-in-law’s side felt quite empty.","She had three sons—one couldn’t return from the border, one was on duty serving the Crown Prince, and the youngest was sharp-tongued and unaffectionate.","Only Cheng Zhao could keep her company and bring her some joy.","Given her mother-in-law’s leniency, Cheng Zhao felt obliged to be filial.","Second Madam then asked quietly, “I hear the Hall of Bright Heritage has been unsettled these days?”","Cheng Zhao smiled. “It will sort itself out soon.”","“How so?”","“Since you’ve heard about it, Grandmother will know too. By then, Eldest Aunt’s anger will naturally cool,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam said, “The old lady insists on elevating the Huan woman, even though you’re clearly brighter and more capable than Huan Qingtang.”","Cheng Zhao merely smiled.","“You want the Hall of Bright Heritage too; Huan Qingtang’s setbacks benefit you, yet you never take satisfaction in them,” Second Madam continued. “That kind of steadiness is rare. Your mother raised you well.”","Cheng Zhao longed to say that it was because the Hall of Bright Heritage was as solid as a mountain—it had never wavered.","The true mistress of the house was only the Dowager Madame.","So, when Elder Sister-in-law Huan Qingtang occasionally stumbled, it didn’t mean Cheng Zhao could easily take over the Hall of Bright Heritage. These were merely petty tricks, not worth celebrating.","Many people couldn’t see that, swaying with the wind, either excited or disappointed—all just superficial.","Huan Qingtang understood this too.","No matter how coldly Eldest Madam Song treated her, she remained composed.","Even if the stewards privately felt she had fallen behind Cheng Zhao, she wasn’t worried.","The final outcome was still uncertain.","Cheng Zhao didn’t want to discuss this with her mother-in-law and make her anxious.","So, hearing her words, she simply smiled and said, “I’m secretly pleased too, Mother, just quietly, on the sly.”","She made Second Madam laugh out loud.","On the fourth day of the fifth month, Cheng Zhao was returning to her family home when she encountered Huan Qingtang at the main gate; she too was going home early.","“Tomorrow I need to attend to Grandmother,” Huan Qingtang explained to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Tomorrow I must attend to my mother-in-law as well.”","The sisters-in-law smiled at each other in harmony and climbed into their respective carriages.","To the stewards who observed them, the two young madams seemed to get along quite well.","At her family home, Cheng Zhao met Helian Xuan, the Prince of Andong—one of her fourth brother’s worthless friends.","“It’s rare to have everyone together like this; how about going to listen to opera and have a drink later?” her fourth brother, Cheng Chao, said.","Cheng Ying had come home a few days early.","Every festival, the Princess Consort Jingnan prepared all sorts of gifts to give Cheng Ying face; even for an occasion like avoiding the midday heat, she would send Cheng Ying over early.","If not for Zhang Yunqi, Third Sister Cheng Ying’s marriage would have been quite a good one.","“I’m not going,” Cheng Zhao said. “I haven’t seen Grandfather yet, and I need to accompany Father for a meal later.”","Cheng Chao looked at her as though she were a ghost. “Accompany Father for a meal? You’re asking for trouble when there’s none to be had.”","Cheng Zhao said, “Aren’t you afraid of getting beaten?”","“He won’t hit me for saying that. But if you go hang around him and say a few more words, you might just earn yourself a good beating,” Cheng Chao said.","Cheng Zhao ignored him.","She wasn’t too annoyed with him, but he had to go and invite Helian Xuan home.","While the siblings were talking, Helian Xuan chimed in a few times.","“Physician Feng’s case is closed; the Duchess can rest easy now,” Helian Xuan said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “I have two generations of elders above me; such matters are not my concern, and there’s nothing for me to rest easy about.”","“Your words are so sharp,” Cheng Chao said disapprovingly. “No one’s provoked you. Have you no respect for the Prince?”","Helian Xuan quickly said, “It’s fine!”","Cheng Zhao turned and left, heading to her grandfather’s courtyard.","Cheng Chao couldn’t help complaining, “She’s so arrogant, her tail practically in the sky. This attitude of hers... don’t take it to heart.”","Helian Xuan had a pair of lovely peach-blossom eyes, but there was no gloom in them now.","“I only fear she’d be indifferent,” Helian Xuan said. “Whether happy or angry, it shows she cares about me.”","Cheng Chao was startled, then abruptly turned to look at him. “You... you’re still thinking about her?”","“What of it?”","“She’s already the Duchess of Chen’s mansion! It’s unacceptable!” Cheng Chao frowned.","“I won’t think less of her. This marriage was connected to me as well; it was my fault,” Helian Xuan said. “I couldn’t convince my mother to withdraw her request to the Empress for the betrothal.”","“Let’s leave aside whose fault it was—don’t you dare cause trouble,” Cheng Chao said sternly.","His sister was as beautiful as a flower; even if she divorced and remarried, she could be an empress. Who would presume to think less of her?","“Not thinking less of her” was hardly some noble virtue.","Only a man out of his mind would think less of her.","His little sister was always haughty and domineering, but that was because she was so beautiful that everyone had to pamper her.","One moment Cheng Chao was annoyed to death with her, the next he was dazzled by her beauty, thinking, never mind, being this beautiful gave her the right to be unreasonable.","“What do you mean?”","“If you make trouble for her, I’m done with you,” Cheng Chao said. “No matter how this marriage began, outsiders have no right to cause her problems. If you bring my little sister grief, our friendship ends here.”","Helian Xuan smiled. “I spoke out of turn. Forgive me.”","Cheng Chao said, “Come on, let’s go to my courtyard. I’ll show you my newly acquired longsword.”","Helian Xuan agreed."]},{"number":138,"title":"Chapter 138: Kiss Under the Pomegranate Blossoms","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao went to her grandfather’s study.","The grandfather and granddaughter talked for a long time.","“... No wonder he told Zhou Yuanshen to produce a ‘crown prince,’” her grandfather said.","The surprise in his eyes reassured Cheng Zhao. It wasn’t that she lacked insight or thought too shallowly—this matter was unheard of, utterly bewildering. Even her grandfather, a veteran minister who had served three reigns, was taken aback.","The Emperor was utterly mad.","“Yuanshen has been wronged,” her grandfather said.","He frowned slightly as he spoke.","He glanced at Cheng Zhao, then shook his head quickly and subtly.","But Cheng Zhao understood what he was thinking: The Emperor insisted on a child raised by the Grand Lady becoming the crown prince, believing that only such a child would be worthy and capable. If Empress Mu Jiang was gone, would other women be in danger?","After all, Zhou Yuanshen seemed completely obedient—he could even present his own beautiful concubine to the Emperor.","But her grandfather did not say this aloud. Such speculation should not come from an elder; it was too sordid.","“Zhaozhao, send for Yuanshen. I want to speak with him,” her grandfather said.","No matter how the Zhou family stirred up trouble, the Grand Chancellor Cheng could ignore it, but he had to ensure his granddaughter’s safety.","“I’ll have Qiubai find Nanfeng, and Nanfeng can locate him,” Cheng Zhao said.","Her grandfather nodded slightly.","This topic was too oppressive and revolting. Her grandfather dropped it to spare Cheng Zhao discomfort and began talking about the Dragon Boat Festival.","Shortly after the midday meal, Zhou Yuanshen arrived.","He came directly to her grandfather’s outer study.","Cheng Zhao left the study to them and went to find her father. She had promised her mother last time that she would speak more with him this visit.","Yet, the father and daughter sat alone, silently playing chess, exchanging no more than five words.","When he spoke, Cheng Zhao could never pick up the thread; what she said seemed to go unheard by him.","“... Fourth Brother brought Helian Xuan home again,” Cheng Zhao finally found a topic. “You should tell him to stop wasting his time with Helian Xuan.”","“He’s an adult now,” her father said.","He never interfered in his children’s social affairs, as long as they did not commit major errors.","“Shouldn’t Helian Xuan return to his fief?” Cheng Zhao asked again.","“The Emperor issued a special decree to keep him here. As long as he has an official assignment, he can serve the Emperor at court. The Emperor trusts him,” her father said.","Cheng Zhao: “Also?”","“There’s another who has won the Emperor’s favor—the Duke of Chen,” her father said.","Cheng Zhao frowned: “What has Helian Xuan done to deserve that?”","“He has some talent,” her father said.","“He hasn’t taken the blame for the Emperor the way the Duke of Chen has. The Duke does all the dirty work. How can Helian Xuan be on equal footing?” Cheng Zhao said.","Her father placed a stone: “Bloodlines.”","Blood was like invisible tendrils reaching into every corner of human hearts, strangely binding people together. When brothers in a family fought, outsiders who tried to mediate often ended up making enemies of everyone.","Helian Xuan was the Emperor’s cousin. His father, Prince An, had long been highly valued by the Emperor.","“Their whole family is mad,” Cheng Zhao said.","She was a bit irritated and slammed her stone down hard.","Her father chided her: “Calm down. Restlessness loses focus.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged the rebuke.","After three games of chess, with nothing left to say, Cheng Zhao rose to take her leave. She then went to her Second Brother’s courtyard, where Eldest Sister and Third Sister were also gathered.","The sisters’ company was much more pleasant.","Still, she did not talk much about the Duke of Chen’s household. Cheng Zhao mainly wanted to avoid causing trouble for her own family.","Suyue came looking for her.","“The Lord Duke is about to return. He asks if you will stay here tonight,” Suyue said.","Cheng Zhao stood up: “I’ll go back too.”","She added, “I’ll come back to stay for a few days after the festival. I’m busy these days.”","“Go on then,” Second Sister-in-Law said. “When you’re free in a few days, let’s play polo.”","She laughed: “This year has been strange. We’ve been talking about polo since the beginning of the year, and it’s already May.”","“I’m not good at polo. You and Eldest Sister are the best at it. Third Sister and I never really liked it,” Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Ying laughed: “I’m pretty good at polo now. Our Prince’s mansion has a training ground, and Sister Dai and I often practice there. You’re the only one who’s still no good.”","The “Sister Dai” she mentioned was Master Dai, who had taught them swordplay as children. Later, Master Dai became Cheng Ying’s companion and followed her to the Prince of Jingnan’s residence.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Leaving Second Brother’s courtyard, Cheng Zhao thought back to how she had learned horseback riding and polo as a child, but never developed much interest.","Later, she was forced to stay indoors and be a proper lady. Her mother forbade her from any recreational activities, allowing only sword practice for exercise.","The Duke of Chen’s mansion had no training ground, even though Second Brother’s family were all martial people. It was simply that the Grand Lady detested such things, and no one dared to touch a sore spot.","To be fair, the Duke of Chen’s estate was vast enough that it would be perfect for building a training ground.","Musing like this, Cheng Zhao reached the main gate.","Zhou Yuanshen was in his court robes, his official cap removed and his hair bound with a jade pin. He stood tall in the May sunlight. Golden rays fell on his face, making his eyes gleam.","He saw Cheng Zhao and took two steps forward to meet her.","“Duchess,” someone called from behind.","Cheng Zhao had just stepped over the threshold. She turned her head at the voice.","As she turned back, she realized her posture was somewhat inelegant, so she turned fully, crossed the threshold properly, and walked over to Zhou Yuanshen.","Behind her, Cheng Chao was seeing off Helian Xuan.","They exchanged greetings.","“... Last time at the Grand General’s mansion, the Old Madam mentioned Your Grace,” Helian Xuan said with a smile.","Zhou Yuanshen: “The Prince is too kind.”","“A’Chao and I were talking about playing polo. Would Your Grace care to join?” Helian Xuan asked again.","Cheng Zhao looked at her brother and said coldly, “It’s not settled yet. We’ll talk later.”","She glared at him.","Cheng Chao was annoyed but did not dare to glare back in public. Having such a little dragon in the family meant swallowing some pride at times.","“We have other business to attend to. We’ll take our leave now, Fourth Brother,” Cheng Zhao said.","Her gaze was fixed only on her brother.","Cheng Zhao forced himself to be polite: “Take care. Come home often.”","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen boarded the carriage.","The carriage was dimly lit, and Zhou Yuanshen remained silent.","He sat in the shadows and finally asked, “It’s still early. Is there something urgent you need to go back for?”","“Nothing. I asked for leave today before coming out,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Then let’s go to the General of the Western Campaign’s mansion,” he said.","She remembered him saying that after the Dragon Boat Festival, when everyone’s mood had calmed, they would go for a visit. But business completed was business settled—there was no need to keep worrying.","“Alright,” she said.","The General of the Western Campaign’s mansion was closer to the Cheng residence, and the carriage arrived quickly.","The gatehouse was not tall, with walls of polished brick and flush mortar joints, and crimson-painted doors. When they knocked, a servant respectfully welcomed the master, calling him “General.”","Cheng Zhao glanced at him.","In the late afternoon sunlight, his eyes seemed bright.","Perhaps this was what his life should have been.","She followed Zhou Yuanshen inside.","The courtyard was indeed not large—smaller even than her Eldest Sister’s—but it was compact, elegant, and rather cozy.","The rear garden was also modest, no bigger than a corner of the Duke of Chen’s back garden, but several pomegranate trees were in full, fiery bloom.","“It’s really beautiful,” Cheng Zhao said. “These flowers are wonderful.”","“Would I lie to you?” he said.","He casually picked several blossoms.","Cheng Zhao could not hold them all, so she cupped her hands, and within moments they were brimming with flowers.","“That’s enough...” Cheng Zhao said, her eyes spotting a stone table nearby. She intended to set them down, wanting to keep just one.","Carrying so many was tiring.","Zhou Yuanshen picked a few more, then came over and placed them next to the pile in her hands.","Suddenly, he lifted her up and sat her down on the stone table.","Cheng Zhao instinctively braced herself against the table, the soft pomegranate blossoms cushioning her palms.","“Zhou Yuanshen, you...”","Before she could finish her words, he kissed her."]},{"number":139,"title":"Chapter 139: Zhou Yuanshen's Satire","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao’s palm pressed down on the pomegranate flowers.","The pavilion was open on all sides, with sunlight slanting in from the side. Though no servants were attending them, it was still broad daylight.","Cheng Zhao was very nervous, afraid of losing propriety.","She struggled, trying to push him away, but because she was leaning back slightly, she couldn’t muster much strength.","The pomegranate flowers beneath her hand were even softer, as if she had crushed them into pulp.","“Zhou Yuanshen...” she murmured his name through clenched teeth.","Zhou Yuanshen deepened the kiss, swallowing her breath.","Cheng Zhao’s hands and waist went limp; she nearly collapsed onto the stone table. He caught her, making sure she didn’t hit her head.","He guided her hands to wrap around him.","“Cheng Zhao, stay here tonight,” he said, his voice hot and scorching, as he gently nibbled her earlobe.","Cheng Zhao: “Tomorrow is the festival...”","“The festival will be busy,” he said. “It’s rare to have leisure today.”","“Let’s return to Ronghua Courtyard; it’s more convenient,” Cheng Zhao refused him.","Her statue of the Child-Giving Guanyin was enshrined in Ronghua Courtyard. Without the desperate longing for an heir, this moment of passion felt rather unworthy.","As if it were merely for pleasure.","Cheng Zhao’s grand plan had yet to be settled; indulgence was truly a luxury, a corruption.","Zhou Yuanshen kissed her again, then let go.","His dark eyes were placid, and his movements were brisk as he turned.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","What did he have to be angry about?","Cheng Zhao frowned slightly. Lowering her head, she noticed her sleeve was stained with pomegranate flower juice. Looking more closely, her right hand, along with the skin on the side of her hand, were dyed red.","Her skin was fair, so the streaks of pomegranate juice were especially conspicuous.","Her sleeve was also stained.","This was a light purple top, the fabric a gift from her second sister-in-law, its color surpassing the morning clouds. Cheng Zhao liked it very much.","“This will be hard to wash off,” she said, examining her sleeve, her frown deepening.","On the way back, Zhou Yuanshen was displeased, and Cheng Zhao was displeased too.","A servant picked a basket of pomegranate flowers and placed it in the carriage, right at Cheng Zhao’s feet.","Cheng Zhao kicked it; a few flowers fell onto the back of her foot, and she shuffled them away.","By now it was dusk, the sun setting in the west, and the light inside the carriage was dim. Zhou Yuanshen shouldn’t have been able to see, but he glanced anyway, and very precisely picked up the fallen pomegranate flowers.","When they returned to the Duke of Chen’s mansion, two lanterns were already lit at the entrance.","Their carriage was held back because several carriages were ahead of them.","Cheng Zhao lifted the curtain.","She spotted Huan Qingtang, along with the Marchioness of Qingyuan—the niece-in-law of the Dowager Madame’s natal family—and Sun Zhisheng.","It seemed one party had just returned, and the other was about to take their leave.","Cheng Zhao let the curtain fall and didn’t move.","Zhou Yuanshen, however, said, “Get out of the carriage.”","Cheng Zhao: “The Marchioness of Qingyuan is there.”","“Cheng Zhao, do you have a guilty conscience?” he asked.","Cheng Zhao was baffled.","What guilt did she have?","The troublemaking children of the Qingyuan Marquis's household, and Sun Zhiya, who was suspected of causing the Fifth Prince’s death and was herself disfigured—all these were the Dowager Madame and the Sun family’s own doing.","What did it have to do with Cheng Zhao?","Cheng Zhao had almost been killed too.","“What guilt do I have?” she retorted coldly.","Zhou Yuanshen: “If you have no guilt, then there’s no need to avoid anyone. If you yield, they’ll think you’re indeed guilty and will try to retaliate even more.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","What he said wasn’t wrong, but that clearly wasn’t what he meant just now.","When you asked if I had a guilty conscience, you truly believed I did.","Whose side are you on?","You couldn’t possibly be on the Sun family’s side. Could it be Huan Qingtang?","Didn’t you say that Huan Qingtang and the Huan family schemed against you at Qing’an Princess Manor? After being schemed against, would you become interested in her out of resentment, and instead help her?","Cheng Zhao was instantly fuming.","On top of that, she was upset about her clothes being ruined by the pomegranate flower juice.","The fabric was expensive; her sister-in-law had specially brought it back from Wu Commandery for her, insisting it suited her perfectly.","Her second sister-in-law always thought of her, and Cheng Zhao couldn’t bear to disregard that care by carelessly ruining a nice outfit. Cheng Zhao cherished her family’s affection for her.","Except for her Fourth Brother, Cheng Chao, who was always at odds with her, everyone else was very kind to her.","Thinking of her fourth brother reminded her of how disobedient he was. She had told him not to hang around Helian Xuan, but he still brought Helian Xuan home. Cheng Zhao really wanted to pick a fight with him.","All these little sparks of irritation converged, and by the time Cheng Zhao descended from the carriage, she was already boiling with anger.","Zhou Yuanshen picked up the basket of pomegranate flowers, handed it to the charioteer and deputy officer, and also got out of the carriage.","The couple walked inside.","When the Marchioness of Qingyuan and Sun Zhisheng saw them, their expressions shifted; Huan Qingtang remained as gentle and amiable as ever: “Lord Duke, Sister-in-law.”","“Elder Sister-in-law,” Zhou Yuanqi said, his tone cold and stiff.","Cheng Zhao also returned the greeting, calling her “Elder Sister-in-law.”","They didn’t speak to the Marchioness of Qingyuan.","Huan Qingtang saw this, simply smiled, and didn’t offer any reminders.","But Sun Zhisheng was furious: “Who do you think you are? No respect for your elders.”","He added, “If I lodge a complaint with the Censorate, accusing Grand Tutor Zhou of being unfilial, how would you deal with that?”","Zhou Yuanshen halted.","The Marchioness of Qingyuan’s face also darkened. She said to Sun Zhisheng, “Forget it. We’re not worthy of the Lord Duke.”","“Auntie,” Zhou Yuanshen said, “The case of Cousin Sun Zhiya killing the prince isn’t settled. There are many doubts that can be reopened. Go ahead and file that complaint with the Censorate. Show me what happens.”","The Marchioness of Qingyuan’s face drained of color.","Sun Zhisheng, torn between anger and fear, also understood the stakes involved and swallowed his temper, falling silent.","Huan Qingtang looked at him with a smile: “The Lord Duke must be tired today. You both go back and rest. I’ll see my aunt off.”","Her words were correct.","But it was her attitude...","Too familiar—perhaps she didn’t mean it that way, but that’s how it sounded to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao didn’t want to listen to their bickering, nor did she want to argue. She stepped over the threshold first and headed back.","She walked onto the covered corridor, her pace quickening.","Zhou Yuanshen watched her striding away, his gaze growing heavier, but he quickly looked away and said to the people at the entrance, “Without the Elder Sister-in-law managing this household, we’d need to hire ten more stewards. We’re indebted to her.”","His tone was sarcastic.","Huan Qingtang was stunned; the smile vanished from her face in an instant.","Zhou Yuanshen finished speaking and also entered the gate.","The Marchioness of Qingyuan and Sun Zhisheng, having swallowed so much humiliation, were outraged; Huan Qingtang’s face was gloomy.","“A rat everyone wants to beat—his bloodthirsty, filthy tricks will eventually backfire. We’ll see how he dies then!” Sun Zhisheng, only fifteen, didn’t hold back when cursing. “Mother, let’s go!”","He helped his mother, and they stormed off in fury.","Huan Qingtang stood still, neither smoothing over Zhou Yuanshen’s blunt words nor moving.","She pondered briefly, wondering where she had gone wrong.","It seemed that Cheng Zhao had been throwing a tantrum, which had dragged her into it.","Zhou Yuanshen was probably having a quarrel with Cheng Zhao and had taken his anger out on her.","Huan Qingtang mused for a moment, watching Zhou Yuanshen’s wide-striding figure disappearing into the distance. She too entered the gate. She went directly to the Dowager Madame’s Shou’an Courtyard.","Meanwhile, Cheng Zhao went to Chengming Hall.","After returning from her maternal family, politeness dictated that she should notify the Eldest Aunt.","“You all leave and don’t know when to come back. Look how late it is,” the First Madam said disapprovingly. “You’re like this, and Lady Huan is the same.”","She then asked, “Go check at the gatekeeper’s lodge and see if Lady Huan has returned.”"]},{"number":140,"title":"Chapter 140: Blame It on Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["When the First Madam asked about Huan Qingtang, Cheng Zhao said nothing.","There was no need for her to lower herself by telling the First Madam that Huan Qingtang had gone to the Shou’an Courtyard.","The First Madam would find out easily enough on her own.","If it came from Cheng Zhao’s mouth, it would only seem like she was tattling, and if word got back to the stewards, it would hurt Cheng Zhao’s reputation and authority.","“Go back now,” said the First Madam. “Next time, don’t be so late. Don’t let pleasure keep you away.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged the order.","As she stepped out of the Chengming Hall, she saw Suyue and Qiubai waiting at the entrance.","The three of them hadn’t returned together. Cheng Zhao had accompanied Zhou Yuanshen to the residence of General Pingxi, leaving the maids behind.","“We arrived first and waited at the Chenhui Courtyard. The Lord Duke said you were in the Chengming Hall,” Suyue said.","Then she added, “The Lord Duke has already returned to the Nonghua Courtyard.”","Cheng Zhao remained silent.","Suyue and Qiubai exchanged a glance, then quietly followed behind her. The walk back was somewhat long, and Suyue cautiously ventured, “Did the First Madam scold you?”","“Not at all.”","“Then why do you seem unhappy?” Suyue pressed. “Did the Lord Duke upset you?”","Aside from the Lord Duke or the First Madam, Suyue couldn’t think of anyone else.","Qiubai, on the other hand, asked, “Or was it the Fourth Young Master who annoyed you back at home?”","Cheng Zhao was speechless.","Her anger rarely lasted long—she would fume for a while, then think, “It’s not worth tormenting myself over this.”","Having grown up surrounded by family who treated her well, she had never truly suffered deep grievance. So her temper flared up easily but faded just as fast.","She broke into a smile.","Suyue and Qiubai breathed a quiet sigh of relief.","“...To be honest, Fourth Brother really overstepped. How can he bear to be apart from Helian Xuan?” Cheng Zhao remarked.","Suyue and Qiubai murmured words of comfort.","Meanwhile, as the mistress and her maids returned to the Nonghua Courtyard, Huan Qingtang finally arrived at the Chengming Hall.","The First Madam Song now treated her with a kind of reckless contempt, demanding in the presence of maids and servants why she had come back so late.","When Huan Qingtang tried to explain, the First Madam Song cut her off: “Don’t make excuses for me. Go to the small shrine and copy Buddhist scriptures!”","She had been punished.","The small shrine in the Chengming Hall was set up in a side chamber, rarely used except for incense offerings on the first and fifteenth of the month—similar to the east side room in Cheng Zhao’s residence, which housed a statue of the Goddess of Fertility.","Spending a whole night copying scriptures in such a chamber was grueling.","Huan Qingtang respectfully acknowledged the order: “As Mother commands.”","One of her maids slipped out unnoticed.","Soon, Nanny Sun, sent by the Dowager Madame, arrived at the Chengming Hall and summoned the First Madam Song to the Shou’an Courtyard.","When Song returned, her face was sallow and livid, yet she forced a smile for Huan Qingtang: “Your filial devotion is enough. Go on back now.”","Huan Qingtang replied, “It’s late. Mother should rest early.”","She turned and left.","Her senior maid couldn’t help but complain on her behalf: “The First Madam grows more overbearing by the day! But judging by her expression, she’ll calm down for a few days at least.”","Huan Qingtang smiled bitterly. “Even if she calms down, it won’t last. Just a temporary lull.”","Then she asked, “What set her off so suddenly this time?”","The maid thought for a moment. “Cheng Shi went to the Chengming Hall earlier. It was probably her stirring the pot. She excels at that.”","Huan Qingtang nodded faintly. “That would make sense. From the moment Cheng Shi set foot in this household, she’s been provoking and causing trouble.”","She added, “Every problem in this house traces back to her. She might be fated to clash with the Duke of Chen’s household.”","“Of course she’s not compatible with it,” the maid said. “When the marriage was arranged, they never even calculated her and the Zhou family’s birth charts—it was the Empress herself who commanded the match.”","She went on, “She’s a troublemaker. Every incident bears her mark! When will the First Madam realize that? If Cheng Shi isn’t removed, the First Madam will never have peace. But she’s shortsighted—instead, she focuses on you, because you’re easier to bully.”","After all, Cheng Zhao was not the First Madam’s daughter-in-law; she was a titled lady of the highest rank, holding power that outstripped the First Madam on both counts—family hierarchy and official honor.","Only Huan Qingtang was vulnerable.","The maid added, “Once your own titled lady status is granted, the First Madam will treat you differently. You’re perfect in every way—just a little unlucky in this one thing.”","Huan Qingtang, too, felt that Cheng Zhao had caused her no small amount of trouble.","For a family to flourish, the inner household must be harmonious and stable, yet Cheng Zhao spent her days stirring up unrest.","The Dowager Madame really should make an early decision and deal with Cheng Zhao—remove the source of chaos once and for all.","Back at the Nonghua Courtyard, Cheng Zhao changed her clothes and washed her hands.","The red flower-stain on her right hand wouldn’t come off easily.","“This garment is ruined,” Nanny Li said. “What did you stain it with?”","Cheng Zhao told her the truth.","“It probably won’t wash out,” Nanny Li said. “This fabric is especially prone to staining.”","“If it can’t be washed, put it at the bottom of the trunk. I’ll have the same fabric made into another one later, so Second Sister-in-Law won’t ask. She complimented this short jacket just today,” Cheng Zhao replied.","As they spoke, Zhou Yuanshen, who had also changed and washed his hands, walked in. He reached out a hand. “Let me see.”","Nanny Li handed him the garment.","“What is this fabric called?” he asked.","Nanny Li answered, “A textile workshop in Wu Commandery produced it—their best batch last year. It doesn’t have a specific name. To get more, we’ll have to wait until this year’s harvest.”","“When will it be available this year?” he inquired.","“Soon. The new silk cocoons are about to come to market,\" Nanny Li replied.","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing more.","They had dinner on the large kang by the window in the inner chamber, eating in silence. Cheng Zhao was no longer angry, but she wasn’t in the mood to engage with him either.","After the meal, Zhou Yuanshen washed up, changed into his underclothes, and lay down on the bed with a book.","Cheng Zhao wanted to comb her hair. She sat at the dressing table and asked Nanny Li to use a fine-toothed comb to ease out the tangles—it always felt soothing.","The couple spoke not a word to each other. Instead, it was Nanny Li and Cheng Zhao who exchanged quiet remarks now and then.","“...I hear she was ordered to copy Buddhist scriptures as punishment,” Nanny Li murmured.","The Nonghua Courtyard had already secured some ears and eyes of its own, and as long as the events at the Chengming Hall weren’t deliberately hidden, Cheng Zhao could keep herself informed.","Cheng Zhao glanced at Zhou Yuanshen from the corner of her eye.","His gaze was still fixed on his book.","“Let them bicker and squabble if they have the energy for it. It has nothing to do with us,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li sighed. “I have a feeling this year won’t be peaceful either.”","Even without Mu Jiang present in the household.","The problem at the Chengming Hall could no longer be concealed.","It was the Dowager Madame’s fault. But with her power and authority, who dared speak ill of her? So that “fault” needed a scapegoat.","Inevitably, it would fall to one of three: Cheng Zhao, First Madam Song, or Huan Qingtang.","Second Madam, because she had no standing at the Chengming Hall, escaped unscathed—which made one think that sometimes simplicity was a blessing.","“I’m young. Even if there’s no peace, I’ll still live well. Back on the boat, I never had a moment when the wooden planks beneath my feet were steady,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li was speechless.","Still, not yet twenty, vigorous and full of spirit—she had the capital to win.","That thought comforted Nanny Li considerably.","Zhou Yuanshen seemed to raise his head casually in their direction, as if deliberately glancing at Cheng Zhao. Though the mistress and her nanny spoke softly, he must have overheard them talking.","Cheng Zhao didn’t look at him.","Nanny Li studied her expression in the mirror, then turned to watch Zhou Yuanshen reading so intently. She fought back a smile, guessing the young couple had fallen out again.","Carefully finishing combing Cheng Zhao’s hair, she said, “Rest early, young madam.”","And with that, she left.","It was late. Cheng Zhao got into bed.","Zhou Yuanshen graciously moved his legs aside to let her pass.","The two of them remained silent."]},{"number":141,"title":"Chapter 141: Gluttonous Despite Being Sated","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao lay on the inner side of the bed.","Her hair was combed smooth, her scalp soothed, and her mind felt much more at ease.","She lay down, ready to sleep, but Zhou Yuanshen got up instead.","Cheng Zhao heard him call out, \"Someone come.\"","She originally didn't want to pay attention, but since Nonghua Courtyard was staffed entirely with her own dowry servants, and he was summoning her people, she sat up.","She listened closely to the movements from beyond the screen.","\"Pomegranate blossoms...\"","She heard Zhou Yuanshen give such an order.","From outside, Qiubai, who was on duty, responded, \"They're on the table in the main hall, brought in by Deputy General Liu.\"","A moment later, Zhou Yuanshen closed the door and carried a basket inside. When Cheng Zhao met his gaze, she felt a hint of confusion.","He then said, \"Isn't it the custom to scatter pomegranate blossoms over the bed canopy during the festival? Since we might not be here tomorrow or the day after, it's better to do it now.\"","But the sight of pomegranate blossoms reminded Cheng Zhao of her clothes stained with the flower's juice, and the marks on her hands that wouldn't wash off. She frowned slightly.","\"We won't scatter them this year,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Pomegranate blossoms could not only be scattered over the canopy but also strung together and hung on the bed curtains.","Cheng Zhao thought she could hand them over to Nanny Li tomorrow, to have her thread them one by one and hang them beside the golden hooks.","As she was thinking this, Zhou Yuanshen had already deftly grabbed the blossoms and tossed them up toward the canopy.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","He threw two handfuls, then said calmly, \"We still have to celebrate the festival.\"","The remaining blossoms were casually placed on the footstool beside the bed.","The man's movements were broad, and several flowers landed on the bed.","Cheng Zhao brushed them over to his side, turned around, and lay down with her back to him.","Zhou Yuanshen let down the bed curtains and covered the horn lantern.","There was the rustling sound of him on his side.","Just as Cheng Zhao thought he was about to sleep, he lifted her blanket and slipped inside.","Cheng Zhao: !","Her protests and questions were swallowed up by him.","He barely needed any kindling; his skin was scorching hot.","Perhaps the sparks that had been kindled in the Pacifying Western General's Mansion had never gone out.","He had held back until now, with no trace of his restlessness visible in the meantime.","His large hand wrapped around her waist.","Cheng Zhao felt as if a few pomegranate blossoms had fallen from the canopy above.","\"Cheng Zhao?\" When a bead of his sweat landed on her, he called her name in a low voice, and with one hand, he cupped her chin, making her look at him.","Cheng Zhao's eyes were dazed. \"What is it?\"","\"You don't like the Pacifying Western General's Mansion, and you don't like the Duke of Chen's Mansion either, do you?\" he asked.","Cheng Zhao thought to herself, that's nonsense.","Whether it was the Duke of Chen's Mansion or the General's Mansion, she liked them both very much.","The wealth and splendor of the Duke of Chen's Mansion were what she had long yearned for. It was just that the real power and riches hadn't yet fallen into her hands.","The General's Mansion was compact and cozy, like an extremely elegant villa. Life there should be tranquil and serene.","They were both very good places.","\"You're talking nonsense!\" she said.","Zhou Yuanshen bent down and silenced her lips.","It was like a midsummer rainstorm, lashing the branches and leaves into a frenzy of quivering.","Cheng Zhao's arms held him tightly, her nails nearly digging into his back.","For a long time, she still clung to him.","It seemed she came back to her senses and patted him lightly. \"Let me get up.\"","Zhou Yuanshen didn't move.","When Cheng Zhao was short of breath, his own breathing was extremely steady and calm. He was always like this, without any noise.","The only time he had made a sound was that time in the carriage.","He wasn't pressing his full weight on Cheng Zhao. Though he was pressed close to her, his hands and legs bore most of his own weight.","It was intimate, yet not heavy.","Both of them were lightly sweating, and Cheng Zhao wanted to go wash up.","Besides, something was digging into her lower back.","When Zhou Yuanshen moved aside, Cheng Zhao sat up slightly and from under her body, she pulled out a pomegranate blossom that had been crushed to a pulp.","It was as if another flower had bloomed on the bedding.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","First he ruined one of her dresses, and now he had ruined her bedsheet.","The pomegranate blossom was lovely and charming. It hadn't done anything wrong while growing on its branch.","The fault lay with this man. It was he who had picked it, and it was he who had crushed the flower, and crushed Cheng Zhao in the process.","Cheng Zhao snorted.","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at her and turned her by the shoulders. \"What's wrong?\"","When he saw the red mark on her lower back, he startled as if shocked, showing some rare emotional fluctuation, and asked repeatedly, \"Does it hurt?\"","Then he said, \"I'll get some medicine.\"","Cheng Zhao watched him as he put on his robe and got out of bed, and called out to him, \"Lord Duke, it's not a cut. That's just the petals and juice of the pomegranate blossom.\"","Under the dim lamplight, it looked like the flesh was broken and bleeding.","Zhou Yuanshen stopped in his tracks.","He turned back, acting as if nothing had happened, casually picked up a cloth, and wiped it for her.","When he finished wiping, he said in a low voice, \"It left a mark.\"","On her lower back, it looked like a pomegranate blossom had bloomed.","Cheng Zhao was a little annoyed. \"I have it on my hands too, and on my clothes and bedsheet.\"","She pushed herself confrontationally close in front of him.","He quietly looked at her, then leaned in and kissed her lips.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","It was impossible to quarrel with him. He had a knack for playing dumb. Sometimes he would ask inexplicable questions. And whether there was an answer or not, he would never ask a second time.","He was so hard to get along with.","While she bathed, Cheng Zhao sighed, feeling that if this went on, her way of talking with Zhou Yuanshen would become very much like her way of talking with her father.","—Better not to talk at all.","Cheng Zhao drifted off to sleep in a daze, feeling as if Zhou Yuanshen had held her for a moment.","The next day was the Dragon Boat Festival, and they had to get up early.","Cheng Zhao was preoccupied and didn't sleep deeply.","She felt someone pull her into an embrace.","She fell into a warm hug.","There were kisses on her forehead, the bridge of her nose, and her lips.","He parted her lips, and she tasted warmth. Very drowsy, Cheng Zhao snuggled into him limply.","The man's fingers stroked her lips and then slipped into her mouth.","Cheng Zhao, drowsy and weak all over, dodged away and muttered in complaint, \"I want to sleep!\"","\"Cheng Zhao, I'd like to kiss you,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao thought, you haven't held back from kissing me, so why are you asking me in the middle of the night?","It was only when she realized what he truly meant that she woke up completely.","The newly changed bedsheet was wrinkled in the grip of her hand, and her slightly bent legs were trembling.","She vaguely heard the rooster crow.","Zhou Yuanshen poured some hot water from the kettle and wiped her clean. For the first time, Cheng Zhao felt herself going limp, her very bones dissolving into softness.","He dressed her.","\"Someone come,\" he called out the door.","Cheng Zhao saw the daylight filtering in. It was almost dawn outside.","She turned over and sank into a dream.","She thought to herself, even if the sky were to fall, she would still need to get some sleep. At this moment, she couldn't even sit steadily, let alone stand.","When the room was fully bright with daylight, she suddenly woke with a start and called out, \"Suyue! Qiubai!\"","Her maids came in.","\"What time is it?\"","Suyue laughed and said, \"Well into the morning. The Second Madam has already gone to the Fan residence. The Dowager and the other ladies have all left to watch the dragon boat races. Please, take your time. The Lord Duke said you were feeling a bit unwell last night and didn't sleep much all night. He specifically told us to let you sleep in.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","As she sat before the mirror to do her hair and makeup, Cheng Zhao was both embarrassed and angry.","Others might not know what was going on, but her mother-in-law certainly would.","How could he have had the nerve to tell others that Cheng Zhao hadn't slept much all night?","How did he find the words to say that?","Would he be drowsy and listless accompanying the Emperor today?","Cheng Zhao had no time to think about it further. She quickly washed up and finally managed to arrive at the Grand General's Mansion before lunch.","Luckily, no one asked a single extra question."]},{"number":142,"title":"Chapter 142: Cheng Zhao, You Should Reward Me","paragraphs":["The Fan household was buzzing with activity.","They had gone to watch the dragon boat race that morning and had already returned.","Zhou Yuanqi remarked, “We ran into the Cheng family. They asked about you.”","Then he added, “Is your diarrhea any better?”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","That illness indeed kept one up all night with a churning belly, and it was a bit unpleasant to talk about—too embarrassing for anyone to inquire too closely or offer too much concern.","That rascal Zhou Yuanshen certainly knew how to come up with an excuse.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Much better now.”","Second Madam said, “I’ve already ordered the main kitchen to prepare fried rice tea. Don’t eat lunch; just drink this instead. When we were young, we always used it as a remedy—more effective than medicine.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","But she was hungry.","She longed to feast on the delicacies laid out at the banquet, not sip on fried rice tea.","All she could do was agree.","Second Madam glanced at her again and suddenly noticed the red marks on her neck. She also took in her complexion—far from the pallid, weak look of someone who had spent the night with a stomach bug; instead, she looked full of life, fresh and dewy.","As a woman with experience, she immediately understood.","Second Madam suppressed a smile.","It reminded her of her own younger days, when there had been a few moments of indiscretion as well.","“...Not eating will make you hungry,” Second Madam added. “Have something light later.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged this.","Before the midday meal, she was forced to drink a bowl of fried rice tea.","It wasn’t bad, actually—toasty, with a slight bitterness, and it whetted her appetite a little.","“Zhao Zhao, what happened to your hands? Did something scratch you?” her Grandmother asked.","Cheng Zhao laughed lightly. “I was playing with pomegranate flowers at home, and the stain won’t wash off.”","Second Madam said, “Still such a child.”","Cheng Zhao hid her hands in her sleeves.","Zhou Yuanqi continued talking about how they had run into the Cheng family that day, as well as Sun Zhisheng and his pack of friends.","“I also saw Third Young Lady’s husband,” Zhou Yuanqi said. “He keeps a mistress on the side. Third Young Lady could do the same, couldn’t she?”","Everyone turned to stare at him.","Then the room burst into laughter.","Zhou Yuanqi often said startling things, and even Cheng Zhao was taken aback by this.","Second Madam tapped him on the forehead, quite firmly. “What nonsense are you spouting?”","Zhou Yuanqi covered his head, muttering that his mother was vulgar.","Her aunt-in-law also said, “Yuanqi, you mustn’t say such things casually. You’re just a child and don’t think twice about your words, but others might take them seriously. They could harm her reputation.”","Fan Xiao, who had been listening with a smile, chimed in, “Inside Yuanqi’s little head, he’s got the most orthodox economic theories on one side, and the most radical, heretical ideas on the other.”","Being treated like a child annoyed Zhou Yuanqi.","What he said was true, after all.","When you considered family background, looks, and talent, Third Young Lady surpassed her husband by far. If he could keep a mistress, why couldn’t she keep one too? She deserved one more than he did.","Zhou Yuanqi loved studying, which often made him oblivious to worldly conventions. There was still a pure, untainted part of him that sought absolute fairness.","For example, he thought that if a rough-and-tumble man could have two wives under the dual-inheritance custom, then his sister-in-law should be allowed to take a husband into her family instead.","—He didn’t dare say that last part aloud because he was afraid his mother and that rough fellow might both beat him, but he truly believed it was the fairer solution.","The conversation shifted to an afternoon game of polo.","Second Madam and Fan Xiao each led a team. Her aunt-in-law, the cousins-in-law, and the three younger female cousins all joined in. Even Grandmother wanted to play, but Zhou Yuanqi objected.","He firmly refused to allow Grandmother to join.","“If you break a bone, I’ll be worried. When I’m worried, I can’t concentrate on my studies. You’d be ruining my future,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Sons and grandsons—they could be a burden.","The Old Dowager doted on him far too much, and she chuckled, “Fine, Grandmother won’t play. I’ll just watch you all.”","The polo match was thrilling. Both Second Madam and Fan Xiao were skilled riders and excellent players.","Cheng Zhao thought to herself, “If my mother-in-law hadn’t married into the Zhou family, maybe she’d be happier.”","A woman with such a fiery temperament must have found it stifling to play the role of a refined noblewoman all the time.","Midway through the match, Second Madam had scored four goals, and Fan Xiao had scored seven.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi were the most nervous.","“Uncle, you have to let my mother win—she’s your sister!” Zhou Yuanqi shouted from the sidelines.","Fan Xiao laughed heartily. “On the battlefield, life and death are at stake—no giving in.”","Second Madam was a little embarrassed. “Go play somewhere else. What nonsense are you shouting? Your mother isn’t going to lose.”","But deep down, she knew that both in stamina and skill, she couldn’t match her younger brother.","Just then, Second Master and Zhou Yuanshen arrived.","Both father and son were still in their official robes.","During the Dragon Boat Festival, most government offices had a day off, but senior ministers had to accompany the emperor—the emperor and empress both watched the dragon boat races.","“...How many goals did you win?” Second Master asked Second Madam.","“Four.”","“Let Yuanshen take over. He can win you six more,” Second Master said.","Fan Xiao protested, “Unfair! You can’t switch players mid-game!”","“Switching players mid-game happens all the time,” Second Master replied. “You’ve been on the frontier for years, but you’ve never seen real combat—that’s why you speak like an amateur.”","He was essentially saying that Fan Xiao had spent all those years doing odd jobs on the border without ever tasting real battle.","Cheng Zhao felt this was the first time she’d heard her father-in-law speak so sharply.","No wonder Zhou Yuanshen and Zhou Yuanqi were not easy people to deal with—they took after their father. Her mother-in-law, on the other hand, was genuinely kind-hearted.","Fan Xiao was momentarily at a loss for words.","He laughed in exasperation. “Fine, then today we must settle this. You have to put up a wager, brother-in-law.”","Then he turned to Zhou Yuanshen. “Yuanshen, do you dare take this on?”","Zhou Yuanshen first glanced at Cheng Zhao.","With everyone watching, Cheng Zhao smiled politely but inwardly wished she could strangle him.","Only then did Zhou Yuanshen turn back and ask Second Madam, “Mother, would you like to make the switch?”","“Yes,” Second Madam said eagerly. “You must beat him!”","“Understood.”","Zhou Yuanshen went to change clothes.","Fan Xiao insisted on setting a stake.","The Fan family had excellent horses, and he put forward one of their finest stallions as his wager.","Second Master responded, “If you win, I’ll give you that treasured lance I’ve kept in my collection.”","“The lance of Hulie, the Northern Di General?” Fan Xiao asked.","“Yes, that very lance.”","“It’s a deal!” Fan Xiao declared.","Soon, Zhou Yuanshen returned in his riding attire. Her aunt-in-law, the cousins-in-law, and the younger cousins withdrew, replaced by their deputies.","The second half of the match was intense.","At one point, a deputy fell off his horse, though fortunately, he wasn’t seriously injured—just a possibly twisted ankle. It wouldn’t leave him crippled or threaten his life.","As dusk fell, the match finally concluded.","Zhou Yuanshen caught up to Fan Xiao and narrowly won by one goal.","He thus secured the lance his father had treasured and won the Fan family’s best horse.","Second Madam was overjoyed.","Cheng Zhao had to admit that Zhou Yuanshen looked far more handsome on horseback playing polo than he normally did.","That night, everyone stayed over at the Fan residence.","There was a private courtyard set aside for Second Madam’s visits, with three main rooms in the center: a hall in the middle, and two bedrooms to its left and right. There were also side wings and a rear-facing annex.","Zhou Yuanqi slept in the side wing; Cheng Zhao and her husband Zhou Yuanshen took the eastern bedroom on the upper floor.","Covered in sweat from the polo match and with limited bathing facilities in the small courtyard, Zhou Yuanshen went to bathe in Fan Xiao’s quarters.","By the time he returned, everyone else had already settled down for the night.","The day had been tiring, with no afternoon nap, and all were exhausted.","He walked softly into the east bedroom.","Cheng Zhao was already lying down.","Zhou Yuanshen climbed onto the bed and said to her, “Let me see your lower back.”","Cheng Zhao replied, “It’s not broken.”","“When will this color wash off? Bring it here so I can see,” he insisted.","Cheng Zhao stopped him, grabbing his hand. “Don’t fool around. Your parents and Yuanqi are all in this courtyard.”","Zhou Yuanshen pulled her close. “I won today.”","“Oh?”","\"Since I've won, Cheng Zhao, you need to reward me,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","\"I'll be gentle.\" He kissed her lips."]},{"number":143,"title":"Chapter 143: This Is Cheng Zhao's Way Out","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen was tireless.","Last night, he had stirred Cheng Zhao twice, and today, after attending to the emperor and playing a game of polo, he still had the energy to torment her.","Cheng Zhao thought the term \"brute\" suited him perfectly.","When it was over, Zhou Yuanshen poured warm water from the thermos and handed it to Cheng Zhao.","He wanted to attend to her personally, but Cheng Zhao refused and took care of herself.","When they finally lay down, the courtyard was utterly silent, as everyone else had already retired.","Zhou Yuanshen quickly fell asleep.","Cheng Zhao, drawn into his rhythm, also drifted into slumber.","The next day, there was nothing pressing. The court officials had the day off. The Second Master and his son did not go to the yamen, and Zhou Yuanqi did not return to school.","Breakfast was taken in their own courtyard.","“... What are you doing this morning?” Second Madam asked them.","“Young Uncle has invited us for wine and an opera performance,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Zhou Yuanqi added, “I don’t want to go out with Young Uncle. He always picks on me.”","Zhou Yuanshen: “No one invited you.”","Zhou Yuanqi: “...”","Second Madam seemed thoughtful.","Cheng Zhao noticed and asked, “Mother, is there somewhere you’d like to go?”","Second Madam said, “I’ve only been to Yuanshen’s General of the Pacified West’s mansion twice. If there’s nothing to do today, we could go take a look.”","Cheng Zhao replied, “I’ll accompany you.”","“You should all come,” Second Madam said, then turned to Zhou Yuanshen, “You too. Your Young Uncle is a man of leisure and often has time for wine and opera. He won’t miss this one outing.”","Zhou Yuanshen agreed.","Around mid-morning, the family went to the General of the Pacified West’s mansion.","It was Third Madam’s third visit, yet she still found the place warm and inviting. The courtyard wasn’t large; it would feel crowded and noisy if filled with people, but for the young couple, it had the peaceful seclusion of a retreat amidst the bustle.","“... Next time you quarrel with your grandmother, just scare her a little. You could move here and stay a few days,” Second Madam suddenly said.","Second Master chuckled: “How could it come to that? Ah Shen is the Lord Duke.”","The Dowager Madame would never let go of the Zhou family’s Lord Duke. What she needed was to tame him, suppress him, make him obedient, a willing pawn and servant.","“Precisely because he’s the Lord Duke, he can move here,” Second Madam said. “If it were Yuan Qi moving here, who would care?”","Zhou Yuanqi: “...”","He suddenly felt like giving his mother a nickname.","But he didn’t dare.","He had studied the classics since childhood, and the sages placed filial piety first. Zhou Yuanqi was all talk; he wouldn’t dare disrespect his mother.","Even if she occasionally drove him up the wall.","Cheng Zhao smiled. “Maybe that day will actually come. If something unexpected happens at Nonghua Courtyard, we can use it as an excuse to move here. If Grandmother wants us to move back, she’ll have to vacate the Chengming Hall for me.”","She even felt it was entirely possible.","The women in the Chengming Hall were dangerously restless, their conflicts too deep, and the Dowager Madame played them against each other. The storm was bound to escalate.","They wouldn’t be able to bear the consequences of those upheavals, so they’d make Cheng Zhao the scapegoat.","The blame, the cause of calamity, would all be pinned on her because she was the newcomer. She was also the Duchess of Chen, who had carved a crack into the Dowager Madame’s reign.","Second Master gave Cheng Zhao a meaningful glance and laughed. “Zhaozhao, why the gloomy talk? You’re still so young.”","He didn’t want Cheng Zhao to see the future as too bleak.","Even though he knew things weren’t smooth.","“It’s just being forewarned is forearmed,” Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanshen only listened quietly.","Second Madam turned to him. “Yuanshen, do you think your wife and I are right?”","“Very much so, Mother,” Zhou Yuanshen said, his expression calm and his tone flat.","Whether he truly agreed or was just humoring her, no one could tell.","Second Madam couldn’t fathom her son’s thoughts, nor did she care to waste the effort overthinking it.","They took another walk around, and Second Madam kept praising it. “Lovely.”","Zhou Yuanqi piped up: “Mother, I think you’re the one who wants to move here yourself.”","Zhou Yuanshen said, “If Mother wants to come, of course, she’s welcome.”","Second Madam patted Zhou Yuanqi’s head and then lightly slapped Zhou Yuanshen’s arm. She sighed. “If you want to live independently, make preparations early. Then you’ll suffer fewer grievances. Now, it’s not worth it.”","She had endured so many years.","The Dowager Madame was old, and Zhou Yuanshen had inherited the title. Good days were right around the corner. It would be foolish to give up now.","“I don’t feel wronged,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Zhou Yuanqi added, “Why would I feel wronged, Mother?”","Then he said, “This is excessive doting. Your sons are fine. It’s only you who thinks we’re put upon.”","He went on, “Too much coddling ruins children. Some people can also get spoiled.”","As he said this, he glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Second Madam reached out and knocked him lightly on the head. “You’re mocking your mother and insinuating about your sister-in-law. That’s improper and rude!”","Zhou Yuanqi: “You’ve made me dumb.”","“Your brain is too sharp. A little dullness would be good—more steady,” Second Master interjected with a laugh.","Second Madam and Cheng Zhao couldn’t help but chuckle.","They also visited the stables.","The pony that had once thrown Zhou Yuanqi off its back, scaring him into a high fever, had grown significantly in just a short time.","Its coat was sleek and shiny, its limbs powerful. It was more beautiful than any other horse in the stables, clearly a fine breed.","“What a magnificent horse,” Second Master said. “Is it docile now?”","“Only to a limited extent. Still a bit of a rogue,” Zhou Yuanshen replied.","Cheng Zhao laughed. “Just like Fifth Brother.”","Zhou Yuanqi: “...”","It had been a while since he last taunted his sister-in-law, and now she was teasing him along with everyone else.","Before he could react, his parents burst out laughing first. Zhou Yuanqi’s face puffed up in frustration, which only made Second Madam laugh harder.","Joyful laughter filled the entire mansion as they toured every corner. They even stayed for lunch.","The mansion was staffed by only six retainers, and the meal was cooked by Vice General Meng’s wife.","It was homestyle fare—not particularly refined—but delicious.","Cheng Zhao ate a full bowl.","Second Madam rewarded the cook with a gold ring, explaining the reason: “The food was excellent, and the Young Madam enjoyed it greatly.”","In the afternoon, they returned to the Fan residence.","They stayed there for two more nights before heading back to the Duke of Chen’s estate.","Upon arrival, everyone first paid their respects to the Dowager Madame.","The Dowager Madame’s complexion was rosy, and she said cheerfully, “Each of you is glowing. It seems the General’s mansion has nourishing feng shui.”","She sounded genuinely complimentary.","Her manner was cordial, her words sincere—nothing seemed amiss.","But Second Madam glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao responded with a proper and respectful smile. “The feng shui elsewhere is refreshing for a change, but home is always better.”","The Dowager Madame smiled again.","The earlier disputes seemed to have been settled with the Dragon Boat Festival as the cutoff.","First Madam and Huan Qingtang also came to pay their respects.","The two of them were equally deferential. First Madam even seemed a bit afraid of the Dowager Madame, her smile almost ingratiating.","“... Your eldest aunt-in-law must have been scolded, and she took a hard blow,” Second Madam said as they walked back.","Song’s demeanor was completely off.","“She went too far in targeting her daughter-in-law. Grandmother won’t tolerate that,” Cheng Zhao said.","Then she added, “As a mother-in-law, eldest aunt-in-law’s behavior borders on cruelty. She’s lost her composure, which is why she’s baring her fangs, Mother.”","Second Madam sighed deeply. “Who could have imagined it?”","She felt a flicker of sympathy for the main branch and their in-laws.","But then she recalled the time she had felt sorry for Mu Jiang after her miscarriage, only to have her kindness thrown back in her face. Second Madam realized that wasting compassion on those who didn’t deserve it was simply foolish.","She didn’t want to be foolish anymore—it would only hold her children back.","She mulled over all the wicked deeds the First Madam had inflicted upon her over the years. There were so many, countless ones easily coming to mind, each and every one enough to make one gnash their teeth in fury. The Second Madam's heart instantly turned cold and hard, and she let out a quiet snort. The Second Master gave a faint, subtle smile."]},{"number":144,"title":"Chapter 144: I Have Already Apologized","paragraphs":["The Dragon Boat Festival had passed, and the weather was warming up.","At times, it was even hot.","The Chen Duke’s household always prepared seasonal clothing one step ahead.","Just as early summer arrived, they were already making autumn garments.","The old woman from the sewing room brought a fabric sample book to the Crimson Cloud Courtyard for the Second Madam to choose from; Cheng Zhao happened to be there as well.","The silks and satins in this book were darker in color, meant for the Second Madam; the First Madam and the Dowager Madame also wore these.","“...These brocade silks are favorites of the Dowager Madame and the First Madam,” the old woman said.","The implication was clear: don’t pick the same ones as theirs to avoid unnecessary trouble.","A small piece of advice could save a lot of hassle.","The Second Madam instinctively curled her lips in disdain. Did she really need more clothes? Her dowry brought in a considerable income.","Her mother also frequently sent her money.","What she coveted from the Zhou family was the prestige of the Duke’s household, which benefited her sons socially. For men traveling outside, the status of a family with meritorious service was invaluable—something money couldn’t buy.","However, the Second Madam’s peripheral vision caught sight of her daughter-in-law sitting quietly nearby, and she thought of her subtle speech and graceful manner.","That little troublemaker was hard to deal with. The Second Madam could spar with the Dowager Madame and the First Madam, but why waste energy complaining in front of a well-meaning housekeeper and airing her grievances about her mother-in-law and sisters-in-law?","If she insisted on doing so, not only would her mother-in-law and sisters-in-law disrespect her, but even the housekeeper would look down on her.","The Second Madam had truly learned many lessons from Cheng Zhao.","Some of these lessons were quite simple, yet she had never understood them before, which explained why she had suffered setbacks everywhere.","“...I really wasn’t paying attention. Fortunately, you pointed it out,” the Second Madam smiled. “The Hangchou embroidered floral fabrics are beautiful. I’ll take these few.”","She pointed to a few samples.","The old woman from the sewing room then brought out another book for Cheng Zhao to choose from.","While Cheng Zhao was selecting, two other embroiderers came to measure the Second Madam.","Cheng Zhao decided to place her order here, without bothering to go to the Luxuriant Splendor Courtyard.","Once everything was settled, the Second Madam asked Nanny Fan to escort them, and each person was given a red envelope containing a silver ingot as a tip.","“Mother Qi, when you spoke to the Second Madam just now, you really scared me. She’s not very gracious. If she had taken your words badly and made a fuss, the First Madam would have scolded you for stirring trouble,” one embroiderer said.","Mother Qi smiled, “That’s unlikely. The Third Young Madam was right there. She understands the situation. Besides, the Second Madam rewarded us, so she won’t cause trouble.”","“Yes, I was a bit nervous, but the Second Madam has really changed. She’s much more composed now,” another embroiderer added.","The Second Household was clearly improving.","It wasn’t just that the Third Young Madam was capable; the Second Madam had also matured.","Compared to their mother-in-law and daughters-in-law, the First Madam Song had grown increasingly irritable lately; the Eldest Young Madam was sullen, seeming like a schemer.","The kindness and gentleness displayed by the head of the family and her daughters-in-law were all façades, and the masks seemed on the verge of cracking at any moment.","“Dowager Madame really should hand the household over to the women of the Second Household,” one embroiderer said.","Mother Qi sighed, “Dowager Madame is like a living Bodhisattva. How could she possibly manage all that?”","The Dowager Madame’s kindness and charity remained unchanged. She was always extremely generous and compassionate toward the servants and her grandchildren.","The men’s autumn garments were chosen and measured in the outer courtyard.","Zhou Yuanshen returned to Nonghua Courtyard rather late that day, delayed by some matters in the outer courtyard.","However, peaches and cherries had already been brought in.","Cheng Zhao, having just entered the Gui Water phase, felt a bit queasy and thought some sweet and sour fruit would whet her appetite.","“...Have they sent anything to Jiangyun Courtyard?” Cheng Zhao asked the young servant Nanfeng who had just come in.","Nanfeng replied, “No.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","What’s the use of having sons after all? In the mundane world, sons inherit titles, and the future of the old marquis depends on his son, but beyond that, they’re utterly meaningless.","Cheng Zhao hurriedly called for half of the fruit to be sent to Jiangyun Courtyard.","Second Madam sent back some pastries in return.","Nanny Li washed the cherries, and Cheng Zhao sat on the kang by the window, flipping through the ledger on the gatehouse while eating. Before long, the plate was empty.","Her appetite whetted, by the time Zhou Yuanshen came in, she was already ravenous.","But he wasn’t alone.","Behind him followed two rough-looking maids and the young servant Nanfeng.","Each of them carried several bolts of cloth.","Cheng Zhao’s eyes immediately caught sight of a pale purple fabric, resembling the cloth from Wu County—the same material as the jacket she had soiled.","She paused in surprise.","Nanny Li and the others were also slightly taken aback.","“Put it on the kang in the east side room,” Nanny Li instructed the maids.","The colors were rich and piled high on the kang. Several senior maids from Nonghua Courtyard came over to take a look, while the maids stood by, not leaving.","“...The workshop you mentioned, the best cloth this year—I picked out ten bolts,” Zhou Yuanshen said with a calm expression. “Take a look, do you like it?”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Suppressing the joy in her heart, she maintained a composed demeanor, “You sent someone to Wu County to buy this?”","“Yes,” Zhou Yuanshen replied. “The new silkworm season just came in, the best batch of fabric. I just found out that the tribute cloth to Wu County also comes from that workshop.”","“My second sister-in-law is the granddaughter of the Grand Princess of Rong’an. Although the Grand Princess has passed, the Yin family still holds influence in Wu County, so the workshop gives them the best first,” Cheng Zhao explained.","She flipped through the fabric laid out on the kang. “They didn’t cheat you. This batch is truly excellent.”","“As long as you like it,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","“Is this an apology gift?” Cheng Zhao teased him.","Zhou Yuanshen looked at her quietly, expressionless, but in front of the entire room full of servants, he leaned in close, almost whispering in her ear, “I already apologized the night I kissed you.”","Cheng Zhao’s face flushed instantly.","She clearly felt a wave of heat rise, burning hot.","Ignoring everyone in the room, she glared fiercely at him.","He straightened up, his dark eyes calm and bright. “This is a gift for the Duchess. It’s modest, so please don’t despise it.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","This man was pushing his luck.","Not only was he disrespecting her, but he was also teasing her!","She didn’t even know where her battlefield was losing ground. All she knew was she was retreating step by step, losing ground and territory.","It was as if he had found the key to controlling her, while she had yet to discover his weakness.","He was so good at putting on airs; and because his personality was a bit like Cheng Zhao’s father’s, she felt intimidated by such a man.","She wasn’t strong yet.","She hadn’t subdued her Fourth Brother, nor had she tamed Zhou Yuanshen. Her heart was frustrated and furious, desperate to go complain to her grandfather and throw a tantrum.","But if things continued like this, her prestige would drop another notch, and she would never have a chance to turn things around. So she simply gritted her teeth and sneered, “Thank you, Lord Duke.”","Nanny Li standing nearby couldn’t help but laugh.","“Let it be for now,” Cheng Zhao said. “We’ll divide it again tomorrow morning.”","The attendants withdrew.","Back in the inner chamber, Cheng Zhao refused to pay any attention to Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen asked her, “Still upset?”","Cheng Zhao felt stuck, unable to rise but unable to fall.","If she said she was upset, it would sound like she was throwing a childish tantrum.","But if she wasn’t upset, he would only become more greedy.","“Zhou Yuanshen, you—how could you say such nonsense just now!” Cheng Zhao couldn’t hold back any longer and gave him a light punch on the shoulder.","Zhou Yuanshen pulled her into his arms.","Cheng Zhao fell into his embrace and, taking advantage of the moment, punched him two more times, trying to vent her frustration.","“Cheng Zhao, if you’re not upset, then kiss me,” he said.","Cheng Zhao was taken aback, her eyes wide. “Are you crazy?”"]},{"number":145,"title":"Chapter 145: The Restless Concubine","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao’s face changed with fright.","She would never do such a thing!","Even if she experienced the utmost pleasure, she considered it extremely base in her eyes—if Zhou Yuanshen was willing to take the initiative, that was his choice; Cheng Zhao couldn’t understand him.","She had never begged him for that kind of thing...","Zhou Yuanshen seemed to pause for a moment.","The corner of his lips curled slightly, a rare hint of a smile appearing on his face: “Kiss me.”","He pointed at his own lips.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","She was silent for a while, then hit him twice more: “Zhou Yuanshen!”","Zhou Yuanshen’s eyes curved gently: “You were the one imagining things, and yet you blame me.”","He lightly pecked her on the lips.","After washing up and getting into bed, Cheng Zhao turned her back to him and wrapped herself tightly in the quilt.","The early summer night was a little warm; the blanket only needed to cover half her body, but she had bundled herself up too tightly and was starting to feel hot.","Zhou Yuanshen had covered the lampshade and lay quietly.","Cheng Zhao stayed wrapped up for a while, nearly breaking into a sweat, then quietly stretched her feet out to cool down.","Zhou Yuanshen: “...”","Most of the time, she was an adult, steady and capable in her actions; occasionally, she had a touch of childishness. This rare innocence was especially precious and endearing.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t move, not wanting to disturb her.","The night passed without a word.","The next morning, Cheng Zhao first went to Crimson Cloud Courtyard to pay her respects, then to Chengming Hall.","By mid-afternoon, after finishing her duties, she returned to Nonghua Courtyard and began distributing the silks.","“Tell them these are from Wu Commandery. These two bolts of light blue are for Grandmother; this one for Eldest Aunt, and this one for Eldest Sister-in-law,” she instructed the maidservants.","Then she added, “The remaining six will be split evenly between our courtyard and Crimson Cloud Courtyard.”","She also said, “This bolt of sapphire blue looks lovely—it can be made into clothes for Yuanqi. It’ll come from my share; send it over to Yuanqi’s place.”","Everyone acknowledged her orders.","After the fabrics were sent out, the Longevity Courtyard sent back two gold hairpins inlaid with red gems for Cheng Zhao; First Madam and Huan Qingtang returned with some pastries.","Second Madam called Cheng Zhao to her place and brought out some expensive fabrics from her own storage, telling Cheng Zhao to choose a few bolts as well.","“I have more than enough fabric,” Cheng Zhao said.","“It’s about courtesy and reciprocity. The servants and maids are watching—you need to pick a couple,” Second Madam insisted.","Cheng Zhao smiled mockingly: “Mother, you’re learning to be hypocritical.”","Second Madam: “... Those two rascals tease me, and now you’ve gone bad too.”","She added with a sigh, “I used to think, between mother-in-law and sisters-in-law, who doesn’t know whom? Why pretend? But I forgot that we’re running a household as a big family.","Even if mother-in-law and the sisters-in-law understand, the servants and maids have their own stories. That false courtesy—it’s all for show.”","“Yes, Mother has had an epiphany,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam then praised the cherries and peaches Cheng Zhao had sent over, saying they were delicious.","That evening, the men returned to the inner courtyard.","The family of five had their evening meal together.","Suddenly, Second Madam said, “June is Zhao Zhao’s birthday. It’s her first birthday since marrying into our family, so we should make it lively.”","Second Master and Zhou Yuanqi looked at Cheng Zhao.","“Which day in June is Zhao Zhao’s birthday?” Second Master asked.","Zhou Yuanshen answered: “The twelfth of June.”","Everyone turned to look at him.","“Eldest Sister-in-law told me,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao nodded: “Yes, I share the same birthday as my Eldest Sister.”","Then she added, “Generally, by the twelfth, it’s already the peak of summer—the hottest time of the year. I don’t really like having birthday banquets; it’s too miserable.”","Second Madam laughed: “Then you and your Eldest Sister are quite fated! To be born on the same day as sisters—such coincidences are hard to come by.”","Cheng Zhao nodded.","That was also why she was very close with her Eldest Sister.","Regarding the birthday banquet, Cheng Zhao said she didn’t want to disturb others; just a simple dinner meal in Crimson Cloud Courtyard would be enough.","“Everyone is annoyed in the heat. If I make a big fuss and invite people, they’ll curse me behind my back,” Cheng Zhao said.","It wasn’t that she disliked showing off, but she only enjoyed celebration when it made everyone delighted and willing.","Not when they had to endure the heat just to humor her.","“That makes sense,” Second Madam said. “Having a birthday that brings curses isn’t very auspicious. Zhao Zhao, you’re perceptive.”","“Then we’ll celebrate it in Crimson Cloud Courtyard,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","That settled the matter.","“Is there any birthday gift you’d like?” Zhou Yuanqi asked her.","Cheng Zhao: “If you obediently say a few nice words, that’s already a very good gift.”","Zhou Yuanqi: “Who do you think I am? I’ve got money.”","“He has money—Grandmother keeps stuffing cash into his pockets. Zhao Zhao, tell him to buy you a set of red-gem head ornaments,” Second Madam said with a laugh.","“That can be done,” Zhou Yuanqi said, very much like a little adult as he nodded.","Cheng Zhao: “I don’t like red gems; I prefer gold pieces.”","Zhou Yuanqi: “...”","The matter of Cheng Zhao’s birthday was only discussed within Crimson Cloud Courtyard and not spread outside.","Another day passed, and when Cheng Zhao went to Chengming Hall to handle business, she saw a rough servants’ woman carrying cherries to Dawn Courtyard.","That servants’ woman was from the outer study, the same one who had moved bolts of fabric to Nonghua Courtyard before. Cheng Zhao remembered her. The woman bowed, and Cheng Zhao glanced into her basket.","Cheng Zhao had been eating cherries for three days.","No matter how delicious something was, eating too much made one uncomfortable—her teeth ached and her stomach soured.","This basket of cherries wasn’t as big as last time, nor did it look as good.","Cheng Zhao thought to herself: “Is Zhou Yuanshen trying to fatten me up like a pig?”","If she didn’t eat, it would be rejecting his good intentions; if she ate, she couldn’t stomach it. She frowned slightly.","However, that day when she returned to Nonghua Courtyard, she didn’t see any cherries.","No one had sent them over.","Cheng Zhao was slightly surprised.","If the cherries weren’t for her, was Zhou Yuanshen supposed to eat them himself?","They didn’t even look very nice to eat.","The main kitchen had some trivial issues, and Cheng Zhao quickly forgot about the cherries.","Two more days went by. At noon, Cheng Zhao went to Dawn Courtyard for a rest, and Zhou Yuanshen happened to be home.","“I came back this morning,” he said.","The page boy Nanfeng went to pick up the lunch, while Suyue and Qiubai also went to the main kitchen.","The couple sat facing each other, making small talk.","When Nanfeng, Suyue, and Qiubai came back in, they heard the couple chatting at the doorway.","Then, Suyue gave Nanfeng a meaningful look.","“My Lord, Young Madam, Miss Wang is at the entrance. She wants to come in and serve you both during the meal,” Nanfeng said, forcing himself to speak.","Cheng Zhao looked up.","In the noble mansion, there were no “Miss” relatives for this kind of role.","Someone addressed as “Miss” was either a visiting guest or a senior maid— a term of respect, not family.","Cheng Zhao thought back to that basket of cherries and the two concubines or maids in Bright Scenery Courtyard who hadn’t been dismissed yet.","She glanced at Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen remained expressionless: “Tell her to go back.”","Then he added, “And inform the Longevity Courtyard that this person is causing trouble by trying to force her way into Dawn Courtyard. Ask Grandmother to send someone to teach her proper rules.”","Cheng Zhao’s anger immediately subsided, and she was even a little puzzled.","Suyue and Qiubai laid out the meal in silence.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen ate lunch together. After the meal, she was about to lie down and rest her energy when someone arrived from the Longevity Courtyard.","The Dowager Madame summoned Zhou Yuanshen to the Longevity Courtyard.","She didn’t call for Cheng Zhao.","Suppressing her curiosity, Cheng Zhao went to Chengming Hall for her afternoon duties as usual.","“...Sister-in-law, is your birthday coming up soon?” Huan Qingtang suddenly asked.","Cheng Zhao: “Not yet, Eldest Sister-in-law.”","“I vaguely remember it is in June?” Huan Qingtang pressed.","“Yes.”","“June weather is hot,” First Madam remarked.","She clearly didn’t want any big birthday banquet; the heat annoyed her, so she looked at Cheng Zhao impatiently.","“If your birthday is in June, holding a night banquet would be better,” Huan Qingtang said with a smile.","First Madam gave her a startled look.","This flattery to set Cheng Zhao up for a fall was all too obvious. In the entire mansion, who other than the Dowager Madame had the privilege or status to hold a night banquet?","A night banquet was a big event, requiring an exceptionally heavy protocol."]},{"number":146,"title":"Chapter 146: Zhou Yuanshen Brings Blessings to Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao listened quietly to Huan Qingtang’s words.","The First Madam kept silent, so Cheng Zhao had to take over the conversation: “It’s not a major birthday, Sister-in-law. Going too grand would be inappropriate instead.”","She added, “The Concubine had a miscarriage not long ago, and the Dowager Madame is not in the mood. I wasn’t planning to hold anything either.”","She extricated herself neatly.","Hosting a night feast for her birthday depended on whether the noble families would grant the favor of attending.","Not only was holding a night feast a major event, but going out for one at night was equally significant.","Such feasts were only held on important days like the Lantern Festival or the Mid-Autumn Festival.","Of course, given the Dowager Madame’s status and position, if she were to arrange a birthday celebration for her granddaughter-in-law during the summer heat, noble ladies would undoubtedly respond to the invitation.","Such an affair would require the Dowager Madame’s personal invitation.","After the tasks at the Hall of Inherited Brightness were finished, Cheng Zhao left, but the First Madam, Madam Song, kept Huan Qingtang behind.","“...Why do you have to put on such airs? If she really holds that night feast, she’ll only gain even more power over us.” Madam Song fumed resentfully.","Huan Qingtang replied, “Mother, since it’s her birthday, we can’t pretend we don’t know. I merely told her it would require considerable effort.”","“You deliberately stirred up her ambition, hoping she’d go and speak to your grandmother. I could hear it clearly, and she’s not stupid,” Madam Song countered.","Unhurried, Huan Qingtang spoke in a calm tone: “Mother, if there were no ambition to begin with, how could it be stirred?”","With this, she had killed two birds with one stone.","On one hand, she gave Cheng Zhao a difficult problem, letting her struggle, increasing the chance of mistakes. On the other, she subtly provoked her own mother-in-law.","The First Madam wanted to lose her temper again but held back, fearing the Dowager Madame.","In a short time, Huan Qingtang had transformed from Madam Song’s confidante into the Dowager Madame’s lackey—an utterly repulsive change.","Madam Song retreated angrily to her inner chambers.","Cheng Zhao returned to the Luxuriant Fragrance Courtyard.","Nanny Li had heard from Suyue about “Miss Wang,” the chambermaid in the Splendid View Courtyard.","“...Please do not be angry. That was a gift from the Dowager Madame, so the Lord Duke had to put in an appearance at least once,” Nanny Li consoled Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao said, “I am not angry. I was just thinking about something.”","That basket of cherries seemed deliberately designed to give the chambermaid in the Splendid View Courtyard a glimmer of hope. And indeed, that chambermaid had shown up at the Morning Radiance Courtyard, attempting to gain recognition in Cheng Zhao’s presence.","At first, Cheng Zhao did not understand. Only later, when Huan Qingtang brought up the night feast, did she see the connection.","She realized Huan Qingtang’s intention was to push her into making a request about the feast.","And now, Cheng Zhao seemed to tie Zhou Yuanshen to both matters. Cheng Zhao did not wish to celebrate her birthday, much less think of a night feast. But what of Zhou Yuanshen?","In principle, Cheng Zhao could host a night feast, for she was also an ultra-rank titled lady. Yet she was too young and lacked the necessary prestige.","Without the Dowager Madame hosting it for her, inviting the noble ladies under her name, it would just be reaching for the stars.","“...Zhou Yuanshen really is skilled at manipulating people,” Cheng Zhao thought.","Not only toward herself, but also toward the Dowager Madame.","Cheng Zhao kept silent about the matter, not mentioning it even when she went to the Purple Bamboo Courtyard for the evening meal.","But once the topic was introduced, it could not be hidden.","The Second Madam asked Cheng Zhao again, “Do you like those overseas glass beads? There is a kind called glazed pearls—you can string them into a curtain.”","“Wouldn’t that be far too extravagant?” Cheng Zhao replied.","“It was on your birthday that I thought of it,” the Second Madam said. “I have some in my small storeroom, and I can ask your little uncle to buy more.”","Cheng Zhao said, “Thank you, Mother.”","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing.","Later that evening, back at the Luxuriant Fragrance Courtyard, Cheng Zhao could not help but ask him, “When you went to the Longevity Peace Courtyard this afternoon, what did you discuss with Grandmother?”","“Concubine Mu has been sent to the family temple. I want to tear down both the Splendid View Courtyard and the Jade Glory Courtyard to expand the inner study hall,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: “Grandmother won’t agree to that.”","Mu Jiang would come back eventually; even if she couldn't, as her father-in-law had said, a new person would fill her place, bear a child, and manipulate Zhou Yuanshen.","\"Mm.\"","\"Then you...\"","\"So I'm asking whether Grandmother would be willing to settle for the next best option and hold a birthday banquet for you. Summer has begun; we can't go out during the day, so a night banquet would be best,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","\"Cheng Zhao, are you afraid?\" Zhou Yuanshen asked her.","Preeminence could earn prestige, but it also attracted trouble and gossip, requiring even more wisdom and luck to handle.","Not only at her age of a teenager, but even at thirty, she might not be able to bear it.","Cheng Zhao shook her head. \"I'm not afraid.\"","\"Then have some new clothes made and some new hair ornaments crafted, and wait for your birthday,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"Lord Duke...\"","He looked at her.","\"Thank you,\" she said.","Zhou Yuanshen stepped forward and gently patted her head.","The bedroom grew warm for a moment. Cheng Zhao felt quite awkward and turned her head away. \"I need to wash up and change clothes.\"","She stood up and left.","Catching a glimpse of Zhou Yuanshen watching her in her peripheral vision, Cheng Zhao quickened her pace.","The contest between Zhou Yuanshen and the Dowager Madame yielded no results within a few days; the Dowager Madame had not yet agreed to hold a night banquet for Cheng Zhao.","Nor had she revealed any hints.","However, news of Cheng Zhao's birthday gradually spread.","Since Cheng Zhao managed the kitchen and the gatehouse and had handled a few things well, her small matters became big affairs within the household.","A few clever minor stewards made her shoes and socks and gave her pouches and handkerchiefs. Cheng Zhao accepted them all and instructed Nanny Li to reward them generously with a big red envelope.","A few more days passed, and the Second Madam found a box of drilled glass beads, each one sparkling, perfectly round, and smooth, resembling precious gemstones.","\"...One kind is a light, pale blue, very clear, and looks just like glass,\" the Second Madam said.","\"If we don't say anything, others will really think these are sapphires,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"The quality is excellent, Mother.\"","She had some glass items herself, but none with beads of such outstanding quality.","Second Madam: \"If this box were full of sapphires, it would be enough to buy half a county's land. I'm not that wealthy.\"","At that, both mother-in-law and daughter-in-law burst out laughing.","The Second Master and Zhou Yuanshen returned first.","Cheng Zhao wanted to show them the beads.","Zhou Yuanshen picked one up: \"Overseas glass is even better than Western glass. Not bad.\"","\"String them into a bead curtain; the shimmering colors would be beautiful,\" said the Second Madam.","\"Yes, you could make a bracelet,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: \"Though glass is pretty, its value is low. Wearing glass beads would be beneath a Duchess's status.\"","\"If someone asks, I'll just say these are sapphires.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","The Second Master said he couldn't tell sapphires from glass at all; Zhou Yuanshen added that the beads were a bit big, making them heavy to wear on the wrist and not worth the trouble.","While they were talking, Zhou Yuanqi came in.","He was carrying something wrapped in red silk awkwardly, struggling with its weight.","The Second Master casually stepped forward to take it: \"What are you carrying... a few catties heavy!\"","Zhou Yuanqi directed the Second Master: \"Father, put it on the table.\"","The Second Master obliged.","He lifted the red silk. It was a gold pig.","A plump pig, cast in gold, adorable and playful.","The Second Madam's eyes widened at the sight: \"Where did you get this? Did your maternal grandmother give it to you?\"","\"Mother, don't worry about that; I didn't use your money,\" Zhou Yuanqi said. \"This is my birthday gift for Third Sister-in-law.\"","\"And she was born in the Year of the Pig,\" he added.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","She stepped forward and weighed it in her hand. “It’s so heavy. How many pounds is this?”","“Five pounds.”","Cheng Zhao was speechless.","Who wouldn’t love five pounds of gold?","But who would want a five-pound pig?"]},{"number":147,"title":"Chapter 147: Rumors Arise Again","paragraphs":["The members of the second branch gathered around the golden pig.","Zhou Yuanqi’s eyes sparkled, wearing an expression that clearly said, “Praise me now.”","But the others had mixed looks on their faces.","Second Master was holding back a laugh, struggling so hard it was almost painful; Zhou Yuanshen touched the golden pig as if patting Cheng Zhao’s head, also looking amused.","Cheng Zhao seemed to want to say something but hesitated.","“What use is this thing?” Second Madam voiced the question for Cheng Zhao.","“It’s valuable,” Zhou Yuanqi said, dissatisfied. “And the symbolism is good too.”","“Displaying it is too tacky, but locking it away in the storeroom would be wasteful,” Second Madam said.","If it were any other gold piece, it could at least serve as decoration on major occasions—somewhat useful.","“Mom!” Zhou Yuanqi’s face scrunched up.","The boy was clearly unhappy now.","Cheng Zhao found herself torn between “indulging the child to keep him happy” and “offering guidance, as family should prioritize education.”","Most of the time, she didn’t want to kill the mood, but Yuan Qi was still very young and at a stage where discipline mattered.","The family had a duty to teach him what was right, to show him what was correct and what wasn’t, rather than spoiling him endlessly.","When indulgence and discipline blurred, a child could easily go astray.","In the brief pause after Second Madam’s words, Cheng Zhao had already made her decision.","She smiled and said, “I was chubby as a child too. This golden pig looks a bit like me. It’s the most precious and auspicious birthday gift I’ve ever received.”","Then she asked Zhou Yuanqi, “Aren’t you sad to part with it? This must have cost tens of thousands of silver taels.”","“Twenty thousand taels,” Zhou Yuanqi replied, a hint of his earlier pride returning, as if he were about to puff up his chest.","Second Madam gasped. “You’ve cleaned out your grandmother’s coffers!”","“I’m going to be Prime Minister one day, and Grandmother is happy to give it to me. The other grandchildren are hopeless—she doesn’t want to leave it to them,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","“Your grandmother won’t live to see that day,” Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanqi: “I’ll burn some paper money and tell her.”","Second Madam: “...”","“Truly precious! Thank you so much, Yuan Qi!” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi smiled, showing a row of little white teeth.","Second Madam looked utterly resigned.","After dinner, Zhou Yuanqi left first, and Cheng Zhao said to Second Madam, “Better he gave it to me than to some shady merchant.”","Second Madam couldn’t help but laugh. “You won’t be able to raise a child properly either. Your heart’s too soft to scold him.”","Cheng Zhao said, “I actually thought about whether to say something to him back then.”","“Then why didn’t you?”","Cheng Zhao replied, “His intention was to celebrate my birthday—out of affection and generosity, not to show off wealth. As long as his nature is good and his intentions are kind, it deserves encouragement. When he grows up, he’ll naturally understand right from wrong.”","No matter what she said, Second Madam always felt her daughter-in-law was “completely reasonable.”","Hearing this, Second Madam nodded. “Well said. He doesn’t really try to flaunt things; he buys whatever he thinks has use. He just has no sense of proportion.”","When he bought calligraphy books, it was because he wanted to practice, not to brag in social settings; when he bought makeup, it was because he thought his mother and sister-in-law would like it.","This golden pig was for Cheng Zhao’s birthday.","Zhou Yuanqi’s nature was kind and sincere, so there was no need to say much more.","Zhou Yuanshen carried the golden pig back to Nonghua Courtyard for Cheng Zhao.","The maids and older servants gathered around, eager to get a look.","“This is the first time I’ve seen such a big golden pig,” Suyue said. “Your maid’s eyes have been opened. Fifth Young Master is truly lavish.”","“No family would be extravagant enough to cast a pig out of twenty thousand taels of gold. I’ve never seen one either,” Cheng Zhao said with a laugh.","“Even thieves would have a hard time stealing it—it’s too heavy. It’ll become a family heirloom,” Qiubai added.","Cheng Zhao then said, “When he’s in his forties or fifties, I’ll bring it out to show him. The expression on his face then will be absolutely priceless.”","This was her “dirt” on his childhood.","The day she pulled out that old ledger would be amusing. By then, he’d definitely be a father, maybe even a grandfather.","They were in-laws and would be family for life; even in their middle age, the two households would still be bickering together.","Hearing this, Zhou Yuanshen glanced at Cheng Zhao.","He said nothing.","Nanny Li then remarked, “Fifth Young Master really went all out. With all his heart and mind, he truly respects you, his sister-in-law. What a wonderful child.”","“Yuan Qi is very good to me,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li stored the golden pig in the treasury. She placed it in the largest chest, nested it inside two layers of boxes, fastened three locks inside and out, and even sealed it with a strip of paper.","It was simply too precious.","That evening, as they were falling asleep, Zhou Yuanshen pulled her into an embrace.","“...You’re ten years older than Yuan Qi,” he said.","The comment came out of nowhere, and Cheng Zhao gave him a puzzled look.","“When he’s in his forties or fifties, you’ll be the Old Madam,” he said.","Cheng Zhao: “Yes.”","“That’s decades from now,” he said, as if lost in thought.","Cheng Zhao didn’t understand what he was so emotional about. When that day came, it would certainly be interesting—it was something she looked forward to.","Her eyes lit up slightly. “Decades from now, I’ll definitely have children and grandchildren all around me.”","Zhou Yuanshen tightened his arms around her. “That’s wonderful, Cheng Zhao.”","The May roses had bloomed, and the night breeze carried their fragrance, refreshing the mind and heart.","Zhou Yuanshen resisted the urge to kiss her and held her as he drifted off to sleep.","Cheng Zhao woke up in the middle of the night because she was too hot.","His body temperature was high, and his embrace was truly too warm.","A day later, Cheng Zhao’s period ended.","Zhou Yuanshen then let himself go unrestrained.","Cheng Zhao felt satisfied and didn’t bother complaining about being tired. The weather was warm, and their clothes were light, making her feel even more energetic and radiant.","Zhou Yuanshen gave Cheng Zhao a set of sapphire jewelry. One of the sapphires was huge—the largest that could be bought on the open market since anything bigger was reserved for the imperial palace.","A sapphire the size of a pigeon’s egg wasn’t allowed for the wives of officials outside the court; only those in the imperial harem had the right to wear them.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t overstep any rules—he gifted Cheng Zhao an expensive piece of jewelry.","There was also a string of sapphire beads.","“...Compared to this, glass doesn’t measure up after all,” Cheng Zhao said.","Even the highest quality glass couldn’t compare.","Precious stones not only had good color but also shimmered more brilliantly in the light.","“Of course,” Nanny Li said.","Then she added, “This jewelry set will also need two or three locks. Just this one sapphire alone is worth as much as the golden pig.”","“I still prefer the golden pig,” Cheng Zhao whispered.","She simply liked gold—its color and shine appealed to her more than gems. That was her personal taste, and there was no reasoning behind it.","Nanny Li was amused by her.","A few days later, word spread within the Duke’s mansion that Second Madam had given Cheng Zhao glass beads as a birthday gift.","“No matter how fine the glass beads are, they’re really not valuable. For her daughter-in-law’s first birthday, Second Madam’s pretty clumsy.”","Neither Second Madam nor Cheng Zhao had expected that the gossip would latch onto this as a point of criticism.","When Cheng Zhao received the beads, she was genuinely delighted; Second Madam had prepared the gift with full joy.","The two of them were happy, yet the servants turned against Second Madam behind her back.","All this talk was meant to ruin the relationship between Cheng Zhao and Second Madam.","There was no need to guess who was stirring the pot.","So Cheng Zhao had people spread the word that her little brother-in-law had given her a five-jin gold pig. Since the boy was so young, his gift could also be seen as coming from her parents-in-law.","The gossip immediately stopped.","Second Madam still heard about it and was very angry.","“Mother, if you get upset, you’ll be playing right into their hands,” Cheng Zhao said. “They’re doing everything to drive a wedge between us.”","Second Madam let go of her anger.","But then she started to doubt herself again. “Isn’t that glass bead too light of a gift?”","Cheng Zhao was speechless."]},{"number":148,"title":"Chapter 148: Compromise","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao had been comforting her mother-in-law, Second Madam.","“Mother, you can’t stop people’s mouths—they’ll say whatever they want. If you give something too lavish, like Yuan Qi’s gold pig, they’ll still curse you,” Cheng Zhao said.","“They’d curse that too?”","“Because you are the Second Madam, and above you are the Dowager Madame and the First Madam. The family hasn’t divided its property yet. The servants would say you deliberately sent something expensive to pressure the Dowager Madame and the First Madam.","They couldn’t possibly give something less valuable than yours, could they? After all, I’m the duke’s wife of the Zhou family, not an ordinary daughter-in-law. They’d have to make a huge sacrifice.","The servants would accuse us—mother-in-law and daughter-in-law—of joining forces to squeeze treasures out of the Dowager Madame and the First Madam, calling us greedy, selfish, and petty, as if we’d never seen anything nice.”","Second Madam was speechless.","“You see,” Cheng Zhao continued, “once someone wants to belittle you, no matter what you give, they’ll find fault.”","Second Madam finally caught on. “You mean someone is spreading rumors on purpose?”","“Yes,” Cheng Zhao said. “When someone is determined to slander you, it doesn’t matter what you send.”","She added to comfort her, “Those glass beads—I’ll string them up later and hang them at the entrance of the eastern side room where we live. I refuse to believe any relative or guest who sees them won’t praise them.”","This finally made Second Madam smile.","Cheng Zhao’s reassurance hit the mark.","“You’re right, Zhao Zhao. If we let every gust of wind rattle us, we’d be playing right into their hands,” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao held her hand. “I’m glad you understand.”","The matter of the gold pig had silenced some critics and partly restored the reputation of her husband’s parents, the second branch of the family.","Still, there were those who continued to gossip.","Who was behind it? No need to overthink—most likely the usual suspects.","At first, Cheng Zhao hadn’t wanted to host an evening banquet. But seeing the situation, she decided she ought to.","She needed to build greater authority to keep the servants and stewards in line, so that no one could throw a stone and send her and her mother-in-law reeling.","No matter how jealous the first branch was or how dissatisfied the Dowager Madame might be, Cheng Zhao had to hold power firmly in her own hands to suppress the troublemakers.","This was crucial because the “king of hell” always sends little devils to do its dirty work. If Cheng Zhao could crush the little devils, the king of hell would be helpless too.","“I need to visit Grand Princess Fukang’s residence. Have the gatekeeper deliver my calling card,” Cheng Zhao said.","“What for?” asked Nanny Li.","“I have a matter on which I’d like to ask the princess for help,” Cheng Zhao replied.","Nanny Li acknowledged the order.","Cheng Zhao wrote the calling card herself, and Nanny Li sent a young maid to take it to the outer gate.","Grand Princess Fukang’s estate promptly returned a reply, inviting Cheng Zhao to come the next day, where the princess would host a luncheon in her honor.","Zhou Yuanshen also announced that he would renovate his private study.","He needed to expand it.","First Madam asked the Dowager Madame, “Why does the duke want to change his study?”","The Dowager Madame replied, “He is young and has his own ideas. However, it would be best not to touch the study.”","First Madam was bewildered.","That day, the Dowager Madame summoned Zhou Yuanshen, who had just returned from court, to Shou’an Courtyard.","The grandmother and grandson exchanged a few casual words.","After dismissing the attendants, the Dowager Madame said to Zhou Yuanshen, “Things have been chaotic lately. The Zhou family should avoid adding more fuel to the fire.”","She added, “Last time a physician died in the Zhou residence, it already stirred up resentment.”","“It will only be female relatives from noble families, five or six tables,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “It’s Cheng’s first birthday since marrying into the Zhou family. We must celebrate it.”","“A luncheon would do just as well,” the Dowager Madame said.","“An evening banquet is fine too,” Zhou Yuanshen replied.","The two of them tested each other’s will.","In the end, the Dowager Madame relented.","“Then go ahead. But there must be no further trouble. You need to warn Cheng to be more restrained this time,” the Dowager Madame said.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “None of the Zhou family’s troubles have been caused by Cheng. Warning her is useless, Grandmother. Keep your own dogs on a leash.”","The Dowager Madame’s face turned pale with rage."]},{"number":149,"title":"Chapter 149: Sharp Tongues","paragraphs":["The Dowager Madame was thoroughly enraged by Zhou Yuanshen.","She had the small kitchen prepare some pastries and went to the palace to visit the Emperor.","The Emperor dismissed his attendants and chatted privately with the Dowager Madame.","On the surface, the Dowager Madame complained of grievances while secretly lodging accusations, saying that Zhou Yuanshen showed her no respect, had no regard for elders, and might even pose a threat to the Emperor.","“Your Majesty, I am truly worried for you. Yuanshen is too sharp and assertive; in time, he may overshadow his sovereign,” said the Dowager Madame.","The Emperor did not take it to heart. “Mother-in-law, why let an outsider harm the bond between you and Yuanshen? I know you are still troubled by the matter of Cheng Shi.”","The Dowager Madame paused. “Your Majesty knows of it?”","“Yuanshen told me,” said the Emperor. “He said he admires Cheng Shi’s beauty and held a birthday banquet to win her favor. His words were frank and open, and this depth of feeling in Yuanshen is quite like me.”","The Dowager Madame smiled kindly. “Who could compare to Your Majesty in matters of deep affection? You honor the younger generation too much.”","The Emperor was pleased.","They chatted for a while, with the Emperor showing the Dowager Madame both respect and reliance, even sharing a political matter from the Southern Frontier with her.","“If we were to dispatch troops, would it be appropriate to send Yuanshen?” the Emperor asked.","The Dowager Madame: “Yuanshen is the only adult male grandson of the Zhou family...”","“Yes, he cannot afford any misstep,” said the Emperor.","He sighed again, “It’s a pity I am not of your bloodline; otherwise, all the princes would be your grandsons, and you would not have to worry daily about the thinning line of descendants.”","The Dowager Madame only smiled.","On the carriage ride back from the palace, her face was clouded with gloom.","Nanny Sun kept comforting her.","“The Emperor now trusts Yuanshen greatly,” the Dowager Madame said, her face dark. “No wonder Yuanshen dares to threaten me, with no regard for seniority or propriety.”","Nanny Sun: “Do not fret, madam. He cannot surpass you in the end.”","“He attends court for half the month; he sees the Emperor more easily than I do. He will seize the chance to strike first and vilify me,” said the Dowager Madame.","Nanny Sun: “You are just upset, overthinking things. Calm your mind.”","“...Truly, I should never have chosen him to inherit the title,” said the Dowager Madame.","Nanny Sun soothed her, “At the time, there was no other choice.”","Zhou Yuanshen was not the Dowager Madame’s “best” choice, but her “only” choice. There was no alternative.","The Emperor lacked vigor. After the Zhou eldest grandson’s death, the title was left vacant, and the court eyed it covetously. What if it were demoted?","The Zhou family’s “hereditary and perpetual” qualification was not so secure either.","A male adult had to take the title to secure it.","The Second Master wouldn’t do—he was too old, and the risk was great; the other children of the Second Branch had not reached adulthood; an adopted child also wouldn’t work.","An heir would have to be young, waiting ten or eight years for him to grow up—by then, it would be too late.","“Dowager Madame, the Lord Duke’s attachment to Cheng Shi is a good thing. She is his weakness; you can use her,” said Nanny Sun.","The Dowager Madame closed her eyes slightly to rest. At this, she opened them.","She had been fixated only on Zhou Yuanshen and the Emperor’s favor toward him, forgetting he also had a vulnerable flank.","That entire Second Branch—old and young alike—were as fragile as paper.","“Nuanyu, you speak truly. I was fooled by my anger,” said the Dowager Madame.","Nanny Sun: “Every time you lose your temper, you fall into the Lord Duke’s trap. You must quell your fire.”","The Dowager Madame took a deep breath.","Upon returning to the estate, she summoned members of both the First and Second Branches to Shou’an Courtyard.","This included the Zhou Yuanshen brothers.","She announced joyfully, “On the twelfth day of the sixth month, we will hold a night banquet. Have the invitations sent out tomorrow.”","Everyone was stunned.","Except for Cheng Zhao, the others all showed shock.","The First Madam struggled to contain herself but finally spoke, “Mother, isn’t this too extravagant? The one named Cheng Shi is young, and it’s not a milestone birthday.”","The Second Madam frowned at her.","The Dowager Madame smiled, “It’s Cheng Shi’s first birthday since marrying into the family. When she turns twenty, we’ll skip the celebration.”","“When I married in, my first birthday was only—” The First Madam, Song Shi, tried to continue.","Huan Qingtang interrupted, her voice slightly raised, covering her words: “Congratulations to my sister-in-law!”","She added, “No wonder my Second Uncle and Aunt gifted my sister-in-law such an expensive present, a five-catty gold pig—it was an early celebration.”","The Dowager Madame acted as if she hadn’t heard of this before, smiling, “What gold pig?”","Cheng Zhao briefly described it.","Zhou Yuanqi looked somewhat proud.","“Truly a rare treasure. Keep it well,” said the Dowager Madame with a laugh.","She then spoke of the birthday celebration.","The Dowager Madame took the opportunity: “The busiest at a night banquet are the main kitchen and the gate. Both happen to be under your management, and as the birthday celebrant, you toil all year without rest. Why not take a break? Let your sister-in-law and your elder aunt handle your birthday.”","She glanced at Zhou Yuanshen. “Yuanshen, what do you think?”","Zhou Yuanshen replied calmly, “Grandmother’s kindness and concern for her juniors are appreciated by my wife and me. However, having my elder sister-in-law and aunt toil for Cheng Shi’s birthday would make her feel uneasy.”","Huan Qingtang interjected, “That’s no trouble. For my sister-in-law’s special day, I’d be happy to do more.”","“My elder sister-in-law has never managed these affairs; mistakes might occur in the midst of celebration, adding bad luck to a good day,” said Zhou Yuanshen.","The Second Madam glanced at him.","Cheng Zhao, like the others, listened silently, refraining from speaking rashly.","Huan Qingtang’s expression flickered, then she regained her smile. “Grandmother trusts my abilities, but you, Lord Duke, do not?”","The Dowager Madame interjected with a laugh, “Lady Huan does have considerable skill in household management.”","“Thank you, Grandmother,” said Huan Qingtang with a smile.","Zhou Yuanshen: “I don’t doubt Grandmother, but ‘practice makes perfect.’ My wife has managed the main kitchen and gate for some time; she can handle it without effort. Elder sister-in-law, if you truly want to show kindness to Cheng Shi, why not give some expensive head ornaments? Neither I nor my wife would refuse gratitude.”","Huan Qingtang had previously shaken off his words, but now she truly changed color.","Her face turned ugly in an instant.","The Dowager Madame’s smile vanished. “Yuanshen, why must you harshly criticize?”","“Gifts should be given according to what pleases the recipient to reflect true friendship. Grandmother, you said you also cherish your granddaughter-in-law. Yuan Qi gave a five-catty gold pig; you should give one too. Good things come in pairs,” said Zhou Yuanshen.","The Dowager Madame: “...”","She nearly smashed her teacup.","The carefully controlled situation was disrupted by Zhou Yuanshen’s few words.","The First Madam, Song Shi, seeing Huan Qingtang and the Dowager Madame cornered, should have stepped in to mediate, but she stood by, barely hiding her schadenfreude.","Zhou Yuanshen looked around, speaking slowly, “I was joking. A gold pig costs twenty thousand taels. Grandmother has so many juniors; how much silver would she have to spend? You need not take it to heart.”","He hinted that the Dowager Madame was stingy, hoarding all the wealth for herself.","The Zhou family title’s assets alone brought in an annual income of three to four hundred thousand taels from rents.","The Dowager Madame held onto the estate’s mountains of gold and silver, unwilling to part with the slightest portion of wealth or status.","After addressing the Dowager Madame, Zhou Yuanshen said to Huan Qingtang, “Elder sister-in-law, I was also joking just now. You are a widow, already living modestly; even if you gave a gift, we wouldn’t dare accept it.”","He brought up Huan Qingtang’s widowhood to subtly criticize her for not knowing her place."]},{"number":150,"title":"Chapter 150: Longing for Offspring","paragraphs":["Within the Shou'an Courtyard, there was a momentary deathly silence.","Second Madam didn't even dare to breathe.","Her son had achieved a complete victory, leaving the Dowager Madame and Lady Huan utterly humiliated. Second Madam was terrified the Dowager Madame would fly into a rage and make an issue out of her faults as a pretext for punishment.","She never quite understood the Dowager Madame's methods, always exposing countless vulnerabilities to her.","Thus, Second Madam shrank her shoulders, doing her best to avoid the Dowager Madame's gaze and preserve her son's victory.","The Dowager Madame did not lose her temper.","She simply looked calmly at Zhou Yuanshen.","After a long moment, the Dowager Madame said, \"It was just a few words, all for everyone's good. How did it turn into a quarrel?\"","\"We were just talking, not quarreling,\" Zhou Yuanshen replied.","The Dowager Madame waved her hand dismissively. \"From what I hear, you're quite dissatisfied. All of you, withdraw. I want to rest.\"","Leaning on Nanny Sun's arm, she stood up and retreated into the inner bedchamber.","The people from the Second Branch quickly left the Shou'an Courtyard.","Only after they had walked a considerable distance past the Chengming Hall did Second Madam finally let out a breath.","She said to Zhou Yuanshen, \"You have such a sharp tongue, and you're too harsh.\"","She truly hadn't expected this.","In Second Madam's heart, her eldest son was very intelligent but also very quiet, like someone who bore many grievances without knowing how to fight back.","Yet it turned out his words were so clear and precise, every sentence piercing the hearts and lungs of the Dowager Madame and Huan Qingtang.","\"Mother, Third Brother only spoke the truth,\" Zhou Yuanqi interjected from the side.","Then he added, \"And you were all complaining about my golden pig. If it weren't for that pig, you'd all be in trouble!\"","Not to mention others, but at least it \"warded off a disaster\" for Second Madam.","The matter of Second Madam giving colored glass to Cheng Zhao had pleased both mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, but someone had indeed used it as a handle to stir up discord everywhere.","It had forcibly driven a wedge between them.","Fortunately, Zhou Yuanqi's golden pig had salvaged Second Madam's dignity.","And just now in the Shou'an Courtyard, Zhou Yuanshen had also used the golden pig to silence his grandmother and widowed sister-in-law, preserving Cheng Zhao's authority over household management.","\"...Yuanqi truly has foresight,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Zhou Yuanqi raised his chin with pride, his mouth pursed arrogantly.","Second Madam longed to burst out laughing but feared that passing maids and servants might witness it and think she was gloating, so she held it in with effort.","The group returned to the Jiangyun Courtyard for the evening meal.","Within her own courtyard, Second Madam felt free to relax and laughed and talked openly.","\"Lady Huan isn't as sharp as before. She actually jumped out on her own this time; in the past, she always hid behind your elder aunt,\" Second Madam said.","\"Grandmother is using her,\" Cheng Zhao responded.","\"That old lady is truly impossible to figure out,\" Second Madam continued.","Since the main branch had no male heirs left, wouldn't it be better for that mother-in-law and daughter-in-law to relinquish the Chengming Hall and move to a side courtyard for some peaceful days?","Given the wealth and status of the Ducal Mansion, would Cheng Zhao treat them poorly?","In this dynasty, widowhood wasn't strictly confining; noble widows could go out socially and dress lavishly. Of course, they wouldn't be surrounded by all the attention like the lady who managed the ducal household's affairs.","Yet the Dowager Madame indulged them, even deliberately keeping them in the Chengming Hall, all because she disapproved of the Second Branch mother-in-law and daughter-in-law.","\"Old Madam wants her children and grandchildren to be obedient, but haven't we done enough?\" Second Madam asked again.","Cheng Zhao ladled her some soup. \"Mother, if you can't understand it, stop trying to.\"","\"I still feel stifled.\"","\"Just think of it this way: to us, Grandmother is a villain,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Why must she choose to act like a villain?\" Second Madam asked.","After all, they were her daughters-in-law and granddaughter-in-law—only not personally chosen by her. Did that justify being so malicious toward them?","\"Mother, good and evil together make up human nature. Evil is simply evil; it's a part of human nature from the start, like how we have eyes and also a nose on our faces. If you insist on asking why our faces have a nose, you're just tormenting yourself for no reason,\" Cheng Zhao explained.","The nearby Second Master and Zhou Yuanqi, father and son, couldn't help nodding in agreement.","Zhou Yuanqi said, \"Third Sister-in-law is not only beautiful, she's remarkably insightful.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Second Master added, \"What Zhao Zhao just said has enlightened me too. Having lived to this age, I still sometimes ask myself, 'Where does evil come from?'\"","Evil isn't something you catch from anywhere else; a person is born with it.","Secular conventions and family teachings teach people to control it; the deterrent of law forces them to conceal it.","Goodness is always praised, everyone agrees that it's innate, and they extol \"human nature is born good,\" never bothering to ask where it comes from.","Second Madam reflected on Cheng Zhao's words and nodded in agreement.","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing, only glancing deeply at Cheng Zhao.","After the evening meal, the young couple returned to the Nonghua Courtyard.","Zhou Yuanshen reached out and touched her head.","Cheng Zhao dodged him. \"You'll dirty my hair.\"","Then she added, \"You handled things quickly and wisely in the Shou'an Courtyard.\"","\"Mm.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","It wasn't that Zhou Yuanshen was inarticulate; he simply found Cheng Zhao beneath his notice, always speaking very little with her.","She turned away to wash up.","Neither of the young couple felt sleepy, so they sat by the large window seat playing chess, letting the gentle night breeze waft in while the room stayed cool.","\"It's going to be the sweltering summer soon; we'll need blocks of ice, won't we?\" Cheng Zhao said. \"It seems this courtyard doesn't have any copper-bracing for ice.\"","\"No, we'll place the ice blocks in brass basins,\" Zhou Yuanshen replied. \"The Chenhui Courtyard has that, so we could move there.\"","Cheng Zhao chuckled. \"If you hadn't insisted on celebrating my birthday, we could have expanded the Chenhui Courtyard to live more comfortably.\"","\"I never thought of expanding the Chenhui Courtyard,\" he said, setting down a piece. \"Cheng Zhao, I'm married. I have my own wife and my own courtyard. Why should I keep living permanently in the inner study?\"","Only unmarried men, or those at odds with their wives, spent all their time in a concubine's courtyard or in the inner study.","Cheng Zhao retorted, \"When I'm pregnant, it won't be convenient for you to still stay in the Nonghua Courtyard.\"","At once, Zhou Yuanshen captured a large swath of her pieces.","Annoyed, Cheng Zhao deliberately messed up the chessboard right in front of him. \"I'm not playing anymore. I'm sleepy.\"","Looking at the disordered go board, Zhou Yuanshen pulled her close.","Cheng Zhao nearly bumped into the low table on the kang.","He embraced her. As she started to resist, he whispered, \"This statue of the Child-Giving Guanyin doesn't seem to be doing its job. Should we get another one?\"","Cheng Zhao covered his mouth. \"Nonsense! That's blasphemy against the holy image!\"","Zhou Yuanshen kissed her.","With lingering, intimate kisses, their lips and tongues entangled. He murmured softly, \"When you do get pregnant, I'll serve you, Cheng Zhao.\"","The word \"serve\" carried a deliberate ambiguity.","Cheng Zhao wanted to retort that she had maidservants to serve her; what need did she have of him? How could his hands be as skilled as a maid's?","But as his mouth moved ambiguously along her snowy neck, the meaning was clear.","Now, everything she heard seemed to carry a hidden implication.","He had corrupted her!","\"Why don't you go see a doctor?\" Cheng Zhao blurted out. \"I just can't seem to get pregnant.\"","\"Alright,\" he readily agreed. \"I'll find a renowned physician sometime. Cheng Zhao, I'm just as eager for a child as you are.\"","The words were pleasant to hear.","Cheng Zhao melted into his arms.","The unpleasantness in the Shou'an Courtyard evaporated along with the slight marks from their efforts, fading from Cheng Zhao's heart.","The next day, before dawn broke, Zhou Yuanshen whispered in her ear, \"I have to go to the Capital Garrison Camp and will return in half a month. Cheng Zhao, I'll make it back in time for your birthday. Take care of yourself while I'm gone.\"","Cheng Zhao murmured an acknowledgment and went back to sleep."]},{"number":151,"title":"Chapter 151: Cheng Zhao's Acumen","paragraphs":["The weather grew hotter day by day.","The morning and evening winds held a slight chill, but by noon the ground was scorching.","The invitations for the Zhou family were written.","More than thirty families had been invited.","Each household's madame would bring one or two young married ladies, or unmarried daughters, to the banquet. There would be at least sixty or seventy guests, maybe over a hundred.","All invitations had to be sent out through the gatehouse.","It so happened that Cheng Zhao was in charge of the gatehouse, so she reviewed each one personally.","She kept aside two copies and headed to Shou'an Courtyard.","The sun was fierce in the late morning. Cheng Zhao felt as though flames were licking at her head, and a thin layer of sweat formed on her back.","The Dowager Madame was chanting sutras.","Nanny Sun instructed Cheng Zhao to go directly to the small Buddhist hall.","Cheng Zhao entered and knelt devoutly on the cushion beside the Dowager Madame, waiting for her.","After a moment, the Dowager Madame finished her chanting, and Cheng Zhao helped her by the hand to the main hall to sit and talk.","The Dowager Madame was not afraid of the heat. No ice had been placed in the main hall of Shou'an Courtyard, so the summer heat lingered without dispersing.","\"Grandmother, we have invited Princess Jiahe as well as Prince Rong's household,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Is there something wrong with that?\"","\"At the end of last year, Princess Jiahe's son was killed at a teahouse on the neighboring street. Rumors say it was the doing of a relative of Prince Rong's household,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Dowager Madame said, \"It wasn't a relative of Prince Rong's household, but a distant relative of one of Prince Rong's secondary consorts.\"","\"What if a conflict breaks out...\"","\"Then we cannot simply invite Prince Rong's household and not the princess,\" the Dowager Madame said. \"We'll seat them at separate tables at the banquet.\"","\"Grandmother, I checked the accounts at the gatehouse. Princess Jiahe hasn't had much dealings with our household. When you celebrated your birthday, she didn't even send a gift,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"I hadn't noticed that. She was heartbroken at the time and probably had no mind for social obligations.\"","\"Grandmother, aren't social obligations arranged by the stewards of the gatehouse? Even if the princess was distressed, it's unlikely the gatehouse would forget our household's gift,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She added, \"Princess Jiahe's husband is surnamed Guo, a cousin of the Empress. In my opinion, she simply doesn't hold our family in high regard.\"","The Dowager Madame said, \"You are indeed meticulous.\"","\"Then we won't invite the princess?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","The Dowager Madame gave her a deep look. \"You are not afraid of offending the Empress? The Empress and the princess are very close.\"","\"It wasn't us who slighted the princess first. She was the one who failed in etiquette. Even if the Empress questions us, we have reason on our side,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Dowager Madame nodded slightly. \"Then we will not invite Princess Jiahe.\"","Cheng Zhao acknowledged the decision.","She braved the scorching sun and went to Chenhui Courtyard for the midday meal.","The Dowager Madame sent someone to summon Huan Qingtang and the Eldest Madam Song.","This evening banquet was held under the Dowager Madame's name. Apart from a few close relatives, all the guests were of high status. The Dowager Madame only gave instructions regarding a few households; the rest of the guest list was drawn up by the Eldest Madam and Huan Qingtang.","When they presented it to the Dowager Madame for review, she had simply considered it from a political standpoint—which families should not be invited and which should be cultivated.","Princess Jiahe and Prince Rong's household had not openly fallen out with each other. The matter between their families had spread all over, and it would also be acceptable to avoid favoritism by excluding both.","\"...Who arranged this?\" the Dowager Madame asked indifferently.","Eldest Madam Song glanced at Huan Qingtang.","Huan Qingtang said, \"Grandmother, when Mother and I discussed it, I thought...\"","\"So it was you who arranged it?\" the Dowager Madame said, turning to Song.","Song quickly said, \"Princess Jiahe and Prince Rong's household haven't had a falling-out.\"","\"Speak the truth!\"","Eldest Madam Song hesitated for a long time before saying in a low voice, \"There is an unmarried daughter in the capital who has had her coming-of-age ceremony but is not yet betrothed—from Princess Jiahe's household.\"","\"Is your nephew in your natal family at the age to seek a marriage?\" the Dowager Madame asked.","\"Yes,\" Eldest Madam Song replied.","\"Then why didn't you send someone directly to speak with the princess?\" the Dowager Madame asked.","Song remained silent.","\"You knew the princess looks down on your natal family and wanted to use the Zhou family's prestige? You think so much for the Song family, but disregard the Zhou family. After you die, will you be buried back in the Song family ancestral graves?\" the Dowager Madame asked coldly.","Eldest Madam Song lowered her head even further.","\"Mother, your daughter-in-law knows she was wrong.\"","After the two left, the Dowager Madame let out a sigh.","In the inner courtyard, no one was sharper than Cheng Zhao. She was perceptive, intelligent, and detailed.","The only flaw was that she was disobedient and did not follow the Dowager Madame's will.","Therefore, no matter how many virtues she had, they were useless. What the Dowager Madame valued most was docility, and not challenging her authority.","\"...The sacrificial fields of the Cheng clan of Wu Commandery are far more extensive than we imagined,\" Nanny Sun said to the Dowager Madame.","The Dowager Madame had long since sent Nanny Sun to investigate this matter.","The Cheng family upheld literary traditions and had an excellent reputation, with renowned scholars and literary figures across several generations. Even the Dowager Madame had assumed that the Cheng family had a similar standing to the Song and Huan families.","But after dealing with Cheng Zhao, she realized she had been mistaken.","Though their prestige was comparable, the Cheng family's foundation was far beyond what the Huan and Song families could match.","\"No less than two hundred thousand mu,\" Nanny Sun added.","The Dowager Madame was greatly shocked. \"That much?\"","It was widely rumored that the Zhou family had more than two hundred thousand mu of hereditary estates, the largest among high-ranking noble houses, but very few knew that the Zhou family actually owned more than five hundred thousand mu of land.","Throughout the capital, no one matched the Zhou family in wealth and prominence.","Families like the Huan and Song families would consider themselves fortunate to have a few thousand mu of sacrificial fields.","But according to Nanny Sun's investigation, the Cheng family owned no less than two hundred thousand mu.","Among the high-ranking noble houses of the capital, this amount of land was enough to rank among the top.","\"No wonder this little monkey Cheng Zhao is so hard to deal with!\" the Dowager Madame said. \"Perhaps it's even more than two hundred thousand mu.\"","She then asked, \"They have accumulated this across several dynasties—quite an accomplishment. Did they indeed harbor Commandery Princess Zhaoyang back then?\"","Nanny Sun shuddered. \"Dowager Madame, why do you bring up that person again? You've been suspecting this quite a lot lately.\"","\"I never saw her corpse,\" the Dowager Madame said.","Without seeing it with her own eyes, she could not be at ease.","Though it was high summer, it felt as though a chilling wind had blown into Shou'an Courtyard, and countless goosebumps rose on the back of Nanny Sun's neck.","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law of the main branch returned under the blazing sun.","When they reached the entrance of Chengming Hall, Eldest Madam Song was filled with anger and said to Huan Qingtang, \"Go back first. Rest after lunch and come again later.\"","Huan Qingtang said, \"Mother, if you want to arrange a good marriage for your nephew, I have an idea, and it won't anger Grandmother.\"","\"Stop gloating,\" Song said, not trusting her.","Huan Qingtang said, \"Princess Jiahe lost her son and is in low spirits, possibly even half mad. There is no benefit for the Song family to form a marriage tie with her.","\"Instead, consider our neighbor Prince Rong's household. The prince's residence has several young ladies of marriageable age. Not only do they have good family status and substantial dowries, but they might even be granted the title of county princess.\"","Eldest Madam Song was somewhat intrigued, though her face still showed impatience. \"Don't I know that? But marrying a concubine-born daughter would still leave me dissatisfied.\"","\"Prince Rong's Seventh Daughter is fourteen this year and will have her coming-of-age ceremony next year. She is the legitimate daughter of the prince's primary consort, the full sister of the heir. I've heard that when she comes of age, the prince will petition for her to be made a county princess,\" Huan Qingtang said.","Eldest Madam Song's heart was stirred even more.","The status of one's natal family also bolstered a titled lady's prestige.","Eldest Madam Song already held the highest-ranking title, but because her natal family was somewhat lacking, she sometimes lacked confidence.","However, the Zhou family was her support, and she could strive for even more.","\"She will also attend this evening's banquet,\" Huan Qingtang added.","\"Come inside and tell me more,\" she said to Huan Qingtang."]},{"number":152,"title":"Chapter 152: Selfishness Hard to Conceal","paragraphs":["Before they knew it, it was early June, and the heat of summer had truly arrived.","The weather grew even hotter. Occasionally, even after nightfall, there was no cool breeze; it was only past midnight that the temperature finally dropped. Dawn brought a brief spell of coolness, but as soon as the scorching sun rose, the heat returned with a vengeance.","Everything at the Duke of Chen’s estate remained as usual.","Cheng Zhao’s birthday banquet was far less grand in scale than the Dowager Madame’s, and preparations were straightforward—it was just an ordinary feast.","The only difference was the need to prepare more lanterns and candles, along with fire prevention measures.","On the fourth day of June, a heavy rainstorm struck.","The next day, the weather cooled down slightly, with a light breeze stirring.","Cheng Chao paid a visit to the Duke of Chen’s estate.","“...My younger sister’s birthday is approaching, and my grandfather and parents wish to bring her back home for a day,” Cheng Chao said, calling upon the Dowager Madame.","It was a custom in the capital for a wife’s family to celebrate her birthday in advance.","The Dowager Madame naturally agreed with a smile.","Cheng Chao presented the Dowager Madame with generous gifts, including a centuries-old ginseng root; in return, the Dowager Madame offered several gifts of her own.","Cheng Zhao arranged the duties for the gatekeepers and main kitchen, informed her mother-in-law, gathered her maids, and returned with Cheng Chao to the Cheng residence for lunch.","Her eldest sister and third sister were already there.","During the meal, their grandfather and father were present as well, as the government offices were closed for the day.","Cheng Zhao thought of Zhou Yuanshen, who had gone to the capital’s garrison. If he had been home, he could have come along for a free meal.","After lunch, they went to their second sister-in-law’s courtyard, where the siblings gathered behind closed doors to talk.","Cheng Zhao spoke of matters concerning the Zhou family.","Somehow, the conversation turned to Princess Jiahe.","“I remember that the day Princess Jiahe’s son was killed was exactly the day I got married. Because of that incident, no one noticed that the Duke of Chen didn’t accompany me on my return visit,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Our family has no ties with Princess Jiahe’s household,” her second sister-in-law said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “The Zhou family doesn’t have deep ties with her either. I checked the gatekeepers’ records—on my wedding day, Princess Jiahe’s gift was quite modest.”","During the Dowager Madame’s birthday celebration, officials, noble families, and even princes and commandery princes from afar had sent gifts. Even Princess Qing’an, who had some grievances with the Zhou family, had sent one.","But not Princess Jiahe’s household.","Even if the mistress was too distressed to manage affairs, the gatekeeper’s steward should have delivered a gift.","Failing to send a gift on such a significant occasion, without any marital connection, could be considered a social severance of ties.","“...My aunt-in-law deliberately invited her just to spite me,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Her family probably wants to form a marriage alliance with the princess’s household. Princess Jiahe lost her son, but she still has a daughter,” her eldest sister remarked.","She added, “I’ve heard that the heir of the Changling Marquis’s Song family has been extremely picky about marriage matches, and many people are mocking them behind their backs.”","“I’ve heard the same,” said her third sister.","She continued, “The Song family even tried to seek a marriage with my mother-in-law’s family, the Duke of Weiguo’s daughter. The Duke of Weiguo’s estate didn’t even bother to respond.”","Cheng Zhao frowned slightly.","She hadn’t known about this.","“Even if the elder branch’s mother and daughter-in-law weren’t plotting against me, my aunt-in-law still intended to use my birthday banquet to find a high-born wife for her nephew,” Cheng Zhao said.","She added, “The Song family seems to have thin resources, even worse than the Huan family.”","Cheng Zhao felt it was rude to say, but she thought her aunt-in-law lacked foresight. At the New Year’s estate rent issue, her aunt-in-law hadn’t even spotted the problem.","When she said this, her eldest sister glanced at the second sister-in-law.","The second sister-in-law returned the look.","“What is it? Is there some secret I don’t know?” Cheng Zhao asked.","The second sister-in-law replied, “The Changling Marquis household truly has little foundation—they don’t even own a single patch of ancestral land.”","Cheng Zhao was taken aback. “How can that be? They have a noble title!”","A title came with granted lands, at least three thousand mu in one continuous parcel.","Even if they hadn’t originally owned such a plot, they would have worked to consolidate their holdings over generations.","But the Song family’s title had already passed through two generations—how could they have no accumulation at all?","“No one from the Song family has held an official post in court for two generations. They come from a scholarly background and may pride themselves on being above worldly matters, but they’re poor at managing affairs. That’s why high-ranking noble families refuse to marry into the Changling Marquis household,” the second sister-in-law explained.","She added, “Of course, some impoverished officials are still willing, but the Changling Marquis family considers them beneath their station.”","Cheng Zhao was speechless.","Staying overnight at her family home, Cheng Zhao and her siblings talked late into the night.","When she returned the next day, she shared all the secrets she had heard with Nanny Li and the others.","Nanny Li was just as astonished as Cheng Zhao.","“At this banquet, we must keep a close watch on the elder branch of the household. Don’t let them use my name to trick anyone into marrying into the Changling Marquis family,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li, Suyue, and Qiubai nodded in agreement.","“When an heir inherits a title, it’s downgraded. With no ancestral land, the Changling Marquis family really should marry a wealthy merchant’s daughter now to strengthen their foundation,” Nanny Li said.","A merchant’s family would gain a title, while the Song family would gain wealth—a natural match.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Even wealthy merchant families aren’t that unlucky.”","Nanny Li fell silent.","Soon, Cheng Zhao’s birthday arrived.","The Dowager Madame’s Shou’an Courtyard sent a glass-clad kang table—gorgeous and expensive—yet it shared the same origins as the glass items in Second Madam’s household.","“Grandmother put so much thought into this,” Cheng Zhao said.","The gift was both dignified and thoughtful, which surprised her.","From the elder branch came a yellow boxwood-based screen, also quite valuable. But from the painting on it, it had clearly sat in their storeroom for years.","Cheng Zhao accepted them all and went to thank everyone in person.","On the eighth day of June, Zhou Yuanshen finally returned from the capital’s garrison.","He had entered the city early in the morning again.","“Every time, he waits at the gate past midnight and comes in at dawn when it opens,” Cheng Zhao remarked to Second Madam with a laugh.","Second Madam replied, “Who would have thought he could be so attached to home?”","Cheng Zhao smiled.","By lunchtime, Zhou Yuanshen was already back from his post.","A small screen stood before the bed in the eastern side chamber of Chenhui Courtyard, blocking the view from outside and the light casting through the window lattice.","Cheng Zhao bit her lip as her hands fumbled to grip his shoulders.","Outside, sparrows chirped incessantly, hiding under the eaves to escape the heat, craning their necks curiously at the commotion inside until a sudden noise startled them into flying off into the blazing sun.","After bathing, Cheng Zhao sat by the window on the large kang, fanning herself.","Zhou Yuanshen glanced toward the inner room.","“...The bed’s leg moved out of place; it’s not broken, just bent. I’ll tell Nanfeng to call someone to fix it,” he said.","Cheng Zhao fanned herself vigorously.","She was flushed and sweating from the heat.","Her maidservant Qiubai brought in chilled fruits, frozen custard, and cold tea, setting them down on the small kang table before retreating.","“How have things been at home these days?” Zhou Yuanshen asked, sipping his tea.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Just the usual affairs, no disputes.”","“How many gifts did you receive? Let me see them later,” he said.","She agreed.","After two weeks apart, he seemed almost unfamiliar.","Cheng Zhao took a bite of the frozen custard, and after a long while, the heat within her mostly dissipated, and she finally felt a hint of relief.","“I’ve brought you something fun,” Zhou Yuanshen said, sitting beside her, calling for his page Nanfeng.","Nanfeng carried in a small box.","Cheng Zhao opened it, and upon seeing its contents, she found herself both amused and exasperated."]},{"number":153,"title":"Chapter 153: Cheng Zhao Steals the Limelight","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen gave her a small \"lantern\" doll.","Like a lantern, it had a handle and a string, with a doll dangling from the bottom.","The doll was very exquisite, also a pig, chubby and plump, with two small red gems embedded at the pig’s mouth.","It was also expensive, because the little pig was made of gold.","\"Are you competing with Yuan Qi?\" Cheng Zhao couldn’t help but laugh, holding the chubby little pig.","As she laughed, most of her sense of distance toward him instantly faded.","Even though their intimacy in the bedchamber had been fervent, the distance between them wouldn’t dissolve quickly just because of that—she hadn’t seen him for half a month, and now he felt unlike himself to her.","Cheng Zhao subtly, imperceptibly breathed a sigh of relief.","Then she added, \"This can’t compare either, it’s less than three taels.\"","The golden pig from Yuan Qi weighed a full five catties.","\"You hold it,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao did as he instructed.","When she gripped the handle and the pig drooped downward, wings suddenly sprouted from its back: golden wings, thin as paper, trembling slightly.","It swayed and wobbled, as if it were flying.","Cheng Zhao’s eyes lit up. \"What an ingenious little thing.\"","\"This isn’t from me, it’s from Little Uncle,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"He heard that Yuan Qi gave you a golden pig, and remembered a goldsmith with remarkable skill, no less than Master Wan, and asked him to craft this.\"","\"Little Uncle is thoughtful,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"The Fan family sent many gifts, and he still specially sent this. I must thank him.\"","Then she asked, \"Why didn’t he send it along with the Fan family’s gifts?\"","Zhou Yuanshen replied, \"Though you’re a younger generation, you’re still a young married woman. He had to avoid suspicion.\"","This clearly wasn’t an ordinary gift.","Fan Xiao was showing respect for Cheng Zhao, and also for Zhou Yuanshen, acting with proper boundaries.","\"Thank you, it’s delightful,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen picked up his tea and took a few more sips, saying nothing.","He still had matters to attend to later, so he told Cheng Zhao to return to Nonghua Courtyard in the mid-afternoon.","Cheng Zhao took Fan Xiao’s gift and showed it to everyone in Nonghua Courtyard.","Everyone praised it.","\"When Little Uncle has some happy occasion, we’ll need to send a substantial gift in return,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Is his birthday coming up?\" Suyue asked.","Cheng Zhao chuckled. \"What birthday? If he remarries, that’ll be a big event.\"","\"True,\" Nanny Li agreed. \"Has he settled on anyone?\"","\"Haven’t heard it mentioned.\"","That day, Zhou Yuanshen didn’t return to Nonghua Courtyard.","The maid Mingyu came to tell Cheng Zhao that Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the General’s Mansion on business and might stay there overnight.","Cheng Zhao, tired from the day, went to sleep alone.","Zhou Yuanshen was very busy; he only had time to come back to Nonghua Courtyard the day before her birthday.","However, Cheng Zhao was anxious about the banquet arrangements and didn’t talk much with him; he wasn’t one for idle chatter either, so the couple exchanged a few simple words and then went to sleep.","On her birthday, the Duke of Chen’s Mansion was bustling with activity. Although the banquet was in the evening, preparations started from before dawn.","Cheng Zhao got up early to dress and put on a set of gold head ornaments.","This set of gold headwear had no gemstones embedded, but the designs were unique and the craftsmanship exquisite—every piece was delicately carved.","From hairpins to earrings, they glistened brilliantly yet retained a artisan’s ingenuity, quite different from ordinary jewelry.","\"Truly beautiful,\" Nanny Li and the others praised her repeatedly.","\"I went to see Grand Princess Fukang last time and asked her to pass my card to Master Wan. He set aside his other work to make this set for me first,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","\"Grand Princess is really loyal,\" Nanny Li remarked.","Cheng Zhao said, \"This time I owe Grand Princess a favor. I’ll have to repay it someday.\"","\"Maybe we can repay it soon,\" Nanny Li said.","Cheng Zhao smiled.","As night fell, lanterns were lit all over the Duke’s Mansion; floor lamps made of translucent horn were placed every few steps. The moon in the sky cooperated, bright and clear, without a single cloud in the night sky.","Zhou Yuanshen came to Nonghua Courtyard to escort Cheng Zhao to the banquet hall.","He saw her wearing a vermilion blouse and a purple-red skirt, the gold ornaments adorning her black hair and slender white neck, shimmering with golden light.","\"Is it time?\" Cheng Zhao asked with a smile.","Zhou Yuanshen seemed to snap back to reality. \"Yes. Let’s go, the small carriage is ready.\"","As she boarded, he helped her up, carefully, afraid of disturbing her hair ornaments.","Cheng Zhao also took every step with care as she slowly got into the carriage.","The guests had all arrived.","Drums and gongs clamored from the opera stage.","Cheng Zhao stepped into the banquet hall amid the lights and noise, and every eye turned to her.","First, there was a moment of silence, then low murmurs erupted into lively discussion.","The banquet passed quickly, yet slowly.","From time to time, titled ladies asked her, \"Which master made these ornaments?\"","Cheng Zhao answered each time that Grand Princess Fukang had specially asked Master Wan to craft them for her.","\"Don’t just stare at the jewelry,\" Grand Princess Fukang said with the biggest grin. \"Look at her too!\"","A lady who was on good terms with Grand Princess teased herself deliberately, \"I want a set too, to wear when I turn forty.\"","\"Don’t make things hard for Master Wan,\" laughed Grand Princess.","Both of them chuckled.","One must first have Cheng Zhao’s looks to wear such pieces.","There were no mishaps during the banquet, except that Cheng Zhao’s maid Qiubai entered twice.","The first time, Qiubai whispered a few words in Cheng Zhao’s ear, and Cheng Zhao didn’t move. The second time, Qiubai spoke a few more sentences, and Cheng Zhao turned to Zhou Yuanshen, who then left the hall.","The banquet ended late into the night.","The dishes were excellent, the wine fine, the opera even better, and the fireworks spectacular.","There were no scandalous novelties, but the feast was joyful and lively—guests arrived delighted and left content.","Cheng Zhao, the Second Madam, and Huan Qingtang saw the guests off at the gate.","Suddenly, the Second Madam asked, \"Where is your Eldest Aunt?\"","Cheng Zhao shook her head.","The Second Madam then asked Huan Qingtang, \"And your mother?\"","\"She may be feeling unwell, went to freshen up. She’ll be along soon, Second Aunt,\" Huan Qingtang replied with a smile.","But even after all the honored guests had left, the Eldest Madam still hadn’t come.","The Second Madam felt a hint of doubt.","Still, she didn’t count on the Eldest Madam and didn’t think too much of it.","After seeing off the titled ladies, there were still some young wives, young ladies, and unmarried young masters who hadn’t left. The Second Madam handed them over to the stewards.","The next day, the entire capital city was discussing Cheng Zhao’s birthday banquet.","\"She was as beautiful as an immortal.\"","In no time, Master Wan’s orders were booked up two years in advance.","Everyone wanted to have the same set of head ornaments as Cheng Zhao’s.","Business at the city’s gold shops boomed.","Even the Empress heard about it and summoned Cheng Zhao to bring her head ornaments to the palace for a look.","Cheng Zhao tidied herself up and went to show the Empress.","They were dazzling under the night’s lantern light, but equally striking in the daytime.","Cheng Zhao was naturally beautiful, and the headwear only added to her charm. Everyone praised both the ornaments and goldwork, as well as the exquisite appearance of the Duchess of Chen.","\"Your Majesty, keep this set in the palace. You can have the goldsmiths copy a set for you, for your own amusement or to give as gifts,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","The Empress admired her tactfulness. \"Then I’ll give you a few pieces of jewelry in return. Leave this set here for now; I’ll send it back later.\"","Cheng Zhao bowed in acknowledgment, then went to a side chamber to change.","Three days after the banquet, at the height of the hottest days of the year, an event occurred at the Duke of Chen’s Mansion.","The Eldest Madam, née Song, moved out of Chengming Hall and into Clear Wind Courtyard, located west of Shou’an Courtyard.","The entire household was shocked."]},{"number":154,"title":"Chapter 154: Sudden Change","paragraphs":["Eldest Madam Song had moved to the Clear Wind Courtyard.","Not as a temporary stay, but a permanent relocation.","She made no secret of it, moving in broad daylight.","The weather was sweltering. The head steward, leading over twenty male servants and more than twenty rough-duty maids, helped the eldest madam move in a grand procession. Every single person was drenched in sweat.","Aside from the Western Flower Hall used for meetings, everything else was moved—including a few withered potted plants in the corner.","The people arriving for discussions were temporarily barred from entering the Hall of Inherited Brightness. They were all told to wait in the pavilion within the bamboo grove diagonally across.","The various stewards asked Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang to sit, while they themselves remained standing, either murmuring among themselves or wearing grave, silent expressions. Their eyes followed the male servants and maids coming and going from the Hall of Inherited Brightness.","“Why is she moving?” one steward couldn’t help but ask.","No one answered him.","Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang sat quietly. A quick-witted stewardess fetched a pot of cold tea and poured some for them.","Cheng Zhao did not drink the tea. She held a round silk fan in her hand, occasionally waving it to stir the breeze.","Huan Qingtang had no fan. She sipped the tea and dabbed the thin sweat from her temples with a handkerchief.","“...Where is the eldest madam?” another steward asked. “I haven’t seen her figure.”","Eldest Madam Song was not present to oversee the move; her whereabouts were unknown.","“What need would the eldest madam have to personally move things? She went early this morning to accompany the Dowager Madame in chanting Buddha’s name,” interjected Nanny Qin, who oversaw the main kitchen.","Cheng Zhao glanced at her.","Nanny Qin returned the look calmly, unafraid.","Cheng Zhao smiled faintly and did not spar with her.","The stewards’ hearts were pounding with their own speculations.","Eldest Madam Song had had several opportunities to move out of the Hall of Inherited Brightness.","For instance, when Zhou Yuanshen inherited the title, moving would have been reasonable—ceding the hall to the second madam. Everyone would have praised her generosity and magnanimity then.","Or when Zhou Yuanshen married, even if she merely broached the subject, it would have shown she was sensible and measured in her conduct.","Again, when Cheng Zhao was granted the title of Peerless Duchess of the State. Yielding the Hall of Inherited Brightness then would have been an act of elder’s kindness.","But she had done none of those things.","Perhaps the Dowager Madame had disapproved, and she dared not act. Still, she had never even brought it up.","The stewards had grown accustomed to this situation.","Within the ducal mansion, order was strict and no wicked servant dared bully their master, so there was no idle gossip behind anyone’s back.","It was widely assumed that Eldest Madam Song would live in the Hall of Inherited Brightness forever, or perhaps cede it to the eldest young madam, Lady Huan, after her dual heir marriage.","No one ever expected that, on the third day after the Third Young Madam’s birthday night banquet—just as the Third Young Madam was basking in glory—the eldest madam would move out.","In the hottest days of the year, during the dog days of summer. Even the resilient bamboo was wilting under the scorching sun.","This was not a voluntary move; it was a forced eviction.","She must have committed a grave mistake!","Occasionally, young maids would come to peek, their curiosity piqued by the whole affair.","“Eldest Young Madame, Third Young Madam.”","A small oil-covered cart pulled up, and Nanny Sun of the Longevity Peace Courtyard stepped down.","Her temples were slick with oil from the heat. She carefully wiped them with a handkerchief before greeting Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang.","As an elder by the Dowager Madame’s side, she held more prestige than many ordinary household masters, so Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang could not merely accept her greeting without standing to acknowledge her.","They quickly rose.","“The Dowager Madame requests your presence at the Longevity Peace Courtyard. Household affairs can wait for the next two days; the stewards may manage at their own discretion. In two days, all duties will resume in the Hall of Inherited Brightness,” Nanny Sun said.","Everyone acknowledged her words.","Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang went to the Longevity Peace Courtyard.","In the Crimson Cloud Courtyard, the Second Madam was deeply agitated.","The weather was unbearably hot. She had risen early to practice with her spear, breaking into a cascade of sweat, which pleased her greatly. After training, she bathed and soaked in tepid water for a good while.","Once she was ready, she draped her half-dried hair over her shoulders and sat down to casually drink some porridge—only then did she learn of the events at the Hall of Inherited Brightness.","She was stunned.","Whatever questions the stewards had, she had them all, too.","She had also sent clever young maids to the Hall of Inherited Brightness to investigate.","She very much wanted to go herself, but Nanny Fan dissuaded her: “What if the Dowager Madame loses her temper? You’d become the target again.”","In the past, the Second Madam would have said, “What’s there to fear? At worst, I’ll get a scolding; my mother-in-law has never held back from scolding me, but I still want to know what happened.”","Now, however, she had to consider her daughter-in-law’s dignity.","If she were reprimanded, her daughter-in-law would suffer along with her.","The Second Madam was so anxious she felt like scratching her ears and cheeks.","Nanny Fan kept urging her: “It’s almost time for the midday meal. The Third Young Madam will return soon. I’ve posted someone to keep watch; as soon as she reaches the Dawn Glory Courtyard, we’ll send a small cart to bring her here at once.”","Unexpectedly, the Longevity Peace Courtyard had already fetched her first.","The Second Madam paced back and forth in her room.","After much deliberation, she had Nanny Fan go to the outer courtyard to summon the Second Master’s attendant and Zhou Yuanshen’s adjutant, and send them to tell the father and son at their respective government offices:","“If there is no urgent matter, return to the mansion immediately—a major incident has occurred at home!” the Second Madam said.","The attendant and adjutant departed.","The father and son did not return, but Zhou Yuanqi came running back from the clan school instead.","The boy wore a linen shirt, his head and face drenched in sweat. His clothes were soaked through and plastered to his back; his little cheeks were flushed crimson, and streams of hot sweat dripped down his face, soaking his collar.","“So thirsty—I need cold tea!” he shouted.","The Second Madam hastily ordered a maid to bring him cooled tea water.","“You look as if you’ve been pulled from the water,” she said. “There was no need to run so frantically.”","“At the clan school, I heard something had happened, so I ran to the Hall of Inherited Brightness to look,” Zhou Yuanqi said. “Third sister-in-law wasn’t there—only the laborers doing rough work.”","The Second Madam was stunned. “You actually went to the Hall of Inherited Brightness... Weren’t you about to collapse from the heat?”","In this weather, running such a huge loop outside, his skin must be nearly burned raw. No wonder he looked like a boiled shrimp.","“Don’t just guzzle water,” she said. She ordered the maid, “Quick, fetch two heatstroke relief pills for the Fifth Young Master.”","The Second Madam instructed the maid to prepare warm water and dumped Zhou Yuanqi into a tub.","The water wasn’t cool—it was just right for washing off the sweat and dissipating some of the heat trapped in his body. She also draped a wet towel over his head.","“You can’t run like this in the sweltering morning heat. People have been known to die of heatstroke,” she scolded him.","Mother and son talked inside the washroom.","“Why was the eldest aunt driven away?” Zhou Yuanqi asked.","The Second Madam: “I only just heard about it. I’m waiting for your sister-in-law to come back—she should know.”","Zhou Yuanqi: “Mother, at the banquet two nights ago, there was one thing I knew that you didn’t.”","“What?”","“Third sister-in-law’s maid, Qiubai, seemed to have beaten the eldest aunt’s nephew from her natal family,” Zhou Yuanqi said. “Third brother handled it later.”","“Qiubai?”","Zhou Yuanqi: “I told you last time, didn’t I? Qiubai is quite skilled in martial arts. Most ordinary people can’t beat her.”","“How did she end up hitting a relative?”","“The eldest aunt didn’t say a word afterward—didn’t even dare complain about Third sister-in-law. And now she’s being driven out of the Hall of Inherited Brightness, so it must be that her nephew did something wrong,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","He grew curious: “What kind of wrong, though? Could he have stolen something?”","The Second Madam: “...”","A boy nearly nine years old, and the first thing he thought of for a grown man’s misdeed was theft.","Anything could be more serious than theft.","Zhou Yuanqi was arrogant, naive, and innocent—yet sharp-tongued and unforgiving.","The Second Madam’s heart softened, and she looked at him with deep tenderness. Yet she felt he would likely face hardship in the future.","Midway through, the Second Master returned.","He showed not the slightest curiosity. Upon hearing the news, his brow didn’t even twitch.","As always, he maintained a calm and detached demeanor, as if it had nothing to do with him, and said, \"What's the hurry? If it's something we're meant to know, they'll tell us sooner or later.\"","Second Madam: \"...\""]},{"number":155,"title":"Chapter 155: Kill three birds with one stone","paragraphs":["At dusk, Cheng Zhao arrived at the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","It was still hot outside.","The sun had not yet set behind the treetops, but the oppressive heat had lessened, and a faint breeze had stirred.","The rising heat from the ground still made it difficult to breathe.","Inside the Crimson Cloud Courtyard, blocks of ice had been placed, the bronze pillars were cool to the touch, and Zhou Yuanqi had taken an afternoon nap in the warm chamber of the main bedroom. The Second Master and Second Madam had not slept; the elderly couple sat in the secondary room chatting idly.","It was mainly the Second Madam who spoke, while the Second Master occasionally offered her a few soothing words.","Cheng Zhao did not come alone—Zhou Yuanshen followed behind her.","The young couple walked unhurriedly, with Cheng Zhao occasionally fanning herself as she went.","When the Second Madam heard from a maid that they had arrived, she hurriedly slipped on her shoes and came out from the secondary room to greet Cheng Zhao.","They took their seats according to rank in the main hall, and without even bothering to summon the meal, the Second Madam urgently asked Cheng Zhao, \"What exactly happened?\"","\"The night before last at the banquet, the eldest aunt’s senior maid was careless and soiled the dress of Prince Rong’s Seventh Daughter.","The senior maid led her to the Hall of Inherited Light to freshen up, but unexpectedly, the young master of the Marquis of Changling Manor barged in by mistake,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam was stunned.","\"So... an accident occurred?\" she asked.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"No. The Seventh Miss is young and quick-witted; she immediately ran out and happened to run into Qiubai.","Unexpectedly, Young Master Song even chased after her to apologize. It was pitch black all around with no one else in sight, and the maids were slow to follow. Qiubai dragged him into a corner and knocked him unconscious.\"","The Second Madam gasped in shock.","\"It was also because the girl from Prince Rong’s household was clever. She has some martial skill—not one of those dainty, helpless little girls,\" Cheng Zhao added.","The Second Madam caught on: \"So this was a scheme against Prince Rong’s household?\"","\"And they used the Zhou family’s name to do it. Even knowing it was improper, Prince Rong’s household wouldn’t dare offend the Dowager Madame, so they’d have no choice but to grit their teeth, acknowledge it, and marry the girl off to the Song family to salvage their reputation,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam’s face changed color repeatedly. \"That’s utterly vicious!\"","She continued, \"And held during your birthday banquet—not only did it use the Dowager Madame, but it also used you. It even ruined your reputation.","The Song family latches onto a fine marriage, and everyone else has to suffer for it. What high-ranking match couldn't Prince Rong’s daughter have made? Why should she endure this kind of misfortune?\"","Cheng Zhao leaned forward and gently patted the Second Madam’s hand. \"Mother, don’t be angry. Their scheme backfired—they tried to steal a chicken but lost the rice instead. Grandmother was so furious she evicted her from the Hall of Inherited Light.\"","Cheng Zhao then recounted the full story to the Second Madam in detail.","From the moment she had sent the invitation to Jiahe Prefecture Manor, she had sensed the First Madam’s ulterior motives.","The goal was to benefit the Marquis of Changling Manor—the Song family.","The situation of the Marquis of Changling Manor was well known among the noble families of the capital, though the Second Madam had yet to learn of it.","For the Song family to marry into a high-ranking household, if they didn’t resort to trickery, it would have been utterly impossible.","Cheng Zhao’s birthday banquet had already greatly displeased the First Madam. But since it couldn’t be changed, she decided to sacrifice everyone else to secure a good marriage for her own natal family.","Because she had anticipated this, Cheng Zhao kept Qiubai vigilant all along.","She even drew up a list of four names—young ladies the First Madam might target.","Cheng Zhao also borrowed several maids from Zhou Yuanshen’s outer study, including Mingyu, and assigned each to watch over one of these ladies.","Although Prince Rong’s Seventh Daughter matched the age requirement, as the daughter of a prince of the first rank, her status was so high that Cheng Zhao thought even her own Cheng family wouldn’t dare consider such a match, let alone the Song family, which surely wouldn’t be so reckless as to aim that high.","Yet, she never expected the First Madam and the Song family to actually set their sights on Prince Rong’s household—and even use the Hall of Inherited Light to do it.","If a noble young lady came to a banquet and accidentally soiled her clothes, she might be frightened and wary if led to a side courtyard to change.","But being invited to the main courtyard of the host’s residence would put her at ease—she would feel both respected and safe.","The First Madam Song had exploited precisely this trust.","Fortunately, Prince Rong’s Seventh Daughter was quick on her feet; and luckily, Cheng Zhao was well prepared, and Qiubai stayed close.","\"...Qiubai knocked out Young Master Song right then and there. When the maids from the Hall of Inherited Light came chasing out, they couldn’t find anyone in the shadows and didn’t dare to make a big scene searching, so they had to pretend nothing happened and walk away.","Then Nanfeng hid him, while Qiubai called the Lord Duke outside. They tied him up and interrogated him,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi listened with bright, sparkling eyes. \"We had no idea at all.\"","\"I didn’t want others to know either. On my birthday, it’s enough to talk about the dishes and my jewelry,\" Cheng Zhao replied.","\"Still, it was quite a gain,\" Zhou Yuanqi commented.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","The Second Madam was still furious. \"What a twisted, vile scheme!\"","\"Mother, when I first married into this family, I told you I would surely have you move into the Hall of Inherited Light. Look, it has already been vacated,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Cheng Zhao was not particularly angry about this incident.","Earlier, in the Longevity Courtyard, the Dowager Madame had not said a single word to Cheng Zhao or Huan Qingtang. Instead, she ordered them both to kneel in the small Buddhist shrine.","Of course, it wasn’t to punish Cheng Zhao, but Huan Qingtang.","Cheng Zhao was merely caught in the backlash.","In this affair, Huan Qingtang had a hand in it. Although she herself had washed her hands clean, the First Madam Song had already shouted aloud that Huan Qingtang had incited her to do it.","Huan Qingtang refused to admit it, saying only, \"Can my mother-in-law’s intentions be defied by her daughter-in-law? Mother, did I ever give you any schemes?\"","The First Madam was left speechless.","She was sent to the Clear Wind Courtyard.","Within a great household, a courtyard named “Clear Wind” was often remote and crude.","The First Madam had lived in the Hall of Inherited Light for many years, which was not only spacious and elegant but also had its own small kitchen. Wherever she went, people flocked around her like stars around the moon.","Now, forced into the Clear Wind Courtyard, her predicament could be imagined.","Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang knelt together in front of the Buddhist shrine for most of the day.","Cheng Zhao did not complain even once.","Because she knew she had gained a great deal from this incident.","\"Yesterday, Princess Rong sent me a pair of emerald bracelets. The quality is excellent—only slightly inferior to that heirloom piece of yours, Mother,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","\"Does she already know?\"","\"The Seventh Miss would certainly have told her,\" Cheng Zhao replied.","\"And she isn’t angry?\"","\"That’s the grace of the Princess. When trouble occurs, she can distinguish right from wrong. Besides, living nearby in the same capital, a complete rift with the Zhou family would serve no purpose,\" Cheng Zhao explained.","She continued, \"Prince Rong’s fief is less than two hundred li from the capital. He also had contributions to the new emperor’s ascension, so by imperial decree, his household was permitted to stay in the capital.","But when it comes down to it, if the Cheng and Zhou families join forces, forcing Prince Rong’s household back to their fief would be entirely within reason.","So why should she insist on laying the blame for the Eldest Aunt’s wrongdoing on me? As a close neighbor, wouldn’t she know the conflicts and divisions between the two branches of the Zhou family?\"","The Second Madam couldn’t help nodding. \"Princess Rong is truly a sensible woman.\"","Then she added, \"Of course, it’s also because the Cheng family and the Dowager Madame are capable enough to keep things under control. Otherwise, shifting blame without any real cost would be too easy for the Princess to resist.\"","Cheng Zhao nodded. \"Exactly right, Mother.\"","The Second Madam felt a little embarrassed at being praised.","Social matters, when fully understood, boiled down to a few rules. Extrapolating from one example to others, the Second Madam had become much more perceptive than before.","\"Will your grandmother have us move into the Hall of Inherited Light?\" the Second Madam asked again.","Cheng Zhao shook her head. \"Not for now. But since it’s empty, there’s hope. Grandmother naturally wants us to compete with Sister-in-law. By vying to outdo each other, we’ll only prove our loyalty more fiercely to her.\"","At Cheng Zhao’s birthday banquet, she gained prestige, significantly elevating her standing among the noble ladies. She also earned the gratitude of Princess Rong, and the Chengming Hall was vacated as well.","Three birds with one stone."]},{"number":156,"title":"Chapter 156: The Duke Is Angry","paragraphs":["Second Madam's emotions were easily influenced by Cheng Zhao.","During the birthday banquet, she had no idea anything was wrong; after listening carefully, she was furious.","First Madam's methods were dirty.","If the scheme succeeded, the Song family would gain an advantage; if it failed, the Song family would suffer no losses.","But whether it succeeded or failed, the Zhou family of Chengguo Estate would be covered in ashes, deeply embarrassed. In the future, when people talked about it, their reputation would be damaged.","Yet Cheng Zhao said that none of the bad outcomes had occurred—instead, she had gained many benefits.","Second Madam suddenly thought: \"Why am I angry? This matter has actually become more interesting.\"","Unlike the Dowager Madame's birthday banquet last time, Cheng Zhao's birthday feast was lively, joyful, and flawlessly carried out to perfection.","\"Zhaozhao, pack your things. Perhaps in a few days the Old Lady will call for you to move to Chengming Hall,\" Second Madam said.","Second Master smiled slightly.","He wasn't so optimistic.","\"No hurry,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Mother, Eldest Aunt and Elder Sister-in-law are too impatient, and that's why they keep giving us opportunities. Let's be patient. A ripe fruit is sweetest—let's wait.\"","Second Master nodded with a smile.","Zhou Yuanqi's childish face also showed satisfaction: \"Patience is a good thing. You are very steady.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing.","The young couple returned to Nonghua Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao ordered water to be prepared—she wanted to bathe. It was too hot, and even the night breeze carried no coolness. Ice had been placed inside the room, providing some shade and coolness.","She had spent most of the day in Shou'an Hall. The Old Lady feared cold but not heat, so Shou'an Hall used no ice, and the small Buddhist hall burned incense, leaving Cheng Zhao's clothes constantly damp.","After a refreshing bath and hair wash, Nanny Li and Qiubai together dried her black hair with towels.","Suyue held a small round fan, standing nearby to cool her.","Zhou Yuanshen also went to bathe.","When he came out, Cheng Zhao had finished her preparations. He didn't rely on maids; he quickly dried his hair with coarse cloth himself.","While waiting for their hair to dry, the couple sat on the heated brick bed by the window.","He read a book; Cheng Zhao took up needlework to pass the time.","He didn't speak, his expression calm. Cheng Zhao glanced at him.","He didn't raise his eyes.","Later, Cheng Zhao's eyes grew tired, and she went to sleep.","The next morning when she woke, Nanny Li quietly asked her: \"Did the Lord Duke seem a little unhappy?\"","\"Probably not?\" Cheng Zhao said.","When Zhou Yuanshen was at home, it was hard for Cheng Zhao to ignore him; when he was away, she rarely thought of him, unless something happened that brought him to mind.","For example, when she returned to her maiden home and found her father and grandfather off duty, she would incidentally remember that Zhou Yuanshen was also an official who had days off.","Her thoughts held no emotion, just drifting to him briefly.","When she was alone, Cheng Zhao had many matters to consider, and it was nearly impossible to think of him unintentionally.","Because they shared little in common; they lived almost separate lives.","Maybe because she rarely thought of him, every time he returned, in Cheng Zhao's mind, it was like he \"suddenly burst into Nonghua Courtyard.\" Like riding smoothly in a carriage only to hit a sudden jolt—she found it difficult to accept his return naturally and smoothly.","She didn't dislike it.","It was like her body—she didn't reject him, but every time he came back after a long absence, when she accepted him, it was slightly painful.","It always took three to five days for her to adapt.","This time when he returned, Cheng Zhao was too busy and tense because of the night banquet. Until last night, she hadn't had the leisure to adapt to him, so seeing him felt unfamiliar.","Had he seemed different?","Wasn't he always like this?","\"Where did you see him unhappy?\" Cheng Zhao wasn't certain and asked Nanny Li.","Nanny Li: \"It doesn't seem that way. I might be overthinking things, which is why I asked you.\"","\"I don't think so either,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She dropped the matter.","That evening, Zhou Yuanshen didn't return to the estate. Cheng Zhao waited for him for the evening meal, but he was nowhere to be seen.","\"Did he say he wouldn't come back?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","The maids shook their heads: \"He didn't say.\"","Then Mingyu, a maid from the outer study, came again.","She told Cheng Zhao: \"The Lord Duke will be staying at the General's Mansion for these two days. He asks the Young Madam not to wait for him.\"","Cheng Zhao smiled and told Suyue to see Mingyu out.","She had her evening meal alone.","Nanny Li said: \"Before your birthday, the Lord Duke also stayed at the General's Mansion for several days. Now he's gone again.\"","\"Maybe something's come up?\" Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li: \"You really don't think he was unhappy?\"","Cheng Zhao frowned and quickly defended herself: \"I didn't provoke him!\"","When he returned to the estate this time, she had specifically gone to Chenhui Courtyard.","If there was anything out of the ordinary, it was that she felt uncomfortable when the slightly makeshift bed in the next room had its leg break—it scared her a little.","It was also hot at the time.","She seemed to impatiently avoid his lips and even let out an \"ah.\"","Afterward, he didn't show any reaction.","They quickly washed up; he checked the bed leg, said it was only bent, not broken; and gave Cheng Zhao the gift Fan Xiao had brought for her.","Then, he went to the General's Mansion, while Cheng Zhao prepared for the night banquet.","And that was the last she saw of him until today.","Far from provoking him, Cheng Zhao hadn't even had time to speak with him about anything.","\"Don't guess. There's probably really some important business,\" Nanny Li said.","Cheng Zhao hadn't thought much of it initially.","Even if he stayed at the General's Mansion, it was reasonable. Not to mention the General's Mansion—if he stayed the night with his chamber concubine at Lijing Courtyard, Cheng Zhao would have found that normal.","But since Nanny Li had brought it up, Cheng Zhao recalled that they had gone to sleep in total silence last night.","She had been tired these past two days, always on edge. After going to bed last night, she deliberately avoided him.","It was too hot. Moving about would only leave them drenched in sweat; the sheets might even become damp. And she was exhausted after kneeling for half a day in the small Buddhist hall at Shou'an Hall—she really didn't want to be intimate with him.","She was probably a little resistant, and he had noticed.","He didn't do anything.","And today, he didn't come back.","Before sleeping, Cheng Zhao suddenly thought: \"Could he have acquired a new beauty and lodged her at the General's Mansion?\"","If that were the case, it would be better for her to be brought back—Yujin Courtyard could house her.","Having a concubine at home and a mistress outside were completely different.","Cheng Zhao didn't want a falling-out with him.","Third Sister wasn't interested in worldly honor, but Cheng Zhao wanted her \"title of Commandery Lady.\" She had no intention of clashing with Zhou Yuanshen, let alone letting the Cheng family become enemies with him.","These thoughts passed quickly through Cheng Zhao's mind.","She meant to think it over deeply, but she was too tired from the day. First Madam had moved out of Chengming Hall, and Cheng Zhao and Huan Qingtang had divided the duties equally. Chengming Hall was currently a battlefield.","Intellectual battles were exhausting, and the weather was hot. When it was hot, Cheng Zhao tired more easily and needed more sleep to regain her strength.","Cheng Zhao felt she should think more about Zhou Yuanshen's situation, but before she knew it, she had fallen asleep.","The next day when she got up, she even forgot that he hadn't stayed in Nonghua Courtyard the night before.","Because whenever she woke up, he had already gone to court.","This day, Cheng Zhao was busy again from morning till night.","The duties for the inner and outer courtyards of Chengguo Estate had been split equally between her and Huan Qingtang. The Dowager Madame hadn't yet said what would happen going forward.","The stewards were all waiting to see which of them—Cheng Zhao or Huan Qingtang—would end up living in Chengming Hall.","As for Cheng Zhao, the prestige she had gained already clearly surpassed Huan Qingtang's.","This did not make her proud; instead, it made her more careful and cautious, determined not to make any mistakes.","On her way to the Lotus Cloud Courtyard for the evening meal, she was so exhausted that she could barely muster any energy.","If her mother-in-law hadn’t asked, “Hasn’t Zhou Yuanshen returned yet?” she wouldn’t have even remembered that Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the General’s residence."]},{"number":157,"title":"Chapter 157: Cheng Zhao Doesn't Care","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao had no idea why Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the General’s estate.","When Second Madam asked about his whereabouts, she answered truthfully.","“Gone to the General’s estate?” Second Madam’s worry only deepened, and she turned to ask Second Master, “Has something happened in the Northern Frontier? Or the South?”","Second Master smiled. “I haven’t seen Yuanshen these past few days either.”","“It must be an urgent dispatch. It couldn’t be about Yuanjin, could it?”","Suddenly, she thought of her second son, Zhou Yuanjin, stationed far away on the frontier.","No matter how far away he was, at the slightest stir, someone would worry for him—Zhou Yuanjin was also a fortunate child.","“Mother, please don’t worry. If Lord Duke hasn’t returned by tomorrow, I’ll go to the General’s estate myself,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam replied, “If he still doesn’t come back, I’ll go too.”","Second Master quickly stopped her. “He has private matters. It’s fine for the daughter-in-law to seek him out, but if you follow as well, you’d be interfering too much. That’s inappropriate—he’s an already married son.”","So Second Madam entrusted the matter to Cheng Zhao.","During the evening meal, Second Madam was still distracted.","She cared too deeply. Cheng Zhao could sense her emotions, and so that night when she returned to the Courtyard of Splendor, she couldn’t help but ponder herself.","“Do you think I should go to the General’s estate to find Lord Duke?” Cheng Zhao asked Nanny Li.","Nanny Li looked at her in confusion. “What are you worried about, madam?”","“If there really is someone, and I stumble upon them, it would only create awkwardness between Lord Duke and me,” Cheng Zhao said.","She wasn’t pregnant yet, and she couldn’t afford to become completely estranged from Zhou Yuanshen.","If she witnessed something, it would be hard to pretend ignorance. Without seeing it with her own eyes, she could turn a blind eye.","“That’s unlikely,” Nanny Li said, her brows twitching. “Lord Duke isn’t that kind of irresponsible man. He knows what he’s doing.”","That was what she said, but even Nanny Li wasn’t certain.","Cheng Zhao hinted that Zhou Yuanshen might have hidden a mistress at the General’s estate.","Nanny Li privately felt the possibility was slim, but what if?","What if, for instance, such a thing was done on a whim, in the heat of a moment. Afterwards, filled with regret, he didn’t know how to tell his wife, concubines, or parents, so he kept her hidden outside.","If Cheng Zhao went and stumbled upon her, should she make a scene or not?","If she didn’t make a scene, Lord Duke would have no fear in the future on this matter. It would go on without end or restraint, causing endless trouble.","A woman outside was not like a concubine bought into the household. Her background was unknown, and some were wild and hard to tame, a great headache for the principal wife.","If she did make a scene, it didn’t seem worth it. Not only would it cause estrangement between husband and wife, but everyone inside and outside would watch the spectacle. The prestige Cheng Zhao had painstakingly built would collapse in an instant.","Caught between a rock and a hard place.","It was better not to go.","Just as Nanny Li was about to speak, Cheng Zhao had already made her decision.","She said, “Nanny, we can’t go directly. I’ll write a letter to my maternal uncle.”","Using the pretext of thanking Fan Xiao, Cheng Zhao wrote him a letter, asking him to mediate on her behalf.","It would be best if there was nothing.","If something did happen, her maternal uncle would stand in front of her to resolve the trouble first. Cheng Zhao wouldn’t bear any blame, nor would she have to offend Zhou Yuanshen.","As a principal wife, one had to learn to play deaf and dumb.","In any case, the situation within the Duke’s estate was unclear, Cheng Zhao had no children, and the marital relationship could not show any visible signs of rupture.","“Young Madam, this is exactly the spirit a principal wife should have,” Nanny Li said. “Knowing to prioritize the bigger picture.”","Cheng Zhao didn’t reply.","As she did her makeup in front of the mirror, her mind kept circling back to Nanny Qin of the main kitchen.","Nanny Qin was the sister-in-law of the head steward, and her sister worked in the Dowager Madame’s Longevity Courtyard. The Dowager Madame had wanted to put the entire main kitchen under Nanny Qin’s control.","But since Cheng Zhao had stepped in with her deep-laid plans, Nanny Qin felt somewhat powerless before her.","Lately, this Nanny Qin had been provoking Cheng Zhao frequently, evidently siding with Huan Qingtang. Meanwhile, seventy percent of the main kitchen staff had already been won over by Cheng Zhao.","After the First Madam moved out of the Hall of Ascending Brilliance, the first battle seemed about to begin.","The outcome of this battle would set the direction of the entire war.","That direction was crucial—it determined where people’s loyalties lay.","Lose the people’s hearts, and even an emperor couldn’t hold his throne.","If Cheng Zhao won this time, Huan Qingtang would lose her advantage entirely in her presence.","Cheng Zhao slowly combed her hair, her mind rapidly reviewing everything that had happened in the main kitchen lately. She had to seize the opportunity and deal Nanny Qin a heavy blow.","Nanny Li brought her some bird’s nest porridge and said softly to comfort her, “Young Madam, don’t be upset. These are all just your guesses.”","“No, she has ill intentions,” Cheng Zhao said. “I must ‘remove’ her.”","Nanny Li was startled. “Absolutely not! If Lord Duke is truly infatuated, doing that would be like losing the bait for the fish.”","Cheng Zhao turned around. “...Who are you talking about?”","“Who are you talking about?” Nanny Li asked, slightly surprised as well.","Cheng Zhao said, “We don’t know what’s going on at the General’s estate. I’m not clear on the inside story. There may not even be a mistress—it’s just our wild guesses. I was thinking about the main kitchen affairs.”","Nanny Li: “...”","She quickly collected her thoughts. “Has Nanny Qin caused you trouble again?”","“This morning, there was a big argument in the main kitchen. The procurement was almost delayed,” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li comforted her a few more words.","After Cheng Zhao drank the porridge, Nanny Li brought water for her to rinse her mouth. That night, Suyue was on night duty, and Nanny Li withdrew from the main bedroom.","Looking back at the direction of the main bedroom, Nanny Li seemed to understand a little why Lord Duke was throwing a tantrum.","Because this mistress truly hadn’t taken Lord Duke’s affairs to heart.","Cheng Zhao was blessed with a face like a flower and a moon-like beauty. It seemed easy to become lost in her. After so many intimate nights between the young couple, it was only natural for Lord Duke to fall for her.","If he observed a little, he could probably see where Cheng Zhao’s thoughts lay.","So, he was a bit upset?","Or perhaps disillusioned, wanting to cool his feelings by distancing himself from her?","The next day, Cheng Zhao wrote a letter and instructed Suyue to personally deliver it to the Pillar-of-the-State General’s estate, to give it to Fan Xiao.","“Ask him to open and read it right there. Wait until he finishes and get his definite response before you come back,” Cheng Zhao instructed Suyue.","Suyue acknowledged the order.","The morning was still hot. Cheng Zhao went to the Vermilion Clouds Courtyard for breakfast, and her mother-in-law brought up Zhou Yuanshen again.","“Your father-in-law said he’ll check on Yuanshen at the yamen today,” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao gave a wry smile. “Mother, you truly dote on your children. Fortunately, they’ve turned out well and haven’t become prodigals.”","Second Madam: “...”","After breakfast, Cheng Zhao went to the Hall of Ascending Brilliance to conduct business.","Second Madam, on the other hand, pondered her words.","She felt that all three of her sons had turned out well because of the overall environment in the Duke’s estate. The Dowager Madame didn’t like them, so they didn’t dare to indulge.","They had to be ambitious.","Since Second Master and she cherished their children greatly, the sons were ambitious but lacked harshness.","All of them were quite good.","“...Isn’t this fortune in disguise?” Second Madam couldn’t help but say to Nanny Fan. “I’m always complaining about this, feeling sorry about that. But when I look back, it seems like the branches are heavy with fruit.”","Nanny Fan smiled. “The outcomes are all good, but the hardships you endured were not few. You must also cherish yourself.”","“It’s nothing much. What mother doesn’t suffer?” Second Madam said.","Looking back at the path she had traveled from a different perspective, it didn’t seem so bad after all.","Cheng Zhao had really helped her see many things clearly.","Second Madam now increasingly felt like an elderly matriarch: kind, gentle, seeing everyone as lovely and everything with tolerance.","If only Zhao Zhao could have a child.","—Of course, she couldn’t dwell on that.","If Zhao Zhao could have one, she would have long ago. Bringing it up would only add to her distress.","Second Madam was straightforward and outspoken; sometimes whatever was on her mind would slip right out, with no restraint at all.","She could not keep urging Cheng Zhao to bear a child in her heart. What if it slipped out next time and hurt Cheng Zhao’s feelings?","That day at noon, Suyue returned to the mansion.","By evening, Zhou Yuanshen and Second Master came back together."]},{"number":158,"title":"Chapter 158: Duchess of the State","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanshen had returned.","Both Cheng Zhao and Second Madam turned to look at him.","He stood in the shadow away from the lamplight, his brow bones prominent and his eyes dark and tranquil. With an expressionless face, he bowed to Second Madam.","“Mother.”","Second Madam’s gaze bored into him as if trying to poke a hole in his face.","“What have you been busy with lately?” she asked.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, “Some matters at the Beijing Garrison. Discussions with my advisers ran late, and I feared spies from the Duke’s estate, so it wasn’t safe to speak here. I stayed at the General’s mansion instead.”","Relief flooded Second Madam’s face. “Still dealing with the garrison’s affairs? I thought everything had settled?”","“Temporarily, it’s not settled yet,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","She exhaled deeply.","Then she scolded him lightly, “If you’re not coming home to stay, you should at least let us know. Zhaozhao and I were both worried.”","With that, she turned to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao performed the traditional woman’s bow. “Lord Duke.”","“Duchess,” Zhou Yuanshen replied flatly.","Cheng Zhao froze slightly.","The once relaxed atmosphere tensed up again in an instant.","Second Master looked at his son, then at his daughter-in-law, and remained silent, saying nothing.","Zhou Yuanqi observed them curiously, mentally adding them to his list of “estranged couples.”","Only Second Madam’s face changed slightly, and she pressed on, “Are you angry with Zhaozhao?”","“No,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","“Then why did you call her ‘Duchess’?” she demanded.","It seemed she was acting as Cheng Zhao’s advocate, as if Yuanshen had wronged her.","Zhou Yuanshen’s eyes were calm. “Mother, she called me ‘Lord Duke’ first.”","“And what’s wrong with that?” Second Madam countered, frowning. “What else would she call you? The General?”","“We’re just being polite,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Isn’t that right, Duchess?”","Cheng Zhao had been called “Duchess” a few times before, and it never ended well.","“The Lord Duke is quite right,” Cheng Zhao replied.","Second Madam: “...”","Dinner passed in considerable silence. Second Madam tried to speak several times, but Second Master subtly restrained her each time.","After the meal, Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen took their leave. Zhou Yuanqi seized the opportunity to slip away as well.","“Why are they fighting?” Second Madam fretted. “They were fine before.”","She then complained to her husband, “And you wouldn’t let me ask.”","Second Master gave a good-natured smile. “Young couples have their ups and downs. There’s no need for you to worry so. Besides, if the mother-in-law meddles in a lovers’ quarrel, a small rift can turn into a big grudge. That’s not worth it. So I stopped you from probing too much.”","Second Madam recalled how, when she was young, an argument with Second Master would have the Dowager Madame personally intervene, and that would only infuriate her even more. She’d felt excluded. Later, when they moved to her mother’s courtyard, her mother would hide away whenever she heard them arguing, never asking a single question. It was then that their relationship grew deeper, right up to today.","“You’re right. I do tend to meddle too much,” Second Madam reflected quickly. “I probably shouldn’t have brought it up at all.”","A couple calling each other “Lord Duke” and “Duchess” might just be a playful front, with flirtation beneath the surface. But now that she had pointed it out, all that was left was anger.","“You did the right thing just now. Your daughter-in-law knows you care. Not too much, not too cold,” Second Master said.","“Really?” Second Madam asked.","“Yes. You’re truly a good mother-in-law,” Second Master laughed.","Second Madam: “...”","She couldn’t tell if she’d been praised or mocked. Ah, well—since it sounded pleasant to her ears, she decided not to argue.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen returned to the Fragrant Blossom Courtyard.","Both of them remained silent.","Cheng Zhao detested hot weather. It made her restless and drained, stripping her of her usual composure. All she wanted was to bathe quickly, change into clean, cool linen clothes, and sit down to fan herself.","“Prepare my bathwater,” she ordered her maid.","She entered the inner chamber.","Zhou Yuanshen followed her in.","When she saw him sit down on the heated brick bed by the window, she circled behind the screen and sat at her dressing table. She untied her hair and slowly combed through the jet-black tresses.","She wasn’t planning to wash her hair tonight.","The man sitting by the window didn’t move or speak.","Cheng Zhao refrained from glancing at him with her peripheral vision, so she couldn’t tell if he was sneaking looks at her.","“Qiubai,” she called toward the door after a moment.","Qiubai entered.","“Is the water ready?” she asked.","“Everything’s prepared,” Qiubai said.","Cheng Zhao let out a sigh of relief, then escaped swiftly from the inner chamber into the washroom.","While bathing, Qiubai asked, “Why didn’t you say anything just now? Is the Lord Duke angry?”","Cheng Zhao said, “Do you think he’s angry?”","Qiubai answered honestly, “I couldn’t tell. The Lord Duke is always like that—he rarely talks much or shows any expression.”","“Exactly,” Cheng Zhao replied. “Who knows what he’s thinking?”","Cheng Zhao tallied everything in her mind: after he returned to the city, she recognized him; she deliberately waited for him at the Morning Glow Courtyard and went through that exhausting ordeal in the heat; she handled his birthday banquet beautifully and didn’t hide anything about Eldest Aunt from him; and when he stayed for so long at the General’s mansion fear it might make things awkward, she specifically asked Young Uncle to check on him instead of going herself.","Absolutely nothing to fault. She had done everything well.","She was a model mistress of the house, an excellent wife.","So what right did he have to be angry?","Confused at first, Cheng Zhao now felt a rising indignation.","What gave him the right?","“I’ll ignore him!” she declared to herself.","Finished washing, she put on thin, light clothes and returned to the inner chamber.","Zhou Yuanshen was still sitting there, slowly sipping tea.","Cheng Zhao didn’t approach. Instead, she went back behind the screen.","Standing there, she said flatly, “Lord Duke, I’m done with the washroom. You may use it.”","“I’ll return to Morning Glow Courtyard for the night,” he said, rising. “Duchess, rest early.”","Cheng Zhao called out, “Qiubai, ask a maid to light a lantern and see the Lord Duke back. The path is dark outside.”","Then she added, “Go slowly, Lord Duke.”","And Zhou Yuanshen left.","An anger swelled in Cheng Zhao’s chest, impossible to swallow or to spit out. She was nearly furious. Amid all the internal and external troubles, he was inexplicably sulking over nothing.","“Madam...” Nanny Li started. “Why did the Lord Duke go to Morning Glow Courtyard?”","Cheng Zhao snapped, “If you must know, go ask him. I certainly don’t.”","She went to sleep.","That night, unusually, Cheng Zhao couldn’t fall asleep easily.","She tried not to overthink it.","Tomorrow, there would be a mountain of tasks. She had taken on several new responsibilities in unfamiliar areas and was still figuring things out. She didn’t show any hesitation because, before marriage, her mother had kept her close and taught her everything hands-on. She had seen it all before. No matter what she did, she looked competent.","But only she knew how vigilant she truly was, how afraid that anything might go wrong.","And yet, she was letting a man distract her.","The more she willed herself not to think about it, the more her mind seethed. She was so angry she wanted to march straight to Morning Glow Courtyard and hash it out with him.","But that would mean surrendering the advantage.","Lose once, lose every time. End up at his mercy.","“Maybe this is an opportunity,” she thought to herself. “Maybe I can turn it around and come out on top.”"]},{"number":159,"title":"Chapter 159: Sister-in-law is More Obedient","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao couldn't sleep. She wanted to read a book, but her mind was boiling with rage, unable to calm down enough to read or practice calligraphy, so she had to get up and do some needlework.","Qiubai, who was on night duty, also got up and moved another lamp closer, saying, \"Be careful not to strain your eyes.\"","Suyue and Nanny Li hadn't slept either, and when they saw the lights on, they both came in.","\"Go to sleep, you all. Nothing major is happening here; I just can't sleep,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"...Are you worried about the Lord Duke?\" Nanny Li asked as she helped separate the threads, concern in her voice.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"I don't know what happened, and that's what's troubling me.\"","\"Shall we go ask?\"","Cheng Zhao shook her head.","If she couldn't detect a problem, then there was none—she firmly believed that.","She wouldn't go and ask.","If he didn't want to talk, he could sulk in silence.","\"If he were my son, daring to throw such an unreasonable tantrum, I'd make him kneel under the scorching sun for an hour. After that, even a 'sulky gourd' would have to open his mouth. My mother-in-law spoils him too much; he hasn't learned enough lessons,\" Cheng Zhao said, jabbing at her embroidery hoop.","The needle almost pricked her own finger.","Nanny Li chuckled. \"He's not your son, either.\"","\"I wouldn't want a son like that. He's lucky he isn't,\" Cheng Zhao said.","That made Qiubai and Suyue laugh too.","After venting a few words, Cheng Zhao felt much lighter in her heart.","The night grew deeper, and finally, feeling sleepy, she went to bed.","The following days remained scorching hot.","When the weather was this sweltering, not just Cheng Zhao had a bad temper and low energy—everyone did, including the Dowager Madame.","Huan Qingtang, who usually wore a perpetual smile, could no longer hold it together. She was also worn out.","Cheng Zhao was even more exhausted than the others, thanks to Zhou Yuanshen.","Ever since Cheng Zhao had sent someone to summon Fan Xiao and told him to go to the General's Mansion to find that man, Zhou Yuanshen had stopped staying at the General's Mansion.","However, he didn't stay at the Nonghua Courtyard either.","He now slept at the Chenhui Courtyard.","He had previously said that, under normal circumstances, there was no need for him to live in the inner study.","Cheng Zhao had initially wanted to dispute this with him on principle.","Last time, he had warned her to always recognize her own husband, and she had never mistaken him since. In return, she never hid anything from him regarding her own affairs.","But when it came to his matters, he was lucky if he told her even twenty percent of what was going on.","Cheng Zhao had no idea what he was angry about this time.","After guessing back and forth and arriving at no answer, Cheng Zhao simply stopped bothering.","If Zhou Yuanshen didn't come to Nonghua Courtyard, her younger brother-in-law, Zhou Yuanqi, did.","\"...Do you want to go to the night market tomorrow? The weather is hot, and the night market along the river is a bit cooler and very lively,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"I'd like to, but I'm tired.\"","\"Aren't any of you taking days off?\" Zhou Yuanqi frowned.","This little bookworm-like kid didn't like being refused.","\"You've reminded me,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"No wonder I'm so tired—it's because we haven't had a day off in a long time.\"","The next day, Cheng Zhao went to the Chengming Hall and openly told the stewards under her charge, \"Tomorrow happens to be the fifth, let's take a day off.\"","Everyone working in Chengming Hall had leave days each month. How those days were taken was staggered, not all at once.","But ever since the previous Chief Steward, the First Madam, had been removed, the place had been in chaos. No one was willing to be the one to bring up this issue.","She planned to go out tonight and enjoy herself until late, then sleep in the next day.","Cheng Zhao didn't ask for anyone's permission; she just informed her own stewards directly.","If she was put in charge, she acted with full authority.","Moreover, she was very ambitious, desperately wanting to split the Chengming Hall in two—so she could manage her half, Lady Huan could manage the rest, and they wouldn't have to interfere with each other.","About sixty to seventy percent of the stewards were convinced by her skills, but, of course, there were also the stubborn ones.","For instance, Nanny Qin from the main kitchen immediately asked, \"Third Young Madam, is this just for this one time, or is it going to be a regular day off on the fifth of every month from now on?\"","\"From now on, every fifth day will be a day off,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Qin pressed, \"Madam, are you sure you have the authority to make that call?\"","She was trying to put pressure on Cheng Zhao with her words.","Cheng Zhao's smile faded, and she asked coolly, \"Do you think I don't have the authority, and if so, who does? Are you planning to take this little matter to Grandmother? Nanny Qin, are you implying that Grandmother is unwilling to allow the stewards and her granddaughter-in-law a day off? Are you questioning Grandmother's kindness and generosity?\"","Nanny Qin faltered for a moment.","During this period, Cheng Zhao had been butting heads with her over issues in the main kitchen.","Cheng Zhao hadn't done much, just observed silently, which in Nanny Qin's eyes made her look weak.","This made Nanny Qin complacent.","So when Nanny Qin jumped out to speak, Cheng Zhao immediately used the Dowager Madame to suppress her.","Nanny Qin was momentarily at a loss for words.","\"Your humble servant dares not say that, Madam,\" Nanny Qin said, anger flashing across her face, looking very unwilling.","\"...These days have been very hectic. Grandmother was just saying yesterday how hardworking the stewards have been alongside us,\" Huan Qingtang said with a smile.","She handed Nanny Qin a way out, taking over the conversation.","At the same time, she let everyone know that she was more favored the Dowager Madame.","\"Sister-in-law is so generous and kind. We'll all benefit from your kindness. Since you've proposed it, let's take tomorrow off as well,\" Huan Qingtang said with a smile.","By saying this, it was as if Cheng Zhao had merely made a suggestion, and she had made the final decision.","She was the mistress of Chengming Hall.","Nanny Qin's defiance had given Huan Qingtang another opening.","\"Sister-in-law, if Nanny Qin could understand me as quickly as you do, I wouldn't have to try so hard. Should the stewards on your side also follow my orders to take tomorrow off? If everyone follows me, then I'd be overstepping my bounds. Let's all take tomorrow off. As for whether we take the fifth off every month from now on, I'll still have to ask Grandmother. But knowing Grandmother's boundless compassion, I'm sure she won't make things difficult for us,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Huan Qingtang was speechless.","The crowd secretly eyed the two young madams.","The Eldest Young Madame, Lady Huan, wasn't unintelligent; she was just a bit aloof. To put it bluntly, when it came to getting what she wanted, she was a bit pretentious, half-hiding and half-revealing.","Compared to her, the Third Young Madam, Cheng Shi, was much more straightforward and decisive.","Some even called her ruthless.","Anyone could see the Third Young Madam's burning ambition.","This ambition might not have pleased the Dowager Madame, but it was a different story in the eyes of the stewards: it gave them confidence. Following her would lead to victory.","She wanted to win, and she would win. Her ambition was to take over the entire Chengming Hall.","People who worked below were just trying to make a living.","On one side, there was Lady Huan, who was vague and hesitant, afraid of getting burned. On the other side, there was Cheng Shi, who was direct and had a clear goal. It went without saying whose support offered more stability.","Therefore, Cheng Zhao's prestige among the stewards increased by a notch.","At that moment, no one spoke out to criticize Cheng Zhao.","Once she had authority, her decisions were met not with doubt but with obedience, and managing the household became very easy.","In the late afternoon, Cheng Zhao went to the Shou'an Courtyard.","Unexpectedly, Zhou Yuanshen was also there.","Seeing Cheng Zhao, the Dowager Madame smiled kindly and asked, \"Has the heat worn you out?\"","She then instructed the maid to serve the Third Young Madam some tea.","Cheng Zhao curtsied to the Dowager Madame, then to Zhou Yuanshen, before taking a seat.","\"You came home quite early today, Lord Duke,\" Cheng Zhao said to Zhou Yuanshen with a smile.","She knew he didn't want to talk to her. But in front of his grandmother, he wouldn't freeze her out; he'd have to respond.","Clearly reluctant but with no choice, Cheng Zhao felt he must be holding back a burning irritation.","She found a kind of mischievous delight in it.","As expected, Zhou Yuanshen glanced at her and spoke calmly, \"I have some matters to discuss with Grandmother.\"","\"What matters?\" Cheng Zhao pressed her advantage.","\"The Emperor will be going out to a summer retreat and a hunt in a few days. I am required to attend the imperial carriage,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","\"Oh, you must be tired,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"The imperial grace is boundless. The titled ladies in the capital must also attend, for the Empress and several consorts will be there,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao was silent.","\"We are discussing this with Grandmother at home, deciding who among the family should go,\" Zhou Yuanshen continued. \"Grandmother said that you and my mother are the most suitable to accompany the party.\"","Cheng Zhao remained silent.","In such sweltering heat, even staying home brought inconvenience everywhere—and now they had to go out?"]},{"number":160,"title":"Chapter 160: Encountering the Duke","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao kept a smile on her face.","She said, \"Accompanying the imperial carriage is a great honor, and also the supreme glory of the Zhou family.\"","Inside, she was cursing wildly.","A perfectly good life in the palace, and they had to go out and suffer. The emperor had nothing better to do. Never mind the heat or cold—at this time of year, the grass in the hunting grounds was deep, and once night fell, the mosquitoes were thick enough to eat a person alive.","What a hardship!","\"This opportunity is for you and your mother-in-law,\" the Dowager Madame said with a smile. \"You must serve the honored guests well, and do your utmost on our behalf before the Empress.\"","She was always smiling like this.","But Cheng Zhao still managed to detect a hint of schadenfreude.","\"Yes, Grandmother,\" Cheng Zhao said, smiling.","She remained expressionless, just as skilled as the Dowager Madame at putting on airs.","Only then did the Dowager Madame ask Cheng Zhao why she had come over in the mid-afternoon.","\"...Tomorrow is the fifth, and it happens to be a day off,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Tomorrow was the twenty-fifth of June.","Counting carefully, Zhou Yuanshen would probably be heading back to the Capital Guard Camp soon.","During his return to the city this time, he had spent most of it sulking. The two of them hadn't had a proper chance to talk. Once he left for the Capital Guard Camp, Cheng Zhao would have some peace.","Cheng Zhao deliberately didn't look at him, to avoid seeming like she was \"gloating over his misfortune.\"","The Dowager Madame's smile faded a little when she heard this: \"You should have asked me first.\"","\"I thought Grandmother would surely agree. This is a charitable act, and the stewards will only be grateful for your generosity,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Dowager Madame gave a faint smile: \"They'll be grateful to you.\"","\"How could they? The Zhou family doesn't have stewards so ungrateful. You have managed the household for years, and the people you employ are loyal and devoted. There's no doubt about that,\" Cheng Zhao said.","If the Dowager Madame refused, it would mean she didn't trust the loyalty of the stewards she herself had chosen; if she agreed, it would give Cheng Zhao the authority to \"act first and report later.\"","The Dowager Madame gave her a long, hard look.","Zhou Yuanshen then spoke up: \"Grandmother, a thousand-li horse needs better fodder. If you won't give the Duchess even this bit of authority, she won't have the nerve to manage the household.\"","The Dowager Madame: \"The Lord Duke certainly knows how to dote on his wife.\"","\"If she had Grandmother's abilities, she wouldn't need anyone else to dote on her,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"She's too weak. If no one helps her, how can she stand on her own?\"","Feigning weakness while issuing a subtle threat.","The Dowager Madame finally nodded: \"Then let tomorrow be a day off. When your eldest aunt ran the household, there were no fixed days off. We might as well start a new rule.\"","\"Staying the same isn't necessarily good. Stagnant water can't raise fish,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Dowager Madame said nothing more.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen left the Hall of Longevity together.","Outside, the sun blazed fiercely, and the mid-afternoon heat wave hit them like a punch.","A small oiled carriage belonging to Cheng Zhao was waiting at the gate.","She climbed in and turned back to ask Zhou Yuanshen: \"Lord Duke, would you like to share my carriage?\"","\"No need. I'm going to the outer study,\" he said.","He turned and strode away.","Cheng Zhao watched his back for a moment, then silently lowered the curtain and told the old servant woman to drive back.","She first went to the Crimson Clouds Courtyard.","\"Yuan Qi said we could go out for a stroll tonight, and I'd like to go too,\" Cheng Zhao said to her mother-in-law. \"Mother, would you like to come?\"","\"It's so hot,\" the Second Madam said.","The house was comfortable. Blocks of ice inside the copper pillars kept it cooler than outside.","Even though the Second Madam practiced with her spear every day and worked up a sweat, that didn't mean she enjoyed the sweltering heat.","\"I heard it's not hot by the river. The night breeze on the river is very pleasant, and the banks are lively. It's a new attraction this year,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam: \"Just you and Yuan Qi? Yuanshen isn't coming?\"","\"Yuan Qi suggested it. I think he has people to play with,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"He didn't invite the Lord Duke.\"","The Second Madam glanced at her.","She wanted to ask: Haven't the two of you made up yet, after all this time?","Why were they still in such a long sulk?","But she was afraid to add to Cheng Zhao's troubles.","She just teased Cheng Zhao with a smile: \"Do you really trust Yuan Qi? He'll probably bring a bunch of children along, and you'll end up as their babysitter.\"","\"I don't mind playing with children,\" Cheng Zhao said with a laugh. \"Kids are much better than adults. They're so obedient.\"","Those a few years older—like Cheng Chao and Zhou Yuanshen—were neither fun nor obedient, and Cheng Zhao would rather spend time with children.","The Second Madam laughed.","\"All that said, Yuan Qi definitely won't bring a bunch of kids just to play with me. He doesn't even like hanging around people his own age,\" Cheng Zhao added.","What's more, Zhou Yuanqi looked down on even his peer Zhou Yuanshen.","\"That's true. Yuan Qi has his nose in the air,\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao: \"Then I'll go back, bathe, and change. I'll head out with Yuan Qi later.\"","The Second Madam: \"Take some guards with you. Just enjoy yourselves. It's fine if you come back a little late.\"","Cheng Zhao said she would.","Back at the Fragrant Courtyard, Cheng Zhao had a couple of pastries, took off her hairpins and ornaments, and took a simple bath.","She changed into a more comfortable linen summer dress and, with Suyue and Qiubai, met Zhou Yuanqi at the main gate of the Duke's mansion.","The sun had not yet set, and the light at the gate was still scorching hot, baking the horses until they squinted, listless and weary.","Zhou Yuanqi wore a lake-blue linen gown, his hair tied in the \"total angle\" style of youth.","Sweat had plastered the hair on his forehead, so he parted it to both sides, revealing a smooth, full forehead and a pair of jet-black, round eyes, like ink-black gems.","\"So hot!\" he said.","Cheng Zhao: \"Get in the carriage quickly. There's a plate of ice inside.\"","Zhou Yuanqi climbed in and pressed his hands against the ice immediately, craving the coolness.","Cheng Zhao fanned him.","Suyue and Qiubai were in the carriage too. One handed him cold tea, bringing it to his lips, while the other wiped the sweat from his brow.","At first, the boy was pleased, thinking they were waiting on him attentively. But when Suyue cupped the back of his head to wipe his face, he suddenly realized they were treating him like a child.","He immediately grew annoyed: \"I don't need to be waited on!\"","Cheng Zhao laughed: \"Fine, then I won't fan you. My arm is getting sore anyway.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","By the time the carriage reached the riverbank, they could already see Fan Xiao in the distance.","And standing beside Fan Xiao, dressed in a blue gown, was Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanqi immediately asked: \"Did you invite my Young Uncle and that brute?\"","Cheng Zhao lifted the carriage curtain to take a look: \"Aren't we meeting up with them? Didn't you arrange it?\"","\"No, I invited your fourth brother,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao frowned: \"What do you want with him?\"","Cheng Chao was always unreliable.","\"He has a lot of friends, and he's good to me. Not as annoying as my Young Uncle,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","\"No one is more annoying than my fourth brother,\" Cheng Zhao said.","One liked to tease Zhou Yuanqi, the other liked to provoke Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao thought, she could easily take her brother-in-law out and avoid everyone—why choose between two evils?","\"...How about we just wander around on our own?\" Cheng Zhao suggested. \"We won't join either side.\"","\"We don't have a pleasure boat. All the boats are rented out,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Ullambana Festival was approaching, and many merchants had already launched their boats. After Ullambana came the Mid-Autumn Festival, another perfect time for boating on the river.","Business was booming right now.","Cheng Zhao: \"You're terrible at making arrangements. Next time, I'll handle it.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","As they were talking, voices came from outside.","\"Duchess, aren't you getting out of the carriage?\" someone asked with a smile.","Fan Xiao was standing nearby.","Cheng Zhao had no choice but to step out of the carriage: \"Young Uncle, what a coincidence.\"","\"You're here to have fun too? Come join us?\" he said.","\"We have our own plans,\" Zhou Yuanqi said as he got out after her.","\"We've rented the same boat as Cheng Chao,\" Fan Xiao said.","Zhou Yuanqi turned to Cheng Zhao and said, “You’re right. Your fourth brother really can’t be relied upon.”","Cheng Zhao was silent for a moment.","“You’ve just never suffered enough from him,” he thought. “My fourth brother has never been reliable.”"]},{"number":161,"title":"Chapter 161: An Appointment with Prince Anton of Anle Commandery","paragraphs":["The summer night was lively.","Just as Zhou Yuanqi had said, the weather made the riverbanks the only cool place, attracting countless people to escape the heat.","Shops lined the river, with lanterns hung high; several painted boats floated on the water, their lights dazzling as if molten gold had been scattered into the river’s heart.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi boarded one of the painted boats.","On the boat were several people, as well as the children of Prince Rong’s household, including the Seventh Miss, who had been saved by Cheng Zhao’s maid last time.","Cheng Chao had not yet arrived.","After polite greetings, Cheng Zhao chose a seat near the western side, from where she could overlook the teeming crowd along the river.","The inky waves rippled with the lamplight, swirling in an air of luxury and decadence.","“...Your Fourth Brother still isn’t here.” Zhou Yuanqi leaned closer.","Cheng Zhao: “Did you set a time with him?”","“I said at the hour of the Rooster.”","“It’s already dark—this is the hour of the Dog,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi: “I’m not playing with him anymore!”","Cheng Zhao chuckled softly and lightly stroked his hair.","Then, a little puzzled, she asked, “Why would you want to play with my Fourth Brother? He has neither skill nor learning.”","“...He’s very clever. He knows so many people and everything about everything,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao curled her lip dismissively. “That’s just petty cleverness.”","“A person like that also has great wisdom,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","“You flatter him. He’s the most disobedient, always going his own way...”","As Cheng Zhao rattled off her brother’s faults, Cheng Chao stepped onto the boat.","Behind him followed several familiar sons of noble families, along with Helian Xuan, the Prince of Andong.","Cheng Zhao’s face darkened.","Zhou Yuanqi glanced at her expression, then at Helian Xuan.","Everyone exchanged pleasantries.","“Little Sister.”","“Fourth Brother!”","Her tone carried a veiled warning. Cheng Chao and the others looked at her, but Cheng Zhao quickly smiled again, as if their impression had been mistaken.","On the second floor of the painted boat was a folding screen. In front of it, they could play pitch-pot; behind the screen, two songstresses played zithers and sang.","The music mingled with the water’s ripples, spreading through the night breeze, adding a layer of extravagant ease.","“...How did you trick Yuan Qi and then also invite Helian Xuan?” Cheng Zhao called her Fourth Brother down to the first floor, and the siblings sat down.","Cheng Chao played with the water while gazing at the riverbank scenery. “Don’t slander me. It was Yuan Qi who made an appointment with me, and he specifically asked me to bring some friends to keep him company.”","“And you just had to bring Helian Xuan? Don’t you have any other friends?”","“I didn’t expect you’d come,” Cheng Chao said. “Yuan Qi hinted that I should bring the prince—he likes him quite a bit.”","“You misread the hint. What does a child like him know?” Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Chao replied, “I’m not stupid.”","The siblings bickered a little more.","Cheng Chao added, “I didn’t actually invite him. I ran into him on the bank. He was also here for some fun.”","“Really?”","“Would I deliberately harm you? You’re my own sister.” Cheng Chao rolled his eyes at her.","“You wouldn’t do it on purpose, but you sometimes can’t tell friend from foe. Those sycophants of yours are way more important to you than I am,” Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Chao retorted, “...I’ve never criticized you, yet you’re always trying to control me!”","“If you ever took my words to heart, why would I need to keep an eye on you all day?” Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Chao was exasperated.","It was impossible to reason with her.","No matter what he said, she always had a comeback—she just had to be obeyed.","What right did she have?","If she loved giving orders so much, why wasn’t she born the eldest sister?","The real eldest sister wasn’t nearly as fond of throwing her weight around.","The more you lack something, the more you crave it. The youngest girl, yet she wanted to play “the eldest proper lady”—Cheng Chao wouldn’t indulge her.","Everyone else let her have her way because they were older and didn’t bother with a child’s tantrums, but Cheng Chao was close to her in age and equally wanted to “be the one in charge.”","“Sooner or later, you’ll have to listen to me,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Look at you, so full of yourself. I’m your elder brother—you’ll just have to accept that! What use is being an ultra-rank titled lady?” Cheng Chao said.","Cheng Zhao: “You’re defying me again.”","Cheng Chao: “...”","With nothing more to say that wouldn’t clash, he turned and left, heading upstairs without another word to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao’s maids, Suyue and Qiubai, were both downstairs. Seeing Cheng Chao leave, they handed Cheng Zhao some river lanterns.","“Where did these come from?” Cheng Zhao asked.","“Bought by the river. The Ullambana Festival is almost here, and many vendors have already started selling river lanterns,” Qiubai said. “Light one and make a wish for yourself.”","Cheng Zhao smiled.","Suyue added, “When the festival actually comes, we might not have time. Let’s do one now.”","Cheng Zhao thought she had a point.","Qiubai took out a fire striker, and Cheng Zhao lit the river lantern.","She set it on the water.","Not far away, others were releasing river lanterns too—scattered lights like stars fallen onto the river’s surface.","Cheng Zhao closed her eyes and murmured a prayer.","She prayed for a good future.","Her marriage, her children, her power, and her prospects—she hoped for smooth sailing, even if bumpy. As long as she got what she wanted, it would be a bright road ahead.","“Lord Duke.”","Cheng Zhao opened her eyes and saw Zhou Yuanshen, dressed in a sky-blue long robe, standing beside her. He must have come down without her noticing.","Remembering how he had turned and walked away that afternoon, she closed her eyes again to continue her prayer, ignoring him.","The boat tilted slightly as Zhou Yuanshen sat down beside her.","Cheng Zhao kept ignoring him.","When she finished her prayer and opened her eyes, Zhou Yuanshen was quietly gazing at the river, not looking at her.","Both were silent.","Cheng Zhao couldn’t help wondering—if he wasn’t going to speak, why had he come downstairs?","But she was too lazy to ask.","If he wouldn’t start talking, she wasn’t about to hand him an opening. She wouldn’t indulge that habit of his, or he’d end up as stubborn as Cheng Chao.","“Third Sister-in-Law!” Zhou Yuanqi called from the staircase.","Cheng Zhao stood up. “Are you coming down to play?”","“Come up—we’re playing pitch-pot,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","There wasn’t much fun in it.","Still, anything was better than sitting in silence with Zhou Yuanshen, so Cheng Zhao replied, “Coming,” and headed upstairs.","Zhou Yuanshen rose almost at the same time and followed her up the stairs.","He still said nothing.","Zhou Yuanqi gave him a look, then whispered to Cheng Zhao, “The Brute’s wandering around like a ghost. Haunting us without a sound. Did you annoy him again?”","“I never annoy him,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi pursed his lips. “Well, watch out—you’ve been unlucky lately, attracting all kinds of filth. It’s almost Ghost Festival.”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","With so many outsiders around, Cheng Zhao couldn’t roughhouse with him, or she’d have pinched his cheeks.","She was already frustrated enough, and now he was deliberately trying to scare her. Her young brother-in-law was growing more disobedient by the day.","But he was cute and amusing, so Cheng Zhao was lenient with him.","Prince Rong’s Heir, Helian Jian, suggested they play pitch-pot.","“...Uncle Prince Xuan, what reward would you like?” Helian Jian asked Helian Xuan.","Helian Xuan smiled. With his peach-blossom eyes, the smile was especially charming. “Whatever you decide—I’m easy.”","“The reward will be a cup of wine,” Helian Jian said.","Cheng Zhao found it ridiculous.","Winning just for a cup of wine—what kind of prize was that? Who actually liked drinking?","She lost interest.","Fan Xiao asked, “If children like Yuan Qi join, what would they win?”","“A word of praise, probably,” Helian Jian said.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","Zhou Yuanqi glanced at him, then at his Young Uncle Fan Xiao, silently chiding: Look at the friends you keep!","None of them were reliable—his Young Uncle included."]},{"number":162,"title":"Chapter 162: Winning Once Is Enough","paragraphs":["The group was listening to music on the painted pleasure boat, amusing themselves with pitch-pot, where the winner would drink a cup of wine.","Since there were seven or eight participants, each person only had four arrows, and the scores were calculated by tallying the points.","A friend of Cheng Chao's took the account book and stood to the side, acting as the judge.","A first arrow that landed in the pot earned ten points, piercing through the ear of the pot earned twenty points, and consecutive ear-shots earned fifty points. Consecutive shots into the pot earned twenty points, while scattered arrows earned five points.","There was also the \"leaning on the pole,\" which earned fifty points. The so-called \"leaning on the pole\" meant an arrow balanced on the rim of the pot, hanging without falling.","Zhou Yuanqi was eager to try, and the Seventh Miss from Prince Rong's Mansion was also at the front—perhaps children are always fascinated by games that aren't meant for them.","Cheng Zhao watched from the side.","Zhou Yuanqi missed his first arrow.","After losing the first shot, unless he could land consecutive ear-shots afterward, victory was essentially impossible.","However, no one among the group was particularly skilled at pitch-pot, and several others missed their first arrows too.","It wasn't until Prince of Andong, Helian Xuan, took his turn that things changed.","He hit his first arrow, then landed consecutive ear-shots, and finally achieved a \"leaning on the pole.\"","In the first round, he was the clear winner.","Zhou Yuanqi had already given up and sidled up to Cheng Zhao, poking her gently: \"This guy is pretty good, isn't he?\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","What's with that tone of yours?","He's got nothing to do with you, so why do you seem so pleased? Not only are you satisfied yourself, but you also want me to think highly of him?","Have you lost your mind like Cheng Chao too?","Cheng Zhao looked at him; Zhou Yuanshen also glanced at his younger brother.","Zhou Yuanqi returned their stares dismissively, ignoring their confusion, acting completely self-assured.","Cheng Zhao realized she couldn't figure out what Zhou Yuanshen was thinking, nor could she understand the riddle Zhou Yuanqi was playing at.","Was she just slow on the uptake?","She had only just scored a minor victory in the matter of managing household affairs.","The first round was won by Prince of Andong, Helian Xuan.","He won with such skill that everyone was convinced, so they all offered their congratulations.","When the second round began, Cheng Zhao decided to join in.","She hit her first arrow and also landed an ear-shot.","But she failed on her second ear-shot, and her \"leaning on the pole\" didn't succeed either.","\"That's very good,\" Fan Xiao said with a smile from the side. \"The Duchess has respectable pitch-pot skills.\"","\"Yes, I managed an ear-shot,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Chao, standing beside her, muttered very quietly: \"You really can't take a compliment; one praise and you're floating up to the sky.\"","No one caught what he said.","Cheng Zhao couldn't be bothered to reply.","This time, Helian Xuan didn't join. Though Cheng Zhao's performance wasn't outstanding, she at least landed an ear-shot, so she won this round.","However, she didn't want to drink. In the sweltering summer heat, drinking alcohol made one's body feel stifled, trapping heat inside, and that made Cheng Zhao particularly irritable.","\"Give it to my fourth brother,\" she said.","Zhou Yuanshen, who was beside her, took the cup and downed the wine Cheng Zhao had won.","No one commented, though Fan Xiao couldn't help but chuckle.","Zhou Yuanqi said, \"If you want to drink, go win your own game.\"","Fan Xiao also asked, \"Would the Duke of Chen like to play a round?\"","Then he added, \"And the Duchess? Let's see who wins between you two.\"","Cheng Zhao replied flatly, \"I'm game either way.\"","Zhou Yuanshen nodded slightly: \"That works.\"","Prince of Andong spoke up: \"I'll give it a try too. Pitch-pot is only fun with more people.\"","Since he was truly formidable, once he joined the game, he was sure to win, so others avoided competing against him. But now he offered to join the match between the Duke and Duchess, and the rest didn't want to embarrass themselves, so they just stood by to watch.","\"...Is the Duchess afraid to lose to me?\" Helian Xuan asked Cheng Zhao.","He was smiling, but his smile was unusually faint, and the depth in his eyes was all the more intense.","Cheng Zhao said, \"The outcome is not yet certain, Prince. Please, go ahead.\"","The three of them played pitch-pot.","Cheng Zhao said, \"I'll throw out a brick to attract jade.\"","She started first.","This time, she hit her first arrow and then landed another one in the pot. But when she went for an ear-shot, the pleasure boat swayed, and her arrow went off target.","Cheng Zhao threw another arrow.","Perhaps because she'd missed the last one, she got flustered, and this one missed too.","A sudden wave of frustration and annoyance rose in her heart.","\"...It's not like I'm performing in front of the Emperor,\" she quickly consoled herself. \"It won't affect my future as a respected matriarch. What does it matter if I lose?\"","In just a breath's time, Cheng Zhao had regained her composure.","She accepted her loss gracefully and stepped back, yielding the spot to Helian Xuan and Zhou Yuanshen.","Fan Xiao praised her again: \"Hitting two arrows in a row is quite impressive.\"","\"Thank you, Young Uncle,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","She didn't need everyone around her to be obedient. But if they weren't obedient, at least having sweet words was something she found quite pleasant.","Only Cheng Chao and Zhou Yuanshen—one her older brother, the other her husband—were neither sweet-tongued nor obedient; they just knew how to annoy her.","Over at the game, Helian Xuan started first.","He resorted to his same strategy as the first round: a first shot into the pot, consecutive ear-shots, and then a \"leaning on the pole.\"","Zhou Yuanqi squeezed over to Cheng Zhao and whispered, \"The Prince has won.\"","Then he added, \"He's quite capable, and he's also very handsome.\"","Fan Xiao chuckled: \"Praising the Prince right in front of your sister-in-law—that's terribly improper, Yuanqi. If anyone else heard, there'd be talk.\"","Zhou Yuanqi looked at his young uncle with the same disdain he showed his brute of a brother: \"What do you know!\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"I don't understand either. Why don't you explain it to us? Why are you so insistent on praising him?\"","Zhou Yuanqi replied, \"I admire people with real talent.\"","\"Then when the Duke beats him later, you can praise the Duke,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi said, \"How can he possibly win now?\"","His words had barely left his mouth when Zhou Yuanshen began.","With his first arrow, he achieved a \"leaning on the pole\"—the arrow balanced on the rim of the pot without falling. He earned fifty points.","Everyone gasped and showered him with praise.","Then came the ear-shots.","Two arrows in a row, both piercing the ear of the pot, totaling seventy points.","\"If the fourth arrow also achieves a 'leaning on the pole,' does the score double?\" Zhou Yuanshen asked.","The record-keeper paused for a moment: \"According to the rules, yes, but...\"","But it was rarely ever done.","Because a \"leaning on the pole\" arrow was suspended in balance and could easily be knocked off by a second arrow.","Upon hearing this, Zhou Yuanshen paused thoughtfully, then threw his fourth arrow.","The relaxed and composed Helian Xuan's expression turned grave. At this point, Zhou Yuanshen was only ten points behind him.","Unless Zhou Yuanshen missed this arrow, he would certainly beat Helian Xuan in any case.","Helian Xuan had never faced an opponent in pitch-pot; he always won without fail.","The onlookers couldn't help but hold their breath.","Fan Xiao moved a few steps closer.","Even the qin music from behind the screen seemed to soften.","Everyone seemed to be holding their breath.","Zhou Yuanshen released his fourth arrow.","Another \"leaning on the pole.\"","The two arrows crossed, their tails quivering slightly, yet both stayed balanced on the rim without falling.","A cheer erupted.","\"The Duke of Chen adds a hundred more points.\"","Zhou Yuanshen hadn't just won; he had won by ninety points.","Winning by ten was a narrow victory; winning by ninety was a crushing, devastating rout that left Prince of Andong thoroughly beaten.","Everyone was stunned with admiration, crowding around to look; all praised Zhou Yuanshen's exquisite skill.","Zhou Yuanqi ran over, picked up the two arrows—they were crossed and balanced without touching each other.","\"Third Brother, you are truly formidable, a master of your time!\" Zhou Yuanqi said, making a deep, scholarly bow to Zhou Yuanshen.","Everyone burst into laughter.","Cheng Zhao couldn't help but smile too.","Zhou Yuanshen reached out and gently tousled his younger brother's hair.","Helian Xuan quickly adjusted his expression, stepped forward, and said, \"Congratulations to the Duke of Chen.\"","\"Your concession,\" Zhou Yuanshen replied.","\"Would you like to have another round?\" Helian Xuan asked.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"Winning once is enough, Prince. That will suffice.\"","Helian Xuan: \"...\""]},{"number":163,"title":"Chapter 163: Zhao Cheng Doesn't Care About Him","paragraphs":["The painted boat made one full circuit and returned to the original shore where it started.","The night had grown deep.","The number of sightseers along the bank had not diminished much. Some sat directly on the ground, conversing freely about this and that, using the water ripples, moonlight, and lamplight as their backdrop, appearing quite unrestrained and carefree.","Fan Xiao and the others were still not content.","But the child Zhou Yuanqi began to yawn.","Cheng Zhao also felt sleepy.","\"We'll leave first,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"Everyone need not see us off.\"","A wooden plank was laid down. Zhou Yuanshen stood in the middle, first helping Zhou Yuanqi down, then reaching for Cheng Zhao.","The three of them landed steadily on the shore. The plank was drawn back, and the painted boat continued to glide, stirring the water.","Helian Xuan stood by the window, watching them silently as they walked away, without uttering a word.","Someone consoled him: \"The Duke of Chen merely had a lucky win. If they played another round, he might not repeat his Trick of the Twin Fishing Rods.\"","Helian Xuan smiled faintly.","His smile did not reach his eyes.","Cheng Chao glanced over in that direction. But he did not walk over, nor did he wish to discuss the affairs of Cheng Zhao and her husband with the Prince of Andong.","In Cheng Chao's view, the discord between himself and his younger sister was a family matter.","Family matters were of utmost importance and could not be shared with any outsider, not even with a close friend like the Prince.","Cheng Chao would complain about Cheng Zhao to his elder brothers and sisters, and he would also curse her in his heart, but he rarely voiced his grievances to outsiders.","On the rare occasions he spoke a few words to the Prince of Andong, leading the Prince to think he was on his side, Cheng Chao felt caught in an awkward position.","He needed to have a stand.","And his stand was naturally his sister.","The trio from the Ducal Mansion of Chen headed home, but they separated into two carriages.","It was because Zhou Yuanshen said, \"Yuanqi, sit with me. I have something to ask you alone.\"","Zhou Yuanqi, either feeling guilty or something else, immediately refused: \"I have nothing to answer. If you want to interrogate me, take it to the magistrate's office.\"","He quickly climbed into another carriage, avoiding Zhou Yuanshen.","The other carriage also carried Cheng Zhao's maidservants.","\"Is he deliberately provoking me just to shake me off?\" Zhou Yuanqi couldn't help but suspect.","But he didn't say it out loud.","Once he voiced his suspicion, it would seem as if he had fallen for the trick, undermining his authority. He intended to become Grand Chancellor and the foremost scholar of the realm someday; how could he act foolishly? If he truly had been thrown off by a provocation, he had no choice but to swallow his anger in silence.","The carriage rumbled forward. No lantern was lit inside, and the compartment was pitch dark.","Initially, Zhou Yuanshen and Cheng Zhao sat facing each other.","After they had traveled some distance, he moved over and sat down beside her.","Cheng Zhao was caught between exasperation and amusement.","\"Why did you move over?\" she asked. \"Aren't you refusing to speak to me?\"","Zhou Yuanshen gently grasped her chin and turned her face toward him.","His kiss landed softly on her lips.","Cheng Zhao did not pull away.","She let out a soft snort.","\"Cheng Zhao, I just needed to sort out some things,\" he said.","\"What things do you need to sort out? Why must you sulk about it?\" Cheng Zhao said.","She hadn't been angry at first. But as she thought about his recent words and actions, an inexplicable rage surged within her, growing fiercer the more she spoke.","\"We talked about this before. I don't want to rehash the past,\" he said.","\"What did we talk about?\"","\"You said that you only wanted to be the Duchess of Chen,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"Even if I were no longer the Duke, you'd still only want to be the Duchess.\"","Cheng Zhao fell silent.","She had indeed said those words.","\"I was muddled for a while, Cheng Zhao. You didn't do anything wrong. I simply forgot what you once said,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao didn't know how to respond.","She was silent for a long while before saying, \"Isn't that good, Lord Duke? You won't easily find a more understanding, generous, and magnanimous wife than me.\"","Zhou Yuanshen gave a short laugh.","It was a scoff, mixed with bitterness, a peculiar smile.","\"Am I wrong?\" Cheng Zhao pressed on.","\"... No,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao still seethed with indignation.","Men were insatiable, always wanting more. When young, they craved affection and wanted their wives to be deeply devoted. As they aged and their wives grew old, they would then complain about their jealousy and possessiveness.","Marriage was meant to unite two families, with both sides supporting each other.","If he sought deep passion in his wife, resentment would surely grow later, accusing her of being too fiery and controlling.","Cheng Zhao had always known what it meant to be a wife, and she understood what a titled noblewoman should be. She had done nothing wrong.","\"Cheng Zhao.\"","\"Hm?\"","\"Cheng Zhao.\"","Cheng Zhao looked at him. In the dark carriage, she could only make out his silhouette.","He called her again, \"Cheng Zhao.\"","Cheng Zhao immediately reached up to cover his mouth. \"Don't call me like that!\"","Zhou Yuanshen pulled her into his arms.","Cheng Zhao tried to struggle, but she ended up straddling his lap.","He embraced her even more easily, holding her tightly, pressing her firmly against him.","Men naturally radiated heat, and two cups of wine had heightened his warmth; he was like a brazier. Even his clothes felt hot, let alone the breath he exhaled.","\"Cheng Zhao,\" he whispered by her ear again.","Cheng Zhao bent her head and kissed him, silencing his words.","Don't call her name anymore.","As if she mattered so much.","If he kept this up, she feared she would be dragged into his emotional whirlpool.","She didn't want to end up covered in wounds.","Zhou Yuanshen supported the back of her head and deepened the kiss.","Cheng Zhao's body went half limp, and she let him kiss her, soft and yielding.","\"Should we take the long way around half the city back home?\" he murmured.","Through a haze, Cheng Zhao heard herself answer, \"Yes.\"","The experience this time proved even better than before. Cheng Zhao was almost swept away by sheer pleasure.","When they returned to the Camellia Courtyard late in the night, they bathed together. She leaned quietly against Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"Cheng Zhao, from now on I won't be greedy anymore. Just the two of us this way—it's good enough.\"","\"Mm.\"","Cheng Zhao seemed to breathe a sigh of relief.","\"So starting now, I'll come to you twice a month, as you once suggested. The rest of the time, I'll sleep elsewhere,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao asked, \"Aren't you upset?\"","\"Husband and wife—it seems this is how most households are. I have no reason to be troubled,\" he said.","\"If this isn't a sarcastic remark, then I'll go along with it,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen hummed in acknowledgment.","He looked down at her expression, which had turned noticeably relaxed. His fingers tightened slightly, just like his constricted heart.","He kissed her.","Cheng Zhao, having gotten what she wanted, was unexpectedly gentle and docile. She returned his kiss as if to satiate him and then send him on his way.","She did not like his excessive involvement in her life.","A proper wife's life was about managing the household, raising children, and overseeing servants.","On Cheng Zhao's side, there were attendants to wait on her and ways to amuse herself.","She lacked a child, but a husband had never been part of the Camellia Courtyard's daily affairs.","Zhou Yuanshen told himself he could manage.","Life merely had to return to how it was before.","He was also very occupied with his official duties, with too many tasks demanding his attention. Once he busied himself and grew distant from the inner chambers, he would not be so angry about her coldness any more, nor would he feel such resentment or discontent.","It was better to pull himself out now, before he fell too deeply.","He kissed her.","His actions carried an air of determination.","Finally, Zhou Yuanshen bit down fiercely on Cheng Zhao's lip, as if intending to leave a tooth mark there, ending his longing and returning everything to the way it was before—the normal, so-called ordinary marriage of a noble house.","The next day, as usual, he rose early to attend the morning court.","In the afternoon, he had to go to the Capital Garrison Camp.","This trip to the Garrison Camp would only last three days, since he would accompany the Emperor on a hunting trip at the end of June.","He went first to bid farewell to his mother. Then he turned toward the Camellia Courtyard. But halfway there, he remembered that she would probably think it was an intrusion and fault him for meddling.","Zhou Yuanshen changed direction and headed to his outer study instead.","He sent Mingyu, his senior maidservant, to inform Cheng Zhao.","It was merely a notification—just to let her know where he was, fulfilling his duty as a husband.","Not that Cheng Zhao would care anyway."]},{"number":164,"title":"Chapter 164: Is Being Cute Effective","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao woke up late, which delayed her sword practice.","She quickly ate breakfast and went to Chengming Hall to handle her duties.","Without Eldest Madam Song’s people in Chengming Hall, it felt much emptier. At the same time, it was more solemn, much more like an official government office. It no longer belonged to anyone, which made everyone fear it.","Nanny Qin was rather restrained today.","Probably because the Grand Madam had agreed to the “fifth day” day off, making both Nanny Qin and Huan Qingtang realize that Cheng Zhao was not someone to be trifled with.","They would have to use subtle tactics.","Mere verbal confrontation against her would be ineffective and only exhaust their own energy.","Cheng Zhao remained composed, focusing entirely on her tasks.","There were two matters concerning the carriage house. The steward tried to demand money, but Cheng Zhao found evidence and refuted him—forcing him to back down in embarrassment. The other stewards also realized Cheng Zhao was astute and could not be easily deceived.","The morning passed quickly, and most affairs were clarified. Since she had nothing to attend to in the afternoon, Cheng Zhao returned to Nonghua Courtyard first.","When she arrived, she found Mingyu, the maid from Zhou Yuanshen’s outer study, was there.","Mingyu was very popular among the staff of Nonghua Courtyard. Every time she came, she could chat cheerfully with them. Suyue had even brought out her treasured fine tea to entertain Mingyu.","Cheng Zhao also liked Mingyu very much. The maid never wasted a word; she always stated her reason for coming clearly and handled matters neatly.","“......His Lordship the Duke has gone to the Capital Garrison Camp and will return in three days. The Second Madam and you may start packing your trunks. The hunting trip will last half a month.” She told Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao smiled slightly. “Much obliged to you.”","Then she added, “It’s hot outside. Stay at Nonghua Courtyard and have lunch with Suyue and the others. Go back to the outer study in the late afternoon.”","“Thank you, Young Madam, but there are matters in the outer study requiring my service as well. I will trouble you another day.” Said Mingyu.","Cheng Zhao did not insist. She told Suyue to reward her and see her out.","After Suyue returned from seeing Mingyu off, Cheng Zhao already had a long face, sitting absently on the kang by the window.","—Or not exactly dazed; she was simply worn out, sitting there blankly, too drained even to be angry.","Nanny Li smiled to comfort her: “It’s not that bad. The hunting grounds are very cool. Where the Emperor goes, it can’t be hot.”","Then she added, “If properly maintained, there won’t be too many mosquitoes. The hunting ground isn’t like the wilds of Fengzhou.”","Qiubai promptly said, “In midsummer, the wilds of Fengzhou have not only mosquitoes but also fireflies—very beautiful.”","Cheng Zhao recalled the fireflies and said, “When Father made me copy books as punishment, Fourth Brother knew he was to blame—he’d dragged me into trouble—so he caught loads of fireflies and set them loose in the courtyard.”","That night, when there was no moon, the stars dotted the sky, and the countless fireflies looked like a Milky Way cascading down—an unforgettable sight.","Cheng Zhao was often driven mad by her fourth brother, but he also had his good moments. Giving her fireflies was only one example.","Though she got mad, it did not sour their sibling bond. In Cheng Zhao’s heart, her fourth brother was still very important. After all, she wouldn’t bother to get angry at someone irrelevant.","“Fourth Young Master does care for you as his sister,” said Nanny Li.","Cheng Zhao: “There are too many people in his heart. For instance, the Prince of Andong refuses to be shaken off no matter what.”","Nanny Li: “So, in the end, you’re upset because he’s close to someone you dislike? You're not bothered by his other friendships.”","“Yes.”","“Not everything can go your way, Young Madam,” Nanny Li said, unusually earnest. “Why harm your sibling bond over outsiders? Even the closest blood ties can be worn thin if drained too much.”","Cheng Zhao thought over the words.","She said, “... I don’t actually hate Fourth Brother. It’s just that there’s a bitter grievance I’ve never let out—the humiliation the Prince of An’s mansion dealt me.”","In her young heart, not having endured many hardships or a separation by life and death, she remained upset over the matter.","Nanny Li and the others had tried to console her many times, but she could not let it go.","“Young Madam, grievances like this—hard to swallow and even harder to spit out—how many will one have to experience in a lifetime? This old servant can hardly advise you. You will have to slowly go through it yourself,” Nanny Li sighed.","Cheng Zhao said nothing more.","With a bit of banter, Cheng Zhao’s resistance to the hunting trip eased somewhat.","Suyue and Qiubai would definitely go with her.","“When we go, I’ll catch you tons of fireflies, put them inside your bed curtains, and use them as lanterns,” said Qiubai.","Cheng Zhao grew a little hopeful and nodded. “Bring the finest thin linen to make a bag for them. It’ll be even brighter than lanterns.”","“All right,” Qiubai laughed.","Cheng Zhao remembered that her mother had twice gone hunting with the Empress Dowager.","“Suyue, go home and ask Mother about the hunting grounds, and what essentials we should bring,” Cheng Zhao said.","Suyue agreed and braved the scorching sunlight to return home.","By the late afternoon, as the shadows shifted westward, Cheng Zhao’s preparations were almost done.","Nanny Li was methodical, and maids like Qiubai managed everything daily in good order, making it easy to find anything.","Cheng Zhao had lunch and took a nap, feeling much more refreshed.","Suyue returned from the Cheng family home.","She was carrying a bundle.","“......Madam sent this for you. All sorts of heat-relief medicines, mosquito-repellent sachets, and so on. Madam also said the hunting grounds are in Ningzhou Prefecture. There, the mornings and evenings in midsummer are extremely cool, and mosquitoes are scarce.” Suyue reported.","Then she continued, “Ningzhou is dry, unlike Fengzhou. Madam also asks if you remembered the mosquitoes in the Fengzhou grasslands and got spooked?”","Cheng Zhao: “......”","“Madam added that the titled ladies can also go hunting and horse-riding, which makes it quite enjoyable. It’s not a hardship. The only nuisance is that traveling there and back takes seven or eight days, and the carriage ride is a bit bumpy and uncomfortable,” Suyue said.","Cheng Zhao understood most of it.","She told Suyue to separate the sachets and medicines and give half to her mother-in-law.","By the time she arrived, Zhou Yuanqi was also there.","“I want to go too!” Zhou Yuanqi declared.","“I’d like to take you to broaden your horizons, but I don’t know if it’s allowed. This is an imperial escort trip—everyone going needs an official rank or a title,” Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanqi: “I’m a child.”","Second Madam: “... Don’t fall back on ‘I’m a child’ only when it suits you. That trick works on your parents, not on the court’s regulations.”","Cheng Zhao laughed out loud beside them.","Zhou Yuanqi grumpily said, “You’re all going. Suppose Grandmother tries to harm me—what then?”","“If you’re not at ease, stay temporarily at your grandmother’s house.”","“I’ll go talk to The Brute myself. If he doesn’t agree, it means he’s useless,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Second Madam replied, “With that attitude, who’d help you? Think up some nice words first.”","“What nice words does he like to hear?” Zhou Yuanqi asked Second Madam.","Second Madam didn’t know.","Zhou Yuanqi then asked Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao: “I don’t have to flatter him.”","The two had no conflicting interests—their goals were aligned, and they each needed the other. Why would she have to flatter anyone?","Besides, Cheng Zhao didn’t need to flatter anyone.","“Go ask Mingyu,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi really wanted to go to the hunt, so he went straight to ask Mingyu.","Mingyu told him: “Talk less.”","Another maid also told him: “Be quick in doing things, no rambling.”","The page Nanfeng said: “Lord Duke hates it when people pester him. If you beg him and talk a lot, he’ll be displeased.”","Zhou Yuanqi understood. The Brute wanted everyone around him to be mute.","Pity that he’d already insulted The Brute a lot—said far too much—and he didn’t know if it could be fixed in time.","He felt quite disheartened.","He decided that he would go grab The Brute by the sleeve, look up at him with a pitiful expression, and say as little as possible.","“...If it works for you, you should try this trick next time too.” Zhou Yuanqi was generous, even trying to pass on the tactic to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao: “...”"]},{"number":165,"title":"Chapter 165: Initiate Ambiguity with Him","paragraphs":["Zhou Yuanqi was a rather strategic child.","Upon learning that his father and elder brother would accompany the emperor on a hunting trip, and finding his father at home, he immediately went to ask which imperial relatives would be joining.","His father, always fond of his children, answered all his questions.","\"Will the Prince of Andong be going as well?\" Zhou Yuanqi asked.","\"He is deeply favored by the emperor,\" his father replied.","\"I thought it was the brute who enjoyed the emperor's favor most,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","\"With all the brushes in your study, you don't only use one. Those for small script and large script each have their own purpose and preference,\" Second Master said.","He added, \"When it comes to utility, your third brother's position is unshakable.\"","\"He can kill, and dares to kill,\" Zhou Yuanqi said. \"He is the emperor's blade.\"","\"The sharpest blade. The Prince of Andong is far from his match,\" Second Master smiled. \"No one can surpass your third brother. You can rest assured.\"","Zhou Yuanqi secretly breathed a sigh of relief.","Then, catching himself, he curled his lip in disdain: \"Why should I be relieved? I don't rely on him. I just worry he might drag down my reputation.\"","\"Yes, Yuanqi will one day be the Grand Tutor of the realm.\"","Zhou Yuanqi nodded, satisfied, lifting his chin: \"Well-deserved. Father, just wait and see.\"","Second Master chuckled good-naturedly: \"I'll get to bask in reflected glory too.\"","Zhou Yuanqi graciously nodded, indicating he didn't mind his father soaking up some of his renown.","Three days later, Zhou Yuanshen returned to the city in the late afternoon.","He first went to the inner study, the Morning Dawn Courtyard.","After bathing and changing, he finally arrived at the Crimson Cloud Courtyard for the evening meal.","When he arrived, his father was back from court and Zhou Yuanqi back from school, but Cheng Zhao had not yet come.","\"...Zhaozhao is sometimes busy. It's still early, let's wait for her first,\" Second Madam said.","She told Zhou Yuanshen, \"We've packed the trunks. Also, some insect-repelling sachets—have you prepared those?\"","\"Already done,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"The accompanying officials will prepare these for us as well. Mother needn't worry.\"","Second Madam said nothing more.","Zhou Yuanqi then brought up his request.","\"Third Brother, I want to go too!\" He tugged at Zhou Yuanshen's sleeve, looking at him with big, pleading eyes.","The child's eyes were jet-black and round, clear and bright. When softened, they were especially endearing.","Zhou Yuanshen looked at him, subtly pulling his sleeve free: \"No.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","The brute, unwavering and unyielding, was indeed hard to handle. Fortunately, he had a backup plan.","\"If you don't take me, I'll go find the Prince of Andong. He and I are on good terms; he'd be willing to bring me along,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","He added, \"I've inquired. Bringing family is allowed. Mother has a third-rank mandate title, and Third Sister-in-Law has a super-rank mandate title. They are officially accompanying the Empress, so they don't count as family members.\"","\"The court situation is complicated. Even the hunting grounds are a battlefield. The adults can barely handle themselves; they won't have time for you,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"I'm not afraid. Maybe I could even help you.\"","\"You are too young, and overly confident in your cleverness. It's easy to be used by others,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Zhou Yuanqi was furious.","He was being humiliated.","What did he mean by \"overly confident in his cleverness\"? He was genuinely brilliant.","But Zhou Yuanqi, needing a favor, held back from retorting.","Then Zhou Yuanshen continued, \"In the hunting grounds, with horses everywhere, there's horse manure all around. Fresh horse manure—they might have you little kids pick it up to burn for campfires at night.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: !","He immediately abandoned the idea of going.","\"Disgusting!\"","Having decided not to go, Zhou Yuanqi felt that his earlier restraint towards his brother was a loss.","So, seizing an opportunity, he threw a few jabs at Zhou Yuanshen, calling him all brawn and no brains, empty-headed—but Zhou Yuanshen completely ignored it.","Cheng Zhao arrived soon after.","She greeted her father- and mother-in-law, then performed a curtsey to Zhou Yuanshen before sitting down.","She said, \"Lord Duke, I have prepared a trunk with ten sets of your summer linen clothes, just in case. If you find yourself short of anything during the trip, please let me know.\"","Zhou Yuanshen gave a slight nod: \"You are thoughtful.\"","\"My duty,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Very considerate.","A good wife.","And a qualified Duchess of the State.","Zhou Yuanshen looked straight ahead, his expression blank.","The evening meal went pleasantly enough. After eating, Zhou Yuanqi was about to leave when he called Cheng Zhao aside. The sister-in-law and brother-in-law spoke under the eaves.","\"Did he agree?\" Cheng Zhao asked first.","Zhou Yuanqi felt his efforts on his third sister-in-law's behalf had not been in vain. His sister-in-law kept his affairs in mind and remembered them.","\"No,\" Zhou Yuanqi said. \"Next time, don't try to plead with him piteously. It doesn't work; he's not swayed by that.\"","Cheng Zhao nodded, indicating she had taken the lesson to heart.","The day before departure, Cheng Zhao told the stewards under her charge: \"I will be away for at least twenty days. For anything, please consult Eldest Young Madame.\"","Then she said to Huan Qingtang, \"Sister-in-law, I'll trouble you.\"","\"You're too kind, sister-in-law,\" Huan Qingtang said. \"Managing the household is the proper duty of the eldest daughter-in-law; it's no hardship.\"","Cheng Zhao exchanged a few more pleasantries with her, then returned to the Fusang Courtyard.","The stewards were uneasy. They wondered if Cheng Zhao would still have a place in the Hall of Inherited Brilliance upon her return. If the Dowager Madame came to depend on Eldest Young Madame, should they switch allegiance, or wait?","Everyone's thoughts were divided.","Huan Qingtang's senior maid was delighted.","\"Young Madame, this is your opportunity. Perhaps in a few days, we can move to the Hall of Inherited Brilliance,\" the senior maid said with a smile.","But Huan Qingtang remained silent.","To move into the Hall of Inherited Brilliance, she needed a title.","She was not the widowed Eldest Young Madame; she would need to be the Duchess of the other branch of the jointly-inherited Chen State Duke Mansion.","The reason the First Madam lived in the Hall of Inherited Brilliance was that she, too, held a Duchess's mandate title. Huan Qingtang had none.","Being included in the imperial hunting entourage should have been the perfect time for the emperor to betroth her to Zhou Yuanshen. All it would take was for the Dowager Madame to summon her, for the emperor to let her go.","Yet, no one mentioned it.","The Dowager Madame hadn't even brought it up.","It was Nanny Sun who had gently hinted the day before, telling her to behave well during this period and not disappoint the Dowager Madame again.","Huan Qingtang's heart tightened.","Had the Dowager Madame given up on her?","If these twenty days passed without incident and no opportunity arose for her to shine, her grandmother's anger would persist. If something did happen under her watch, it would be her fault, and she wouldn't be able to escape responsibility.","What was a hot potato?","This was it.","She couldn't take it, and she couldn't refuse it. And the people around her, optimistic and blind, thought it was good luck.","Huan Qingtang traced back the source of the problem.","It all came down to Zhou Yuanshen.","If only, earlier, she had been a bit more ingratiating, a little ambiguous and flirtatious regardless of propriety, things might have gone smoother now.","Her charm could easily handle a man.","Huan Qingtang sighed softly.","The senior maid kept chattering, offering suggestions. Huan Qingtang cut her off: \"Prepare some insect repellent and heatstroke remedy, then send them to Lord Duke. Tell him to take care along the way.\"","\"Who will deliver them?\" the senior maid asked.","Huan Qingtang was silent.","The senior maid understood her intention and said, \"Shall this maid accompany you?\"","\"Stooping like that would be beneath my dignity,\" she said.","\"Young Madame, you cannot afford to be aloof. You'll lose to Cheng if you are. Since you want to send it, you should deliver it yourself,\" the senior maid said.","Huan Qingtang pondered.","She nodded: \"Go prepare it. I'll take it to the Morning Dawn Courtyard.\"","Then, thinking it over, \"No. I'll take it to the Fusang Courtyard.\"","Straight to Cheng Zhao's face.","Open and aboveboard.","Sometimes, that could be more effective."]},{"number":166,"title":"Chapter 166: Duke Chen Guo Eats Vinegar","paragraphs":["Huan Qingtang arrived at Nonghua Courtyard.","It came as a surprise.","Cheng Zhao had changed into a homespun linen blouse and was seated in the eastern side room, eating chilled milk curds. When her maid announced the visitor, she almost thought she had misheard.","“Invite the elder sister-in-law in,” Cheng Zhao said, setting down her bowl.","Suyue brought water for her to rinse her mouth, asking softly, “What’s she doing here?”","“We’ll find out soon enough,” Cheng Zhao replied.","After rinsing, she straightened her collar and went to the main hall to see Huan Qingtang.","Huan Qingtang wore a light purple linen short jacket embroidered with sprigs of crabapple blossoms, paired with a plain white silk long skirt. The outfit surprisingly highlighted her refined yet subtly alluring grace.","She was evidently a beauty, carrying herself with composure since becoming a widow.","Cheng Zhao generally admired such beauties, but Huan Qingtang’s bearing too closely resembled that of her own third sister. With her sister’s precedent setting the standard, Cheng Zhao’s gaze toward Huan Qingtang cooled considerably.","“Why has the elder sister-in-law come?” she asked with a smile, then ordered the servants to serve the finest tea.","Huan Qingtang handed a small bundle to Cheng Zhao. “These are insect repellents and heat-relief remedies. I thought they might come in handy when accompanying the imperial entourage to the hunting grounds.”","“I’ve already prepared some. Still, many thanks to the elder sister-in-law,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Sister-in-law, these are for the Lord Duke. He is the head of the household, and our very lives depend on him,” Huan Qingtang replied.","Cheng Zhao was startled.","Having been through many things recently, she was no longer easily surprised, yet this caught her off guard.","She looked at Huan Qingtang.","Huan Qingtang, perhaps thinking she had successfully provoked her, fixed her gaze on Cheng Zhao. “If you don’t wish to pass them along, just throw them away.”","Cheng Zhao was taken aback.","After a moment’s pause, she laughed.","It was faintly amusing, so her smile carried no deeper meaning—just simple mirth. “Elder sister-in-law, why would I discard your kind gesture? It must have been difficult for you to bring them over. Cuiyun Courtyard is close to the inner study; you could have delivered them there directly.”","She added, “Couldn’t get in? I’ll speak to the Lord Duke and ask him to tell Nanfeng to let you in next time.”","Huan Qingtang’s expression tightened. “Sister-in-law, your words are unkind. There’s no need for us to be like this. Harmony is what matters.”","Cheng Zhao replied, “First you accuse me of being unkind, then you blame me for not being harmonious. Weren’t you the one who broke the peace?”","Huan Qingtang’s face changed color dramatically.","She still carried her proud demeanor, but after enduring several exchanges, she realized Cheng Zhao wasn’t taking the bait at all—she was utterly outmatched.","She stood up. “It’s getting late. I’ll take my leave now.”","She turned to leave.","From behind, Cheng Zhao raised her voice slightly. “Elder sister-in-law, go slowly.”","After she left, Suyue and Qiubai were both irritated.","“She’s declaring war on you, young mistress,” Suyue said, quick-tempered as ever. “And you’re letting her off?”","Cheng Zhao wasn’t angry.","She found the whole thing absurd from start to finish.","Everyone understood that there was no room for two legitimate wives beside the Lord Duke. Cheng Zhao knew it, and Huan Qingtang knew it too. What amused Cheng Zhao was that Huan Qingtang had gone out of her way to come and try to anger her.","She wasn’t about to get mad.","Even if the joint heirship arrangement went through—the worst-case scenario—Cheng Zhao wouldn’t give an inch.","“...Should we throw the bundle away?” Qiubai asked.","Suyue said, “Throw it! It’s disgusting to look at!”","Cheng Zhao replied, “Why throw it? Give it to the Lord Duke.”","Qiubai looked at her with concern.","Suyue blurted out, “Have you gone mad with fury?”","“If I don’t pass it on, it looks like I lack magnanimity as the legitimate wife. If I do give it to him, it proves she had a private exchange before having a proper title among us—she’ll owe me a debt for the rest of her life,” Cheng Zhao explained.","Even if the worst happened and the joint heirship succeeded, Cheng Zhao would still have the upper hand over her.","Huan Qingtang had handed her a weapon; throwing it away in a fit of pique would have been a wasted opportunity.","The next day they were set to depart.","That evening, Zhou Yuanshen came to Nonghua Courtyard.","As in the early days of their marriage, his visit meant they would share the same bed.","Cheng Zhao had already bathed.","After letting her hair dry, she applied osmanthus hair oil, filling the room with a rich, fragrant scent.","She loved the smell; it put her in a good mood.","However, Zhou Yuanshen seemed to dislike strong odors intensely. After their wedding, she only used this oil when he wasn’t around.","Since she wouldn’t be able to wash her hair for several days during the trip, Cheng Zhao indulged herself with it tonight.","When Zhou Yuanshen entered, she was sitting on the couch by the window, reading.","She was flipping through a historical text she had read countless times before.","“...That’s osmanthus,” he remarked.","“Yes, hair oil,” she confirmed.","“Very fragrant,” he said, his tone unreadable—whether displeasure or praise was unclear.","His dark eyes were serene.","Cheng Zhao put down her book.","“Should we turn in early?” she asked. “We have to rise early tomorrow.”","Zhou Yuanshen gave a low grunt of assent.","As he stood up, his gaze fell on the small bundle lying beside the couch.","He frowned slightly. “What’s this?”","Cheng Zhao studied his expression. “Have you seen a bundle like this before, Lord Duke?”","“It has a tassel on it. I’ve seen this kind of tassel a few times.” His frown deepened. “How did it end up here?”","Cheng Zhao noticed the light pink tassel tied at the bundle’s knot.","“The elder sister-in-law brought it over,” she said.","Zhou Yuanshen’s face darkened.","“She said they were insect repellents and heat-relief remedies, specially for you, Lord Duke, fearing you might suffer while away,” Cheng Zhao explained, picking up the bundle and handing it to him.","Zhou Yuanshen swept his hand across, sending the bundle rolling to the floor.","Some medicine bottles inside clinked crisply.","The servants waiting outside held their breath, their footsteps freezing. For a moment, Nonghua Courtyard fell into absolute silence.","Zhou Yuanshen’s chest rose and fell subtly.","He kept telling himself to stay calm.","Cheng Zhao had no feelings for him—he could accept that. She had never promised anything.","He had held back.","An ordinary marriage would suffice; many couples were no better than they were. Cheng Zhao knew how to enjoy life, and their nights together were pleasurable.","He had many great matters to attend to; he couldn’t afford to be distracted.","He thought he had managed well.","But now, Cheng Zhao had handed him this bundle, making it painfully clear that she held no place for him in her heart.","He was an outsider to her.","Given the chance, she would push him away.","Just like the Dowager Madame had—treating him like a stud.","Zhou Yuanshen’s resentment surged like a towering wave.","He stepped forward and fiercely pulled Cheng Zhao into his arms.","He held her tight.","She was in his embrace, her jade-like skin delicate. He refused to believe he couldn’t leave a mark on her.","He refused to believe he was so ineffective as to leave no trace in her heart.","He leaned down and kissed her.","Her lips were so soft they seemed to melt at his touch. Zhou Yuanshen had wanted to bite down hard, to make her feel pain—to make her feel him.","But his teeth clenched, and he couldn’t bear to hurt her.","How could he ever bear to harm her?","He squeezed his arms tighter, longing to press her into his very being.","“Cheng Zhao!” All his burning rage and frustration dissolved into that single cry, as if trying to prying open a crack in the door to her heart.","“Cheng Zhao, look at me,” he demanded, gripping her chin.","Her gaze was dazed upon him.","“Look at me.” His eyes seemed to glisten with moisture.","Cheng Zhao looked bewildered, then rose slightly to wrap her arms around him, kissing him.","Her face obscured his line of sight.","She was in his arms—but she was not truly in his eyes."]},{"number":167,"title":"Chapter 167: Words Between the Lines","paragraphs":["The window was half open, and a basin of ice sat within. A gentle breeze drifted in, carrying a hint of cool refreshment.","Cheng Zhao was exhausted and fell asleep quickly, but Zhou Yuanshen did not sleep.","He rose, picked up the bundle from the floor, and called for the night attendant: “Throw this away.”","Utterly inauspicious.","The maid Suyue answered and entered.","Suyue took the bundle—inside, various jars and bottles had already shattered into pieces.","She recalled Huan Qingtang’s provocative sneer, then looked at the bundle the Lord Duke had discarded with such disdain, and could not help but chuckle.","The breath Suyue had been holding finally escaped.","The Lord Duke truly was an excellent master!","The Nonghua Courtyard returned to quiet.","The maids had all retired, the night cicadas in the treetops had fallen silent, leaving only the chirping of crickets by the wall.","Zhou Yuanshen returned to the bed and let down the thin gauze curtain.","The horn lamp had not yet been covered, and Cheng Zhao was already fast asleep. A pale yellow glow fell upon her face; a lingering flush colored her cheeks, set off by her dark hair, like petals in full bloom.","Zhou Yuanshen touched her cheek, his heart soft and full, yet with a faint, aching tightness.","He let out a barely audible sigh, covered the horn lamp, drew Cheng Zhao into his arms, and closed his eyes to sleep.","The next morning they set out, meeting the imperial carriage procession at the west city gate.","Second Madam rose early and first instructed Zhou Yuanqi: “Be good at home. Your third brother said his outer study has people on duty—if you sense any danger, go there for help.”","Zhou Yuanqi murmured in assent.","He was not too pleased, but fearing he might be made to pick up horse manure, he did not insist on going along.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam, dressed properly, took a small oiled carriage to Shou’an Courtyard to take their leave of the Dowager Madame.","“When you are away from home, be cautious in word and deed everywhere. Do not disgrace the Duke’s household,” the Dowager Madame instructed.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam acknowledged.","With departure imminent, they complied with the Dowager Madame in everything, so as not to provoke harsh words from her.","That would be terribly inauspicious.","Second Madam was superstitious about such things.","Seeing their agreeable attitude, the Dowager Madame said nothing more and instead gave them some pastries, telling them to eat them on the road to tide themselves over.","“Grandmother, we will take our leave now,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","The Dowager Madame gave a slight nod.","Cheng Zhao bade farewell with her mother-in-law, and as they came out of Shou’an Courtyard, the two women exchanged a smile.","Zhou Yuanshen would not travel with them; he was to accompany the Crown Prince. Second Master was to travel with the other officials and likewise would not ride in the family carriage.","Eight carriages drove out from the Duke of Chen’s residence.","Second Madam and Cheng Zhao each brought two maids and had their own carriages, but to chat and pass the time on the road, they rode together.","A basin of ice was placed in the carriage, and the morning breeze was a bit cool—not too hot.","“My archery is excellent. Later I’ll shoot a pair of white foxes for you, to make fur collars for autumn and winter,” Second Madam said to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao: “Thank you, Mother. Your archery must be outstanding—I look forward to seeing it for myself.”","Second Madam: “I’m not as good now. But in my youth, I could boast of ‘hitting a willow leaf from a hundred paces.’”","“Mother, your skills are still sharp.”","“You child, always flattering me. I’m getting so giddy I’ll have to shoot you two white foxes for sure,” Second Madam laughed.","Then she added, “Ah Shen’s archery is even better. Later, have him shoot a few white foxes for you too, so you can make a white fox cloak.”","“If we truly get several fine white foxes, this trip will not be wasted,” Cheng Zhao said.","The two women chatted and laughed all the way, and soon the carriage reached the city gate.","The imperial procession arrived shortly after, along with the carriages of various noble families. Outriders cleared the way, imperial guards stood strict watch, and the grand column streamed out of the city in full majesty.","Out of curiosity, Cheng Zhao peered ahead and behind when the procession turned a corner.","Second Madam did not scold her daughter-in-law for being less than dignified; instead she smiled and said, “Let me have a look too.”","Cheng Zhao stepped back, and Second Madam also examined the front and back.","Then she clicked her tongue: “Can’t see the head or the tail—so many carriages, at least a hundred.”","She asked Cheng Zhao, “With such a grand display for a hunt, don’t the censors scold the Emperor?”","Cheng Zhao knew a bit of historical anecdote and smiled as she said, “It seems the founding emperor established the hunting custom. One year, some ministers said it wasted resources and should be abolished.","The founding emperor was receptive to advice. But then, for two consecutive years, there was severe drought in the south during spring planting and floods in the north during the autumn harvest; after floods and droughts came plagues, and the fields were strewn with the starving dead.”","Second Madam: “Every dynasty has bad years.”","“And then there was war on top of it—it took five or six years to recover,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: “That was decades ago. I didn’t live through it. Your maternal grandmother probably remembers it.”","“My grandfather lived through it. He always said that we who have never gone hungry treat small matters as big ones. In the face of hunger, everything else is trivial.","The Cheng clan of Wu Commandery had accumulated great wealth for generations, and my grandfather was the eldest legitimate son. If even he had to go hungry, you can imagine how bad those years were,” Cheng Zhao said.","“...And afterward, it was blamed on not going hunting?”","“The founding emperor had to find some pretext—could he just let the censors say the Son of Heaven had lost virtue and heaven had sent down calamity?” Cheng Zhao said.","“Hush! Don’t let anyone hear you!” Second Madam covered her mouth.","Usually, it was Cheng Zhao covering Second Madam’s mouth.","“These years haven’t been without bad harvests either. How much turmoil there was in the late years of the late emperor—we lived through it ourselves,” Second Madam said.","“Yes, it has nothing to do with hunting. But precisely because of that incident, the censors have never dared to oppose the Emperor’s hunts,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: “Seems like he doesn’t go every year.”","“The Emperor himself doesn’t want to go every year—it’s exhausting,” Cheng Zhao said.","The first day’s journey was not too tiring.","That night they lodged at the estate of a wealthy local family. This household hosted the Emperor’s hunt every time; the estate was unused in between but well maintained—almost a secondary palace.","The guard was strict.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam, along with several other noble ladies, were housed in the same courtyard.","Someone knocked at the courtyard gate.","The stewardess entered and said to Cheng Zhao, “Duchess of Chen, the Lord Duke requests a word with you.”","Cheng Zhao had not yet changed her clothes. She thanked her and secretly slipped a red envelope of money to the stewardess before stepping out of the courtyard.","Armed guards stood at the gate.","Cheng Zhao emerged and saw Zhou Yuanshen in soft armor.","The night had no moon, and the starlight was faint, tracing his silhouette with a thin frost-like outline.","Upright, unyielding—all the refined elegance of a noble scion had vanished; his stance was straighter than a spear shaft.","His face was without expression, colder than frost and snow.","“Lord Duke,” Cheng Zhao said, performing a formal bow.","Zhou Yuanshen: “Not yet retired for the night?”","“I was speaking with Mother, waiting for the hot water to be distributed,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen: “If there’s any inconvenience, just say so, and have people handle it. You are the ladies of the Duke of Chen’s household—no one else in this courtyard outranks the two of you.”","Cheng Zhao smiled and acknowledged.","“One more thing: you must remind Mother that once we reach the hunting grounds, she must not take up a bow. Riding is fine, but shooting is not allowed,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao’s heart tightened slightly: “Why...”","“This time, the bows and arrows will bear marks—whoever takes one will have it recorded under their name,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “One can never be too careful.”","Cheng Zhao looked at him deeply.","He returned her gaze and gave a barely perceptible nod.","Cheng Zhao understood.","“I will tell Mother. Please rest assured,” she said.","Zhou Yuanshen: “If the pace is quick, we’ll arrive in three days. Endure for two more days.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged again.","When he finished speaking and turned to leave, Cheng Zhao called out after him: “Lord Duke.”","Then she added, \"Lord Duke, you must be careful in every way as well. Please take good care of yourself—Mother will worry.\"","\"All right.\" He did not turn back, but answered her before walking away.","Cheng Zhao returned to the courtyard.","She relayed Zhou Yuanshen’s words to Second Madam.","Second Madam said, \"What a pity. I was hoping to hunt a white fox for you.\"","\"Mother, as long as our family is safe and sound, won’t there be white foxes in some hunting ground or another in the future?\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam trusted her daughter-in-law above all, and she nodded at once. \"Well said!\""]},{"number":168,"title":"Chapter 168: The Duke's Love for His Wife","paragraphs":["At noon on the day they reached the hunting grounds, a violent rainstorm broke out.","The rain fell with tremendous force, pouring down like a waterfall, the spray it kicked up joining sky and earth into one blur, the curtain of water separating people who stood only a step apart.","Cheng Zhao sat in the carriage with her mother-in-law.","“...Zhao Zhao, come sit closer,” Second Madam said.","The Zhou family’s carriage was luxurious and sturdy, and it did not leak even under the drenching downpour, but the curtains could not keep out the rain, and half the carriage was already damp.","Second Madam sat near the inner wall.","She pulled Cheng Zhao close, the two women—mother and daughter-in-law—huddling together to keep the rain seeping in from the windows and the carriage door from soaking their shoes, socks, and skirts.","“This rain won’t last long,” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao replied, “Yes.”","Then added, “It should be quite cool after the rain. We’ve been lucky with the weather on this trip, Mother.”","Second Madam smiled. “You really are a good child.”","Cheng Zhao was beautiful, intelligent, sweet-tongued, and optimistic. With her by her side, Second Madam’s spirits stayed high no matter what circumstances she faced.","She was not just a spoonful of honey—she was also a steady pillar of support.","As the two women chatted, someone lifted the carriage curtain.","A huge oil-paper umbrella blocked the rain at the door, and with it, the light filtering in. A man in dark soft armor, wearing a straw rain cape and conical hat, his face hidden in shadow.","“Mother,” he said.","“Are you soaked? Why not get in and wait out the rain?” Second Madam leaned forward as if to feel whether he was wet.","With her own children, Second Madam doted on them as much as possible.","“Not wet,” Zhou Yuanshen said. “Take this umbrella, both of you. There’s still twenty li to go. The carriages ahead are moving forward one by one, so you may have to wait another hour.”","“We’re in no hurry,” Second Madam said. “The two of us can keep each other company in conversation. Waiting is no trouble.”","Zhou Yuanshen gave a slight nod.","He looked at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao said, “Lord Duke, rest assured. Mother and I will look after each other.”","Zhou Yuanshen made a soft sound of acknowledgment.","His gaze traveled over her, from head to foot, and only when he saw that not a drop had touched her did he let the wet carriage curtain fall back into place.","He tucked the oil-paper umbrella away on the carriage, then turned and left.","The official road was muddy and slick, and the rain was still heavy. He had walked a good long stretch through the downpour, coming all the way over just to check on his mother and Cheng Zhao.","He had worried they might not know what was happening and would sit there anxious for no reason.","Some people’s carriages kept leaking, soaking them through. He had also wondered whether their own carriage could be trusted. If they got wet, with nowhere to take shelter and having to sit in damp clothes for an hour or two, they might fall ill.","If that had been the case, he had already planned how to send the two of them on to their destination first.","Fortunately, none of his fears had come to pass. Zhou Yuanshen’s steps were still quick on the way back. Only this time, unlike the urgent pace of his arrival, his stride was light and brisk.","“Yuanshen has always been very attentive,” Second Madam said to Cheng Zhao.","“The Lord Duke is filial,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile. “Mother, you truly raised a fine son. When I have children of my own someday, you must help me—teach me how to manage and guide them.”","“Your own mother is far more capable than I am,” Second Madam said with a laugh.","Cheng Zhao replied, “I’ll take lessons from both sides. There’s no harm in having more wisdom.”","Second Madam laughed.","She gently took Cheng Zhao’s hand and said with a smile, “Yuanshen dotes on you too. He came all this way just to see how you were.”","“We are husband and wife. I care about the Lord Duke as well,” Cheng Zhao said.","Hearing this, Second Madam turned to glance at her.","As a woman who had weathered many years of marriage herself, Second Madam seemed to understand now what had caused the silent quarrel between her son and daughter-in-law some time ago.","But this kind of matter, the mother-in-law had better not interfere, nor even hint at it—let the young couple sort it out themselves.","No sooner had these words been spoken than someone else arrived.","\"Elder sister.\"","Cheng Zhao heard Fan Xiao's voice.","Second Madam was taken aback.","She lifted the carriage curtain and saw Fan Xiao, quite surprised: \"Why have you come as well?\"","\"I came to receive the convoy at Ningzhou Prefecture, elder sister,\" Fan Xiao said with a smile. \"The rain has let up a little; the carriage ahead has already started moving.\"","Behind Fan Xiao stood Second Master, dressed in a straw rain cloak and wide-brimmed hat.","He normally couldn't leave the procession, but Fan Xiao was inspecting the carriage train and had deliberately brought him along.","\"Is the carriage leaking?\" Second Master asked.","Second Madam: \"No, it's not.\"","\"Sitting must be rather tiring—bear with it a while longer,\" Second Master said.","Second Madam: \"No matter how tiring it is, it's not as tiring as you riding on horseback. We're sitting comfortably, so don't worry about those of us who are at ease.\"","Second Master simply smiled.","The carriage ahead finally moved at last.","High-summer rain comes swiftly, falls violently, but stops just as fast.","In but a moment, sunlight spilled across the official road, and each muddy puddle shimmered with blazing rays like rippling waves of light.","Beside the hunting grounds stood a temporary palace; behind the palace stretched countless dwellings.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam were assigned to a courtyard near the front. This time it was a separate residence—they shared it with no one—just the two of them, mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, along with their maids and serving-women.","Some of the trunks had gotten damp; Suyue was briskly putting things in order, with Qiubai helping alongside—the two worked quickly.","No sooner had Cheng Zhao sat down than Qiubai brought over a set of Cheng Zhao's spare clothes, shoes, and socks: \"Young Madam, you should change in a moment.\"","Second Madam remarked, \"Your maids are very efficient.\"","\"They're like my right and left hands—I can't do without them for a single moment,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","\"Then in the future, we'll pick two good lads from among the household-born servants in the outer courtyard for them. Once they marry, they can stay by your side and continue serving,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao: \"I'll wait a couple of years.\"","Until she obtained the rights and standing befitting a \"Duchess of the realm,\" only then would she choose husbands for Suyue and Qiubai.","As they were speaking, a female official from the Empress's side arrived: \"It's time to pay your respects to Her Grace, Duchess of Chen.\"","Cheng Zhao acknowledged and rose.","She and Second Madam changed their clothes and shoes, re-did their hair, and dusted on a light layer of powder before going to see the Empress.","The Empress looked somewhat weary.","Three days of travel by carriage had worn Empress Guo out thoroughly. She normally didn't practice martial arts, ride horses, or wield spears—sitting still for so long was a trial for her.","She still had to muster her spirits.","\"On this hunting expedition with His Majesty, you must all observe propriety. However, don't constrain yourselves too much—you may ride horses and go hunting. I look forward to seeing what you can do,\" the Empress said with a smile.","Everyone acknowledged in unison.","\"Your Grace, I heard that a white wolf will be released this year,\" a young lady said with a smile.","Second Madam didn't recognize her.","Cheng Zhao, however, had seen her before. She whispered to Second Madam: \"The daughter of the Chief Justice of the Grand Court, Miss Liu.\"","At mention of the white wolf, the faces around them showed a flicker of alarm.","White wolves are extremely rare—the leaders of all wolves, the overlords among wolf-kings—and they have always been wild; no one has yet bred a white wolf in captivity.","\"There is indeed a white wolf,\" the Empress said with a smile. \"That is precisely why I must caution you all: if you go hunting, take care. Go in larger groups, keep the guards close, and don't wander off alone. The white wolf has been trained for less than three months—its wild nature is still strong—so be wary of it harming anyone.\"","The ladies and young misses murmured among themselves in quiet discussion.","Empress Guo's niece, Guo Hanzhang, turned her gaze toward Cheng Zhao.","Sitting close to Cheng Zhao and favored by the Empress, she was a bit unrestrained. She asked Cheng Zhao, \"Duchess of Chen, will you be joining the hunt?\"","Cheng Zhao shook her head. \"I'm not skilled at archery or riding. I'll just watch from the side.\"","Guo Hanzhang seemed rather disappointed."]},{"number":169,"title":"Chapter 169: A Nighttime Date for the Couple","paragraphs":["The Empress received the ladies.","She spoke a few words but seemed drained of energy, her smile strained.","This time accompanying the imperial carriage, none of the high-ranked consorts had come—only she alone, and she was struggling.","Her niece Guo Hanzhang said at her side, “All the ladies must be weary. Please go rest. Less than three li from the traveling palace is the grassland outside the hunting grounds. If you are not too tired, you may go out riding.”","The young ladies were excited; most of the married women, like the Empress herself, ached all over from three days of jostling in carriages.","The guests took their leave and withdrew.","Guo Hanzhang also walked out, with her mother.","“...The Duchess of Chen truly is unskilled at riding and archery?” she asked Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao: “Yes.”","“The women’s academies in the capital also teach riding and archery,” Guo Hanzhang said.","Cheng Zhao: “I never attended any women’s academy. I was instructed at home from an early age.”","—The Cheng family had not only clan schools, but also women’s academies.","Guo Hanzhang heard her words and glanced at her: “The Cheng clan of Wu Commandery indeed has deep foundations.”","“Our ancestors were diligent, and we descendants simply enjoy the fruits of their labor. When it comes to foundations, none can match the Guo clan,” Cheng Zhao said.","Guo Hanzhang: “...”","Her gaze was complicated.","Somewhat irritated, a touch disdainful; she wanted to seize on a fault of Cheng Zhao’s and make an issue of it, but feared her aunt’s anger. Empress Guo and Grand Princess Fukang were both very fond of Cheng Zhao, and they were both right here. If Cheng Zhao complained, Guo Hanzhang would be scolded—not worth it.","She left first.","Second Madam then said to Cheng Zhao, “Before your grandmother, you praised the Zhou family as number one in foundations in this dynasty.”","“Words are just embroidery patterns. Stitch whatever colored thread you like—effortless, Mother,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: “...”","She couldn’t help laughing.","Mother-in-law and daughter-in-law returned to their courtyard and spoke of Guo Hanzhang.","Second Madam said that Guo Hanzhang was spoiled and willful by nature, and the Guo family’s standards were too high, so she had reached eighteen without being betrothed.","“If I were Madam Guo, I would be anxious,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: “Anxious over what? With Grand Preceptor Guo’s standing and power at court, Guo Hanzhang’s nobility rivals that of a princess. She may pick whichever sort of man she wishes.”","Cheng Zhao: “It’s precisely because she rivals a princess that it’s frightening. The court’s weakness is beginning to show; the north and the south are both unsettled. Suppose a vassal state sends troops to raid and harass, then comes to the capital to beg forgiveness and demands a ‘princess’ for a peace marriage—and the emperor has no daughter of marriageable age. Then the unmarried daughters of meritorious noble families would be in danger.”","Second Madam: “...You think very far ahead.”","“Others may not need to consider such things, and parents may not want to think about them, but when disaster comes, crying is too late,” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam took her hand: “Zhao Zhao will be a good mother one day.”","“Yes.”","They both laughed.","Come nightfall, Zhou Yuanshen actually appeared.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam were mildly surprised.","Second Madam asked him, “Were you not with the others up front?”","“Today’s duties are finished, and I have some free time now. I’m not staying here—just came to check in,” he said.","Second Madam looked at him, then at her daughter-in-law, and couldn’t help smiling. “You two talk for a while. I’ll go refresh myself.”","She excused herself.","Cheng Zhao poured him a cup of tea and asked, “Did you get soaked this morning?”","“Just my trouser legs.”","“Do you have enough clothes to change into?” Cheng Zhao asked again.","“Enough.”","“The trunk I packed for you wasn’t wet. The clothes in it are all dry,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen hummed in acknowledgment.","He reached out.","Cheng Zhao paused briefly, then placed her hand in his palm.","His palm had thin calluses, hard knuckles, and he clasped her hand with slight force. “Go out riding.”","Cheng Zhao looked at the sky outside, surprised. “Now?”","It was already dark.","“It’s cleared up, and the starlight outside is bright enough. The next few days, I’m afraid I won’t be able to accompany you,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: “We’re both escorting the imperial party—this is official duty. I don’t need you to accompany me, since I still have to attend to the Empress.”","They weren’t out for pleasure.","Simply put, the emperor and empress were the ones enjoying themselves; everyone else’s task was to make the imperial couple happy.","“Then steal a moment of leisure,” Zhou Yuanshen said, giving her hand a gentle tug.","Cheng Zhao rose to her feet, following his lead. “I’ll change into riding clothes.”","“Good.”","Her various garments had already been sorted by Suyue, close at hand—available at a moment’s notice. Even away from home, Cheng Zhao was never thrown into disarray.","Qiubai helped her change.","Cheng Zhao informed her mother-in-law.","Second Madam smiled. “Go along. With Yuanshen leading you, everything is safe.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged.","They hadn’t had dinner yet, and she didn’t feel like eating.","Ningzhou Prefecture was dry. There had been a sudden downpour during the day, but after it cleared in the afternoon, by evening the moisture had mostly evaporated, and the ground wasn’t too muddy.","Zhou Yuanshen picked two horses. Behind them followed his two deputy commanders; Cheng Zhao brought Qiubai along, and they headed for the hunting grounds.","The sky over Ningzhou Prefecture was high, and the night vault was especially clear; the brilliance of the stars rivaled moonlight. Once the eyes adjusted, it didn’t feel dim, and riding came easily.","Because the grassland was less than three li from the traveling palace, they could ride at a slow, easy pace.","There were no mosquitoes.","The night breeze was exceptionally fresh—a night after a rainstorm, with cold that carried the chill of mid-autumn.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen rode side by side.","She thought of asking what the true purpose of this hunt was, what the emperor intended to do. But then again, even if there were no ears behind the walls, knowing too much was bad for her.","What if she let a little slip later and brought trouble upon Zhou Yuanshen?","Husband and wife ought to respect each other’s boundaries.","Zhou Yuanshen had already gone beyond protocol to warn her; Cheng Zhao now wisely kept her mouth shut, not hindering him.","“...Back with the Empress, there was a Miss Liu—the daughter of the Grand Court officer—who said this hunt would release a white wolf,” Cheng Zhao said, finding a topic.","“A male wolf,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao caught the implication and smiled. “What, there’s a female one too?”","“Half a year ago, hunters reported to the authorities that the pack had two white wolves, one male and one female. They led the pack raiding villages, killing at least three hundred people. The male was picky—it wouldn’t eat cattle or sheep; it singled out young children to eat,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao’s smile faded.","She shuddered hard. “Damned beast.”","“Wolves are beasts by nature, regardless of color. Eating meat is their instinct. The local hunters lost over a dozen men before they laid traps and captured the male. The female escaped, and for most of the year, there was no sign of her. That kind of creature has a certain cunning—she’s likely still a menace,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao’s expression grew grim. “Then why put the male wolf out as prey? What if it escapes, and all that effort is wasted? Then it will keep terrorizing the villages.”","“Many people lack your foresight and your caution, Cheng Zhao,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao understood.","The emperor wanted to show off.","Even though capturing the beast had cost so much sacrifice, the emperor hadn’t killed it—he was saving it for the hunt to make a grand display, using it as a centerpiece prize.","The villagers who died in the wolf’s claws, the children swallowed into its belly—meaningless in the emperor’s eyes.","Cheng Zhao’s face turned unpleasant.","Her mood instantly grew heavy.","She remembered her father-in-law once saying over dinner that he had never witnessed the spectacle of a wise sovereign and worthy ministers, even having lived through two reigns.","As a woman, Cheng Zhao did not participate in state affairs, but she could faintly grasp the helplessness behind her father-in-law’s words.","If governance were clear and proper, that white wolf would already be nothing but a pelt—a very small matter, yet one that revealed a few grains of sand from a vast desert of troubles.","\"We've arrived,\" Zhou Yuanshen said."]},{"number":170,"title":"Chapter 170: Cheng Zhao, You Smell Nice","paragraphs":["They had already reached the meadow beside the hunting grounds.","The entire hunting ground was made up of woods, low hills, a small river, and grasslands, covering over a thousand mu. And closest to the temporary imperial residence was a large stretch of pasture.","At the edge of the pasture lay the forest.","Zhou Yuanshen let out a whistle.","Cheng Zhao looked at him, puzzled.","Then she saw, among the trees not far off, a glow rising—flickering, drifting up and down, dotting the shadows of the woods.","Cheng Zhao froze for a moment. \"Fireflies?\"","\"Mingyu told me that you wanted to catch fireflies on this trip to the hunting grounds,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"I only mentioned it in passing.\"","\"I sent someone ahead to arrange it. The villagers nearby had caught quite a few, and I bought them,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao watched the fireflies filling the woods, and for a moment, she was transported back to their days in Fengzhou.","Those days had their share of pain, but most of the time, they were happy and joyful.","She and Fourth Brother had been by their parents' side.","Her mother was no longer the mistress of the Cheng household—she was simply a mother, a wife. When night fell, they would dine together, and her mother would speak at length with her father.","They even discussed music manuscripts.","Back at the grand Cheng residence, her mother was always surrounded by stewards and senior maids helping her manage the household; she had little time to converse with her father, and even less to spend quietly with her children.","The matriarch of a clan always had to make sacrifices, unable to enjoy stillness and leisure.","Cheng Zhao's heart was full of confidence and drive, because the memories of Fengzhou had nourished her. She had received so much.","Her parents had built her a high foundation, and so she was willing to climb even greater peaks.","These were pleasant memories.","Cheng Zhao couldn't help but smile. \"Thank you. That was thoughtful of you.\"","\"I'm glad you like it,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao reached out a hand to him.","She said nothing, but Zhou Yuanshen understood. He urged his horse closer to hers.","The two horses drew level.","He leaned over slightly, one arm wrapping around her waist. Cheng Zhao used his strength, looped her arms around his neck, and swung onto his saddle.","Zhou Yuanshen gathered her into his arms.","Cheng Zhao turned her head to look at him. \"The fireflies are beautiful. Thank you, Lord Duke.\"","Zhou Yuanshen tightened his arms, pulling her closer to his chest. \"I'm not good at pleasing women. As long as you like it, it wasn't for nothing.\"","Cheng Zhao said she liked it very much.","Zhou Yuanshen held the reins with one hand and cupped her face with the other, kissing her.","The kiss grew longer, more entangled, and his breathing turned heavy.","The pasture was vast. His deputy general and the others, along with Qiubai, had stopped by the main road and hadn't followed them. Zhou Yuanshen tapped his horse's flank, and the animal trotted along the edge of the woods.","\"Cheng Zhao... sit in my lap,\" he said in a low voice.","His breath was too tight, his scorching heat enough to melt Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao's body went soft.","She was about to say that it really wasn't convenient, when his hands gripped her waist and helped her turn around.","The horse moved at a slow pace.","The night wind blew gently past her ears, stirring her clothes.","Zhou Yuanshen loosened her hairpin.","Her hair tumbled down like a waterfall, then was tossed by the wind, tangling around him.","Beneath the pale starlight, his sleeves had ridden up slightly, revealing veins like wandering dragons winding between his sleeves and the backs of his hands.","Cheng Zhao was startled—she hadn't known he was so strong.","A pair of hands, bearing nearly all of his strength, and yet so fierce.","As she lay against his shoulder, breathing in short, soft gasps, the horse had already circled around to the stream, with nowhere further to go.","He gently circled her waist with one arm and stroked her hair with the other. \"Cheng Zhao, you smell wonderful.\"","The night breeze carried the scent of grass and flowers, but neither could compare to the perfume she gave off—a fragrance warmed by her body, intoxicating.","Cheng Zhao was blank for a long moment.","She leaned into him, soft and spent.","When he kissed her, she kissed him back, letting him take whatever he wished.","Zhou Yuanshen helped her dismount, then straightened her clothes for her.","Cheng Zhao belatedly realized what they had done—something quite scandalous—right there on horseback.","And she, having savored the taste, had not resisted in the slightest.","Zhou Yuanshen looked better than he had in recent days, with a hint of a smile on his face. \"Tired? Let's sit and rest a while.\"","\"It rained not long ago; the grass is probably damp,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"This spot should be fine.\"","He took off his outer robe and laid it on the ground. Cheng Zhao reached out and touched it—it wasn't too damp, so she sat down beside him.","He had her rest her head on his lap and lie back slightly.","\"Zhou Yuanshen, I think I just saw a pair of yellow-green eyes,\" Cheng Zhao suddenly said. \"Is it a ghost?\"","\"Are you afraid of ghosts?\" Zhou Yuanshen asked.","Cheng Zhao: \"I am. Being haunted is rather unpleasant, and besides, the Ghost Festival is coming up soon.\"","\"Probably just fireflies,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","As he finished speaking, he whipped his head around.","There, among the trees, a pair of yellow-green, glowing eyes was staring straight at him, unblinking.","He stared back.","The eyes vanished in an instant.","That was a wild beast—if not a wolf, then a boar, or a bear.","Either way, a fierce predator.","The hunt hadn't begun yet; the stock for hunting hadn't been released into the woods, and the woods were maintained daily. There shouldn't be any large beasts.","Zhou Yuanshen looked again.","The eyes were gone.","He gently stroked Cheng Zhao's cheek. \"Let's go back? It is a bit damp after all.\"","Cheng Zhao said yes.","She said, \"My hair has come loose.\"","Zhou Yuanshen helped her mount the horse first. Once she was in the saddle, he handed her the reins, then used both hands to coil up her hair and pin it back with her golden hairpin.","The horse suddenly quickened its pace, breaking into a run. Zhou Yuanshen, caught off guard, nearly lost his seat, and Cheng Zhao tumbled back against him.","\"Did you kick the horse's flank?\" she asked.","Zhou Yuanshen knew the horse had sensed a threat and bolted on its own.","He didn't turn back, but he had a guess in his mind. Not wanting to alarm Cheng Zhao, he nodded. \"Time to head back.\"","By the time they returned to the imperial residence, it was well past midnight.","Second Madam had already gone to sleep.","Cheng Zhao asked Suyue, \"Is there still hot water?\"","\"Yes, there is.\"","\"I want to bathe and wash my hair,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She had worked up a sweat on horseback.","Suyue: \"So late?\"","\"Tomorrow we're not going hunting. After seeing off Your Majesty, we can come back and catch up on sleep. A little later won't matter,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Suyue acknowledged this and hurried off to prepare.","Cheng Zhao's bath and hair washing were both hasty that night, and she finished quickly.","She waited for her hair to dry, chatting with Suyue and Qiubai.","She mentioned the fireflies.","Qiubai suddenly said, \"Young Madam, the miniature crossbow that Lord Duke gave you—I brought it along.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"Why did you bring that?\"","\"It's compact and handy. You can use it to defend yourself,\" Qiubai said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","As for Zhou Yuanshen, after seeing Cheng Zhao off, he returned to the meadow once more.","He paced along the edge of the woods, inspecting for half an hour.","There was nothing unusual.","But he knew something was off—there was a stray beast in these woods, a cunning one, hiding where no one could find it.","If it hurt someone tomorrow, the consequences would be severe.","After returning, Zhou Yuanshen sent guards to search the area, ordering them to carry crossbows and torches and comb the hills through the night.","He went back and managed a quick nap.","By dawn, his deputy general came to report that from midnight to daybreak, sixty guards had been split into twenty teams of three, sweeping every corner from front to back.","No fierce beasts had been found.","\"Still, we must be cautious everywhere during today's hunt,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","That morning, the Emperor changed into his riding gear, performed a simple ceremony of worship to heaven and earth, and led the party out to hunt.","It was a grand and imposing spectacle.","Some of the ladies also went along.","The Empress did not go, and Cheng Zhao and Second Madam stayed by her side.","“Brownnoser,” Miss Liu whispered to the person next to her.","She did not name anyone.","But those around them glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Except for a few titled wives of advanced age, everyone else had gone hunting, yet Cheng Zhao remained beside the Empress—a display of sheer sycophancy."]},{"number":171,"title":"Chapter 171: Crossing a Threshold","paragraphs":["On the first day of the hunt, there was no shortage of amusing incidents.","By the meadow beside the hunting grounds, quite a few tents had been set up. Even those who didn't care to ride or shoot could sit inside and chat at leisure.","Cheng Zhao stayed with her mother-in-law, several titled ladies, and Empress Guo, none of whom went to the hunting grounds themselves.","Whatever topic the ladies present turned to, Cheng Zhao could always pick up the thread. Not only did she follow along, she answered with wit and propriety, bringing a smile to Empress Guo's face again and again.","\"Second Madam Zhou is like me, not one for talking much,\" said one lady. \"But this daughter-in-law of yours is so eloquent—she's truly a lucky star that's entered your home.\"","Second Madam Zhou gave an awkward smile.","She could speak, mind you. She just didn't know how to trade pleasantries and empty formalities.","She'd never once in her life been able to bandy verbal jabs; the hidden meanings beneath the words were lost on her.","With her daughter-in-law around, Second Madam Zhou could simply enjoy the good fortune and sit to the side with a smile.","As it happened, that worked wonderfully well.","Not speaking wasn't so terrible—at worst, people would secretly call her dull. But speaking wrongly, that would be laughed at for ages. Given the current standing of the Duke of Chen's household, everyone was willing to praise Second Madam Zhou for her composure.","Hearing the lady's words, Second Madam Zhou had a rare flash of inspiration and said to the Empress, \"It's all thanks to Your Majesty's keen eye, choosing such a fine girl to grace the ducal mansion.\"","\"Her Majesty's vision isn't the only thing that's keen,\" the lady added. \"Her heart always thinks of us too—truly the most benevolent and kind.\"","Then she went on: \"Your Majesty, my daughter is also of marrying age. Could you perhaps bestow a fine match upon her as well?\"","Second Madam Zhou was taken aback.","She had racked her brains to offer that single compliment to the Empress, and it left her cheeks burning with embarrassment—flattering others had never sat well with her.","But now this lady had, following in the wake of Second Madam Zhou's remark, asked the Empress to arrange a marriage.","Did Empress Guo just marry people off at a whim?","Back then, it had been due to the affairs of the Cheng and Zhou families, the succession to the throne, and perhaps other inner machinations Second Madam Zhou knew nothing about, that the Empress had made the match.","Aside from Cheng Zhao, Empress Guo seemed never to have arranged anyone else's marriage.","Second Madam Zhou immediately looked to Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao smiled and gave her a slight nod, then glanced at the Empress, and back at her.","Though they were speaking in silent signs, the two had already built a rapport.","Second Madam Zhou understood Cheng Zhao's meaning: she hadn't said anything wrong, and no matter how others picked up the thread, it wasn't her fault.","And Cheng Zhao was further hinting that she should say no more—the Empress's expression was still passable, and this was a small matter; best not to stir the pot.","Second Madam Zhou put on her smile again and said nothing more.","Her face ached from smiling.","The Empress was about to say something when an inner attendant came to report: \"Your Majesty, His Majesty has brought down a deer.\"","The Empress didn't go to the hunting grounds herself, but she had sent a eunuch from her side; any amusement was reported back.","This was already the third messenger to return with news.","The Emperor had killed no small amount of game.","And this conveniently changed the subject from talk of betrothals.","\"An auspicious start, Your Majesty,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","\"Yes, today has been quite productive. His Majesty has always excelled at horsemanship and archery,\" said Empress Guo with a smile.","Second Madam Zhou dared not stare at the Empress, but she couldn't help thinking to herself: With the Emperor being what he is, excellent at riding and shooting, is he?","Just as that thought crossed her mind, another attendant hurried in: \"Congratulations, Your Majesty. His Highness the Crown Prince has shot a wild boar.\"","The assembled ladies all rushed to congratulate the Empress, each vying to praise the Crown Prince's valor and skill.","Second Madam Zhou thought again: That pale and haggard Prince, how does he have anything to do with bravery?","But everyone sang his praises, and meant every word.","She recalled how Cheng Zhao had said that words were like painted flowers—everyone traced the same patterns with different colored threads, and there was no need to feel embarrassed.","So everyone did it. Not doing so only made a person look foolish and made it hard to mix in society.","That hurdle that Second Madam Zhou had struggled with all her life—the sense that \"toadying was humiliating\"—suddenly, she found herself over it.","What was the big deal after all?","\"To bring down a wild boar before even turning ten—His Highness the Crown Prince has not only luck, but real skill,\" Second Madam Zhou said. \"It's clear he trains diligently in riding and archery. No wonder His Majesty and Your Majesty dote on him so; no wonder the courtiers trust him.\"","Cheng Zhao glanced at her, a smile on her face.","Second Madam Zhou knew why Cheng Zhao was pleased: she had spoken those words smoothly, without a hitch.","She was surprised at herself.","Once the hurdle in her heart was crossed, the words just came to her lips.","The delight in Empress Guo's eyes didn't fade; she smiled more brightly still. \"At the banquet tonight, His Majesty may well honor him.\"","The ladies chorused that it was only fitting—to have such a Crown Prince was a blessing for courtiers and commoners alike.","Empress Guo was greatly pleased.","As everyone chatted on, the lady who had earlier asked for the Empress to bestow a marriage match rather awkwardly brought the matter up again, hoping Empress Guo might find a good husband for her daughter.","\"Madam Liu, what sort of son-in-law does Madam want?\" the Empress asked, genial enough to play along.","It was only then that Second Madam Zhou learned this lady was the wife of Liu Zheng, the Chief Justice of the Grand Court. When her daughter had left earlier, she had mocked Cheng Zhao as a \"sycophant.\"","With that in mind, Madam Liu's words seemed less simple than they appeared.","Second Madam Zhou looked over at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao didn't meet her eyes, nor did her expression change. She just sat there quietly.","\"One who can match the Duke of Chen's talents—that would be a gift from Your Majesty,\" Madam Liu said with a smile.","\"The young lady of the Wu Commandery Cheng clan was matched with the youngest duke in the land; what sort of man shall we match for Miss Liu?\" Empress Guo asked, smiling.","Someone couldn't help but chuckle.","Madam Liu's face reddened slightly.","Second Madam Zhou took private satisfaction from the Empress's gentle barb, but she didn't let it show.","Just then, the Empress's niece, Guo Hanzhang, returned.","\"Your Majesty, His Highness the Crown Prince has brought down a boar,\" Guo Hanzhang said. \"He also shot a fox, and declared before His Majesty, the Grand Tutor, and the Grand Preceptor that he would make you a pair of fox fur gloves to keep you warm through the winter. His Majesty praised his filial devotion.\"","Empress Guo's features all brimmed with joy.","The realm governed by filial piety, a Crown Prince who was filial possessed virtuous character—that was what a heir apparent ought to have.","Before the assembled ministers, the Emperor was bound to praise him.","The titled ladies, one after another, offered their congratulations once more.","\"Your Majesty, there is one more matter—the attendants wouldn't have dared to report it, for fear of spoiling your good spirits,\" Guo Hanzhang said.","The smile on Empress Guo's face receded by half. \"What happened?\"","\"A spy has been caught,\" Guo Hanzhang said. \"A Northerner, from the Beidi. And word is, he has accomplices.\"","Second Madam Zhou immediately looked at Cheng Zhao.","The expression on her own face was one of surprise and worry; as for Cheng Zhao, she looked, like the other titled ladies, a little confused, a little lost.","Beside her, Madam Liu spoke up: \"Second Madam Zhou, why do you look so startled? Oh—you spent years in the border regions, didn't you? Your natal family is the Grand General's mansion.\"","Cheng Zhao turned to look at Madam Liu.","Second Madam Zhou's heart tightened, but seeing her daughter-in-law sitting there so calmly, she hastily suppressed her anxiety.","\"Every time there's talk of spies, people die,\" Second Madam Zhou said. \"I did indeed live at the borders for years. One lives on edge there. Do forgive me, Your Majesty.\"","Empress Guo waved a hand: \"This is Ningzhou Prefecture—far from the Beidi. Every year, when His Majesty goes on this hunt, there are always some harebrained schemers who come sniffing around. Nothing new under the sun.\" She waved off Guo Hanzhang's topic and added, \"Go on back to the hunting grounds and enjoy yourself. No need to wait on me here.\"","That was partly for pleasure, and partly to keep an eye on the Crown Prince on the Empress's behalf.","Guo Hanzhang assented and left.","Cheng Zhao watched her go, then quickly turned her eyes away.","By noon, the hunters still hadn't returned, but the ladies needed their midday meal.","The ladies of the Duke of Chen's household had a tent to themselves.","Back in their own quarters, with the two maidservants keeping watch at the front and back, Second Madam Zhou finally whispered to Cheng Zhao, \"Zhaozhao, what happened? Why did spies show up during the hunt? And why did Yuanshen tell us not to use our bows and arrows?\""]},{"number":172,"title":"Chapter 172: A Storm Arises","paragraphs":["The word \"spy\" was an old wound in Second Madam's heart.","The moment it was mentioned, she became especially tense. Back in the borderlands, spies were like thick clouds on a sweltering summer day—their appearance meant a violent storm was coming.","People would die.","How many depended on luck and on the commander's ability to respond.","After more than a decade of peaceful years in the capital, Second Madam had not forgotten the bloody scent in those old dreams.","\"Mother, the Lord Duke knows what he's doing, and the two of us are cautious. Even if something does happen, it has nothing to do with us,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam replied, \"...I thought we were just here for leisure.\"","\"These years, the Emperor has been bloodthirsty, and the court situation is complicated. 'The tree desires stillness, but the wind will not cease.' The Emperor may want to simply enjoy himself, but others won't allow it,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam sighed deeply.","Cheng Zhao passed her a bowl and chopsticks: \"Mother, eat something. You need strength to react to whatever comes.\"","Second Madam took them.","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law ate their midday meal in silence, rested briefly, then went to attend the Empress.","By mid-afternoon, many of the young ladies had returned, presumably worn out from play.","They chatted excitedly about the morning's hunt.","When the Empress asked who had performed best, they hemmed and hawed, some said the Emperor, some said the Crown Prince.","Only Miss Liu, the daughter of Liu Zheng, the Chief Justice of the Court of Judicial Review, spoke with innocent boldness: \"Naturally, General Zhou and the Prince of Andong came out on top.\"","Guo Hanzhang asked coldly, \"Who is General Zhou?\"","\"I only know General Zhou—don't you recognize him, Miss Guo?\" Miss Liu asked.","The scene fell silent.","\"Oh, you mean Grand Tutor Zhou,\" the Empress said with a smile.","She looked toward Cheng Zhao. \"Grand Tutor Zhou bagged a great many prey today.\"","\"He accompanied the Emperor and looked after the Crown Prince well. If he bagged so many, I imagine the Crown Prince must be delighted, Your Majesty,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Empress Guo laughed: \"I didn't say he wasn't doing his duty.\"","Then she teased, \"Do you want to call him Grand Tutor Zhou, or General Zhou?\"","\"My husband holds many offices, all by His Majesty's grace. Whatever title he's called with is his glory, and he certainly wouldn't mind,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Everyone laughed.","They all knew the Zhou family wielded immense power, and Cheng Zhao was smooth-tongued and tactful—no one was willing to court that trouble.","Soon evening arrived.","Bonfires were lit on the field, and they sat on the ground in the borderland style, roasting meat around the flames.","But all around stood sword-bearing guards, three rings of defense—not even a fly could get through.","Cheng Zhao remembered Zhou Yuanshen saying that the bonfire was fueled by horse dung.","She tried to push the thought away, but the more she tried, the deeper it sank, until she could almost smell horse manure. The roasted lamb leg smelled delicious, yet she had no appetite at all.","The Emperor was in fine spirits today.","The singing girls kept in the imperial retreat danced and sang around the fire—something unseen in the capital—and the crowd watched with great interest.","The Emperor lavishly praised the Prince of Andong and Zhou Yuanshen, who had taken the most prey and were nearly neck and neck.","The accompanying senior ministers were all veterans, and they deferred to the Emperor's glory, barely taking more than one or two animals each.","Cheng Zhao glanced over.","She met Zhou Yuanshen's eyes.","Zhou Yuanshen showed no particular expression, only gave her a slight nod; as he turned his face, she caught sight of the Prince of Andong, Helian Xuan, as well.","Helian Xuan's gaze rested on her face, his cheeks bearing a smile, but there was no warmth in his eyes.","Cheng Zhao turned her head away.","The night was lively, and everyone was entertained. Cheng Zhao ate none of the roasted meat and drank no wine.","When it ended, it was deep in the night, and each retired to their quarters.","Zhou Yuanshen came again to the courtyard shared by Cheng Zhao and Second Madam.","This time, Second Madam couldn't hold back and asked him, \"Why are there spies?\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"It has nothing to do with us, Mother.\"","\"Ah-Shen, I don't feel at ease about you and your father,\" Second Madam said. \"And your little uncle is here too.\"","\"Father and little uncle are safe—they don't stand in anyone's way,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Madam grew even more anxious: \"Then you stand in many people's way—does that mean you're in danger?\"","\"I won't come to harm,\" he said.","Second Madam's heart raced: \"Ah-Shen...\"","\"Mother, I rarely lose. And even if I do lose, with my current strength, I can afford the loss,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"Mother, I'm no longer a teenager.\"","Second Madam stared at him, her eyes slightly glistening: \"You're a grown man now.\"","\"Mother, rest assured,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Madam nodded firmly: \"I trust you!\"","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing more, but called Cheng Zhao out for a private word between husband and wife.","\"...The Emperor wants me to kill someone for him,\" he told Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao asked, \"Is it Liu Zheng, the Chief Justice of the Court of Judicial Review?\"","Zhou Yuanshen's eyes flickered, seeming a shade brighter than usual: \"How do you know?\"","\"I heard from my grandfather that the Emperor has been displeased with Liu Zheng for a long time, and has attacked him several times. This time, of all the officials and families, the Court of Judicial Review sent the most people—and my little uncle also serves there,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Court of Judicial Review was not a department that normally accompanied the imperial entourage.","Cheng Zhao had deduced how many people each yamen had sent based on the number of wives present.","The Chief Justice, the Deputy Chief Justice, two chief clerks, and three fifth-rank officials were all there; aside from Fan Xiao, all had brought their families.","That far exceeded protocol.","Combined with old grievances, Cheng Zhao judged that at least one of the Chief Justice and the Deputy Chief Justice would be settled here. The others would be promoted on the spot, so that upon returning, no court dispute could arise.","—Cook the rice while it's still raw.","And Liu Zheng's wife and daughter were unusually flamboyant, showing that they thought their family's standing was high enough to suppress the Guo clan.","And suppress imperial power as well.","The Emperor felt wary and hateful, yet had no reasonable way to remove the Chief Justice, so he had to take a different path.","\"It is him,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","\"He's very close to the Prince of Andong. Zhou Yuanshen, word may have leaked about this—those with shrewd eyes can see it coming. Watch out for them playing the mantis behind the cicada,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Emperor wanted to kill Liu Zheng, and Zhou Yuanshen was merely an unfeeling blade.","If Liu Zheng sensed it and turned around, allying with the Prince of Andong to kill Zhou Yuanshen, wouldn't that be even better? Zhou Yuanshen already held too many offices.","Even just the Capital Military Camp alone was enough to intimidate the court.","\"The hunt lasts eight days—it'll likely happen in the latter half. I'll be careful,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","She thought to herself that if she hadn't figured it out, Zhou Yuanshen wouldn't have told her the specific person, the specific matter—just a brief mention to ease her mind.","That night, lying in bed, Cheng Zhao couldn't sleep.","She felt it was unfair for Zhou Yuanshen.","The Emperor only wanted to use him. When a minister became too much of a hindrance and the Emperor proved too incompetent to handle it, his only recourse was murder.","If it succeeded, Zhou Yuanshen got a small reward and bore the ill reputation; if it failed, Zhou Yuanshen would be the one to suffer.","Zhou Yuanshen had to make sacrifices, to take risks, in exchange for the strength to contend with his family's forces, enough to resist the Grand Madam's control over him.","Cheng Zhao couldn't put the feeling into words.","In matters of court and government, she could not help him in the slightest.","She only prayed for his success.","She hoped he would stay focused, not care about anyone else, not be distracted—staying the course on his path, climbing to the summit of power.","Even if, by then, she and he had gone their separate ways, she would still be proud of him.","The hunt reached its fourth day, calm and uneventful. The Emperor was cheerful every day.","And when he was happy, everyone else could breathe a sigh of relief.","However, on the afternoon of the fourth day, Cheng Zhao and Second Madam were sitting idly with the Empress when the Empress’s trusted eunuch stumbled in, sobbing: “Your Grace, the Crown Prince has been carried off by a wolf.”","The Empress seemed not to understand: “Carried off by a wolf?”","“Grand Tutor Zhou has gone after him, and His Majesty has taken men as well.” The eunuch burst into tears.","The Empress’s face went deathly pale, yet she still found it hard to believe; she could not quite collect herself.","Second Madam strode swiftly outside.","Cheng Zhao followed her.","“Qiubai, fetch my crossbow,” Cheng Zhao ordered.","Qiubai acknowledged the command.","The maidservant beside Second Madam went to bring two horses, a bow, and twenty arrows. Mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, each with a maidservant in tow, rode off into the mountain forest under the direction of the guards.","The two women exchanged not a single word with each other, yet their coordination was flawless."]},{"number":173,"title":"Chapter 173: Killing Two White Wolves in a Row","paragraphs":["The mountain forest was in chaos, swarming with people who darted about like headless flies.","The guards only held the perimeter or stayed close to their respective masters; they did not patrol the woods during the hunt.","“Zhaozhao, the guards said the white wolf carried the Crown Prince off toward the northwest. There are horse droppings and hoof prints over here. This way.” Second Madam had a strong sense of direction.","Cheng Zhao was poor at horseback archery. The moment she entered the forest, she became dizzy and lost all sense of direction, so she had no choice but to stay close to her mother-in-law.","They rode on for a little while longer until they heard voices and the sound of hooves.","A flash of white was heading straight toward them from the distance.","Behind it, someone shouted in pursuit.","Second Madam had sharp eyes. She could already make out the white wolf—and the Crown Prince dangling from its jaws.","The Crown Prince was thin and frail, like a little kitten; while the white wolf was far bigger than Second Madam had imagined—not wolf-sized, but bear-sized.","“Zhaozhao, retreat now!” Second Madam barked.","Cheng Zhao saw it too. The white wolf, chased by the guards, was charging straight for the two of them.","Her mount went mad with fright, backing up and turning around frantically.","Cheng Zhao’s horse-riding skills weren’t up to much anyway. She yanked the reins and kicked the horse’s flanks, but still couldn’t stop it.","“Mother, Mother, run! My horse is about to bolt!” Cheng Zhao shouted over her shoulder.","She couldn’t control her horse—she not only failed to rein it in, but nearly got thrown off.","“Zhaozhao, sit steady, don’t pull on it.” said Second Madam.","Second Madam’s horse was frightened too, but she knew how to handle it, and it stood its ground steadily.","Cheng Zhao’s maid had gone chasing after her; Second Madam’s own maid stayed behind her.","Second Madam nocked an arrow. When the distance closed, she loosed one.","It struck the white wolf straight in its left eye.","The wolf howled in pain and rolled on the ground, but still didn’t let go of the Crown Prince.","It had him by the scruff of the neck.","The Crown Prince might have been knocked unconscious earlier, but now he was awake—just weak and helpless, letting the wolf drag him about like a torn sack.","Blood was seeping from his neck where the wolf’s fangs had pierced his skin. He tilted his head to look at Second Madam, his eyes full of pleading.","Second Madam couldn’t help but think of Zhou Yuanqi.","The white wolf swiped its paw and knocked the arrow out of its left eye. Second Madam was stunned.","She hadn’t expected the beast to be so intelligent—and so ferocious.","Her horse shuddered and tried to flee.","Second Madam knew the next shot wouldn’t land as cleanly. Her gaze darted around and spotted a branch.","The white wolf dropped the Crown Prince and charged straight at her horse. On the ground, the Crown Prince tried to scramble to his feet, but his whole body was limp. He squirmed in place twice but couldn’t even manage to sit up.","Second Madam nocked another arrow.","She fired again, and again it hit the wolf’s left eye.","The wound went three times deeper than before.","The wolf howled even more fiercely, rolling on the ground. It tried to claw the arrow out again, but now it couldn’t—the arrow was buried deep in half its skull.","It went mad and lunged at Second Madam. She grabbed the branch beside her as her horse spun to run.","Seizing the moment of the leap, Second Madam snatched the branch, snapped it off, and landed firmly on the ground. The wolf pounced at her.","A hand that trained with a spear year-round was not only steady and precise, but immensely strong.","As the wolf came at her, Second Madam thrust the branch into it.","The freshly broken end was sharp, and Second Madam drove it deep into the wolf’s lower throat.","The branch pierced clean through its neck.","It tumbled to the side in agony, thrashing wildly, trying to shake the branch loose. Blood choked its throat, and it could barely breathe.","Second Madam hurried over to lift the Crown Prince from the ground.","By the time the Emperor and the Prince of Andong arrived, they saw the white wolf writhing on the ground in its death throes, and the Crown Prince, barely breathing, lying in Second Madam's arms.","The Crown Prince clung tightly to her with both hands: \"Mother...\"","Second Madam momentarily thought it was Zhou Yuanqi. She gently stroked his head, then pressed her hand against the bite mark on his neck, where blood was gushing out.","\"Don't be afraid,\" Second Madam said.","The Prince of Andong's expression shifted slightly.","Cheng Zhao had no idea what was happening with her mother-in-law. Her mount carried her forward at a gallop, and soon she lost her bearings.","Qiubai chased after her from behind, shouting from a distance: \"Young Mistress, Young Mistress! Fifth Miss!\"","Cheng Zhao called back to her: \"Qiubai, I'm over here.\"","They could hear each other's voices, but the mountain forest was too vast. The trees stood in tangled clusters, and no matter what, Cheng Zhao and Qiubai simply could not see each other.","\"Fifth Miss, don't move from where you are!\" Qiubai yelled.","Cheng Zhao understood, but her horse understood nothing and kept running wildly ahead.","What a cowardly horse.","When Cheng Zhao encountered the second white wolf, her horse went mad—she felt for the first time what it was like for a horse to lose its mind.","It was scared out of its wits.","This time, Cheng Zhao didn't care about risking a fall. She abandoned the horse, leaping straight off its back.","Fortunately, the ground beneath her was soft dead grass. Something scratched her, and the skin on the back of her hand burned with pain, but she herself was unharmed.","Her horse scrambled to its feet and fled.","Cheng Zhao didn't run, because she spotted Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen's short dagger had already plunged into the white wolf's belly.","Blood poured from the wolf's underside, soaking its fur through, yet it showed no fear of death and kept attacking Zhou Yuanshen.","Zhou Yuanshen was covered in wounds, and so was the wolf.","Ignoring her own pain, Cheng Zhao shot an arrow at the wolf.","She missed.","Her small crossbow's bolts required extremely precise aim, and Cheng Zhao had never practiced that skill—she lacked her mother-in-law's talent.","She ran forward, and Zhou Yuanshen saw her: \"Fall back!\"","She reached his side and thrust the crossbow into his hands.","Zhou Yuanshen took it.","This kind of short crossbow was his specialty.","He told Cheng Zhao the mechanism could fire in rapid succession, but she wasn't skilled at it.","With a mere raise of his hand, the crossbow was strapped to his wrist, and the remaining four bolts fired in quick sequence.","His aim was deadly accurate—he could hit the target in pitch-pot toss every single time, let alone with his favored crossbow.","The white wolf finally collapsed.","Of the four bolts, three went through its eyes and into its brain.","The last one pierced through its throat as it howled.","The wolf stopped moving, and Zhou Yuanshen slumped to the ground, spent.","Cheng Zhao grabbed his arm to steady him.","He wore black soft armor, but the left shoulder of the armor had been torn open by wolf fangs, blood streaming from the wound.","Cheng Zhao pressed her hand against his shoulder: \"Zhou Yuanshen, how are you? Are you hurt elsewhere?\"","\"Just minor wounds.\"","\"We just saw the white wolf that had the Crown Prince in its jaws. There's another one here,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen pointed: \"This is the female wolf.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"It was lying in wait here all along?\"","She shuddered, realizing for the first time that all creatures possess a kind of intelligence—some animals were frighteningly clever.","\"Fifth Miss!\" Cheng Zhao heard Qiubai's voice again.","\"I'm here!\"","Qiubai found her.","Then several guards and Zhou Yuanshen's second-in-command arrived.","Zhou Yuanshen could still manage to ride, barely. He ordered the guards to lay the white wolf's carcass across his horse's back to carry it back.","The second-in-command told him: \"Liu Zheng is dead.\"","\"How did he die?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","\"An arrow through the chest, Young Mistress,\" the second-in-command said.","Cheng Zhao's eyelids twitched.","\"Whose arrow was it?\" she asked.","Zhou Yuanshen's voice was very soft: \"Don't ask, Cheng Zhao. Everything is under control, there's no need to worry.\"","Cheng Zhao said no more.","Zhou Yuanshen returned to the courtyard of the temporary palace ahead of the others, ordering the military physician to tend to his wound; he also told Cheng Zhao to apply some medicinal powder to the scratch on the back of her hand.","By evening, the group returned one by one.","The Emperor summoned everyone to the front hall of the temporary palace.","Several major events had occurred that day."]},{"number":174,"title":"Chapter 174: Who Is the Mantis Behind the Cicada","paragraphs":["The Emperor sat in the seat of honor, with the Empress seated beside him.","The Crown Prince sat in the position directly below the Emperor, his neck wrapped in thick white gauze that no longer showed any trace of blood, though his face was deathly pale.","He was already frail, and now his lips and eyes seemed drained of color, his whole form like a figure sculpted from clay.","And he was young at that.","In short, he looked ghastly, more unsettling than even the Emperor himself. Those present averted their eyes, not daring to look at him for long.","\"Lucky for him, he was born from the Empress's womb and became the legitimate heir. Otherwise, the position of heir apparent would never have fallen to him,\" someone muttered inwardly.","No wonder the Emperor had hesitated for so many years, refusing to name him.","Grand Preceptor Guo's power behind him was too formidable; the Emperor had to compromise, yet he also promoted men like Zhou Yuanshen to chip away at Grand Preceptor Guo's authority.","The court was like a small boat adrift in a storm, and those within it felt the sway; even onlookers could sense the turbulence.","Everyone feared for their own safety.","Men and women stood apart, on the left and right respectively. This was not a formal court session, yet the solemn atmosphere was no less stifling; few dared to breathe too loudly.","\"Liu Zheng is dead,\" the Emperor spoke first.","Liu Zheng, the Chief Justice of the Court of Judicial Review, was dead.","When someone dies, it should normally fall to the Chief Justice to investigate the case.","Now it was the Junior Chief Justice, Pei Yanhe, who stepped forward.","Liu Zheng was a protégé of the Dou clan of Piguo Duke's manor; his loyalty lay not with the Emperor, but with the Dou family. And Pei Yanhe also had the backing of the Pei clan, which was no small force.","\"Your Majesty, the arrow that struck Lord Liu was a Geng-metal arrow,\" said the Junior Chief Justice.","At this hunt, every arrow bore a mark, indicated by the heavenly stems and earthly branches; whoever drew arrows had them recorded under their own name, to determine to whom the game belonged.","The arrow markings used a unique pattern reserved for the imperial household, and this time they had been improved so they could not be imitated in a short time.","After the Junior Chief Justice spoke, without waiting for the Emperor to ask, he continued: \"The Geng-metal arrow was drawn by Grand Tutor Zhou Yuanshen.\"","Dead silence fell.","Second Madam raised her head. She looked first at the Emperor, then at her own son, and her expression changed involuntarily.","No wonder Zhou Yuanshen had warned them not to take any bows and arrows.","Whatever scheme this was, the moment you took an arrow, it could be used against you—used to kill.","Better to avoid the trouble entirely; best not to take any at all.","Second Madam had taken arrows later herself, but she had used them all to save the Crown Prince. She had not fallen into the trap—so how had her son walked right into it?","No one spoke.","Liu Zheng's wife and daughter had long since cried themselves senseless; the mother and daughter huddled together like drenched quails, their earlier arrogance and insolence gone, standing at the back.","\"Zhou Yuanshen killed my father,\" Miss Liu said through sobs. \"I beg Your Majesty to see justice done, to avenge my father and make Zhou Yuanshen pay with his blood.\"","Zhou Yuanshen stood silently, his face expressionless.","He had already changed into fresh clothes, his shoulder wound bandaged. He had lost a great deal of blood, and his face was pale too.","That paleness made him seem somewhat guilty.","The court officials seemed to understand. Zhou Yuanshen had no personal grudge against Liu Zheng; he was merely acting as the Emperor's blade.","But to act so openly—he would still need to figure out how to close the matter.","\"When Liu Zheng was struck by the arrow, my horse was also struck by an arrow,\" the Emperor said.","The Junior Chief Justice had clearly already investigated everything thoroughly and reported: \"Yes, Your Majesty. Your horse was also struck in the left leg by a Geng-metal arrow.\"","The Emperor smiled.","The smile was rather hearty.","He said: \"Now that is rather amusing.\"","He glanced at the recording clerk nearby.","Even at a hunt, the Emperor was always accompanied by his officials and a clerk.","The clerk stepped forward and said to the Junior Chief Justice: \"Lord Pei, before entering the hunting grounds today, Your Majesty exchanged arrows with Grand Tutor Zhou.\"","A stir rippled through the crowd.","Whispers broke out all around.","\"I took the Geng-metal arrow. By Junior Justice Pei's reasoning, it was I who shot Liu Zheng, and then wounded my own mount?\" the Emperor asked.","Pei Yanhe had not anticipated this turn. He fell to his knees at once: \"This subject is guilty! I was remiss in my investigation and have besmirched Your Majesty's ears.\"","\"My dear ministers, if I was seated on my horse, how could I have shot Liu Zheng and also struck my own horse?\" the Emperor pressed.","He then addressed another official: \"Lord Hu, you were at my side the entire time, were you not?\"","There were always several veteran ministers around him.","The summoned Lord Hu stepped forward: \"Your Majesty, this minister never left your side even for a step. Your Majesty's horse was wounded by a concealed arrow.\"","\"And Liu Zheng?\"","\"Naturally, Lord Liu was also struck by a concealed arrow. The spy we captured earlier must have accomplices,\" said Lord Hu.","The Emperor gave a cold laugh.","The Junior Chief Justice hastily said: \"Lord Hu speaks with reason. With a single bow and a single quiver of arrows, it appears someone plotted this for a long time—they wanted not only to assassinate Lord Liu but also to attempt regicide, and pin the blame on Grand Tutor Zhou.\"","And so, Zhou Yuanshen's suspicion was cleared.","Cheng Zhao glanced at Zhou Yuanshen.","He remained expressionless.","He had not defended himself; his father and his young maternal uncle also stood silent.","Anyone could see how Liu Zheng had truly died.","\"Your Majesty, the spy has revealed that he does indeed have an accomplice. Furthermore, a wolf's head was tattooed on his left shoulder,\" the Prince of Andong stepped forward and said to the Emperor.","By now the Emperor had a raging headache, and his temper was on the verge of murderous fury.","But he had to endure.","He said: \"So the spy has revealed his accomplice's identity?\"","\"He would only tell Your Majesty, and refused to speak to the guards. Even beaten to death, he would not talk,\" said Helian Xuan.","The Emperor shot a look at Helian Xuan: \"I entrusted this matter to you, and you did not handle it well.\"","Helian Xuan lowered his gaze: \"Your Majesty, forgive this subject.\"","\"Bring the spy up. He is no doubt responsible for shooting Liu Zheng,\" said the Emperor.","The Junior Chief Justice hastily said: \"Your Majesty is most wise.\"","The spy was brought before them.","He did not speak the official language well, and said he barely knew his accomplice—the two had met only a few times.","However, his accomplice also had a wolf's head tattooed on his left shoulder. He might have destroyed it by now, but the mark of such a tattoo is hard to erase.","The Emperor rubbed his brow: \"Search everyone. The women are to go behind the screen and be checked one by one as well.\"","The crowd was stricken with alarm.","But no one dared plead for mercy; they each went to disrobe.","The search went on for the better part of an hour, and came up empty—no one had a tattoo on their shoulder.","\"...It is only Grand Tutor Zhou's shoulder that is wounded, mauled bloody by the white wolf, so no trace can be seen anymore,\" said the Junior Chief Justice who conducted the examination.","\"Then pray to Heaven to turn back time, so you can go back and look at Grand Tutor Zhou's uninjured shoulder instead,\" said the Emperor.","The Junior Chief Justice fell to his knees in haste: \"This subject would never dare!\"","The Emperor looked at this one, then at that one.","He said to Zhou Yuanshen: \"Step forward, Grand Tutor Zhou, let me see your wound.\"","Zhou Yuanshen walked up to the Emperor.","Four guards surrounded them, shielding them from view.","Zhou Yuanshen's bandage was unwrapped and the wound exposed for the Emperor to see.","The Emperor looked for a few moments and said flatly: \"There is no tattoo. But this wound is quite severe.\"","Zhou Yuanshen dressed and stepped back.","He bowed in salute: \"Though this subject is wounded, I congratulate Your Majesty on obtaining two white wolf tails.\"","The Emperor could not help but laugh with pleasure: \"Two white wolf tails—that is certainly cause for celebration.\"","One official seized the moment to say: \"Your Majesty is without equal in a thousand years, and Heaven has seen fit to send a pair of twin white wolf tails.\"","\"The male wolf was killed by Second Madam Zhou, and the female wolf by Grand Tutor Zhou. The two of you, mother and son, were both wounded in the endeavor—a service of no small merit,\" said the Emperor.","The Crown Prince, seated below, rose to his feet, supporting his frail frame, and bowed to the Emperor: \"Father, I beg you to richly reward Second Madam Zhou—she saved your son's life.\"","\"It is a good thing that you know gratitude,\" said the Emperor.","He pondered for a moment. \"I hereby confer upon Second Madam Zhou, née Fan, the title of 'First-Class Guardian Lady of the Realm.' As for Grand Tutor Zhou, I no longer know what more I could possibly bestow upon him.\"","After a pause, he continued, \"And what of that enchantingly beautiful songstress? Bring her forth—I shall grant her to Grand Tutor Zhou as a gift.\""]},{"number":175,"title":"Chapter 175: Disobedience Will Be Punished","paragraphs":["The atmosphere in the great hall was too delicate.","The emperor's reward to Second Madam Zhou was genuine and substantial.","She had already held the title of a third-rank honored lady, and he now directly elevated her to first-rank Lady Guardian of the Realm—a distinction granted to her alone, above her husband and her son.","After announcing the honor, the emperor also declared he would grant her a salary.","Her annual stipend was two thousand dan of grain.","Not only did she receive the title, but also a salary—this reward was heartfelt.","But the reward to Zhou Yuanshen carried a subtle edge.","Gifting singing girls was the sort of thing done in private, over drinks, as a way for a superior to draw a subordinate closer.","It was a gesture that said, \"We're in this together, you're my confidant, and from now on we'll share the spoils,\" bridging the distance between them.","It was never a public, courtly commendation from an emperor to his minister.","That just wasn't proper.","This kind of gift felt more like a slap in the face.","Second Madam Zhou stepped forward and bowed: \"This subject thanks Your Majesty for your boundless grace.\"","Zhou Yuanshen also stepped forward to bow.","Many people glanced furtively at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao's expression remained calm. She neither smiled nor changed her face, appearing oblivious to the probing looks around her.","Soon, eunuchs brought in two singing girls.","Both were beautiful—seductive and alluring.","But the emperor said only one was to be given away.","He looked at the two of them and laughed abruptly: \"Why are there two?\"","\"Your Majesty, they are twin sisters,\" said the eunuch.","\"They don't look much alike,\" said the emperor.","\"Some twins don't look alike, but there is a hint of resemblance,\" the eunuch replied.","\"What are their names?\" the emperor asked.","The two singing girls bowed gracefully, their movements and expressions exquisitely coquettish.","\"This slave is Xian Si.\"","\"This slave is Xian Xiang.\"","The emperor studied them, then said to Zhou Yuanshen: \"I can't give both to you—that would make me seem too indulgent, and I'd fear you'd become spoiled by favor.\"","The words \"spoiled by favor\" struck every heart in the hall like hammer blows.","The emperor then pointed at one girl: \"This one, called Xian Si, I give to you.\"","He pointed at the other: \"Prince of Andong, this one, called Xian Xiang, goes to you.\"","Helian Xuan seemed to pause for a moment, then quickly stepped forward to bow in thanks: \"This subject thanks Your Majesty.\"","\"You are my cousin,\" the emperor said. \"When good things come, you won't be overlooked. No need for you to fight for anything.\"","Everyone tried to parse these words but remained baffled.","Cheng Zhao stood there and understood the whole matter clearly.","Zhou Yuanshen had killed Liu Zheng for the emperor.","But he hadn't told the emperor how he'd done it—and he'd even swapped the arrows.","He wanted the emperor to vouch for him, to pin the blame on the spy, keeping his own hands clean. The emperor had caught on quickly.","To have his own blade turn against him—the emperor was furious.","But Helian Xuan had meddled in the middle.","If Liu Zheng had not died, he and Helian Xuan would have framed Zhou Yuanshen as the spy. With Liu Zheng dead, Helian Xuan was too alone to carry the plan.","He'd tried to force a frame-up.","Whether by luck or deliberate self-harm, Zhou Yuanshen had wounded his own shoulder, clearing himself of suspicion as a spy.","Cheng Zhao still clearly remembered the scar on Zhou Yuanshen's shoulder—it looked like a wolf's head with no eyes. He'd always said he would tell her the story, but something kept interrupting, and he'd never gotten around to it.","Without the tattooed totem, and with even the scar itself hard to verify—since the white wolf's bite had been so severe—Helian Xuan's plan collapsed.","For his own selfish motives, he had nearly derailed the emperor's design, and the emperor was deeply annoyed.","Helian Xuan had been disobedient; Zhou Yuanshen had concealed the truth. Both men, once the emperor's most trusted, were pursuing their own agendas.","The emperor was angry, but not too angry. He gave one singing girl to each as a minor punishment—these two women were likely the emperor's spies as well.","After the emperor finished with the rewards, Zhou Yuanshen and Helian Xuan bowed in thanks and withdrew, each taking his beauty.","The spy was still kneeling.","The emperor picked up his sword, walked over, and struck at the spy's neck.","Blood sprayed everywhere.","The women and civil officials may never have known that blood from the neck could spray so high, so far—a few droplets even landed on the Crown Prince's face.","The Crown Prince wanted to cry but dared not, trembling all over.","Someone let out a startled scream—not just among the women, but among the elder ministers too.","Cheng Zhao closed her eyes.","Her thought was: \"Thank goodness Yuan Qi isn't here.\"","These filthy sights shouldn't be seen by Yuan Qi; he would be frightened.","Last time, at the Zhou family's Chenming Hall, the emperor had killed the same way.","This did not build his authority—it only ruined his reputation.","Cruelty in a monarch was a grave taboo.","\"Drag him out,\" the emperor said.","The spy, head lolling, eyes wide in death, was dragged away by the guards.","Blood trailed all the way to the gates of the hall, and the stench of gore lingered in the air.","The emperor then announced that Vice Minister of the Grand Court Pei Yanhe was promoted to Grand Court Minister, and Fan Xiao was promoted to Vice Minister of the Grand Court.","— As if to compensate Zhou Yuanshen, since Fan Xiao was his maternal uncle.","With that handled, the emperor declared the hunting expedition concluded. Everyone was to return and pack, departing at an auspicious hour tomorrow morning.","Second Master stood behind the other officials the whole time, silent.","Fan Xiao did not look too pleased either.","That night, Zhou Yuanshen did not come to Cheng Zhao's small courtyard.","Second Madam was troubled, trying to comfort her: \"Zhao Zhao, don't worry.\"","\"Mother, Concubine Mu at home isn't dead, just sent to the estate; and there are two beautiful chambermaids too. One more concubine won't make a difference,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: \"...You don't have to put on a brave face with me.\"","\"A matron must first put on a brave face, and slowly, she learns to let things go,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Then she couldn't help but add: \"Congratulations, Mother. First-rank Lady Guardian of the Realm, with a salary to boot. From now on, you can walk among the titled ladies with your head held high.\"","\"I should be happy. But when I think of A-Shen's wound and that singing girl, I can't muster any joy,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao turned to comfort her instead.","Seeing that Cheng Zhao was not downcast—though not exactly cheerful either—Second Madam felt slightly relieved.","The next day, on the return journey, Zhou Yuanshen could not ride a horse due to his injury, so he also traveled by carriage.","Second Madam came over and swapped with Cheng Zhao: \"I'll take care of A-Shen for a stretch. We'll switch again after the midday rest.\"","Cheng Zhao agreed.","Second Madam didn't waste time inspecting her son's wound. She said straightaway: \"That singing girl the emperor gifted you—you cannot touch her.\"","Zhou Yuanshen nodded: \"I understand.\"","\"You know it in your head—good. You can't get involved with a woman like that. It's not just about the emperor; don't hurt Zhao Zhao's heart either,\" said Second Madam.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"Did she say something?\"","\"What could Zhao Zhao say? She's always been virtuous and magnanimous,\" Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanshen paused for a moment, then said: \"She's the Duke's wife.\"","\"That remark sounds sour.\"","\"It's the truth,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Madam: \"I know—you're complaining that Zhao Zhao doesn't keep you in her heart, that she doesn't get jealous. You're still young. Wait another ten years, and you'll know how good a wife like this is.\"","Then she added: \"Zhao Zhao may be a young girl, but she knows how to control her temper. Don't hold grievances against her over this.\"","\"There's none, Mother,\" Zhou Yuanshen said, changing the subject. \"Mother, what happened with that male wolf?\"","Second Madam then recounted the events of the previous day in detail to Zhou Yuanshen.","\"...If the branch hadn't been sharp enough, you and the Crown Prince could both have been in danger,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Madam: \"No battle is ever a sure thing—you win by luck in the moment.\"","\"Mother, you win. None of the titled ladies in the capital are as honored as you. They rely on their husbands and sons, but you rely on yourself,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Second Madam: \"From now on, if anyone bullies Zhao Zhao, I can use my status to pressure them and stand up for her. She truly hasn't had it easy.\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"...\"","His mother, who had suffered years of oppression from her mother-in-law and sisters-in-law, didn't seek revenge after rising above them—she only wanted to shield her daughter-in-law."]},{"number":176,"title":"Chapter 176: Husbands Are More Reliable","paragraphs":["Second Madam finished her conversation with Zhou Yuanshen and yielded her seat to Cheng Zhao.","Thus, on the way back, it was Cheng Zhao who looked after Zhou Yuanshen.","The two of them talked a great deal, yet they never once touched upon the beauties gifted by the emperor.","\"...Helian Xuan has been stirring the pot quite a bit this time,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"He's close with the people at the Court of Judicial Review, which shows he's thick with the Dou family.\"","The Dou family were now sworn enemies of the Zhou family, because Consort Dou's fifth prince had died within a formation on the very grounds where the Zhou family had held their religious rites.","\"The emperor trusts him,\" Zhou Yuanshen said flatly.","\"Is he secretly amassing wealth for the emperor?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanshen looked at her, a glint of interest in his eyes again. \"Did you guess that, or did you hear it from someone?\"","\"His father, Prince An, spent many years in Fengzhou, dealing with pirates, sea raiders, and maritime merchants. Silver flowed through their hands like water. If the emperor hadn't tacitly approved—or if the master behind it all wasn't the emperor himself—how could they have been allowed to make that money?\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen listened quietly, then asked her, \"Is your maternal family also in Fengzhou?\"","\"Yes,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"My grandfather and uncles also deal in maritime goods, so I know this business is extremely lucrative. Without powerful backing, it's money you can earn but not live to spend.\"","\"Indeed, it started with Prince An—father and son have been collecting wealth for the emperor ever since,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","The inflows and outflows of the national treasury were all watched by court officials; but the emperor also had his private desires, and although his personal coffers had their ways of being filled, who would ever complain of having too much money?","Even an emperor.","\"What you did and what he does are different things—why would he target you?\" Cheng Zhao asked. \"Or is it the post at the Capital Guard that unsettles them? Quite a few powerful clans have aligned themselves with Prince Andong.\"","The emperor had put the Capital Guard under Zhou Yuanshen's command, and in a short time Zhou Yuanshen had purged dissenting elements, sweeping the influence of the great clans clean.","From now on, should any emergency arise, the entire capital would be under Zhou Yuanshen's control.","And the Zhou family, with its extremely thin numbers and lack of tangled marriage alliances with the aristocratic clans, had the heads of those clans feeling uneasy.","\"Do the powerful clans actually believe the dynasty is about to change hands?\" Cheng Zhao asked again.","Zhou Yuanshen looked at her calmly. \"What do you think?\"","\"It wouldn't fall to Prince Andong, in any case,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Human hearts are too complicated, and the situation is chaos,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","The couple talked on about many things.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen always spoke in hushed tones, sitting close together, with Zhou Yuanshen's deputy generals riding guard outside the carriage.","As for what had happened at the hunting grounds, Cheng Zhao also took her own guesses and put them to Zhou Yuanshen.","This time, Zhou Yuanshen answered everything without holding back.","\"...Killing someone behind the scenes is fine, but not out in plain sight. When I suggested changing the arrows, the idea was to pin it on the spies,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","It was exactly as Cheng Zhao had guessed.","\"The emperor was quite angry about that. He's grateful you solved his biggest headache, but also annoyed that you didn't follow his orders and used him instead,\" Cheng Zhao said. She pointed at his left shoulder. \"As for this, you were lucky to run into that white wolf—misfortune turned to fortune.\"","Zhou Yuanshen gave a soft hum.","\"Prince Andong knows this secret of yours,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"He shouldn't have known. That means the man has dealings with the Northern Di royal court as well.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"You tried to tell me several times before, but never did. Was it something you couldn't say?\"","\"No,\" he said.","Then he carefully recounted the whole matter to Cheng Zhao.","The story was not complicated.","Years ago, Zhou Yuanshen's maternal grandfather, Old General Fan, wanted to select a group of spies to go to the Northern Di and assassinate their Chanyu. Zhou Yuanshen had been serving in the army under a false name, and no one knew he was the general's grandson.","Because of his striking appearance, he could pass as a wealthy merchant's son, traveling with a caravan into Northern Di territory.","He was kept by the royal court, which both tested him with suspicion and tried to turn him, wanting him to become a Northern Di spy, infiltrating the imperial clan in the capital and passing back intelligence.","Spies had to bear a tattoo, so they carved a wolf's head onto Zhou Yuanshen's shoulder.","\"...There was an accident when they were dotting the eye, and they planned to finish it later. I made my move that very night—I assassinated the Chanyu,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao stared at him in shock.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"When there's no perfect opportunity, strike at the earliest moment. Catching them off guard is what works best.\"","After the Chanyu's death, Old General Fan led the army into Northern Di, and Zhou Yuanshen worked from within.","They won a complete victory, shattering the Northern Di so thoroughly that only two tribes managed to flee.","\"When the war was over and I remembered to deal with the wolf's head, the skin on my shoulder had already healed over. I took a rough blade to it once, cut it to shreds. But a faint outline still remained,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","There was a scar on his shoulder with a vague shape, and he couldn't explain it away.","\"So the white wolf practically saved you, sparing you from being caught in a scheme,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Fortune and misfortune lean on each other,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao thought to herself, this wasn't fortune and misfortune leaning on each other—it was that he had no fear of death and gambled with his life to win a sliver of a chance.","Whether as a spy or fighting the white wolf barehanded, he had not shown a trace of cowardice.","When you cast life and death aside, you find new life in return.","By the evening of the second day, the group returned to the village estate where they had first stayed on the outward journey.","They would reach the capital by tomorrow evening, and everyone wore a look of relief.","Back here, the heat was noticeably more intense.","At the hunting grounds, mornings and evenings were cool, and although it was hot under the noon sun, the tents carried a coolness that seeped up from the earth, making one vaguely feel it was early autumn.","The capital, meanwhile, had just passed the Ghost Festival, and the summer heat had yet to dissipate.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam stayed in the same small courtyard as before, sharing it with several other titled ladies and young misses.","Their attitude toward Second Madam had become noticeably more respectful.","Second Madam found it a bit hard to get used to.","Cheng Zhao said to her quietly, \"Mother, you need to put on the proper airs. Once we're back at the Ducal Mansion, you must stand tall as well.\"","Second Madam: \"That's easier said than done. I'll need time to ease into it.\"","Cheng Zhao smiled.","As the two women were talking, the maid Suyue announced from the doorway: \"Young Mistress, Miss Guo is here.\"","Cheng Zhao lifted the door curtain herself and invited Guo Hanzhang inside to talk.","But Guo Hanzhang shook her head. \"Duchess of Chen, why don't we take a stroll?\"","Cheng Zhao agreed.","The two of them walked slowly along the field ridges, with maids and guards following behind.","\"I do admire you quite a bit,\" Guo Hanzhang said. \"You're very well suited to being the mistress of a household.\"","\"Because of the sing-song girl matter? I didn't lose my composure or give people a show?\" Cheng Zhao asked with a smile.","Guo Hanzhang: \"That's not all. Your mother-in-law received her title, your husband turned defeat into victory, and your relatives got promotions. You showed no visible hand in any of it. With your abilities, you could have—so clearly, you chose to let others take the credit, not eager to show off yourself. A good mistress of a house puts the family's power above personal gain, focuses on the big picture, and lets small matters go—that's how you keep wealth and status for generations.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"Miss Guo, you flatter me. They earned what they got; I didn't do much at all.\"","Guo Hanzhang saw that Cheng Zhao wouldn't reveal anything more and didn't press further.","\"Miss Guo, if you don't mind my asking—you seem extraordinarily interested in my household. Are you taken with someone in it?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","The implication was: are you also sweet on my husband?","Because that would explain why she paid so much attention to Cheng Zhao, even studied her.","\"How would you feel about me becoming your fourth sister-in-law?\" Guo Hanzhang asked.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\""]},{"number":177,"title":"Chapter 177: Take a Look at Your Husband","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao was somewhat surprised.","She didn't know Guo Hanzhang well. She had heard that the girl was hot-tempered and prone to outbursts, and many young ladies of noble birth had suffered at her hands.","Still, there was no conflict between her and Cheng Zhao.","But her grandfather excelled at political balancing. The Cheng family's marital alliances were mostly with powerful, influential houses. To form another connection with the Guo clan at this point would be like pouring oil on fire — hardly wise.","\"How does Miss Guo come to know Cheng Chao?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","She wasn't in a hurry to take a stance; instead, she probed with an indirect question.","\"It's been more than a year or two,\" Guo Hanzhang said. \"If he dares to say he doesn't know me, I'll break his legs.\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"I'm close in age to my fourth brother, and we've bickered since childhood, but he doesn't share private matters with me. I hadn't heard of this before.\"","Guo Hanzhang said, \"Hearing it now is just as good.\"","\"Miss Guo should let the elders on both sides decide,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"It's precisely because that won't work that I'm asking you. You're deeply favored by your aunt, and you know her better than I do. You're also the Duchess of Chen, so you carry weight with your family's elders. Help me come up with a plan — how can I get this settled?\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"Miss Guo, since you know my temperament, you should also know I won't agree to such a request.\"","Guo Hanzhang: \"...\"","After a moment of silence, they turned back. Guo Hanzhang still urged her, \"Keep it in mind.\"","Cheng Zhao made no reply.","Back at the small courtyard, Second Madam called Cheng Zhao over to ask, \"That Miss Guo — did she give you any trouble?\"","\"No, she just said a few words to me. Private words between women,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"She has a bad temper, and she has heavy backing. Be careful you don't suffer a loss,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao smiled. \"Mother, rest assured.\"","The next day, everyone rose in the middle of the night to make ready and set out on the road.","Because they departed two hours earlier, they returned to the capital by mid-afternoon.","The carriage procession dispersed at the city gate.","The imperial carriage of the Emperor and Empress, with an honor guard clearing the streets and heavy security, advanced slowly toward the imperial city; everyone else returned to their own residences.","The road back to the Duke of Chen's mansion almost overlapped with the imperial route, so Cheng Zhao and the others found a restaurant by the city gate to have a meal and wait.","The deputy general went ahead to report their return.","The Zhou family sent a head steward; and from the Grand General's mansion came the Old Madam, accompanied by Zhou Yuanqi at her side.","The Old Madam first went to see Zhou Yuanshen: \"I heard the wound is severe?\"","\"Just a flesh wound,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","The Old Madam said, \"Good. A man who can fight a white wolf single-handedly is truly brave.\"","\"I nearly ended up in the wolf's belly. It was Cheng Zhao who sent me her crossbow, which allowed me to turn the tide and win,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao quickly said, \"It was no trouble at all. It was your archery that did the work.\"","The Old Madam looked at one, then the other, smiling, and said to Cheng Zhao, \"Zhaozhao saved his life. How many people, at a critical moment, only cause more harm? Zhaozhao saw the white wolf and still didn't clutch the crossbow to her chest — that too is courage. When faced with danger, most people dare not hand their protective weapon to another.\"","Cheng Zhao felt a bit embarrassed at the praise.","She truly hadn't thought that deeply at the time.","She trusted Zhou Yuanshen's archery and knew the crossbow would be more formidable in his hands.","At a moment of life and death, she certainly wanted to save her own skin as well — but Zhou Yuanshen was more reliable than the weapon, so she chose the better option.","Now that her grandmother was praising her so highly, she could hardly bear it.","\"Zhaozhao is a lucky star,\" Second Madam said.","Zhou Yuanqi interrupted, asking what exactly had happened.","Cheng Zhao was skilled at telling a story and explained the matter in a few brief sentences.","Zhou Yuanqi curled his lip. \"Good thing I didn't go. You all went to survive a tribulation.\"","\"Yes, it was terrifying,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi heard that his mother was now a First-Rank Lady of the State with a stipend, so he bowed respectfully to her. \"Congratulations, Mother.\"","He added, \"Mother, if you were a man, you'd already be a Grand General.\"","He added further, \"Father is only a general, Third Brother is only a general, Fourth Brother is only a junior officer — all the men in the family combined don't match you.\"","Second Madam: \"...You don't have to make so many enemies for me.\"","Everyone burst into laughter.","The Old Madam patted Zhou Yuanqi's head, praising him for his wit, saying children spoke without forethought, and told them not to take a boy's words to heart.","Only Second Master affirmed Zhou Yuanqi's statement: \"Yuanqi has spoken the truth at the core of the matter. Madam has indeed been held back.\"","Second Madam felt a little pleased, and also a little shy.","She had been raised under a very strict education by her mother, and after marriage, she had been suppressed by her mother-in-law and sisters-in-law. She was not used to being the center of such praise.","Cheng Zhao had been constantly encouraging her, building up her confidence.","\"Mother deserves this. One day, Mother will be an honored Old Madam like Grandmother,\" Cheng Zhao said.","And so Second Madam thought to herself: She must stand firm, gain prestige, so her children would have more confidence and support.","True to that resolve, she straightened her back.","After they had eaten their fill, the imperial carriage should have entered the royal city. It was getting late, and the Zhou family's carriage returned to the mansion.","By the time they reached the Duke of Chen's residence, it was dusk.","After the Ghost Festival, the days grew shorter, and night fell earlier than usual. Lanterns were already lit at the entrance of the Duke of Chen's mansion.","No one was waiting at the gate.","Zhou Yuanshen ordered the servant to remove the threshold plank and let the carriage go straight in.","Cheng Zhao returned to Nonghua Courtyard, feeling as though a yoke had been lifted from her, her mood greatly brightened.","The courtyard was in perfect order. Mother Li told the young couple to rest first, while she and the maids would handle the unpacking.","The baggage was brought in first; they would organize it in the morning, so as not to make noise and disturb them.","Cheng Zhao asked Zhou Yuanshen, \"My Lord Duke, how will you bathe?\"","\"As long as the wound isn't touched, it's fine,\" he said.","\"Do you need me to change the dressing?\" Cheng Zhao asked again.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"No need. I'll go to the Imperial Medical Bureau tomorrow to have it changed.\"","Cheng Zhao nodded.","The bath barrel was only half filled, the water reaching his waist, to prevent any splashes from dampening the gauze on his shoulder.","Cheng Zhao wrung out a cloth and carefully wiped his back and neck.","\"...Tell me if it hurts,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"No need to worry. My skin is tough.\"","Cheng Zhao smiled.","When she moved to the front to wipe his chest, her eyes glanced downward — then quickly looked away.","She was a little embarrassed.","Zhou Yuanshen caught her hand. \"What, is your husband not worth looking at?\"","\"Don't be silly,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Hurry up and finish bathing so we can sleep. There's a mountain of things to do tomorrow.\"","Zhou Yuanshen leaned in and kissed her lips.","Cheng Zhao, afraid that any sudden movement would pull at his wound, didn't dare move.","So he guided her hand, stroking his skin with a gentle but firm pressure.","His chest was solid, with no softness at all; his flesh was hot in the warm water as she trailed her hand downward.","Cheng Zhao ended up half-crouching, half-kneeling beside the bath by the end, because she could no longer stand steady on her feet.","When he was satisfied, he finally released her.","Cheng Zhao quickly checked his wound.","It seemed that he had been a bit too vigorous, and a bead of blood had seeped out.","\"...Does it hurt?\" she asked with worry.","Zhou Yuanshen said, \"It doesn't hurt.\"","Cheng Zhao, seeing this, felt a flash of irritation. Men, for the sake of a taste of pleasure, could bear pain and pretend all was well.","After drying him off, it was nearly the middle of the night.","Cheng Zhao hadn't had time to wash her hair, so she took a quick bath and returned to bed.","Zhou Yuanshen wasn't asleep yet; he was sitting up in bed, reading by the lamp, waiting for her.","When she got into bed, he covered the lamp, and the couple settled down for the night.","\"Cheng Zhao...\"","\"Hm?\"","\"Never mind,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao groggily opened her eyes and looked at him. Seeing that he clearly didn't want to say more, she closed her eyes and went back to sleep.","The next morning, when she woke early, she heard him say, \"Settle her in the Jade Brocade Court.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"What?\"","\"You don't need to worry about it. Have Grandmother's people go take care of her,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\""]},{"number":178,"title":"Chapter 178: Cheng Zhao Seizes the Opportunity to Amass Wealth","paragraphs":["On the second day after returning to the capital, the Chen Duke’s household was in an uproar.","The emperor’s decree naming Second Madam as “First-Class Guardian Lady of the State” had been delivered to the Zhou family; the Ministry of Rites had also prepared a treasured seal, for Second Madam was to receive a salary as well.","The entire household was stunned.","From the Dowager Madame at the top down to the gate keeper’s boy, all wore expressions of astonishment.","The Dowager Madame led the way, and the Chen Duke household set up an incense altar. Second Madam received the imperial decree.","Cheng Zhao had long since ordered red envelopes prepared, and every person present received a reward.","The Second Branch’s mother-in-law and daughter-in-law were beaming with pride.","When it was done, the Dowager Madame summoned the women of the household to Shou’an Court.","She asked what had happened.","Cheng Zhao was eloquent; she spoke on behalf of her mother-in-law, recounting the events at the hunting grounds to the Dowager Madame.","Then she said, “Grandmother, His Majesty received a pair of white wolf tails. It is said that white wolves are the mounts of heavenly deities, and that they descended to offer these tails in congratulation of His Majesty’s eternal reign.”","She added, “Mother and the Lord Duke each brought down one white wolf. That is why His Majesty bestowed such great favor upon the Zhou family. Of course, it was also out of respect for your venerable face, or else the favor would not have been so generous.”","Before the entire room of serving maids and matrons, the Dowager Madame smiled radiantly, as if sharing in the glory: “The Second Branch’s wife has truly distinguished herself this time. From now on, stop complaining about this and that.”","As if her complaints had only been because Second Madam was low in status and without talent, not because she had been treated unfairly.","Second Madam was furious.","She had discovered that she could handle social engagements outside the household now. Even if others said unpleasant things, they did not cut her to the bone.","It had nothing to do with her.","But her mother-in-law was family, and she knew Second Madam all too well. She always stabbed at her heart with steady, ruthless precision, leaving Second Madam wretchedly uncomfortable.","She looked at the Dowager Madame’s kind and benevolent face and thought to herself: “I’d like to see what becomes of you, so fond of tormenting people!”","She did not put on the hypocrisy she had learned outside, nor did she lose her temper. She simply sat in silence.","Cheng Zhao smiled and spoke on her behalf: “Grandmother, with Mother having such great fortune, shouldn’t we celebrate a little?”","She added, “If you find it a bother, just reward Mother with some money. She has not only brought honor to herself, but also to the Chen Duke household.”","Huan Qingtang sat nearby, listening in silence.","It was only when Cheng Zhao said this that she glanced at her.","The Dowager Madame’s expression cooled considerably, her smile fading: “Have I been stingy with your branch?”","“Grandmother is always fair in rewards and punishments; this has nothing to do with your usual kindness toward us,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile. “Mother will now have to attend social functions. She must have new jewelry and clothes made, otherwise going out as a First-Class Noble Lady without proper appearance would only damage the Zhou family’s prestige.”","She paused, then continued, “Grandmother, please reward Mother with fifty thousand taels of silver.”","Second Madam’s eyes widened slightly as she looked at Cheng Zhao.","By now she was no longer angry—she almost wanted to laugh.","The maids and matrons seemed to draw in a sharp breath.","Huan Qingtang said, “Sister-in-law, that is beyond proper bounds.”","“If the First Branch’s wife later receives the title of First-Class Noble Lady, just ask Grandmother for fifty thousand taels as well. I won’t be jealous. My own title came earlier, so no need to make up for it,” Cheng Zhao said with a laugh.","Then she added, “Grandmother, Mother’s title is truly extraordinary. Shouldn’t we seize this fortune and blessing?”","The Dowager Madame smiled ambiguously: “Cheng Shi has a tongue that truly rivals all others.”","She had been backed into a corner, unable to rise or descend with dignity.","“Still, Cheng Shi has reminded me. Mid-Autumn Festival is approaching, and there will be many banquets ahead. If you all appear too shabby, it will disgrace the Duke household,” the Dowager Madame said.","She mused for a moment, then declared, “Very well, I’ll take out fifty thousand taels. Fan Shi, you get thirty thousand for yourself—go buy some gold and jewels, and have more clothes made; Cheng Shi and Lady Huan each get ten thousand.”","Cheng Zhao quickly pulled Second Madam to her feet: “Thank you, Grandmother!”","Huan Qingtang also rose and bowed: “Thank you for your great kindness, Grandmother. This granddaughter-in-law accepts with shame.”","“Sister-in-law, you’re benefiting from my mother’s good fortune. And so am I,” Cheng Zhao said.","Huan Qingtang: “...”","The Dowager Madame gestured for them all to sit.","Soon, Nanny Sun brought out small boxes and distributed them to the three women, who each took one.","Inside each small box were banknotes; Second Madam’s portion was noticeably heavier.","The Dowager Madame’s magnanimity, wealth, and generosity were witnessed by all, adding further to her authority.","“...Cheng Shi, the beauty bestowed by the emperor is not like the concubines in the household. You cannot treat her the way you treated Ah Jiang. Her food, clothing, and daily needs must not be neglected in any way. If the emperor should take offense, I will hold you responsible.","You said it yourself—this household rewards and punishes fairly. Now that you’ve been rewarded, if you err later, Grandmother will not hesitate to punish you.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged with a “Yes.”","Huan Qingtang smiled and spoke up: “Grandmother, the Secondary Wife has been at the manor for some time now. She should have calmed down. Shall we have her brought back?”","The Dowager Madame hesitated: “I’ll send someone to check on her later.”","Then she glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Mu Jiang had been sent to the manor by Zhou Yuanshen’s own decision.","That punishment was the Lord Duke’s prerogative. Cheng Zhao would not oppose him on it, so she merely said, “Grandmother, may I inform the Lord Duke of this matter?”","The Dowager Madame: “Just mention it, so I don’t have to send someone specifically to tell him.”","Cheng Zhao acknowledged again.","The group left Shou’an Court.","Although the Ghost Festival had passed, the capital was still sweltering. Cheng Zhao and the others took a small oiled carriage back.","Cheng Zhao did not board the carriage. Instead, she stepped forward and blocked Huan Qingtang: “Sister-in-law, matters within our branch’s quarters are best left alone by you.”","“Sister-in-law, the Secondary Wife is a matter for the Duke household as a whole. Don’t overstep your bounds and provoke Grandmother’s displeasure,” Huan Qingtang said with a smile.","Second Madam found her irritating just by looking at her, but constrained by propriety, she could not curse at a widowed niece-in-law. She was not as base as First Madam, Old Lady Song.","“What concerns the Duke household is also what concerns our branch, sister-in-law,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Huan Qingtang’s expression shifted slightly.","Cheng Zhao said no more, turned to help Second Madam into the small oiled carriage, and the two mother-and-daughter-in-law rode off first.","Huan Qingtang’s maid was furious: “Young Madam, look at her nerve.”","The weather was hot, yet Huan Qingtang felt cold sweat break out on her palms and back.","Zhou Yuanshen had gone hunting and come back with a songstress, one bestowed by the emperor, and Huan Qingtang’s mood had soured.","The emperor was supposed to give Zhou Yuanshen a woman—but why a songstress, and not a sanctioned marriage for the dual heir line?","She couldn’t help but wonder if it was because she hadn’t gone, whether the plans had changed, and her good fortune had been downgraded to this petty affair.","And then Second Madam had been named a First-Class Noble Lady.","When Zhou Yuanshen had inherited the title, there had been his grandmother, his aunt by marriage, and his wife—three grand ladies of the highest rank—so Second Madam had never qualified.","Huan Qingtang had always consoled herself that within these inner quarters, Second Madam was far beneath her.","Compared to her own situation, Second Madam had been even more stifled.","But she had never imagined Second Madam would rise.","Once the Dowager Madame passed away, Second Madam’s position would be even more exalted.","And what did Huan Qingtang have?","Cheng Zhao had made it unmistakably clear: the entire Duke household belonged to their Second Branch.","Ever since Huan Qingtang lost her husband and bore no son, the wealth and glory of the Duke household had slipped through her fingers.","She bit down hard on her back teeth.","“Go to Qingfeng Court this evening and see Mother,” Huan Qingtang said.","First Madam, old lady Song, had merely been moved to Qingfeng Court. The Dowager Madame had not placed her under house arrest; she was still free to come and go.","She must be thrilled to hear that Second Madam has been granted the title of First-Class Noble Lady, right?"]},{"number":179,"title":"Chapter 179: To Appreciate Again and Again","paragraphs":["The Second Madam returned to the Crimson Cloud Courtyard and began counting the silver notes.","It was indeed thirty thousand taels.","\"How wealthy the old lady is!\" the Second Madam marveled. \"Fifty thousand taels, and she can produce them as easily as that!\"","Cheng Zhao was not surprised to learn of the Grand Madam's wealth; she had aimed for that exact amount deliberately.","\"Mother, let's go buy some new headpieces,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam replied, \"I have more jewelry in my storeroom than I can ever use.\"","\"Those are older styles now. Let's get some fresh designs. We'll leave the ones in the storeroom untouched,\" said Cheng Zhao.","Then she added, \"There are also new fabrics in fashion. Let's go buy some of those too.\"","The Second Madam brightened. \"That works for me. I've always wished for a daughter—the two of us going shopping for clothes and jewelry together.\"","\"You'll have three daughters-in-law soon. We'll have plenty of chances to stroll about together,\" Cheng Zhao said with a laugh.","The Second Madam nodded repeatedly.","Nanny Fan chimed in beside them, mentioning which fabric shop had the best materials, which goldsmith's workmanship was most exquisite, and which of the household seamstresses made the finest garments.","As they talked, the Second Madam brought up Huan Qingtang: \"She benefits from our good fortune too—she'll get ten thousand taels.\"","\"Either way, it's Grandmother's money. Giving a little to her doesn't hurt,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Mother, 'the moon, once full, begins to wane.' It's not a bad idea to share some of it.\"","Everyone laughed at that.","Nanny Fan and several of the senior maids praised Cheng Zhao for being perceptive and generous.","The Second Madam chuckled, \"I wouldn't mind sharing with anyone else—just not with her. Her attitude is truly unpleasant.\"","Then she added, \"Oh well, I won't stoop to her level.\"","She had been about to say that Huan Qingtang was also a pitiful soul—married less than three years before becoming a widow, with no children to show for it—somewhat tragic.","But then again, with her scheming ways and covert provocations, she was even more infuriating than the First Madam. Sympathy was hard to muster.","The Second Madam had learned to keep her surplus sympathy in check.","As they were chatting, a senior maid from the Hall of Longevity arrived.","The maid had come looking for Cheng Zhao.","\"What's the matter?\" the Second Madam asked.","She guessed it was about the songstress.","The senior maid no longer dared to slight the Second Madam—when asked, she answered respectfully: \"The Grand Madam sent me to inform the Third Young Madam that the new concubine has been settled into the Jade Brocade Courtyard. Two second-class maids, four junior maids, and two rough-work servants have been assigned to her.\"","In the old days, this maid would have looked only at Cheng Zhao, with a troubled expression, and would never have casually revealed the Grand Madam's instructions to the Second Madam.","The winds of change were blowing through the household—even the staff at the Hall of Longevity had shifted.","\"Does the upkeep of the Jade Brocade Courtyard come from the household accounts, or does the Duke cover it himself?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","The senior maid replied, \"Naturally from the household accounts.\"","Cheng Zhao smiled and nodded, then instructed Suyue to tip the maid and see her out.","The Second Madam's enthusiasm promptly faded.","She looked at Cheng Zhao, as if about to say something. But Cheng Zhao clearly had no desire to discuss it, and changed the subject, bringing up the upcoming Mid-Autumn Festival.","After the festival, several families in the capital would be holding weddings, and Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam chatted about which banquets they would attend.","That night, the Second Madam had trouble sleeping.","The Second Master asked her what was wrong.","\"That songstress—she's remarkably seductive, and your son is a young man. The Jade Brocade Courtyard sits right next to the inner study. We have no idea what goes on behind those doors,\" the Second Madam said.","The Second Master asked, \"What does Ah Shen say?\"","\"He says he knows what he's doing,\" said the Second Madam.","The Second Master replied, \"Then trust him.\"","\"My heart is heavy. I'm afraid Zhao Zhao will be hurt. She's cheerful all day long, quick with her words, and has never faced any real hardship,\" the Second Madam said.","Then she added, \"With the matter of the former concubine, you could say she was magnanimous and let it go. But with someone new coming in, it's hard for her not to feel uneasy.\"","\"Ah Shen won't do that—he's a boy with a conscience,\" said the Second Master. \"And Zhao Zhao knows her own mind.\"","The Second Madam had a tendency to coddle her children, magnifying their difficulties by tenfold.","The children, in turn, knew how to handle things themselves—they weren't nearly as soft-hearted as their mother.","The Second Master reassured her repeatedly.","The next day, just as they were about to set out for the fabric shop, a eunuch arrived from the palace.","This time, it was someone from the Empress.","The Empress had bestowed gifts upon the Second Madam: a hundred taels of gold; twenty bolts of rare tribute fabric; and eight sets of gold-and-gem-studded headpieces.","There were also pearls, various jades, and more.","The treasures, dazzling and abundant, filled the main hall of the Crimson Cloud Courtyard.","Nanny Fan gave each of the delivering eunuchs a red envelope stuffed with silver notes.","The chief eunuch also told the Second Madam: \"His Highness the Crown Prince would have come to see you himself, but his studies keep him occupied.\"","The Second Madam waved her hands hastily. \"I would never presume to trouble the Crown Prince.\"","\"The Empress said that when you have time, you should request an audience and come to the palace to visit the Crown Prince,\" the chief eunuch said with a smile.","The Second Madam acknowledged this.","After they had been sent off, she sent for Cheng Zhao to come and take her pick.","\"See what you like!\" the Second Madam said cheerfully. \"We've truly struck it rich this time—we can save all the money on clothes and jewelry.\"","\"Gifts from the Empress carry prestige, and wearing them will do us credit,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","\"The Empress also invited me to the palace. I'm honestly a little nervous,\" the Second Madam admitted.","Cheng Zhao took her hand and laughed. \"Who isn't nervous entering the palace? Mother, I'll go with you—and let me soak up some of that glory too.\"","\"Having you with me puts my mind at ease,\" said the Second Madam. \"When shall we go?\"","\"In a few days,\" Cheng Zhao replied. \"On the first day of the eighth month.\"","\"That's perfect. Gives me time to prepare,\" said the Second Madam.","If she went right away, she'd be too tense. She needed a moment to compose herself.","The Crimson Cloud Courtyard was filled with laughter and pleasant chatter.","The Second Madam wanted to split the fabrics and headpieces evenly with Cheng Zhao, but Cheng Zhao said that dividing up imperial gifts seemed inappropriate.","\"Don't store the fabrics away,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Have them all cut and sewn into garments for autumn and winter. Then we can wear them to show Her Majesty.\"","The Second Madam agreed.","The Second Branch of the household was in unusually high spirits.","Even the servants spoke with more confidence than before.","Cheng Zhao reaped benefits as well: while she had been away at the hunt, all matters of the Hall of Inheritance had been left to Huan Qingtang, and the stewards had wavered, uncertain whom to follow.","When she returned—with her mother-in-law receiving the title of First-Class Lady of the State and the Grand Madam openly rewarding the Second Madam—Cheng Zhao herself was regarded with greater esteem.","The stewards under her kept their loyalty and resolve, except for one or two particularly troublesome figures with deep backers.","Thus, despite Cheng Zhao's absence, Huan Qingtang had achieved nothing. Her influence had stalled.","If anything, some of the stewards on her side were growing restless.","\"Without Mother's backing, I would have had my work cut out for me when I came back,\" Cheng Zhao remarked to Nanny Li and the others.","\"The Second Madam's title could not have come at a better time,\" Nanny Li said with a smile.","\"Everyone says I'm my mother-in-law's lucky star, but truthfully, she brings me fortune as well,\" said Cheng Zhao.","With the misunderstanding between her and Zhou Yuanshen cleared up, and everyone in the Second Branch leaning toward her, Cheng Zhao could now scheme with both hands free.","Her mood was fairly good.","Moreover, no one in the Harvest Court dared mention the new concubine of the Jade Brocade Courtyard in front of her, so her spirits remained untroubled.","Zhou Yuanshen's external injuries had not fully healed when he left for the Capital Guard Camp.","\"Take care of yourself.\"","Both husband and wife said the same words.","Zhou Yuanshen fixed his gaze on her.","Cheng Zhao smiled and said, \"Watch out for that shoulder wound.\"","\"And you—mind the affairs of the household,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","On the first day of the eighth month, Cheng Zhao accompanied her mother-in-law to the palace to pay their respects.","This time, she also met the Princess of Jingnan, the mother - in - law of her third sister.","After coming out of the Kunning Palace, the Princess of Jingnan invited Cheng Zhao and the Second Lady to visit their mansion some other day. Cheng Zhao wanted to see her third sister, so she agreed."]},{"number":180,"title":"Chapter 180: Not Wanting to Wait on Her Ladyship","paragraphs":["The Duke of Chen's mansion had been busy preparing for the Mid-Autumn Festival these days.","For the festival, the Zhou family would hold a banquet under the night sky, and all the clan members would come—an enormous number of tasks.","Fortunately, Cheng Zhao handled it with ease. After straightening out the matters at the front gate and the main kitchen, she took her regular day off, as she did every fifth day.","On her day off, Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam went to visit the Jingnan Prince's mansion as guests.","The Princess Consort even specially asked her to visit Cheng Ying's courtyard.","Cheng Zhao had been there several times.","The sunlight was not harsh, and the tree shadows were thick along the path. Cheng Ying led the way, the two sisters strolling slowly hand in hand, chatting about trivial matters.","\"...I've never heard anything about Fourth Brother and Guo Hanzhang,\" Cheng Zhao told her third sister.","Third Sister laughed. \"Ah Chao did mention it, but he wasn't really keen on Guo Hanzhang; it was Guo Hanzhang who kept pestering him.\"","\"Grandfather wouldn't agree.\"","\"Ah Chao wouldn't agree either—he's not stupid,\" Third Sister said.","\"Mainly it's because the political situation is too complicated. I always feel it's like a battered ship in a storm, about to capsize at any moment,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She lowered her voice and told Third Sister about how, during the hunting party, the Emperor had personally cut down a spy on the spot.","Cheng Ying said, \"I heard about that. It was truly...\"","Truly baffling.","What reason could a sovereign have for personally killing a spy?","Apart from earning a reputation for \"bloodlust,\" what could he possibly gain?","\"Sometimes he's mad in a chaotic way; sometimes he's mad in a very orderly way,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Ying remarked, \"The Crown Prince, at least, is decent—gentle and refined. Unfortunately, he's frail and sickly, and his talents are too ordinary.\"","In such a situation, everyone was seeking balance; no household felt safe.","Grand Preceptor Guo seemed glorious on the surface, but who knew where he'd fall the next moment?","The Cheng family was a scholarly clan.","Grandfather commanded the respect of all scholars under heaven. The Cheng family had a good reputation, abundant wealth, and reliable marriage alliances, with deep roots.","No matter how much the imperial court swayed, the Cheng family's foundation was solid.","Governments came and went like flowing water, but powerful clans stood like iron. The Cheng clan was the most steadfast among them, because reputation was more enduring than wealth or status.","—Perhaps that was what Old Madam Zhou had been pursuing; she had always wanted to turn the Duke of Chen's mansion into a scholarly clan.","\"...Guo Hanzhang has latched onto me again. She thinks I have some ability and that I can help her,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Ying chuckled. \"She has good judgment—she knows to pick someone capable.\"","\"You're teasing me too?\"","\"If Ah Chao were willing, I honestly can't think of anyone better suited than you to plead his case,\" Cheng Ying said.","Then she added, \"But I'd advise you not to get involved. Ah Chao isn't enthusiastic about this matter. He often says he doesn't want to serve a young mistress.\"","Cheng Zhao's face immediately fell. \"He must have been bad-mouthing me behind my back again.\"","\"Yes, he said serving you is trouble enough; if Guo Hanzhang were added to the mix, he'd never have a moment's peace,\" Third Sister said with a smile.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","She really was wasting her worry on him.","Third Sister then shared a small secret with Cheng Zhao: \"Last time during the Ullambana Festival, we ran into Princess Rong by the riverbank. She talked with Mother for quite a while; just a few days ago, she also arranged to go offer incense together.\"","Princess Rong was a close neighbor of the Duke of Chen's mansion, and Prince Rong's Heir, Helian Jian, had a good relationship with Fan Xiao.","\"...The Rong Prince's mansion has a Seventh Miss who's just come of age. Last time, my eldest aunt had designs on her,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"I heard from Mother that Princess Rong intends to form a marriage alliance with the Cheng family.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"What did Mother say?\"","\"The Rong Prince's mansion keeps a low profile, and Prince Rong doesn't scheme. It's relatively safe. But it would be somewhat of a climb for them,\" Cheng Ying said.","Cheng Zhao: \"A marriage that climbs upward naturally requires more effort.\"","\"The Rong Prince's mansion is also afraid of trouble. With the court situation as it is, they're uneasy too. If we form a marriage alliance, no matter what happens to that household in the future, the Cheng family would have to do everything in its power to help. If all is well, fine; if trouble comes, it would be a severe blow,\" Cheng Ying said.","Cheng Zhao sighed.","She couldn't help grumbling about Cheng Chao again. \"His matters are the only trouble. All five of us siblings, the rest of us save the family from worry.\"","Cheng Ying patted her hand and smiled patiently.","Her courtyard was not far from the main residence, and they arrived soon.","In front of the courtyard gate was a large grove of spotted bamboo—Cheng Ying's favorite—and beside it a plum grove, lush and green at this time of year.","Cheng Ying's residence was elegant, from the gaps in the brick walls to the garden gate, everything refined.","Most of the maids and matrons in the courtyard were Cheng Ying's dowry attendants—much like the situation in Cheng Zhao's own courtyard.","When Cheng Zhao arrived, it was like the young miss of the house had come; everyone welcomed her warmly.","The two sisters sat in the side chamber drinking tea, still discussing the hunting party matters.","Cheng Ying finally said, \"Only one Liu Zheng and one spy died—that counts as quite smooth. This time, several factions were plotting trouble. If even one thing had gone wrong, the Emperor himself might have met his end at the hunting grounds.\"","Cheng Zhao was shocked.","Being in the middle of it all, not knowing the full picture, she had never thought so deeply.","Zhou Yuanshen seemed afraid of scaring her, so he hadn't mentioned any of it.","It wasn't until Cheng Ying spelled it out.","\"It's really possible. This unpredictable, mad Emperor is a genuine headache—better to have a weak, young new sovereign who's easy to manipulate. Before Grand Preceptor Guo has his hand over everything, it benefits everyone,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Then she added, \"No wonder there was that stray arrow aimed at the Emperor's mount!\"","It was like fishing.","The Emperor wanted to catch Liu Zheng.","That fish was too big, and the Emperor might be dragged under and drowned.","The way it all ended covered up almost every problem.","\"Third Sister, the Prince of Andong is very ambitious. And unfortunately, Fourth Brother is close to him,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Ying said, \"Ah Chao isn't a fool; rest assured on that point. In matters of right and wrong, he knows where the line is.\"","Cheng Zhao was about to say something more when someone called out from the courtyard: \"A'Ying! A'Ying!\"","Soon, a young gentleman in a white-apricot robe entered the side chamber, holding a bouquet of osmanthus.","It was Cheng Ying's husband, Zhang Yunqi.","Zhang Yunqi was strikingly handsome—rosy lips, white teeth, brows and eyes like dots of ink—and his figure was tall and elegant, his dress luxurious.","The osmanthus branches in his hand were very fragrant.","The floral scent, like the man himself, attracted attention easily.","Cheng Zhao turned away disdainfully, thinking: \"A handsome exterior concealing a worthless fool.\"","She didn't call him brother-in-law.","No one in the Cheng family bothered to acknowledge Zhang Yunqi.","Zhang Yunqi didn't seem to mind. He smiled and said, \"Fifth Sister.\"","Cheng Zhao slightly inclined her body as a form of acknowledgment.","\"These are for you, A'Ying. They bloomed at Fahua Temple—they smell even better than the ones at home.\" Zhang Yunqi handed the flowers to Cheng Ying.","Cheng Ying took them. \"Thank you.\"","She instructed a maid, \"Put them in a vase in the main hall, not in the inner chamber. I can't bear such a heavy fragrance.\"","The maid acknowledged and left with the flowers.","\"How are you free to come back at this hour?\" Cheng Ying then asked.","Zhang Yunqi said, \"As soon as I picked the flowers, I hurried back to show you.\"","\"Thank you.\"","\"I came back and heard Fifth Sister was here. I won't disturb you—I'll come back for dinner tonight,\" he said. \"Fifth Sister, do visit often.\"","Cheng Zhao said nothing.","Zhang Yunqi turned and left.","Very lively, very cheerful.","Only after he was gone did Cheng Zhao ask, \"Does he come back often?\"","\"Occasionally,\" Cheng Ying said.","\"And that mistress of his—how is she now?\" Cheng Zhao pressed. \"Has he made a fuss about bringing her into the household?\"","\"She doesn't want to come into the household either. The prince's mansion, for all its wealth, is full of rules, and the servants are snobbish. Coming in wouldn't lead to a good life,\" Cheng Ying said.","Cheng Zhao: \"And you?\"","\"I'm fine, Zhaozhao,\" Cheng Ying said. \"Don't worry about me.\"","Cheng Zhao couldn't say anything more."]},{"number":181,"title":"Chapter 181: Encounter on a Narrow Path","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao sat with her third sister for a long time.","She also saw Master Dai.","Master Dai was a distant relative of the Cheng family. She had also taught Cheng Zhao swordsmanship and later followed Cheng Ying as her personal companion.","Even now, Cheng Zhao still practiced with her sword in the early mornings, though occasionally she was too busy to keep up.","However, she did not seek to refine her skill; it was merely for exercise, and her swordsmanship was quite ordinary.","“......Master Dai hasn’t changed much,” Cheng Zhao said.","“The Fifth Lady has grown into her looks, more beautiful than before,” Master Dai said.","Cheng Zhao smiled and asked about her children.","“Her boy is thirteen this year and studies beside the steward in the prince’s household. I plan to have him learn accounts for ten years; in the future, he can manage my dowry properties,” the third sister said.","“That is well arranged. A bookkeeper can always earn his keep,” Cheng Zhao remarked.","The third sister: “It’s mainly because he’s shy and introverted; otherwise, I would cultivate him to be a head steward.”","“Teaching according to one’s talents—only you truly know how to judge people, Third Sister,” Cheng Zhao said. “And what of Master Dai’s daughter? She must be nearly ten by now.”","“She is passionately devoted to the sword, even more so than Master Dai. I sent her away to study formally,” the third sister said.","Cheng Zhao: “Then in the future, you will not be short of capable hands.”","Master Dai smiled beside them: “I only hope they can both serve the young lady well; otherwise, the money would be wasted. The young lady has spent a great deal on us, mother and child.”","“Whether a tree becomes timber depends on heaven’s will. Third Sister values you, which is why she is willing to invest such effort. Your unwavering loyalty by her side has already repaid her,” Cheng Zhao said.","The third sister nodded: “Exactly so.”","The conversation was lively and warm.","When the midday meal hour arrived, Cheng Zhao prepared to return to the main courtyard.","“I’d rather not leave my mother-in-law too long with the princess consort. She dislikes socializing,” Cheng Zhao said.","“Second Madam is a down-to-earth woman, and my mother-in-law is not one to be petty or controlling. Don’t worry—they’ll get along just fine,” the third sister said.","Even so, the two sisters hurried back to the main courtyard of the prince’s residence.","Indeed, the Second Madam and Princess Consort Jingnan were deep in pleasant conversation.","Prince Jingnan had earned his non-imperial title through military merit. On that account, the princess consort and the Second Madam had no shortage of topics to share.","“It’s about time, isn’t it? Quickly bring out the meal—we are both hungry,” the princess consort said, smiling as she saw the two sisters return.","She then asked Cheng Ying, “Has Dongbai returned?”","Zhang Yunqi, styled Dongbai, had been granted this courtesy name by the late emperor himself.","During the late emperor’s reign, he had shown exceptional favor to Prince Jingnan, making him nearly the foremost among his close ministers.","“He gathered some osmanthus flowers and sent them to me,” Cheng Ying said.","The princess consort smiled: “He never has any serious business. Now that he’s back, he doesn’t even come for a meal—off running somewhere again.”","“There are female guests present, so it isn’t proper for him to come over,” Cheng Ying said with a smile. “He knows his manners well enough.”","Cheng Ying’s beauty was so striking it rivaled frost and snow. Even with the faintest smile, she appeared ethereal and moving. Every word that came from her lips sounded extraordinarily refined.","When she spoke of her husband, her tone was relaxed and natural; toward her mother-in-law, Princess Consort Jingnan, she showed a certain affection.","The Second Madam glanced at Cheng Zhao.","She had expected Cheng Zhao to chime in with a compliment for Zhang Yunqi, but Cheng Zhao remained silent.","The Second Madam recalled what Zhou Yuanqi had once said—that everyone in the Cheng family held Princess Consort Jingnan in high regard, yet none of them paid Zhang Yunqi any attention.","When it was time to be sweet-tongued, her daughter-in-law’s words were honeyed; but when principles were at stake, her daughter-in-law drew clear lines between gratitude and grievance.","The backbone of a scholarly family was stiffer than the Second Madam had imagined.","Over the past half year, the Second Madam had come to see a whole new side of the world and understood many truths that had eluded her for most of her life.","The midday meal was lavish.","The princess consort had also invited her other two daughters-in-law to join as companions.","The young ladies of the Zhang household got along harmoniously, each with a pleasant countenance and gentle disposition.","After the meal, Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam lingered briefly before taking their leave.","As they were about to depart, a maid from the second gate rushed in and said urgently to the princess consort: “Your Highness, the princess has arrived, and she seems in a great hurry.”","The princess consort’s expression shifted slightly.","Cheng Ying asked calmly, “Which princess? This is rather without context.”","The eldest daughter of Princess Consort Jingnan had been titled Princess Deyang.","Just two days earlier, the princess had visited her natal home, and all had been well. Moreover, when the princess returned to the residence, she would send word ahead; she would not arrive in the middle of the afternoon like this.","Furthermore, even if she were in haste, the guards at the second gate would not dare to stop her, and there would be no need for a maid to come all the way to announce her.","This particular maid looked unfamiliar—perhaps recently assigned to the second gate.","Cheng Ying sensed immediately that something was off, while the princess consort, caught up in her concern, had not yet noticed.","“It is Princess Qing’an,” the maid said, lowering her head.","The princess consort: “......”","When she realized it was not her own daughter, her heart settled back into place, and her composure returned: “Why has Princess Qing’an come? There was no advance notice, not even a card sent.”","The heir’s wife rose to her feet: “Mother, I’ll go and see.”","The princess consort nodded: “Tell her not to rush into anything. I still have guests here. Entertain her first.”","The heir’s wife acknowledged and left.","With this commotion at hand, and knowing the Second Madam and Princess Qing’an did not see eye to eye, Cheng Zhao took her leave.","The princess consort and Cheng Ying saw them to the door.","Unexpectedly, Princess Qing’an was heading toward the main courtyard.","As soon as she saw the princess consort, she rushed over and tried to seize her hand: “Your Highness, you must help me this time! My husband—he’s been taken by the prefect of the capital!”","Princess Consort Jingnan was taken aback.","Princess Qing’an was about to say more, but her eyes swept over the unfamiliar faces, and she stopped herself.","She recognized the Second Madam of the Zhou family.","That time when she had hastily arranged a match between Huan Qingtang and Zhou Yuanshen, coming out of nowhere, the Second Madam had been present.","That incident had rankled the Second Madam for many years.","“Your Highness, we will take our leave then,” Cheng Zhao said.The princess consort’s hand was still held by Princess Qing’an, so she only smiled and said to Cheng Ying, “See them to the main gate.”","Then to Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam: “Please forgive us for the poor hospitality. Do come again another day.”","Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam acknowledged with polite words and turned to leave.","Princess Qing’an did not release the princess consort’s hand, and unwilling to face the strangers, she turned her face to the other side and remained silent.","Cheng Zhao and the Second Madam boarded their carriage at the gate.","“......What happened to her husband?” the Second Madam asked, curious.","“We’ll send someone to find out later. Since the prefect has arrested him, he must have committed some offense,” Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam: “She seems to be on quite good terms with the princess consort.”","“Last time we visited, the princess consort mentioned she had some friendship with Princess Qing’an. She didn’t try to hide it,” Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam had forgotten this detail: “Is that so? I wasn’t paying attention at the time.”","“Just trivial matters,” Cheng Zhao said.","Back at the Duke Chen residence, the Second Madam was still thinking about Princess Qing’an’s husband’s trouble and planned to have the Second Lord make inquiries.","However, when the Second Lord returned, Zhou Yuanqi came along with him, and the matter was soon explained.","“......The river transport on the Jinzhou section of the Qu River has been controlled by them for years. This time, someone died, and that’s what brought it to light. It was a steward from the Sun family—the natal family of grandmother—whom Princess Qing’an’s husband had beaten to death,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","The Second Madam: “......”","She had thought it was merely some gossip to amuse herself, but it turned out the story came full circle back to their own household.","The Second Madam quickly sent for Cheng Zhao.","When Cheng Zhao returned to the Nonghua Courtyard, she had already heard the news.","It had been told by Nanfeng, a servant of Zhou Yuanshen, to Qiubai, who in turn relayed it to Cheng Zhao.","“Merit-based noble families fighting over interests—this is common enough,” Cheng Zhao said.","\"Your grandmother is likely to be upset. Since someone has died in the Marquis of Qingyuan's household, they will certainly come to complain to her,\" said the Second Madam.","At that moment, a senior maid from Shou'an Courtyard arrived at Jiangyun Courtyard.","\"Second Master, the Madam Dowager requests your presence at Shou'an Courtyard,\" the senior maid said.","Zhou Yuanshen was stationed at the capital garrison, so matters at home required the Second Master to step in.","The Second Master smiled good-naturedly and said, \"Go ahead and eat without me.\"","He rose and left."]},{"number":182,"title":"Chapter 182: Refusing to Be the Duke's Wife","paragraphs":["Second Master went to Shou'an Yard.","He didn't return after the evening meal, and as the hour grew late, Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi each went back to their own quarters.","“...I heard from Mother that your birthday is coming up soon,” Cheng Zhao asked him. “Is there anything you want?”","Zhou Yuanqi's birthday was the tenth day of the eighth month.","According to her mother-in-law's view, children didn't make a big fuss over birthdays, lest they prove unable to bear such fortune.","—Children were all too prone to dying young; parents had to take care in every regard.","Every year on Zhou Yuanqi's birthday, Second Madam would make some socks and shoes, Second Master would prepare some little trinket or stationery; and there would be a bowl of longevity noodles.","On the day itself, there was barely any mention of it, as if nothing were happening at all.","Cheng Zhao laughed and said, “In Wu Commandery there's an old custom: children who haven't come of age hide by the door to eat their longevity noodles. I used to hide as a child too.”","It was to ward off little ghosts.","Ghosts saw children as easy targets, envied their good fortune in eating well, and would come to snatch them away.","So one ate longevity noodles in secret.","Second Madam said the capital had no such custom, but the rule of “keeping it concealed and unmentioned” was much the same everywhere.","“Last time he gave you a gold pig. You can return a lavish gift when he comes of age. You shouldn't give too much at this time,” Second Madam had specifically cautioned Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao said she understood.","She asked Zhou Yuanqi whether he wanted writing brushes, ink, paper, an inkstone, a fine book, or something like a pouch or sachet.","“I lit a blessing lamp for Grandmother at Jin'an Temple. Every year on my birthday, Mother goes to make an offering of oil money. If you have the heart, you can also make an oil offering—that can count as your gift to me,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao was surprised. “You lit a lamp?”","“Temples don't only light lamps for the dead. The living can light blessing lamps too,” Zhou Yuanqi said.","“...You're quite filial, yet you have no taboos at all.” Cheng Zhao laughed helplessly.","Zhou Yuanqi said, “Grandmother cares least about such things. It's the thought that counts.”","Cheng Zhao promised him that on the tenth, she would go with him and her mother-in-law to Jin'an Temple to pray.","The two parted and each returned to their own courtyard.","Of the fabric for clothes that Zhou Yuanshen had bought for her in Wu Commandery last time, she had selected a set for Zhou Yuanqi and sent it to the needlework room.","The needlework room was now also under Cheng Zhao's management.","Cheng Zhao told Suyue to give extra reward money: “Tell them to work quickly and get this outer robe done for Yuanqi. If it's done very well, each embroideress will receive an additional reward from me.”","She added, “They've just cut the winter clothes, so they have Yuanqi's measurements.”","Suyue acknowledged the order.","She soon returned, smiling. “I gave five taels of reward silver and told them not to delay their current duties, and not to invite gossip for you.”","The clothes Cheng Zhao had sent over were purely extra work.","The senior embroideresses in the needlework room earned only two taels a month in wages; the rest earned even less. Five taels of reward silver from Cheng Zhao, with the promise of more, meant no one would complain.","Besides, she managed the needlework room.","“For a child's birthday, one shouldn't be too showy,” Nanny Li said.","Suyue said, “I made it clear the clothes were for the Fifth Young Master. The head supervisor merely noted that the stitching should be fine, but the embroidery can't be too elaborate.”","“She's a supervisor who understands the way of the world,” Nanny Li said.","With nothing pressing to do, Cheng Zhao brought out her sewing basket.","Her monthly cycle had come again.","While she did needlework, the maids clustered nearby, organizing threads and chatting about this and that to pass the time.","But Cheng Zhao was lost in thought.","Still no pregnancy.","She'd been married for nearly a year now.","In less than a year, she had accomplished quite a lot. Looking back, even Cheng Zhao herself couldn't help but marvel.","She hadn't dared to think highly of herself, yet her achievements were considerable.","Now, everything was moving in a good direction except for the lack of an heir.","“Will the Lord Duke be back by the Mid-Autumn Festival?” Nanny Li asked.","Cheng Zhao said, “He should be.”","Nanny Li very much wanted to say, “Let's hope this Mid-Autumn Festival can be peaceful, and we can spend the holiday together as a family.”","But she couldn't say such a thing.","The moment she said it, something was sure to go wrong.","Cheng Zhao embroidered a sachet, stitched properly with a pattern of proud bamboo—something the young scholars could carry out.","Inside she would put herbs for clearing the mind, and give it to Yuanqi to take to the clan school, as her “birthday gift” to him.","“...You haven't made a pouch for the Lord Duke yet,” Nanny Li reminded her with a smile.","Cheng Zhao said, “Didn't I make one already?”","“The one you cut apart doesn't count,” Nanny Li said with a chuckle.","“It does count.”","Nanny Li: “...”","After a pause, Nanny Li smiled and coaxed her again, “Make one for him too. You're making one for the Fifth Young Master; when the Lord Duke sees it, he's bound to grow envious.”","Cheng Zhao stitched slowly along the lines of the bamboo leaves, unhurried: “When he has nice things, I don't grow envious. Why must he be envious of what belongs to others?”","Nanny Li watched her without moving: “Aren't the two of you getting along well? Why does it sound like you're sulking?”","“Nothing of the sort.”","“Is it because of that songstress, the one the emperor bestowed?” Nanny Li asked.","Cheng Zhao said nothing, continuing her needlework in silence.","Suyue and Qiubai signaled Nanny Li with their eyes to stop bringing it up.","They figured their mistress must be hurting inside, still putting up a front before others. In private, she was having trouble holding it together; if they pressed on, she might cry.","The inner room fell silent.","Cheng Zhao didn't cry. Her needle and thread never wavered, and she calmly finished the day's embroidery.","The bamboo-patterned sachet was simple to make; Cheng Zhao finished it in two evenings. Qiubai brought out their treasured spices, and Cheng Zhao followed the old recipe, weighing out the portions and filling the sachet.","A sachet with a refined color, exquisite stitching, and a fresh, clean fragrance was done.","Cheng Zhao also found a small box to store it in.","On the tenth day of the eighth month, Cheng Zhao informed her stewards a day in advance that she would not hold meetings that day. If anything urgent arose, they could refer it to the eldest mistress, Huan Qingtang, and she would handle it later.","She went early to the Vermilion Cloud Yard.","Qiubai carried a bundle with her.","Second Madam was just putting new shoes on Zhou Yuanqi.","“...What have you prepared? Clothes?” Second Madam asked with a smile.","Cheng Zhao said, “Mother has a good eye.”","She unfolded it: a brand-new sapphire-blue outer robe, embroidered with auspicious cloud patterns, the workmanship extremely attentive and the cut just right.","Second Madam immediately had Zhou Yuanqi put it on.","“Very nice. The new clothes are bright in color,” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao chuckled inwardly, “The bright color makes him look even more like a porcelain doll, exquisitely carved.” She didn't dare say it aloud, fearing it might irritate her young brother-in-law, so she merely said, “He looks wonderful. Yuanqi is really growing up—he's gotten much taller.”","Zhou Yuanqi lifted his chin proudly.","With new shoes on his feet and new clothes on his back, he was very pleased: “Thank you, Mother. Thank you, Third Sister-in-Law.”","Cheng Zhao then brought out his sachet.","“...There's borneol. I like this fragrance,” Zhou Yuanqi said, in a generous mood today.","Second Madam also took it to smell: “How was this blended?”","“There's a recipe. I'll write you out a copy later,” Cheng Zhao said.","“We can wear this kind of sachet in the summer too,” Second Madam said.","Second Master had given Zhou Yuanqi a fine inkstone. But he'd gone to the yamen at dawn for court, so Cheng Zhao hadn't run into him.","The lawsuit over the man beaten to death by Princess Qing'an's consort had been sent to the Court of Judicial Review. Cheng Zhao didn't know how it would turn out.","Nor did she know how the Grand Dowager would respond, or whether she would intercede for her natal family.","Cheng Zhao and the other three set off for Jin'an Temple.","The temple's incense was booming, and with the Mid-Autumn Festival approaching, the pilgrim traffic was thick as clouds.","The Duke Chen's Mansion had informed the people in advance. A senior monk, accompanied by several young novice monks, was waiting at the courtyard gate specifically to receive Zhou Yuanqi.","Zhou Yuanqi went to light the prayer lanterns. It was in the western part of Jin'an Temple, where there was a door and ordinary pilgrims were not allowed to enter.","From a distance, Cheng Zhao also saw other pilgrims led by senior monks.","She vaguely caught sight of Helian Xuan and his mother, the Dowager Consort An.","Cheng Zhao frowned slightly.","However, they didn't encounter them during the prayer. Maybe Cheng Zhao was mistaken.","In the morning, they not only had to pray but also sit on their knees to listen to the senior monk chanting scriptures. When it was over, Cheng Zhao felt sore and tired.","Her menstrual period had just ended yesterday, and she was still weak. She slightly closed her eyes to rest.","After Zhou Yuanqi finished, he ran away, saying, \"I'm going to pick osmanthus flowers in the back mountain. Mother, you and Third Sister-in-law wait for me in the vegetarian dining hall.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Kids never seem to get tired.","\"Zhaozhao, it's time to go over there and have the vegetarian meal,\" said the Second Madam.","Cheng Zhao said, \"I'll come right away. Mother, you go first.\"","She wanted to take a break.","There would be a young novice monk waiting to lead her the way, so she didn't have to worry about getting lost.","Cheng Zhao quietly adjusted her breath. When she felt that she had regained some strength and was about to stand up, someone hugged her from behind.","She was shocked and instinctively struck back forcefully with her hand."]},{"number":183,"title":"Chapter 183: Cheng Zhao, Aren't You Close with Your Husband","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao was nearly frightened out of her wits.","Her first thought was “kidnapper,” so she fought back with all her strength, desperate to escape.","She kicked and flailed wildly.","“Cheng Zhao, it’s me!”","Her struggling stopped. Slowly, she turned to look at the man beside her.","Zhou Yuanshen seemed a bit tanned, his eyes brighter than ever, his face expressionless as he watched her.","Cheng Zhao’s heart was pounding violently.","It felt as if someone had pushed her to the edge of a cliff—her terror at its peak, her survival instinct surging—only for her to realize the drop beneath her was just a shallow ditch, barely half a man’s height.","She felt relief wash over her.","She sat there, drained, and once her racing heart steadied a little, she looked at the man crouching beside her and gave him a hard punch.","“You scared me half to death!”","“Who would have thought you’d be this jumpy,” he said coolly. “Besides me, who else could embrace you?”","Cheng Zhao: “Plenty of people. Kidnappers, mountain bandits, even enemies—if they wanted to kill me here, they’d tie me up first.”","Zhou Yuanshen: “...”","He simply stared at her in silence.","Cheng Zhao saw not a shred of guilt in him, and the fear from before surged back. She hit him twice more.","Zhou Yuanshen caught her hand, leaning in slightly. “My footsteps, my breathing, the scent of me—even the way I hold you. Are you unfamiliar with all of it, Cheng Zhao?”","His eyes were utterly still, his gaze sharp as ice.","The August sun was still warm, its light streaming into the meditation room through the window—half falling on the statue of the Buddha, half behind him.","Yet his eyes seemed like deep winter.","Cheng Zhao: “You’re being unreasonable. You scared me to death, and now you’re turning it around on me.”","Zhou Yuanshen gripped her chin. “Even as husband and wife, there’s no need to be so separate. Cheng Zhao, from the very start of our marriage, you’ve been the one saying ‘willingly and gladly.’”","He added, “You only wanted to be the Duchess of Chen, and I’ve done everything to support that. I’m still the Duke of Chen—throwing me away before I’ve served my purpose, isn’t that a bit cold and heartless?”","Cheng Zhao: “...”","She was stunned.","She’d been scared out of her skin, and now she was somehow the one in the wrong?","What exactly was he nitpicking about?","“... I’m not talking to you. You’re like talking to a brick wall!” Cheng Zhao stood up. She needed to find her mother-in-law.","She was usually sharp-tongued, but this was the first time she felt truly at a loss for words.","Zhou Yuanshen was twisting everything—mixing right and wrong, calling a deer a horse.","With all this muddled reasoning, Cheng Zhao couldn’t even sort out where the argument had started; and her heart was still racing. She needed some quiet.","She turned to leave, but Zhou Yuanshen pulled her into his arms.","She stumbled, nearly falling into him.","He held her tightly, kissing her.","In her peripheral vision, Cheng Zhao could see the door of the meditation room ajar, could feel the solemn Buddha behind her. She pushed at Zhou Yuanshen.","But she couldn’t push him away. Panicked, she gasped, “Zhou Yuanshen, Zhou Yuanshen...”","“Hm!”","He heard her, yet pressed even closer.","The door seemed to shift slightly. Cheng Zhao looked up and saw the gap from before was gone—someone had closed it from outside.","Sunlight was blocked, the doorway cast in shadow, with a figure silhouetted against it.","The shadow wore a jade crown—not the usual attire of a deputy general.","Cheng Zhao bit Zhou Yuanshen hard.","She held back a little, afraid of drawing blood, and kicked at him as well.","Maybe she landed the kick—he loosened his grip and let her go. Or maybe she missed—his face stayed expressionless, no sign of pain.","He stepped back two paces.","Adjusted his robes, and without another word, he opened the door and left.","A gust of wind seemed to sweep into the meditation room, then out again. Had it not been for the lingering sensation on her lips and her slightly askew sash, Cheng Zhao might have thought it was all an illusion.","She stood there, silent for a moment.","“... From the very start of our marriage, you’ve been the one saying ‘willingly and gladly.’”","Cheng Zhao’s ears rang with Zhou Yuanshen’s words.","So this was where her battle had begun to unravel?","That one phrase, “willingly and gladly,” had surrendered her stronghold into his hands, giving him the secret to controlling her from then on.","Cheng Zhao took a moment to compose herself.","Someone at the door said, “Amitabha,” snapping her back to the present.","“Madam, are you ready? The vegetarian meal is about to be served,” a young monk said from the doorway.","Cheng Zhao responded: “I’m ready now, I’ll be right there.”","She straightened her robes, smoothed her hair, and stepped out.","As she left the meditation room, she felt eyes on her. She looked up and saw a pavilion slightly above and off to the side of the room.","A man in a plain pale blue robe stood there, watching her steadily.","It was Helian Xuan, the Prince of Andong.","Cheng Zhao had vaguely noticed him when she first entered the temple.","Their eyes met, but he didn’t approach or acknowledge her—just kept looking.","Cheng Zhao recalled their past, and then his actions at the hunting grounds in Ningzhou Prefecture, and felt a flicker of wariness.","“Duchess of Chen, the dining hall is that way,” he said.","His voice wasn’t loud, but just audible.","The young monk walked beside Cheng Zhao.","She asked, “Little master, where were you just now?”","“Waiting up ahead, madam.”","“Did you happen to see the Prince of Andong? Did he come down?” Cheng Zhao asked.","The monk glanced over. “No, he didn’t.”","“Is he also a regular visitor here?”","“The Dowager Consort sponsors a perpetual lamp here,” the young monk said.","“But isn’t the Dowager Consort at her fief?” Cheng Zhao pressed.","The monk said, “She doesn’t come herself—someone donates oil money on her behalf. She’s kept the lamp lit for many years.”","Cheng Zhao understood and stopped asking.","She went to the dining hall.","The Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi were there, along with many distinguished guests—including Dowager Consort An.","Seeing the dowager consort, Cheng Zhao, regardless of their relationship, had to step forward to pay her respects as the Duchess of Chen. Etiquette could not be neglected—especially in public, where appearances mattered.","She smiled and bowed: “Dowager Consort.”","Dowager Consort An seemed to not recognize her fully, studying her for a few moments. “Is it Young Madam Zhou? So you married into the Zhou family, didn’t you?”","“The dowager consort has such a good memory—better than people half her age. Indeed, it’s the Zhou family,” Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Her words carried veiled sarcasm; the dowager was overacting.","The two families had been neighbors in Fengzhou back then. There’s no way she forgot who Cheng Zhao was. She was just putting on this act.","“It’s impressive how you’ve grown into a lady. When you were little, you were such a mischief. Now you’re so proper and refined—it’s clear the Zhou family knows how to raise people.” Dowager Consort An chuckled.","She’d subtly insulted Cheng Zhao’s upbringing in her parents’ house.","“Children always grow up, Dowager Consort. You’ve been confined to your fief these days, rarely in the capital—your words have gotten rather muddled. I almost thought you were having senior moments, and my heart ached for you,” Cheng Zhao said.","Dowager Consort An: “...”","There were a few ladies with her, all accompanying her to burn incense.","From the very first exchange, it was clear that something was off between them.","Both were smiling, their tones warm and gentle—but their words were charged.","The more they spoke, the more acrid it became.","Dowager Consort An’s smile was starting to crack.","Cheng Zhao might as well have said she’d grown senile from living so long in the countryside, spewing nonsense.","“My mother is in good spirits, thank you for your concern, Duchess of Chen.” Helian Xuan, who had somehow entered the dining hall, greeted her with a brilliant smile.","His handsome peach-blossom eyes made his smile seem all the more genial.","Cheng Zhao finished her greetings and returned to the table where Second Madam was seated.","Zhou Yuanshen sat nearby, his face utterly devoid of emotion.","Second Madam asked, “What were you two talking about?”"]},{"number":184,"title":"Chapter 184: Without Cheng Zhao's Consent, It Shall Not Stand","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao didn't elaborate further.","She whispered to Second Madam, \"I'll tell you slowly later, Mother.\"","Second Madam didn't press the matter.","Beside Dowager Consort An, apart from several noble ladies accompanying her, there were two beautiful young women.","\"Isn't that...?\" Second Madam whispered to Cheng Zhao again.","One of the two young women was Xian Xiang, the singer - girl bestowed by the emperor to Prince of Andong.","Unexpectedly, in such a short time, she had changed her appearance and was now dressed like a maid from a wealthy and noble family.","A well - bred maid in a noble family was not only elegant in appearance but also noble in manner, nothing like a woman from the entertainment industry anymore.","Second Madam was a bit surprised.","Thinking of another girl in her own family, she felt annoyed again.","Cheng Zhao looked over and found the other girl familiar too. After a closer look, she realized it was the younger sister of Wenliang, her estate guard.","Although she had grown up, her features were still recognizable, and Cheng Zhao remembered her. Wenliang had brought her to play in front of Cheng Zhao twice.","After the estate guard Wenliang was drowned because of Helian Xuan, his parents and younger sister had taken refuge in the Prince of Andong's Mansion to smooth things over.","Now, it seemed that this girl had become a favorite around Dowager Consort An.","A pang of sadness welled up in Cheng Zhao's heart.","Lunch was quickly finished.","Zhou Yuanshen remained silent, and so did Cheng Zhao and Second Madam during the meal.","Zhou Yuanqi looked from one to another, neither understanding nor caring.","After the meal, they prepared to go down the mountain.","At the foot of the mountain, they unexpectedly met Helian Xuan and his mother again.","Helian Xuan stepped forward specifically to pay his respects. \"Lady of the State - protecting, Duke of Chen, and Duchess of Chen.\"","Cheng Zhao and the others returned the courtesy.","\"Does Your Highness have something to discuss?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Helian Xuan pointed to the \"senior maid\" Xian Xiang beside him. \"She wants to visit her younger sister in the Duke of Chen's Mansion.\"","\"This is inappropriate,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Helian Xuan smiled handsomely and recklessly. \"Even for a maid, if the host is kind - hearted, it should be okay for her to meet her sister. Moreover...\"","He looked at Cheng Zhao quietly. \"A beauty bestowed by the emperor is at least more honorable than a concubine. If the mistress agrees, it's acceptable for her to receive guests.\"","\"Your Highness, the Duke's Mansion is a traditional place. The Dowager is very particular about rules. As long as the Dowager is alive, the Zhou family has its own way of receiving guests, which involves sending a visiting card through the doorman and then getting a reply.","If the Prince's Mansion follows proper etiquette, you can first send a visiting card to our mansion. I'll show it to the Dowager. If the Dowager can't make a decision, she can ask His Majesty to decide.\" Cheng Zhao said.","She was reasonable and extremely imposing.","Second Madam enjoyed listening to her.","If Cheng Zhao disagreed, then it was a definite no.","When others tried to refute her rejection, she could come up with countless reasons.","She was smart - witted and quick - tongued.","The Prince of Andong clearly had \"ulterior motives\" in wanting his concubine to visit the Duke of Chen's Mansion.","Regardless of his purpose, the \"concubines\" in the Zhou family's inner courtyard did not receive guests. This rule was acceptable even among the noble families.","If the Prince of Andong was dissatisfied, then he could ask the emperor to make the decision.","Let's see if the Dowager's face carried more weight or the imperial bloodline was more solid.","\"Madam, you are magnanimous. Please let this servant go and see her younger sister. She is timid and cautious. Being in a strange place alone, I'm afraid she'll be scared.","I just want to see her for a moment. You can send a maid to accompany me, and I'll never do anything overstepping.\" Xian Xiang looked at Cheng Zhao and bowed gracefully.","Before Cheng Zhao could react, Second Madam was furious.","What a charming act!","Her younger sister, Xian Si, who had entered the Duke's Mansion, was even more beautiful and her manner was even more gentle.","Second Madam vented her anger by glaring fiercely at her own son.","Zhou Yuanshen looked at Cheng Zhao.","\"Your Highness, asking your concubine to plead is either looking down on yourself or looking down on me. So, in the future, when interacting with the Prince's Mansion, do we have to inform this lady first?\" Cheng Zhao asked Helian Xuan.","She stared at him and walked right up to him.","Helian Xuan: \"No...\"","\"If so, I'll spread the word so that all the titled ladies will know how to deal with the Prince's Mansion in the future.\" Cheng Zhao said.","Helian Xuan could no longer maintain his smiling face.","Dowager Consort An had been listening for a while.","She wanted to help, but found she had nothing to say. Cheng Zhao was sharp - tongued and every word hit the nail on the head.","\"Xian Xiang, step back. Don't be rude,\" Helian Xuan said after a moment of silence, making a compromise.","Then he said to Cheng Zhao, \"I've offended you, Duchess of Chen.\"","\"Your Highness is indeed well - mannered. It's obvious that the Dowager has taught you well,\" Cheng Zhao said.","In the end, she returned the words that the Dowager had said about her earlier.","Dowager Consort An's face turned ugly, but she couldn't lose her temper.","As a dowager consort with several titled ladies accompanying her, even if she suffered a setback in words, she couldn't damage her \"kind and gentle\" image.","These social graces were too important, so she had to endure it.","Although Cheng Zhao's words were sharp, her expression remained calm all the time, and every veiled meaning had a basis.","The titled ladies who had heard the Dowager's words earlier and saw Cheng Zhao's counter - attack couldn't help but applaud her.","\"If you don't provoke this Duchess of Chen and don't go against her, her temper can be quite satisfying.\"","\"In the future, we have to respect her even more. She's not someone to be trifled with.\"","On the other side, the Prince of Andong and his mother got into the carriage. The \"maids\" didn't follow the carriage.","After the carriage curtain was lowered, the Dowager's face darkened.","\"Mother, calm down,\" Helian Xuan comforted her.","The Dowager said, \"You spoil that concubine to please the emperor and even want to send her to the Duke of Chen's Mansion as a guest. As a result, we've been ridiculed! We've lost face.\"","She added, \"If Cheng Zhao had any regard for the past, she shouldn't have treated you like this.\"","Helian Xuan remained silent.","\"Back then, you even argued with me. Now you can see clearly, right? She's cold - hearted, and you can never warm her up,\" Dowager Consort An said.","\"Mother, that's all in the past,\" said the Prince of Andong.","Dowager Consort An was still angry. \"Disagreeing to your relationship with her was the wisest decision I've ever made. You'll understand later.\"","Helian Xuan was silent.","He turned his face to the other side, looking at the swaying shadow of the carriage curtain.","He didn't listen to a single word of what his mother was still saying.","Cheng Zhao had never forgotten him.","She was just angry.","She was so angry that she cared about him, which was why her words were so sharp.","He remembered that meditation room again, and how Cheng Zhao had refused Zhou Yuanshen...","Helian Xuan's heart was like a scorched and yellowed leaf, ready to shatter at the slightest touch.","The people from the Duke of Chen's Mansion also got into their carriages one after another.","Zhou Yuanqi didn't want to go home. \"I'm going to my maternal grandparents' house. My grandmother has left delicious food for me.\"","On his birthday, everyone in his maternal grandparents' house would prepare gifts for him.","\"I'll take you there,\" Second Madam said.","Then she said to Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen, \"You two go back first.\"","Zhou Yuanshen agreed.","Cheng Zhao still wanted to have dinner at her maternal grandparents' house. But she thought she needed to have a serious talk with Zhou Yuanshen.","He had confused her in the meditation room just now.","As a result, even after squeezing and mocking Dowager Consort An and Helian Xuan, she still couldn't relieve her anger.","She couldn't just admit defeat like this in a muddle - headed way.","\"Mother, the Duke and I will go back first,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She got into Zhou Yuanshen's carriage."]},{"number":185,"title":"Chapter 185: Apologies to Each Other","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen returned to the residence together.","He was silent, and Cheng Zhao sat in silence as well.","She wanted to reason with him, but could not find a thread to start. Every sentence she might say required her to think through what came after.","Zhou Yuanshen's problem was the first she had ever encountered in her teenage years. She was unpracticed \"here.\"","She could not control it.","While Cheng Zhao was lost in thought, time passed swiftly, and the carriage arrived back at the Duke of Chen's mansion.","Zhou Yuanshen descended from the carriage first; turning back, he saw Cheng Zhao. The maidservants were not nearby, so he reached out to help her down.","Cheng Zhao looked at him.","Their eyes met, and Zhou Yuanshen's gaze held no emotion whatsoever.","\"Get down, Duchess,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","She did not take his hand, and climbed down from the carriage on her own.","Once inside the gates of the mansion, a small oil-paper carriage waited at the entrance, prepared especially by the head steward.","This had not been the custom before.","The last time, the steward had asked Cheng Zhao, saying that when she and the Second Madam returned from outings, they had to wait for the small carriage, which was inconvenient.","Now that Cheng Zhao had gained authority, she did not even need to give orders—the steward would rack his brains to please her.","At the time, Huan Qingtang and a few of her confidants had looked displeased, yet they could not object.","Cheng Zhao stood beside the small carriage and asked Zhou Yuanshen, \"Will Your Grace go to the study, or return to Nonghua Courtyard?\"","A smile hung on her face.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"I'll go to Chenhui Courtyard; there are some trivial matters to attend to.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"Then I shall go back first.\"","The coarse-clothed servant woman who drove the carriage set down the stool, and Cheng Zhao stepped up onto it, sitting steadily.","As soon as she returned, the people at Nonghua Courtyard said to her, \"Go quickly and look in the side chamber.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"What is it?\"","She turned onto the western corridor and entered the side chamber, where she saw an entire row of lanterns laid out on the table.","About thirty or forty of them, each small and exquisitely crafted.","Cheng Zhao did not care for large lanterns; her favorites were the small ones that Liao's workshop had specially made years ago, each about the size of a little rabbit. Hung up, they looked brilliant without dimming the radiance of the moonlight.","Moonlight and lamplight could complement each other.","That time, it was her Second Sister-in-law who had bought them for her—twenty in total—and she had hung them all on the branches of the trees in the courtyard, truly beautiful.","Unfortunately, on the night of the Lantern Festival, it rained, and everyone in the house was sound asleep—no one noticed. The lanterns were soaked through, and by the following year they had grown moldy.","Cheng Zhao had felt great regret.","When she went to buy more, she learned that these lanterns did not sell well, so Liao's workshop had stopped producing them; they would have to be specially ordered. But the Liao workshop's business was booming, and ordered lanterns took over half a year to be made.","After that, Cheng Zhao had followed her parents to their post in Fengzhou.","She had not forgotten the matter entirely, but things she had liked in childhood, she was too lazy to seek out again.","Now she saw them once more.","She still liked them very much.","Half the size of an ordinary lantern, yet the craftsmanship was in no way sloppy—just as exquisite and ingenious.","\"...Who sent these?\" Cheng Zhao's eyes brightened slightly.","Qiubai smiled and said, \"His Grace had Nanfeng deliver them.\"","Cheng Zhao was momentarily startled.","\"His Grace bought them? How did he know?\"","Nanny Li said from the side, \"He probably went to ask the eldest young lady, or perhaps the third young lady. The two ladies always keep your preferences in mind.\"","Cheng Zhao's eldest sister and third sister remembered all of her likes and dislikes.","Cheng Zhao gently stroked the lanterns.","\"I loved them when I was little, and I love them now,\" she said softly.","Qiubai: \"Such delicate little things—people of any age would love them.\"","Cheng Zhao nodded.","Nanny Li then said, \"This time, don't hang them on the trees. Never mind rain—even dew will make them moldy. Hang them under the eaves instead.\"","She added, \"Take the lanterns under the eaves and hang them on the trees in the courtyard; swap them around.\"","Cheng Zhao agreed wholeheartedly: \"Very good.\"","She composed herself and said to Nanny Li, \"Make sour radish and duck soup tonight; I haven't had it in a long while.\"","Nanny Li: \"I wonder if the main kitchen has a fresh duck on hand. I'll go and check.\"","She hurried off.","Cheng Zhao then said to Qiubai, \"Go to Chenhui Courtyard and say that I'll wait for His Grace to dine with me this evening.\"","Remembering what had happened earlier, and fearing he might make an excuse to decline, Cheng Zhao added, \"Sour radish and duck soup tastes better the longer it's simmered. Tell His Grace not to hurry. He can take his time with his trivial matters.\"","Qiubai acknowledged and left, soon returning to tell Cheng Zhao, \"His Grace is in the outer study. He says he'll come once he's done.\"","Cheng Zhao breathed a sigh of relief.","She had not realized she was holding her heart up slightly.","With that settled, Cheng Zhao finally had leisure to admire the small lanterns.","She kept a few aside, intending to give them to her nephews, nieces, and nieces on the other side; she kept one for herself to play with. The rest were to be hung under the eaves.","It was not yet Mid-Autumn Festival, so she would not light them for now—just looking at them brought her joy.","The sun had not yet set, and the sour radish and duck soup had yet to reach its full flavor, when Zhou Yuanshen returned to Nonghua Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao stood under the eaves, a last sliver of sunset falling upon her skirts; her face was pale as jade, and even in the shadow of the eaves, she seemed to glow in the darkness.","\"The lanterns are beautiful,\" she said.","Zhou Yuanshen drew near.","He tilted his head up, gazing at them quietly: \"You used longer threads; hanging them this way does look better.\"","\"On Mid-Autumn night, I'll light them and open the doors and windows. With the glass beads on the door of my inner chamber as accompaniment, it will surely be lovely,\" Cheng Zhao said.","As she spoke, she stepped a little closer to him.","Zhou Yuanshen naturally leaned in as well, and before he knew it, his hand brushed against hers.","The skin of the back of her hand was a little cool, and instinctively he grasped it.","Cheng Zhao did not pull away; instead, she interlaced her fingers with his.","The couple stood there, gazing at the lanterns.","The sunset faded bit by bit, and by now there was little left to see in the lanterns—neither of them was truly looking.","\"The sour radish and duck soup still needs a while longer. Would Your Grace care for a game of chess?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanshen tightened his grip on her hand: \"No chess.\"","Cheng Zhao drew closer to him.","The husband and wife returned to their chamber.","Nanny Li had, the day before, brought in new bed curtains for Cheng Zhao, embroidered with the osmanthus blossoms of mid-autumn—orange and yellow, warm and softly bright.","The folding screen had not yet been taken away.","Cheng Zhao fell into his arms.","\"...Cheng Zhao, are we husband and wife?\" he asked. \"Not Duke of Chen and Duchess, but you and me.\"","Cheng Zhao hesitated.","She thought of Mu Jiang shut away in the family shrine, of the bed companions in Lijing Courtyard, of the new concubine in Yujin Courtyard, and her heart tightened slightly.","She wrapped her arms around Zhou Yuanshen's neck: \"I won't mistake you again.\"","She added, \"Twice now—last time I didn't see clearly, and this time I didn't recognize it was you. I've reflected on it. Whatever the reason, it was my fault.\"","Wrong was wrong.","When wrong, one corrects it.","Cheng Zhao's marriage was very good.","Like a fine bolt of cloth, tailored into a splendid garment. As for the thread and effort spent, and what scraps might be left over, those did not belong to the splendid robe of marriage.","When the garment was fine and noble, being wrong had no excuse.","Cheng Zhao looked at the lanterns and understood this.","\"I apologize to you, Your Grace.\" She gently kissed the corner of his mouth. \"Will you forgive me?\"","Zhou Yuanshen seemed to be waiting for a different answer.","But he, too, was skilled at compromise—like Cheng Zhao, casting the scraps aside.","He kissed her back with force.","\"Make me a sachet,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao: \"All right.\"","The bed curtains fell, burying them in shadow as they sank into each other.","That night, Cheng Zhao enjoyed a bowl of exceptionally savory pickled radish and duck soup. It had been simmering until the small hours of the night before being served, so the flavors had fully melded with just the right amount of heat."]},{"number":186,"title":"Chapter 186: Husband and Wife Reconciled","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao ate very well; Zhou Yuanshen and the sour radish duck soup both brought her equal delight.","She fell into a deep sleep.","When she rose early the next morning, Zhou Yuanshen had not yet left.","\"...No court session today. Later I need to go to the Eastern Palace to see the Crown Prince; and then to the General's Mansion to meet with Uncle.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","A maid helped him change clothes.","Cheng Zhao, fully dressed, came over to adjust his belt: \"Will you stay the night at the General's Mansion?\"","\"Not necessarily.\" He said.","He caught hold of Cheng Zhao's hand. \"I'll be staying at Chenhui Courtyard. You can come find me if anything comes up.\"","Cheng Zhao let him hold her hand.","After a long moment, she said, \"There's nothing major happening in the inner quarters. You needn't worry about these things.\"","Then, after a pause: \"I didn't ask you to stay at Chenhui Courtyard. The agreement of twice a month at Nonghua Courtyard was only a temporary measure. If you'd rather stay here, there's no harm in it.\"","Zhou Yuanshen looked at her deeply: \"You don't want me to stay at Chenhui Courtyard?\"","Cheng Zhao, paying no mind to the servants still present, leaned her head against his chest.","Zhou Yuanshen reached out and drew her into his embrace.","Suyue, who had just carried in a kettle of hot water, quickly withdrew; only Nanny Li remained in the inner chamber to await orders—and then Nanny Li also left.","Cheng Zhao pressed closer to him, seeming to let out a soft sigh: \"If only we were thirty years old.\"","\"What would that change?\"","\"By then we'd have children, and our position would be secure.\" Cheng Zhao said.","At that point, spending two nights a month together, discussing matters whenever they arose, respecting each other, without all this clinging—that would work.","They would each have their own duties, not interfering with each other.","\"It will happen, Cheng Zhao. We will have a secure position.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Then he asked again, \"Would you be willing to have me stay at Nonghua Courtyard on a permanent basis?\"","Last time, he was the one who said he would only sleep there two nights.","Now he couldn't very well swallow his own words—he needed a step down.","Cheng Zhao had already offered him a ladder by pressing close to him; yet he still had to fuss, insisting on asking outright. Couldn't he just shamelessly come by every day without making her say it?","\"Then you can't complain later that I've kept you trapped at Nonghua Courtyard,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"If you're not staying at the General's Mansion, it's better you come back to Nonghua Courtyard. It's at least more comfortable than Chenhui.\"","Zhou Yuanshen lowered his head and pecked her on the lips: \"Alright.\"","The corner of Cheng Zhao's mouth curved up: \"I need to do my hair now. I'll go to Mother's place for breakfast.\"","While having her hair combed, Cheng Zhao thought: this matter should be considered settled by now, shouldn't it?","She hadn't recognized him at the temple. Although she had a hundred reasons to excuse herself, considering the little lantern, Cheng Zhao decided to admit her fault.","She would take a step back.","She would also overturn the decision they had made out of pique.","They had to try again.","In moments of life and death, Cheng Zhao trusted Zhou Yuanshen greatly. Not because there was any romance between them, but because she trusted his sense of responsibility, his capability.","And, his integrity.","She knew that as long as he could kill the white wolf, he would ensure Cheng Zhao's safety.","\"Safety\"—that word was more dependable than affection, and Cheng Zhao was willing to stake her future on Zhou Yuanshen's ship.","\"Cheng Zhao, we've been married for nearly a year now, but truthfully, we haven't spent much time together,\" he said. \"Even when we go out, it's on business. Would you like to take a trip?\"","\"The two of us?\"","\"Why not? After Mid-Autumn, the weather will be clear and pleasant—neither hot nor cold. I'll find an excuse, and we'll go out for a fortnight or more.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"What kind of excuse could you come up with?\"","\"Seeking a physician?\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","\"Could also say we're going to worship Buddha. Head east—near the salt fields there's an island with a highly efficacious statue of Guanyin.\" He said it with a perfectly straight face.","Cheng Zhao: \"Well, let's see if I'm in the mood.\"","\"If not, no matter—we can wait until next spring,\" he said. \"I'm quite busy lately anyway.\"","Cheng Zhao let out a breath of relief.","\"Very well, let's plan it that way for now, and see when we can get this trip underway.\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","She found herself looking forward to it a little.","The couple finished washing up and went to Jiangyun Courtyard for breakfast.","Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi had returned quite late the previous night, and Zhou Yuanqi had simply stayed in Second Madam's warm room.","Second Master had left early in the morning for court; he rarely ate breakfast at home.","When he got up, Zhou Yuanqi was still wearing yesterday's sapphire-blue outer robe.","At his waist hung a sachet.","Zhou Yuanshen asked: \"What's that?\"","\"Ask me that when you don't already know.\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","\"Let me see.\" Zhou Yuanshen reached out his hand toward him.","Zhou Yuanqi refused: \"You'll get it dirty with your hands. You can look at it with your eyes just the same.\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"...\"","After the meal, Zhou Yuanqi was to go to school, and Zhou Yuanshen had to rush to the Eastern Palace.","The brothers left together.","They had only just reached the door when they heard Zhou Yuanqi cry out, \"Ah!\"","Then his young, sharp voice erupted in fury: \"Thief! Give it back!\"","Second Madam craned her neck out of curiosity: \"What's all the fuss about?\"","A maid went out to check.","She saw Zhou Yuanshen striding away swiftly—taking long, fast steps, even breaking into a run—while Zhou Yuanqi's little short legs simply couldn't catch up.","\"The Lord Duke snatched Fifth Young Master's sachet and bolted. Fifth Young Master couldn't catch him, so he shouted.\" The maid said.","Second Madam couldn't believe it. She hurried to the door to take a look.","Of course, she saw nothing; both sons had already run out of sight.","She shook her head with a laugh: \"Now that's a rare sight—her sons actually roughhousing with each other?\"","It was a pity they'd only started their quarrel after leaving the courtyard; Second Madam hadn't witnessed it with her own eyes, and she was rather regretful. She called the maid over to ask for details again and again.","Cheng Zhao likewise found it hard to imagine.","She was certain Zhou Yuanshen had merely walked briskly, not run—the maid must have exaggerated.","But snatching his younger brother's sachet... that was completely unexpected of him, even for Cheng Zhao.","So much so that when Cheng Zhao went to Chengming Hall to handle her duties, a smile lingered at the corner of her lips, barely suppressible.","The matron in charge of the storehouse asked her: \"Third Young Madam is in a good mood today?\"","The storehouse was managed by Huan Qingtang, but most of her underlings got along well with Cheng Zhao and were happy to chat with her.","Cheng Zhao used a casual excuse: \"I was just saying to Fifth Brother that we'd play some cuju this afternoon.\"","\"You know how to play cuju?\" The matron asked with a smile.","Cheng Zhao: \"How would I? Fifth Brother said he'd teach me. I found the idea rather amusing, so that's why I was smiling.\"","Then she added: \"Cuju is too hard to control; kicking the shuttlecock is better.\"","\"Young men of Fifth Young Master's age don't take to shuttlecock,\" the matron said. \"They think it doesn't look impressive enough.\"","Everyone laughed.","Cheng Zhao was in the middle of chatting and laughing with them over whether it was better to kick a shuttlecock or play cuju with a nine-year-old boy when Huan Qingtang finally arrived.","A gentle, sweet smile graced her face: \"What's got you all laughing so merrily?\"","Someone relayed the story to her.","Huan Qingtang said: \"Kicking the shuttlecock is more fun.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"I think so too.\"","With that, the topic was dropped, and they moved on to the day's duties.","The day passed uneventfully.","At noon, Cheng Zhao sent Qiubai to fetch a cuju ball and a shuttlecock, so they could get some exercise before supper—which would also help Fifth Brother eat a heartier meal.","At Jiangyun Courtyard, Second Master had already returned and was with Zhou Yuanqi and Cheng Zhao, the three of them discussing how to play cuju.","Not as a match—more like how you'd kick a shuttlecock.","They also had to set up a rule for winning and losing.","\"I'll go first.\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"Your hands have to be behind your back. If the ball touches them, you lose.\"","Second Master said with an easygoing smile: \"You two can use your hands if you need.\"","\"Father, you've got no faith in me.\" Zhou Yuanqi complained.","Second Madam laughed and said, “Close the courtyard gate so that no one from outside can see us, or they’ll laugh at us.” She added, “I want to kick the ball too.”","Cheng Zhao said, “Mother, the two of us are on the same team. Father is with Yuanqi on the other.”","When Zhou Yuanshen hurried back to the Jiangyun Courtyard for the evening meal, he happened to see Cheng Zhao balancing the cuju ball on her head.","“That’s called ‘Buddha’s Pearl on the Peak’ — well done, Zhao Zhao!” Second Madam cheered from the side.","Zhou Yuanshen: “...”"]},{"number":187,"title":"Chapter 187: Keep the Silver","paragraphs":["Second Madam spotted Zhou Yuanshen first.","She smiled and greeted him: \"Yuanshen, come here quickly.\"","Then she added with a laugh, \"You play cuju sometimes in the army too, don't you? Would you like to join?\"","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at Cheng Zhao, whose cheeks were flushed pink.","Her hair had been redone into a low bun, tight enough not to come undone; her temples were a little mussed from the ball, with a few strands of loose hair sticking to her face, making her look even more radiant than the flowers.","\"Who's winning?\" Zhou Yuanshen asked.","Cheng Zhao's ball dropped.","She quickly calculated: \"Mother and I won seven. Father and Yuanqi lost—mainly because Yuanqi was holding the team back.\"","Zhou Yuanqi shot her a glare: \"I'm a child.\"","People with overly sharp minds tended to be a bit clumsy with their hands and feet; on top of that, his legs were still short, which made things very inconvenient—he always played poorly.","He usually looked down on spear fighting, and naturally he also looked down on rougher sports like cuju and polo.","If it came to debating knowledge or guessing riddles, none of them could match him by a hair—everyone has their strengths and weaknesses; he excelled in plenty of areas.","Zhou Yuanqi comforted himself with that thought, but the sight of that brute made his anger flare again: \"Give me back my sachet!\"","Everyone turned to Zhou Yuanshen.","His expression didn't change in the slightest: \"What sachet?\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","Utterly shameless—he'd already forgotten, just like that, within a single day.","\"Father, he stole my sachet!\" Zhou Yuanqi turned to the old man.","Second Master wiped the sweat from his forehead, chuckling: \"I don't oversee the Court of Judicial Review—don't bring your case to me.\"","\"Mother...\" Zhou Yuanqi changed direction.","Second Madam: \"I can't do anything about this either. It's not like I could beat him, could I? Besides, I'm not the one who made that sachet.\"","She handed a spear to Zhou Yuanqi's hand.","Zhou Yuanqi looked at Cheng Zhao again.","Cheng Zhao suppressed a smile: \"Did the Lord Duke really snatch Fifth Brother's sachet?\"","\"Would I lie about it?\"","\"Just took a look,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"I was afraid it would get torn in the fuss—didn't take it.\"","\"Then give it back to him, and I'll make you a sachet and a pouch within ten days. I'll use different spices—won't be inferior to his.\" Cheng Zhao said.","Then she added, \"Yuanqi's is made a bit simple—suits a child, not the Lord Duke.\"","Only then did Zhou Yuanshen say: \"Mingyu was sent to your courtyard, given to your senior maid. I never asked for your things.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","Zhou Yuanshen took the ball. \"How do you keep score?\"","\"Every time the ball is kicked without touching the ground counts as one,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen: \"... That's too easy. In that case, I won't play—otherwise you'll only complain that it's unfair.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"You've got some nerve!\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"What are you putting up as a wager if you lose?\"","\"I'll give you ten thousand taels—mmph!\"","Before Zhou Yuanqi could finish, Cheng Zhao had already covered his mouth.","Just now, her mother-in-law had said Zhou Yuanshen and his men played cuju for fun in the army; and Zhou Yuanqi had only managed eight kicks in total just now. Beating him would be easy.","What a waste of ten thousand taels of silver.","Zhou Yuanqi's eyes went wide, and he shook off her hand.","Cheng Zhao spoke for him: \"If you win, the sachet matter is settled once and for all—Fifth Brother won't bring it up again.\"","Zhou Yuanshen gave no definitive answer, but he took the ball.","He kicked it with his knee, and the ball rose smoothly to his shoulder; then up over his head, before falling back down to be caught by his foot.","Second Madam kept calling out tricks from the side—things like \"immortal picking a peach\" and \"linked hook moon\"—Cheng Zhao couldn't understand them, and neither could Zhou Yuanqi.","But they both understood what they were seeing.","Once the ball was in Zhou Yuanshen's hands, it never touched the ground. He could keep heading the ball dozens of times in a row, and it showed no sign of dropping.","Zhou Yuanqi was dumbfounded.","\"Do you train martial arts in the army, or do you play ball?\" he asked.","Cheng Zhao whispered: \"You saved your ten thousand taels, didn't you? You almost gave it away for nothing.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","He murmured to Cheng Zhao, \"That brute is showing off! You should practice hard and beat him someday.\"","\"Why don't you win against him yourself?\"","\"I won't measure swords with a soldier—it would tarnish a scholar's reputation,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: \"Just admit you can't beat him—that doesn't tarnish any scholar's reputation either.\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"...\"","Everyone had worked up a sweat, including Zhou Yuanshen.","The maids brought water for them to wash their faces and hands.","Because of all that exercise, everyone was hungry at dinner and ate a little more than usual.","After the meal, Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen returned to Nonghua Courtyard.","\"You enjoy playing cuju?\" Zhou Yuanshen asked her.","Cheng Zhao: \"I have to try a bit of everything, for fear people might say I don't know how. But there are too many things, so I never master any of them.\"","Her energy was limited, and her obligations were many. Aside from running the household, she'd only scratched the surface of everything else.","She glanced at Zhou Yuanshen: \"Whereas you—whatever you're good at, you excel at it completely.\"","\"The frontier is rather dull—there's really only that sort of thing. Pitch-pot, archery, horseback riding, ball games,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","When stationed there, not every day was spent preparing for battle. On the contrary, most of the year was just standing guard.","So they had to find ways to pass the time.","Young men with hot blood and boundless energy naturally burned it off on those pastimes.","Now that he was back in the capital, he found that noble young masters played these games too—just more reservedly and elegantly than Zhou Yuanshen and his men did.","\"If you like cuju, next time I play with your little uncle, you can come and watch,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao said that would be fine.","When they returned to Nonghua Courtyard, they found that the Dowager Madame's senior maid had arrived.","The Dowager Madame wanted Zhou Yuanshen to go to Shou'an Courtyard.","\"So late?\" Zhou Yuanshen frowned. \"If Grandmother has nothing urgent, I'll go see her tomorrow morning.\"","\"My lord, the Dowager Madame instructed that you—\" The maid looked troubled, casting an imploring glance at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao then said, \"Go on, you should go. I have needlework to do anyway; I'll be up late.\"","She told the maid to wait at the door.","She had her own people change Zhou Yuanshen's clothes, swapping out the garments that were damp with sweat.","The night breeze in August wasn't cold, but still, it was wise to guard against catching a chill.","\"It's probably about Concubine Mu,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Last time, Sister-in-law suggested bringing Concubine Mu back, and it's right in time for the Mid-Autumn Festival.\"","Zhou Yuanshen: \"I'll go see.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"If Grandmother insists that she come back, just let her come back. Focus on the important matters—don't waste your energy quarreling with Grandmother over trivial things. If it's something else, we'll discuss it later.\"","She wanted Zhou Yuanshen to stay focused.","Focused on the path to power.","If he climbed to the top, Cheng Zhao reaped the benefits too—they were husband and wife, two crickets on the same string.","At other times, as long as it didn't sacrifice Cheng Zhao's own interests, Cheng Zhao felt compromise was acceptable.","Cheng Zhao had drawn her boundaries very clearly: Concubine Mu was a concubine—she didn't threaten Cheng Zhao's position, so they could coexist; but if Huan Qingtang wanted to be a co-wife through dual inheritance, that was absolutely out of the question.","So when it came to Concubine Mu, any compromise was fine; but on the matter of Huan Qingtang's dual inheritance, she would not yield a single step.","The Dowager Madame's \"blows\" were not gentle gusts of wind—behind her stood the emperor.","And the emperor was already irritated by Zhou Yuanshen's disobedience during the hunt.","\"Alright, I'll go take a look,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He changed and went out.","Cheng Zhao brought out her sewing basket, had the maids help her separate the thread, and called for Nanny Li to fetch some fine fabrics as she picked out materials to make a sachet for Zhou Yuanshen.","First a sachet, then a pouch."]},{"number":188,"title":"Chapter 188: Cheng Zhao Tried to Persuade Him to Stay","paragraphs":["After Zhou Yuanshen left, Cheng Zhao went to wash up, first bathing, then washing her hair.","Afterward, she sat on the heated kang by the window doing needlework, letting her hair air-dry as she waited for Zhou Yuanshen to return.","She picked out a lake-blue fabric and started making a sachet, planning to embroider orchids and orchid grass on it.","\"...If the matriarch orders Concubine Mu to come back and the Duke refuses, the matriarch will definitely let Xiansi out to cause trouble,\" Suyue said as she helped Cheng Zhao sort the embroidery threads.","Cheng Zhao stitched a few lines and said with a smile, \"Suyue, you have good insight.\"","\"Don't tease me, miss,\" Suyue said, a little embarrassed. \"Tell me—do you think what I said is right?\"","\"Very much so,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Suyue grew bolder: \"But once Concubine Mu returns, she'll surely bear a grudge against you. It'll be endless.\"","\"Remember when we went out to sea before? The waves kept coming, one after another, and even then you said, 'Endless, never-ending,'\" Cheng Zhao said.","Suyue said, \"I was about to get seasick then, and I just wished it would stop and the waves would calm.\"","\"But when a ship is at sea, the waves never truly calm. When one storm ends, the next begins. Old sailors never get seasick, and they never fear. Wishing for it to end and quiet down—that's impossible,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Unless you go ashore.","Unless you stay far from that stretch of sea.","\"I understand, young madam,\" Suyue said. \"But aren't you angry?\"","\"I'm not a goddess. Even if I get angry, will that stop the waves?\" Cheng Zhao said.","Suyue was speechless.","Qiubai and Nanny Li laughed from the side.","This time, Cheng Zhao's embroidery was more refined. She spent a good while just comparing color combinations, and when she stitched, she did so slowly.","The night had grown deep when Zhou Yuanshen returned from Shou'an Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao's eyes were tired from the needlework, so she put her things away.","Zhou Yuanshen picked up the piece and looked it over, saying, \"The color is lovely.\"","\"What kind of fragrance do you like?\" Cheng Zhao asked. \"Last time I made one for Fifth Brother, it was to refresh the mind.\"","\"What do you suggest?\"","\"These days there are dried tangerine peels and such. Add a bit of osmanthus—it suits the season, fresh and pleasant,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Then go with that.\"","Cheng Zhao acknowledged him.","Zhou Yuanshen went to bathe and emerged quickly from the washroom.","The couple lay down, and Cheng Zhao asked about the matters at Shou'an Courtyard.","\"Is it about Concubine Mu?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"Just as I expected,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Mother has been granted the title of First-Rank Lady, so the household's direction is shifting. Mother-in-law is bound to bring Concubine Mu back.\"","Zhou Yuanshen gave an \"mm\" in agreement.","Then he added, \"She also spoke of some matters concerning the Sun family of Qingyuan Marquis's household.\"","A steward from the Sun family had been beaten to death by the husband of Princess Qing'an, over the canal transport business.","The two families had been reaping enormous profits from the water channels in the Jinzhou section. That was the \"gray zone\" allowed to nobles beyond state interests—nobody reports it, so no one prosecutes it.","Many aristocratic households held such money-making ventures.","The two families had seemed quite amicable, so suddenly this had turned into a case of manslaughter.","\"...Princess Qing'an couldn't get the Court of Judicial Review to act, and the prince consort is still imprisoned. Memorials are flying into the imperial study like snowflakes,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","\"And what does the matriarch think?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","\"She told me to tell Uncle the matter should be shelved for now,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"The matriarch doesn't want the affair blown out of proportion?\"","\"Exactly.\"","Cheng Zhao could understand that line of thinking.","If this case were pushed too far, the hidden revenue in other places would also be dug up, damaging the interests of many families, making the Suns the target of everyone's wrath.","The matriarch didn't want her natal family to end up in the eye of the storm.","She wanted this settled quietly.","If not for the prefect of the capital meddling in the matter, the Sun family might not have reported it to the authorities—they would have settled it privately instead.","\"His Majesty summoned me specifically today to ask about the transport along the canal,\" Zhou Yuanshen added.","Cheng Zhao was surprised. \"...Has Grandmother already gone to the palace?\"","\"Yes.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"Does the Emperor also wish to calm things down?\"","\"One move affects the whole board—His Majesty fears trouble even more,\" said Zhou Yuanshen.","Cheng Zhao: \"It's still because of Grandmother. If it were any other family, this could have been blown out of proportion. They would certainly have seized the chance to execute a batch of people and purge the official ranks.\"","Zhou Yuanshen turned his face in the shadows to look at her.","\"Do you think that would work?\"","\"That's just how governance works. Leave it alone in peacetime, and nothing happens; but when something comes up, you seize the moment to patch up the old holes,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The history books she had read all did it that way.","Governing a great state is like cooking a small fish—it needs a slow fire. Stitch here, mend there. Cleaning up officialdom is never about wielding the axe on a grand scale.","Seize one incident, execute a group of people, inspire the people; and intimidate another group, forcing them to lie low and spit out what they've swallowed.","In this way, officialdom stays clean for at least three to five years.","That's enough. Repeat it again and again, and the dynasty can last a hundred years, even several hundred.","Treating the court is like managing the inner household—there is never a one-time, permanent solution. It's like vines: cut down one batch, and the next batch is already sprouting with fresh shoots.","Zhou Yuanshen reached out and pulled her into his arms. \"Cheng Zhao, you have deeper insight than most people.\"","Cheng Zhao nestled against him. \"Just book-learning.\"","\"You've managed the household extremely well—the stewards all respect you. That's not book-learning; that's real ability,\" he said.","Cheng Zhao smiled.","Zhou Yuanshen lowered his head and kissed her lips.","Cheng Zhao tangled her tongue with his, wanting very much to ask him—if Consort Mu came back, would he have to divide his nights between her courtyard and the Lijing Pavilion?","She didn't ask, because her breath was sealed by his. In that moment, there was nothing else to think of but the two of them.","Cheng Zhao clung to him tightly, her legs wrapped around his body, refusing to let go even as her muscles ached.","\"Zhou Yuanshen,\" she whispered.","Zhou Yuanshen replied, \"What?\"","\"You'll sleep in the Nonghua Courtyard for the rest of this month,\" she said.","As for next month—we'll see then.","\"All right,\" said Zhou Yuanshen.","The bed curtains swayed. Cheng Zhao lost her breath, yet still she didn't loosen her hold.","Zhou Yuanshen was pleasantly surprised and put in even more effort.","The next morning, the sky outside was bright. Nanny Li woke Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao was still deep in sleep—she was too tired, too drowsy.","\"Madam, we're about to be late for the Chenming Hall,\" Nanny Li said.","Cheng Zhao woke with a start.","She hurriedly drank a bowl of bird's nest porridge, washing up, combing her hair, and changing clothes in haste before rushing off to the Chenming Hall.","By the time she sat down, she still wasn't fully awake.","She was in a daze.","The voices of the stewards and Huan Qingtang drifted past her ears, and she listened half-heartedly.","It also helped that all the tasks in her charge were already properly arranged—there was nothing pressing to handle, so she could afford to slack off.","\"...For the Mid-Autumn Festival, the Grand Lady and the Second Grand Lady want vegetarian meals,\" said Nanny Qin from the main kitchen.","Cheng Zhao snapped fully awake.","She slowly turned to look at Nanny Qin. \"The Grand Lady never told me.\"","\"She mentioned it yesterday, Madam,\" Nanny Qin said coolly. \"The Grand Lady always eats vegetarian anyway—she just wants a whole table of vegetarian dishes made separately for the festival.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"We'll need a separate stove. But is there enough time?\"","\"There are pots in the main kitchen that are usually reserved for making vegetarian dishes for the Grand Lady. We can just switch them out temporarily,\" Nanny Qin said.","Cheng Zhao noticed how lightly she brushed it off and glanced at her without showing any reaction.","Huan Qingtang then told Cheng Zhao that the Grand Lady wanted to bring Concubine Mu back, and that they should have the gatehouse prepare a carriage and arrange for someone to go to the family shrine.","Cheng Zhao acknowledged the orders as well.","With these two matters, Cheng Zhao was now fully awake.","By mid-afternoon, once her tasks were done, her thoughts kept drifting back to the matter of the Grand Lady's vegetarian meal for the Mid-Autumn Festival.","The Grand Lady rarely observed vegetarian fasting during festivals. Why would she suddenly bring this up now?"]},{"number":189,"title":"Chapter 189: She Knew the Secret of Nonghua Residence","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao returned from Chenming Hall and went directly to her mother-in-law's Jiangyun Courtyard.","\"...Concubine Mu will be returning soon, so you need to stay calm,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam was initially annoyed; but after thinking it over, she said, \"It's not necessarily a bad thing. Given Mu Jiang's temperament, there's no way she'll ally with Xian Si.","They live close to each other, so they might even start fighting first. Both of them are the Emperor's people—let them tear each other apart. You'll have some peace.\"","Cheng Zhao smiled faintly.","\"Xian Si probably wouldn't dare to provoke Concubine Mu,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: \"If they don't want to stir things up, there are others who won't let them rest easy. Lady Huan is full of mischief.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"True.\"","\"Don't worry, we won't cause you any trouble on our end,\" Second Madam said. \"You've already won more than half the battle.\"","After a pause, she said, \"As for Ah Shen...\"","Second Madam was afraid Zhou Yuanshen might lose his composure.","Whether it was Xian Si, Huan Qingtang, or Mu Jiang, they were all beauties, and they lived close to his Chenhui Courtyard.","Late at night, with a beauty adding fragrance to his inkstone—and it being perfectly proper—why wouldn't he indulge?","Second Madam wanted very much to lock her son in Nonghua Courtyard, but she also feared that interfering too much might backfire.","\"The Duke is steadier than all of us, Mother. Don't worry,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam glanced at her with surprise: \"Can he be trusted?\"","Cheng Zhao was tempted to say that trustworthiness didn't matter as long as he didn't lose the battlefield.","Just as she was about to speak, someone knocked at the courtyard gate.","It was not yet dusk, and the sunlight fell on the gnarled branches of the courtyard trees, with a few yellow leaves on the path looking as though they had soaked up the sun, making the bluestone road feel warm and bright.","Second Madam looked over: \"Who's come?\"","The Second Master and his three sons always walked right in—who ever knocked?","Besides, the door wasn't locked.","Whoever knocked didn't push the door open either.","\"Let me go see,\" Nanny Fan said.","Soon, Cheng Zhao and Second Madam saw Madam Song, the First Madam.","Madam Song was wearing a rose-purple soft silk sleeveless jacket and a plain skirt, followed by a maidservant and a coarse-robe woman.","The woman carried a food box in her hand.","Cheng Zhao and Second Madam exchanged a glance, both baffled.","\"Sister-in-law, why have you come?\" Second Madam stood up, no smile on her face, practically blocking the threshold of the hall.","\"Sister-in-law, I've come to see you. I made some osmanthus cakes to share with you and the Duchess.\" Madam Song smiled.","Her smile looked genuine.","She added, \"Mother also said I should get out and about more instead of moping in my room. I haven't congratulated you yet—congratulations on receiving the First-Class Lady title.\"","Second Madam frowned.","Given all the past grievances, one after another, she truly didn't want to deal with Madam Song.","But then Madam Song invoked the Grand Lady.","The Grand Lady had permitted her to come to Jiangyun Courtyard. Since the family hadn't been divided and she was a widowed sister-in-law with a title, Second Madam couldn't afford to be rude.","After all, Second Madam was now a woman of status.","Status sometimes came with too many constraints. In the old days, she would have thrown Madam Song out.","\"First Aunt, how thoughtful of you,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile, subtly pulling Second Madam back two steps to let Madam Song in.","She then instructed the maidservants to serve tea.","Madam Song sat down, sipped her tea slowly, and seemed to find a topic.","\"...Cheng, I heard that the concubine is returning again. What a hardship for you, child,\" Madam Song said.","Cheng Zhao's expression didn't change: \"When we sent her away, it was only because her mind was muddled and she wandered off recklessly. The Duke and Grandmother feared she might come to harm. Now that she's recovered, it's only right for her to return—she is a concubine of the Zhou household.\"","“Even so, you’re still hurting inside.” Madam Song said.","She glanced at Cheng Zhao, then quickly lowered her gaze.","But Cheng Zhao caught the sharpness in her eyes, and that barely suppressed hatred.","“I want to help you, in my own way. I heard you keep a statue of the Child-Giving Guanyin in your side chamber? Let me copy sutras for you—you can offer them before the Buddha, and they’ll be more efficacious.” The First Madam said.","Many households kept Bodhisattva statues.","Cheng Zhao’s Child-Giving Guanyin wasn’t something she advertised, but she hadn’t gone out of her way to keep it secret either.","Yet the First Madam knew all about it.","Cheng Zhao never doubted that her own courtyard, Nonghua Court, leaked information—that wouldn’t make her lose her composure.","She merely smiled and said, “How could I let my aunt by marriage copy sutras for me? That would be disrespectful to you, and the Bodhisattva wouldn’t bless me.”","“We’re one family—no need for such formality.” The First Madam said.","The Second Madam interjected: “Sister-in-law, if you have a purpose, just say it outright. Such a grand gesture is too much for my daughter-in-law—you’d be taxing her good fortune.”","The First Madam: “...”","No matter how much of a troublemaker someone was, facing the Second Madam’s bluntness was enough to drive them mad.","The First Madam could have used any number of arguments, as she had before, invoking her status and rank to pressure others.","But when that approach failed, the Second Madam’s straight-talking words were like a knife to the chest.","The First Madam’s breath hitched: “Sister-in-law, your words are truly embarrassing.”","“I see you have nothing important. The hour is late—those who’ve gone to court or school will be returning soon, and it’s inconvenient for you to stay here. Nanny Fan, see the guest out.” The Second Madam said.","The First Madam couldn’t help herself—she stood up abruptly.","She strode toward the door.","After a few steps, as if remembering her mask of civility, she hesitated, trying to say something more. But the Second Madam added: “And don’t come again, sister-in-law. Your Qingfeng Court is so far away—all that walking just to visit will wear your legs thin.”","The First Madam nearly broke her composure: “You...”","She turned and left.","Cheng Zhao smiled at the scene.","The Second Madam took no pleasure in it. Seeing her sister-in-law reminded her of the past, and irritation bubbled up.","She said to her maids, “Send this food box back too—return it exactly as it was sent!”","Nanny Fan dispatched a quick-witted maid to return it to the First Madam.","“She came deliberately to provoke me. We’ve only just started having better days, and she won’t let it rest.” The Second Madam said, fuming.","Cheng Zhao soothed her: “Mother, don’t be upset.”","“She’s not selling pity, nor is she making snide remarks—I can’t figure out her intent.” The Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao: “She came to tell me she knows many things about Nonghua Court—for instance, my Child-Giving Guanyin.”","“Has she lost her mind? That statue was gifted to you by your mother—it went through the front gate with everyone’s knowledge. Who doesn’t know about it?” The Second Madam said angrily.","Cheng Zhao fell into thought.","Like the Second Madam, she was puzzled by Madam Song’s purpose.","If not for that jealous glare, Cheng Zhao might have thought she was just a fallen woman paying a social call.","That look...","“Mother, perhaps it was Sister-in-law who said something, stirring up Aunt.” Cheng Zhao said.","“That Lady Huan is truly meddlesome.” The Second Madam fumed. “She’s so sly and underhanded that we can’t catch her doing wrong, can’t deal with her.”","She added, “The ‘revenant spirits’ she controls—one after another—she sends them out with her ‘spells’ to do harm.”","Cheng Zhao laughed.","The Second Madam asked why she was laughing.","Cheng Zhao said, “The Duke also called them ghouls. His view coincides with Mother’s.”","“This is what Yuanshen told me—to be careful and not to be too soft-hearted,” said the Second Madam. “I am now the Lady of Zhenguo. If I fall into their trap, the loss would outweigh any gain. The Crown Prince’s side has always had its eye on me. If I were to be used by someone, the damage would be great.”","Cheng Zhao took her hand. “Mother, you are very vigilant, and you have always done well. It’s just that they deliberately toy with you, making it seem as if you were the one at fault.”","The Second Madam squeezed her hand in return.","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law said no more.","Soon, the Second Master, Zhou Yuanqi, and Zhou Yuanshen all returned.","Laughter and cheerful chatter immediately dispelled the eerie, ghostly air that Madam Song had brought in, and the Courtyard of Crimson Clouds returned to its warm and joyous atmosphere."]},{"number":190,"title":"Chapter 190: Cheng Zhao Guesses Right","paragraphs":["Before the Mid-Autumn Festival, Mu Jiang returned to the residence.","This time, she came back quietly, going straight to Li Jing Court.","The Dowager Madame did not, as she had in the past, summon the entire household to meet Mu Jiang—in former days, she probably would have.","However, the Dowager Madame did have Li Jing Court tidied up, adding several pieces of furniture, and assigned two people to attend to Mu Jiang.","Neither was a pretty maid this time. One was a stewardess from the Dowager Madame's own service, around forty years old, with considerable acumen; the other was a senior maid, experienced and worldly.","Cheng Zhao heard about it in Cheng Ming Hall.","\"The day after tomorrow is the festival. My stewards shall all rest for one day,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Besides giving them extra bonuses, she also granted them a full day off.","Huan Qingtang's expression soured: \"Sister-in-law, the Mid-Autumn Festival is a busy time.\"","\"All the tasks have been delegated. There won't be any trouble on my end. As for your side, Sister-in-law, you may manage it yourself—Grandmother hasn't asked me to meddle,\" Cheng Zhao said.","For a moment, the atmosphere in Cheng Ming Hall grew tense.","Those on Cheng Zhao's side stood by, listening with interest; those under Huan Qingtang, some disappointed and some fearful, kept their heads lowered in silence.","Huan Qingtang had no time to rearrange her staff's rest arrangements now.","In great noble families, there were ways to win people's loyalty during major festivals—Huan Qingtang might never have learned them. For instance, it never even occurred to her to give the stewards a \"day off\" for the Mid-Autumn Festival.","That was far more respectable than mere bonuses. It could win devotion and also demonstrate her insight and capability.","If Cheng Zhao had simply done the same or let the matter pass entirely, it would have ended there. But Cheng Zhao had to show off right in front of her.","Cheng Zhao's ambition was plain: to let every steward know that she was more capable and more suited to running the household than Huan Qingtang, so that they would all shift their loyalty to her.","That day, Huan Qingtang went back to rest after lunch. When she woke, her ribs ached.","She was startled, thinking she might be ill; then she realized—she had simply been fuming with rage.","She sat on her bed, silent for a long time.","On the fourteenth of the eighth month, Cheng Zhao divided her small lanterns into several portions, keeping eighteen for herself and giving the rest away as favors.","She sent them to her nephews and nieces, her maternal cousins' children, and also to Second Madam and Zhou Yuanqi.","For the remaining two, Cheng Zhao carried them into the palace.","She brought Second Madam along as well.","Second Madam was dressed in full ceremonial court robes. Even so, she still fumbled slightly with such elaborate attire.","\"...When did you request an audience?\" Second Madam asked her.","Cheng Zhao: \"Mother, didn't we agree last time to visit the Empress again before the festival? I made the request in advance.\"","Second Madam: \"I completely forgot.\"","\"Forgot even to pay respects to the Empress?\" Cheng Zhao leaned in and teased her in a low voice.","Second Madam lightly patted her hand: \"You rascal!\"","Cheng Zhao smiled.","Second Madam asked again: \"Are you simply going to greet her, or is there a purpose? Just to ingratiate yourselves?\"","\"I also want to see whether His Majesty will attend the Chen Duke's mansion banquet tomorrow night,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam was startled.","\"He's going again?\"","The Emperor had gone several times, and each visit had brought no good.","Second Madam was truly afraid.","\"Banquets usually don't observe vegetarian abstention. I checked the main kitchen's records—there's never been such a rule. And His Majesty will perform the Mid-Autumn sacrifices to heaven and earth, so he'll fast for three days. On the festival day itself, he still can't have meat,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Her voice was very low.","Second Madam stared at her in shock: \"You can deduce so much just from a small matter like 'vegetarian food'?\"","Cheng Zhao gave a faint smile: \"Anything out of the ordinary suggests something hidden. I'm just making wild guesses.\"","Not only the fasting, but also Mu Jiang.","Grandmother agreeing to bring Mu Jiang back at this time—that was also unusual.","Cheng Zhao thought again of Mu Jiang's child.","Mu Jiang was Zhou Yuanshen's woman, yet the child she had been pregnant with last time was definitely not Zhou Yuanshen's.","The Dowager Madame knew.","Mu Jiang was like a sacrificial offering, placed upon the altar. Add to that the vegetarian fast, and Cheng Zhao let her thoughts wander.","At any rate, it cost nothing to be cautious. Better to overthink than to be caught off guard.","Cheng Zhao feared she might sicken her mother-in-law, so she didn't share this guess with her.","\"You may well be right,\" Second Madam said.","\"The Empress will probably know as well. If our gifts please her, she might give us a subtle hint. Either way, just listen to what she says,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam grew more nervous: \"I won't be able to catch any subtle hints.\"","Cheng Zhao laughed.","\"I'm here,\" she said.","Second Madam: \"Thank heavens for you.\"","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law went to Kunning Palace and paid their full respects to the Empress.","Cheng Zhao presented her small lanterns.","As Qiubai had said, no matter how old people were, they still delighted in little trifles—they were full of charm.","The Empress had seen all kinds of lanterns, but she was still enchanted by this little one: \"It's lovely.\"","She examined it closely, sighing repeatedly: \"This is exquisite craftsmanship. Only the Liao family's workshop could make it; no other house could produce such a thing.\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"Your Majesty, those revolving lanterns—the Liao family's workshop also makes them the best.\"","\"Your Majesty, I do have many revolving lanterns. I've grown tired of them. Yours is far more amusing,\" the Empress said with a smile.","Second Madam sat nearby, thinking to herself how lonely the deep palace truly was.","It wasn't that the Empress liked small lanterns—she simply liked the little spark of novelty Cheng Zhao brought with her, something to dilute the lifeless stillness of the inner court.","For so many years, the Empress had held little authority, and the Emperor did not much favor her.","The Empress was fortunate—she had a powerful father who had secured her position; he had also secured for her son the position of Crown Prince.","Cheng Zhao chatted with the Empress about lanterns, while Second Madam drifted off into her own idle thoughts, half-listening, trusting that her daughter-in-law would handle the conversation.","\"...When the time comes in the future,\" the Empress said.","Second Madam snapped back to attention, pricking up her ears. Why did she mention the future?","She glanced at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao smiled: \"The heir apparent is still a sovereign, even if not yet enthroned. That would be too great an honor for my mother.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","This involved her?","What had she missed?","The Empress looked over, and Second Madam hurriedly smiled, saying nothing.","\"You mother-and-daughter-in-law are too honest,\" the Empress said. \"I am the same. I always end up at a disadvantage. But what seems a loss may be a blessing in disguise—not necessarily a bad thing.\"","She added: \"If the Crown Prince goes out and you two happen to meet him, tell him not to drink too much. Lately he's become too fond of wine, at such a young age.\"","Second Madam's heart jumped.","That was practically explicit, wasn't it?","After leaving Kunning Palace and boarding their own carriage, Second Madam relaxed her back and shoulders.","\"Does the Empress mean that the Emperor and the Crown Prince will both leave the palace for the festival?\" Second Madam asked.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","\"Zhaozhao, your guess was spot-on.\" There was a hint of admiration in Second Madam's eyes. \"That's wisdom.\"","Then she asked: \"What were you two talking about—about 'the future'?\"","Cheng Zhao laughed: \"The Empress said the Crown Prince wants to honor you as his adoptive mother. Did you not hear that?\"","Second Madam: !","She was stunned.","She truly hadn't heard—she had been too busy feeling sorry for the Empress at the time.","She'd likely been too tense, and when she tensed up, things slipped past her.","\"The Empress said, when the time comes in the future—after the Crown Prince ascends the throne and has real power—then they'll raise the matter again. Both mother and son are grateful that you saved the Crown Prince's life,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: \"That won't do.\"","\"What the future brings, no one can say.\" Cheng Zhao smiled. \"The Empress only mentioned it in passing. There's no saying we might not come into conflict with the Guo family—who knows whether we'll end up at odds. In that case, the Crown Prince would surely side with his maternal relatives, and all this would quietly come to nothing.\"","Second Madam agreed wholeheartedly.","The mother-in-law and daughter-in-law then returned together."]},{"number":191,"title":"Chapter 191: She Wants to Take a Desperate Gamble","paragraphs":["In the dead of night, Mu Jiang couldn't sleep.","The weather was fine—not a wisp of cloud in the night sky. A bright moon hung high beyond the back window, casting snowy moonlight across the window lattice.","Mu Jiang dragged a chair over, sat down beneath the back window, and watched the tree branches sway with the moon shadows.","Her mind kept circling back to what Nanny Sun had said to her when she'd first gone to the ancestral hall.","“...Such a pity. He should have been a crown prince.”","Those words rang out crystal clear.","Mu Jiang had revisited them countless times. Between the Grand Lady's attitude, Third Brother's behavior, and the emperor's murderous intent toward the imperial physician, she finally understood.","She had thrown up more than once.","Really thrown up—body and soul defiled, making her retch until she nearly brought up yellow bile.","She had wept bitterly, too.","She had even tried to end her own life.","Mu Jiang was no ordinary adopted daughter. She had lived in the Grand Lady's warm chamber well into her teens—she was the most honored young lady of the entire Duke of Chen's mansion.","Her clothing, food, and everyday expenses were modeled after a princess.","Before Zhou Yuanshen inherited the title, Mu Jiang had already set her sights on him. She thought he was worthy of her. He was handsome, silent, tall, and capable. Even if he never got the dukedom, the emperor and the Grand Lady would give Mu Jiang a generous dowry so they could live well together.","Then the family's heirs all died, and Zhou Yuanshen became the Duke of Chen.","Mu Jiang still remembered that night. She'd been so excited she didn't sleep a wink.","She kept saying, \"My judgment is really good.\"","The man she'd chosen had risen through adversity to gain power and status, and they would share in the glory together.","She expressed her feelings to him.","He frowned, dodged her simply and bluntly; from then on, Mu Jiang could hardly ever get near him. When he wanted to slip away, he was as smooth as an eel—impossible to catch.","But in Mu Jiang's heart, he already belonged to her.","She went to ask the Grand Lady whether they could be betrothed. The Grand Lady said at the time: \"Of course not. If you like Yuanshen, I'll let you be his concubine.\"","Mu Jiang thought the Grand Lady was joking.","She was Mu Jiang, the orphan girl the emperor had picked up—she was like a princess.","And she had been raised personally by the Grand Lady.","What elite family couldn't she marry into? Why become a concubine? It was absurd.","\"Then I'd rather marry into some outside family,\" Mu Jiang said petulantly.","The Grand Lady smiled faintly. \"Childish talk. A-Jiang, your grandmother has always doted on you. You wouldn't force me to use your deed of indenture to keep you in line, would you?\"","Deed of indenture.","Two words, and all of Mu Jiang's vanity shattered into nothing.","She stared at the Grand Lady in disbelief.","Wasn't she like a princess? How could she still have a deed of indenture?","That was when Mu Jiang understood: she was merely an orphan girl the Grand Lady had raised. If she was obedient, the Grand Lady would hold her up high; if she wasn't, she had a deed that could be sold at will.","Accepting this wasn't easy.","Mu Jiang compromised and moved out of the Grand Lady's Shou'an Courtyard to Lijing Yard.","Her treatment didn't change much. She was still the most honored young lady of the Zhou family—all the best food and finest things went to her first.","The First Lady and Second Lady both had to pamper and revere her like a precious daughter.","A girl raised in a honey pot all her life had little backbone and no spine. Mu Jiang simply accepted it.","Nanny Sun once told her: \"The proper wife of the Duke's mansion must come from a distinguished family. Her natal clan needs influence to support the Zhou family in mutual alliance—that's the purpose of marriage.\"","The man she'd set her heart on would marry some lady of noble birth.","Mu Jiang thought: among all the fine ladies in the capital, how many lived better than she did?","But that wasn't right.","She hardly knew any aristocratic women of high rank. She was so honored, and everyone in the mansion fawned over her—yet few people outside sent her invitations to visit.","So...","Did all the great families know perfectly well that she was just an \"orphan girl\"?","She could eat well and drink well, but she bore no bloodline of a great house, and there was no family behind her. So she had no value worth cultivating.","Perhaps that was exactly what the Grand Lady had put out word about.","Mu Jiang should have woken up then.","But she didn't.","A person won't turn back until they've reached the edge of the cliff. Mu Jiang still held onto a great deal of hope—she still had a brighter future ahead.","Until she reached this point.","Has she resigned herself now?","Mu Jiang pressed a hand to her chest. No, she hadn't.","She had once carried a \"crown prince\" in her womb. She could walk a wider, broader road.","Once she succeeded, she would vent every ounce of her anger.","The Grand Lady who deceived her, Cheng Zhao who humiliated her, and cold-hearted Zhou Yuanshen—they were all her enemies.","She would make them wish they were dead!","\"Madam, aren't you asleep yet?\" asked the maid on night duty.","Moonlight filled the room. Seeing her sitting there, the maid fetched an outer robe and came over, draping it over her shoulders.","Mu Jiang couldn't be bothered to look at her.","\"I'm going to sleep,\" she said.","\"Tomorrow morning you're to go to Shou'an Courtyard for breakfast and accompany the Grand Lady in chanting sutras. I'll call you then. Please rest easy,\" the maid said.","Mu Jiang lay down quietly and said only after a long pause: \"No need to call me tomorrow. I won't go to Shou'an Courtyard. Tell the Grand Lady for me.\"","The maid was briefly startled.","Then she acknowledged the order, not daring to argue.","Mu Jiang closed her eyes.","She wouldn't be living in this courtyard for much longer. Soon, she would move into the palace.","Even if it disgusted her to serve the man she'd always thought of as her \"father,\" she could do it.","Once she gained power, her days of glory would begin.","Actually, thinking about it, this path was easier to walk.","If she had another son in the future, and with the Zhou family's support her son became crown prince and then emperor, she would be the Empress Dowager.","And then, Zhou Yuanshen would probably love her even more, wouldn't he?","Mu Jiang fell asleep.","During the day of the Mid-Autumn Festival, the Duke of Chen's mansion had nothing pressing to do—all the preparations had long been finished. No business was conducted that day. Cheng Zhao's stewards were all given leave, while Huan Qingtang's stewards were on duty; some sat down and immediately began making snide remarks, insinuating that Huan Qingtang was incompetent.","At noon, a table of food and wine was laid out in Jiangyun Yard.","The second branch of the family celebrated the festival a day early.","Cheng Zhao drank several cups with her in-laws and Zhou Yuanshen, and her cheeks instantly flushed pink, a glow spreading from her forehead down to her neck.","\"Your tolerance for liquor!\" the Second Lady was startled. \"Are you dizzy? Quick, go brew some hangover soup.\"","\"Not too dizzy—it just shows on my face too easily,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanqi also drank a few cups of sweet wine, which was low in alcohol. He felt nothing and teased Cheng Zhao for being useless with drink.","\"I had no idea you were this bad with alcohol,\" the Second Lady laughed. \"Next time, I won't dare urge you to drink.\"","Cheng Zhao looked tipsy, but her spirits seemed fine.","After lunch, she and Zhou Yuanshen returned to Nonghua Yard.","Zhou Yuanshen touched her cheek—it was hot and soft. He whispered, \"Want to take a soak in the bath?\"","Cheng Zhao: \"Don't you dare think of tormenting me. I'm already drunk.\"","\"Only you soaking—I don't have time to keep you company. I still need to go out for a bit. Something's come up,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","Zhou Yuanshen dropped a kiss on her lips. \"If you ask me to stay, I'll stay.\"","Cheng Zhao laughed in exasperation.","She pushed him out.","\"No matter what happens tonight, don't be afraid,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao understood what he meant. \"I'm not afraid.\"","Only then did Zhou Yuanshen leave.","Cheng Zhao didn't take a bath. She curled up on the couch and fell asleep.","That sleep was deep and pleasant. When Cheng Zhao woke up, it was mid-afternoon, and she felt utterly refreshed.","A hint of rosy color still lingered on her cheeks.","As Suyue applied her makeup, she remarked that the skin beneath the powder was naturally flushed, prettier than any rouge.","Cheng Zhao dressed up in fresh attire and went to the Scarlet Cloud Court, where she joined her mother-in-law to head together to the Night Banquet at the Leaning Sunset Pavilion."]},{"number":192,"title":"Chapter 192: Thinking of Her","paragraphs":["Second Madam led Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanqi to Yixia Pavilion.","The Yixia Pavilion of the Duke of Chen's estate was reserved for banquets.","All the elder members of the clan had arrived.","The entire Zhou clan revered the ducal household above all, and they gathered around the Dowager Madame, offering words of good fortune.","Huan Qingtang sat beside the Dowager Madame. Not formally seated, just keeping her company in conversation.","Mu Jiang, curiously, was absent.","\"Second daughter-in-law, now that you hold the rank of First-Class Lady of Honor, you may sit at the lower end,\" the Dowager Madame indicated a seat.","Second Madam felt the words carried a sting of mockery.","She used to miss such barbs, but now she simply chose not to dwell on them. She replied, \"Yes.\"","Cheng Zhao took her seat beside Second Madam.","Zhou Yuanshen had not yet arrived.","The Dowager Madame asked, \"Where is the Lord Duke?\"","\"The Lord Duke went out this afternoon and will return shortly. He won't delay our moon-viewing, Grandmother,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"He should be back by now,\" the Dowager Madame said. \"Someone, go to the gate and inquire where the Lord Duke went this afternoon.\"","No sooner had she spoken than Zhou Yuanshen returned.","He had changed into a brand-new dark robe embroidered with gold cloud patterns—dignified yet noble—and walked in slowly.","Everyone looked at him.","Cheng Zhao looked at him too.","Lanterns hung everywhere throughout the Duke of Chen's estate.","The weather was fine, and the moonlight made the courtyard bright as day, with the glow of the lanterns adding to the splendor and brilliance.","He seemed to stand in the midst of that light, as radiant as the lights themselves.","Cheng Zhao averted her gaze, wiping the brilliance from her eyes. She could not afford to drown in things too dazzling, for she would forget the path she had to walk.","\"Lord Duke.\"","The crowd greeted him, rising to their feet one after another.","He was the head of the household.","And he carried that role well—every step steady, with the gravity befitting a family's master.","\"Grandmother,\" he said as he stepped forward. \"Everything outside is arranged. I've brought in some men from the general's mansion, so you may rest easy.\"","No one quite understood what he meant.","The Second Dowager—Zhou Yuanshen's great-aunt by marriage—smiled and asked, \"Lord Duke, why would you summon men from the general's mansion?\"","The Dowager Madame's expression tightened slightly.","Cheng Zhao observed quietly: The possibility that the Emperor might visit the Zhou household for the Mid-Autumn Festival was information that should have been known only to the Dowager Madame alone.","Apart from Cheng Zhao's own unauthorized deduction, everyone else was kept in the dark.","This was the Dowager Madame's exclusive piece of intelligence.","Sometimes, secrets were power. When a secret belonged to her alone, she remained the true hand of authority within the family.","Now, Zhou Yuanshen knew it too.","Whether the Emperor had tipped him off or he had sensed it himself, he had broken the Dowager Madame's monopoly.","Within this household, power was slowly slipping from the Dowager Madame's grasp.","The Dowager Madame's face soured at once, and everyone felt the tension thicken, though they did not understand why, glancing from one person to another.","\"The Mid-Autumn Festival is a festive occasion—extra hands are welcome. The Lord Duke has been thoughtful,\" the Dowager Madame said. Then she added, \"Take your seat.\"","Huan Qingtang rose with a smile. \"Grandmother, I'll take my seat as well. This is the Lord Duke's place.\"","\"Grandmother, let the elders be seated first. There's a spot for me among the Cheng family. This is a family banquet, so let us sit according to age,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","He had openly contradicted Huan Qingtang, making his stance clear.","Between him and Huan Qingtang, there could never be anything more.","This was not disrespect toward his widowed sister-in-law, but rather a clarification of any ambiguous relations.","Huan Qingtang's expression stiffened for a moment.","After offering his respects again, Zhou Yuanshen took his seat beside his mother.","First Madam, née Song, had also arrived.","One by one, everyone settled into their seats, and the opera on the stage began. Fireworks exploded across the distant night sky, and all over the city, displays of light added to the bustling merriment.","\"...Has your headache cleared?\" Zhou Yuanshen leaned in to ask Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao replied, \"It's passed now.\"","\"Give me your hand,\" he said.","She gave him a startled look, as if she hadn't heard correctly.","He said again, \"Hold out your hand to me.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","At this night's feast, over a hundred members of the clan had gathered, all of Zhou blood, and their seats were near the front—countless eyes were on them.","Though Cheng Zhao felt it was highly improper, she still extended her hand.","He handed her an ornate tassel.","It was shaped like a little rabbit, woven from colorful threads—delicate, pretty, and novel.","\"It's lovely,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"I passed by a street stall and saw a peddler selling these. One look and it seemed so bright and cheerful, I thought you'd like it,\" he said.","Warmth spread through Cheng Zhao's heart.","\"Thank you,\" she said softly.","Zhou Yuanshen had already straightened back up in his seat.","Second Madam had leaned over to peek at them while they were whispering.","She saw it and laughed, \"Yuanzhen takes after his father. Even to this day, his father will pick up any amusing trinket or tasty snack he finds on the street and bring it home.\"","Second Madam had an easygoing nature; she never cared whether a gift was costly or even palatable.","Whatever Second Master brought home, she was delighted, cherishing simply the warmth of being thought of.","Cheng Zhao pressed her lips into a smile and fastened the tassel to the front of her robe.","Her garment was made of light-colored fabric with understated silver-thread patterns—quietly luxurious—and it paired perfectly with a brightly colored tassel.","\"Very pretty,\" Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao liked it a great deal too.","Mu Jiang was the last to arrive.","She was as flamboyant as ever, in a flame-red dress with ruby-adorned headpieces, her jewels glittering.","Yet her cheeks were slightly hollowed, and even with powder, she looked a little haggard mind.","\"The Secondary Wife has arrived,\" the Second Dowager said with a welcoming smile.","Mu Jiang stepped forward to pay her respects to the Dowager Madame, then found a seat and settled in quietly, without making a scene.","\"I heard from the Lady Huan that quite a few stewards have been given leave today,\" the Second Dowager began with a smile.","Someone asked curiously, \"Why is that? Is something auspicious happening?\"","Cheng Zhao looked up briefly.","The Dowager Madame smiled. \"This is a kindness granted by Lady Cheng. On festival days, she lets the stewards enjoy the holiday as well.\"","\"Strictly speaking, servants attending their masters shouldn't need holidays—loyalty should come first. Though the Duchess's consideration for human bonds is kind, it may also put the stewards in an awkward spot,\" the Second Dowager laughed.","Cheng Zhao did not reply.","The Dowager Madame said with a smile, \"The young have their own ideas. She is a young wife, and the stewards are all veterans—it's fitting for her to extend this grace.\"","\"The Dowager Madame is lenient, both in showing compassion to servants and in giving the younger generation room to learn,\" the Second Dowager praised.","After all that, they had circled back around to complimenting the Dowager Madame.","Second Madam was seething.","This time, she did not swallow her anger as before. She spoke up: \"Aunt, with all the Mid-Autumn arrangements handled properly, there's no need for so many stewards about. Lady Cheng has real skill in managing the household—even the Dowager Madame has praised her for it.\"","The Second Dowager paused, then smiled. \"Well said.\"","The Dowager Madame said nothing.","After speaking, Second Madam felt a twinge of uncertainty, seeing that no one rebuffed her. She looked at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao smiled with encouragement and gratitude: \"Thank you for your trust, Mother.\"","Mu Jiang could not help but let out a cold snort from the side; First Madam thought to herself that there would surely be entertainment tonight—someone had deliberately provoked Second Madam into saying such things, all to set Cheng Zhao up for a fall.","Huan Qingtang sat quietly nearby.","Cheng Zhao was grateful for Second Madam's defense, and Huan Qingtang was equally grateful to Second Madam.","Once the chaos broke out, let Huan Qingtang's stewards see whose plans were superior, and whose were nothing but empty gestures to buy goodwill.","The tide would turn soon enough."]},{"number":193,"title":"Chapter 193: Cheng Zhaoxian Strikes First","paragraphs":["The night banquet at the Duke of Chen's estate was lively.","Laughter and cheerful voices filled the air.","It was still early, and the main feast had not yet begun. Cheng Zhao rose and said to Zhou Yuanqi, \"Fifth brother, come here. I have something to tell you.\"","Zhou Yuanqi obediently got up.","The sister-in-law and brother-in-law walked to the entrance of Yixia Pavilion, then veered off a little further. Cheng Zhao lowered her voice and said to him, \"Can you do me a favor later?\"","Zhou Yuanqi: \"You want to stir up trouble?\"","\"Strike first to avoid being caught on the back foot,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"Your heart has turned black, and that gives your hand more power, like black iron. Black iron is black, after all.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...Is that how scholars give compliments?\"","Zhou Yuanqi said that scholars were above flattery, and he was simply speaking the truth.","\"What do you want me to do?\" he asked.","Cheng Zhao explained it to him in detail.","In short, he needed to act like a little child.","Zhou Yuanqi frowned as he listened, then sighed: \"Using a cleaver to kill a chicken. A lifetime of wisdom, ruined by a woman's hand. Fine, I'll help you.\"","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","A nine-year-old lamenting the ruin of his lifelong wisdom—it was rather amusing.","\"I'm more reliable than a brute,\" he added.","Cheng Zhao nodded in agreement and praised Zhou Yuanqi a little more.","Zhou Yuanqi felt her praise was a bit stiff and formulaic, but flattery was like honey—taking a taste cost nothing, so he was happy to swallow it.","He ran off.","\"Have Nanfeng and Qiubai help you,\" Cheng Zhao called after him.","She then called Qiubai over and told her to lend a hand.","Qiubai acknowledged the order.","With that small matter arranged, Cheng Zhao returned to her seat.","Second Master had come over, and Second Madam was speaking with him in low tones; Zhou Yuanshen was being pulled aside by an elder from the clan, who was bringing up the topic of \"drainage for the sacrificial fields,\" trying to steer the conversation toward irrigation canals. Zhou Yuanshen kept deflecting.","No one noticed that Zhou Yuanqi had disappeared.","Zhou Yuanshen only asked Cheng Zhao: \"What did you go out for?\"","\"Just checking around, making sure nothing was out of place. You heard me earlier—I gave half the stewards the night off. If anything goes wrong, it'll be called my arrogance and mismanagement,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Nothing major will happen,\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao nodded.","Only then did Zhou Yuanshen notice that Zhou Yuanqi's seat was empty. He asked quietly, \"Where's Yuanqi?\"","His younger brother was the most strict and proper of them all—on festival days, he never ran off to play, always sitting properly in his place with manners stricter than any old tutor's.","\"I've asked him to play a little prank,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile. \"Better safe than sorry. If nothing happens, then I was just being paranoid.\"","As for the consequences, Cheng Zhao felt she could handle them now. Her wings had grown stronger, and she could deal with most unexpected situations.","\"The Duke need not worry,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen said nothing more.","In the middle of the evening, Zhou Yuanqi returned.","Cheng Zhao went out again and met with Qiubai and Nanfeng, going through the situation once more.","Huan Qingtang, Eldest Madam Song, and Mu Jiang all noticed her coming and going; the Grand Madam had noticed as well.","\"She's that nervous?\" Song thought to herself.","Eldest Madam Song was more tormented than the others, because with the change in status, the looks the clansmen gave her had changed too.","She had been forced to move out of Chengming Hall.","She had become a true widow. Without Chengming Hall, her title of honored lady couldn't keep the clansmen's respect.","The Zhou family were snobs—they knew very well which way the wind blew.","If Cheng Zhao died, then Huan Qingtang would have no right to take charge of Chengming Hall, because she wasn't the Duke's wife. Song could then come back.","Eldest Madam Song glanced at Cheng Zhao once more.","Mu Jiang had returned to the estate, and there was also the new concubine Xian Si—the time for Eldest Madam's comeback had arrived.","But not tonight.","Tonight was too rushed. She wasn't ready.","So tonight, she would have to endure some cold treatment. She simply kept her eyes fixed on Cheng Zhao and Mu Jiang.","\"Mother, the Duke's new concubine—why hasn't she been called over to the table? Tonight is the Mid-Autumn Festival, a family reunion,\" Eldest Madam Song said with a smile.","Grand Madam: \"Yes, why did we forget her?\"","She called Huan Qingtang forward to ask.","Huan Qingtang said, \"That matter falls under Sister-in-law's jurisdiction. I wouldn't dare meddle.\"","Cheng Zhao stepped forward, still smiling, and said, \"Grandmother, the new concubine has just arrived. Such a grand occasion might frighten her.\"","\"We could also grace her with a reward,\" Grand Madam said with a smile. \"Cheng, you're still young—don't be too harsh as the mistress of the house.\"","Cheng Zhao's smile didn't waver.","She was about to say more when Zhou Yuanshen cut in: \"Grandmother, Cheng is indeed too young. She was only following my orders. It was I who decided the newcomer shouldn't appear at the festival.\"","\"You...\"","\"On behalf of Cheng, send a lantern and a few delicacies to Yujin Courtyard,\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"That's hardly neglect.\"","He added, \"The clansmen are all here, and it's only proper that a concubine doesn't join the table. Though Grandmother has grown lax with such rules lately.\"","Grand Madam's face darkened slightly.","Before grandmother and grandson could continue, the head steward hurried in and whispered something into Grand Madam's ear.","Her expression brightened, and her smile became natural and genuine. \"I'll go see.\"","She reached toward Zhou Yuanshen. \"Duke, come with me.\"","The two of them left.","The clansmen exchanged glances, not knowing what was happening.","A moment later, the Emperor and the Crown Prince, dressed in plain clothes, entered Yixia Pavilion.","Led by Second Master, the crowd rose from their seats in a flurry and knelt, shouting, \"Long live Your Majesty.\"","Eldest Madam Song was startled.","She still remembered the last time the Emperor came to the Zhou estate and had a physician executed. She trembled. Huan Qingtang, kneeling, kept darting glances at the moving figure, her brow slightly furrowed.","Mu Jiang shuddered, felt a wave of nausea, and forced it down.","She lowered her head, constantly adjusting her expression until a hint of tears glistened in her eyes, making herself look pitiful and delicate.","\"No, I'll leave tonight!\" Mu Jiang hardened her resolve. \"I can go into the palace with him. I never want to see that old hag's face again.\"","She had to find a way.","She would speak to the Emperor.","If she just said the word, she could leave the Zhou estate. It would be rushed, but better than being humiliated. She could figure out her next step later.","She was young and beautiful.","\"Today is a family banquet. No need for such formalities—rise,\" the Emperor said in a gentle voice.","The crowd still kowtowed before slowly getting to their feet.","Grand Madam said with a smile, \"Everyone, take your seats.\"","She yielded the seat of honor to the Emperor, seating herself and the Crown Prince in the seats on either side.","Once the Crown Prince was seated, he bowed to the Emperor. \"Father, I have brought a gift for the Lady of the State.\"","Second Madam froze.","The Emperor called her forward. Suppressing the unease in her heart, she walked up and knelt.","A eunuch disguised as an attendant behind her held out a small box, presenting it respectfully to the Crown Prince.","The Crown Prince opened it and showed it to the Emperor.","It was a pair of golden rabbits, crafted by the imperial workshop, with rubies set as their eyes—charming and full of life. Quite delightful.","\"I wish to bestow this upon the Lady of the State,\" the Crown Prince said.","The Emperor nodded.","The eunuch took the box and gave it to Second Madam.","Seeing the trinket, she couldn't help but recall how her son Zhou Yuanqi had once had a five-catty golden pig made from pure gold, and a smile of warmth and affection crept unbidden across her face.","She looked up at the Crown Prince. \"Thank you, Your Highness.\"","The Crown Prince looked as if he had received some tremendous encouragement.","Perhaps Second Madam's expression led him to believe his gift had been a great success.","He seemed rather pleased.","Second Madam took the small box, bowed once more, and then settled back into her seat."]},{"number":194,"title":"Chapter 194: Give Her Twenty Lashes","paragraphs":["At the Mid-Autumn Festival banquet, the Zhou family did not set off fireworks.","The Crown Prince still disliked fireworks.","Yet the lanterns were brilliant, the opera lively; the food was delicious, and the vegetarian dishes added last minute were fine and thoughtful. The festive atmosphere was thick.","But everyone was tense.","Whether clan members or the ducal household staff, no one dared relax.","With the Emperor and the Crown Prince seated there, who would dare cause trouble?","The vegetarian feast was prepared solely for the Emperor and the Dowager Madame.","There was a food-tasting eunuch, dressed as an attendant, who remained by the Emperor's side throughout, sampling his dishes and serving him.","Huan Qingtang had calmed herself, but her gaze kept flickering to the food-tasting eunuch beside the Emperor.","The Emperor seemed hungry; tonight's vegetarian dishes suited his taste well.","“......Whose kitchen is this vegetarian fare from? It rivals the head chef at Jin'an Temple,” Huan Qingtang heard the Emperor say.","The Emperor did not deliberately lower his voice. His conversation with the Dowager Madame was audible from where Huan Qingtang sat.","The Dowager Madame smiled and said, “We do have a few cooks in the household skilled in vegetarian cuisine.”","“Very good. My mother-in-law practices vegetarianism and deserves good cooks. My lifelong wish is that you enjoy a comfortable life,” said the Emperor.","“All by Your Majesty’s grace,” the Dowager Madame replied with a smile.","The Emperor smiled knowingly.","Huan Qingtang understood.","When a filial child sees their mother living well, they feel a sense of accomplishment, as though they themselves have achieved something great.","It was a way of affirming himself.","So even the Emperor needed such reassurance from the Dowager Madame.","Huan Qingtang glanced over again.","“......If lard or anything else found its way into the vegetarian dishes, the tasting eunuch would certainly notice,” she thought.","The food-tasting eunuch was specifically tasked with that and would never make a mistake.","The Emperor ate sparingly.","Until he finished, and the vegetarian dishes stopped being served, nothing went wrong.","Huan Qingtang’s heart sank.","This was Nanny Qin’s golden opportunity—a small sabotage on such a day could have caused trouble for Cheng Zhao.","Could Nanny Qin have lost her nerve?","No one had known the Emperor would come. Even if Nanny Qin had panicked, the plan was already set—there was no last-minute retreat.","The vegetarian feast was bound to have a problem, so why hadn’t it?","If nothing went wrong, then Cheng Zhao’s decision to give the stewards the day off for Mid-Autumn was flawless—and then...","Huan Qingtang suddenly felt as if she had stepped into emptiness. She was deeply unsettled.","Why wasn’t things going as she had wished?","She glanced at the Emperor again.","The Emperor had already looked her way. Perhaps he had noticed her stealing glances at him, and he frowned slightly.","The Dowager Madame noticed and said, “Lady Huan, come forward.”","Huan Qingtang was startled.","When a person is lost in thought, being suddenly called is the worst. She knocked over her own wine cup, spilling liquor across the table.","She rose in a fluster.","The Dowager Madame said, “Lady Huan, go see what other opera acts are available. Switch to two new ones.”","She casually named two plays and told Huan Qingtang to arrange the change.","Huan Qingtang acknowledged the order.","The Dowager Madame then gave Nanny Sun a signal, and Nanny Sun left with Huan Qingtang.","Once they were outside the Yixia Pavilion, Nanny Sun’s face darkened: “Eldest Young Madam, why were you staring at the Emperor the whole time? He was quite displeased.”","Huan Qingtang’s legs went weak: “I, I was just...”","“Why can’t you keep your composure?” Nanny Sun pressed. “If you are so feeble, how can the Dowager Madame ever entrust you with heavier responsibilities?”","Huan Qingtang tasted bitterness in her mouth.","She lowered her head and apologized: “I lost my composure, Nanny Sun.”","“Return to your room to change clothes. Do not come out again,” said Nanny Sun.","Huan Qingtang trembled, her face turning pale.","Not return to the banquet?","Then tonight, the only absentees from the Mid-Autumn family dinner would be herself and Zhou Yuanshen’s new concubine?","What position would that leave her in? By tomorrow, the stewards would surely gossip about this. Her authority would be ruined.","She looked at Nanny Sun with anguish: “Nanny, I will be more careful. I was only watching His Majesty because I feared something might go wrong with the vegetarian dishes.”","She added, “Nanny Qin holds some grudge against my sister-in-law, and I was worried she might stir up trouble.”","Nanny Sun understood.","Huan Qingtang had clearly hinted to Nanny Qin and urged her to cause a scene.","To sabotage the Dowager Madame’s vegetarian feast!","What filial impiety that was.","“You...” Nanny Sun’s face stiffened with anger. “You conspired with Nanny Qin to make trouble? Do you realize... go back to your room and reflect!”","Huan Qingtang stopped walking: “Nanny Sun, I am still the Eldest Young Madam of the Zhou family. I will speak to Grandmother myself.”","“Are you asking me to have you locked up?” Nanny Sun said coldly.","Huan Qingtang knew she was “in for it” today, and she had no choice but to play her trump card: “Then who will keep that Cheng Shi in check, Nanny Sun? Does Grandmother want to see her reign alone?”","Nanny Sun was caught off guard.","Huan Qingtang lifted her chin slightly: “I will bear this alone. Nanny, go arrange the opera acts. I will change and return to the Yixia Pavilion.”","She turned and walked away.","Huan Qingtang returned to her own courtyard, hastily changed clothes, took several deep breaths, devised a plan for making amends, and went back to the Yixia Pavilion.","Unexpectedly, by the time she returned, the Emperor had left his seat, and the Dowager Madame was gone too—but the Crown Prince was still seated.","Mu Jiang had also disappeared.","It was common for guests to leave the banquet midway to refresh themselves, so Huan Qingtang sat down.","Her mother-in-law, Madam Song, glanced at her, and Huan Qingtang smiled back at her.","In the adjoining small hall used for resting and changing clothes, the Emperor sat quietly, discussing the Sun family’s irrigation canal with the Dowager Madame.","“I have been keeping those memorials on file without acting on them. Mother-in-law need not worry. Let them argue a few more days, and they will forget,” the Emperor said.","“Princess Qing'an’s consort...”","“The Court of Judicial Review will release him. He beat a steward to death, but since the Sun family won’t press the matter, a few days in prison is enough,” the Emperor said.","The Dowager Madame seemed to exhale with relief: “Once this is settled, my burden is lifted.”","“Yuanshen mentioned it to me, saying you were troubled. He also helped with this matter,” the Emperor said.","The Dowager Madame smiled on the surface but felt bitterness within: “Yuanshen is a filial child.”","“A pity he isn’t my son,” the Emperor said. “The one Mother-in-law holds dear—the one you have raised with your own hand—only then can I entrust him with the realm.”","The Dowager Madame thought of Mu Jiang.","But Mu Jiang burst in at that very moment.","She stumbled in like a startled fawn, as though afraid someone would stop her.","She knelt before the Emperor and the Dowager Madame.","The Emperor was briefly taken aback.","The Dowager Madame frowned: “A Jiang, have you no sense of propriety?”","“Your Majesty.” Mu Jiang, without looking at the Dowager Madame, turned her tearful eyes to the Emperor.","The Emperor felt a surge of irritation.","Mu Jiang’s disregard for the Dowager Madame stirred his anger.","A mere orphan girl, how dare she show such disrespect to the Dowager Madame?","“Your Majesty, I have recently been longing for the steamed mutton soup from the imperial kitchen—I’ve eaten it several times at the palace. Could you grant me the favor of letting me come and enjoy it again?” Mu Jiang’s tears fell like pear blossoms in rain. “Your Majesty...”","Her voice trembled at the end.","A little affected, perhaps.","The Dowager Madame understood and looked at Mu Jiang in shock, wanting to stop her.","“Do you want me to take you into the palace?” the Emperor asked.","“Yes, Your Majesty.”","“Who do you think you are?” the Emperor even smiled. “Have you grown bold enough to swallow a leopard’s heart?”","The Dowager Madame said softly, “Your Majesty, please calm your wrath.”","“Mother-in-law, you have indulged her too much, and now she dares to act so recklessly. In my very presence, she shows no discipline,” the Emperor said.","Then he ordered, “Someone, drag her out and give her twenty strokes of the board, so she learns her lesson.”","Guards disguised as attendants came to pull her away, and Mu Jiang froze in shock.","She suddenly screamed, \"Your Majesty, I am, I—\"","Her mouth was covered.","The Dowager Madame pleaded, \"Your Majesty, tell the executioner to be gentle. She's young—don't leave her crippled.\"","\"She's useless, Mother-in-law,\" the Emperor said. \"She has no sense of rank in her eyes, and she doesn't strive to improve. You and I both wished for a son—the Crown Prince—so why has it suddenly become so difficult?\"","Mu Jiang's eyes split wide with fury.","What madness was she hearing?","The Emperor—who, exactly, did he want to bear him the Crown Prince?"]},{"number":195,"title":"Chapter 195: Warding Off Disaster for Cheng Zhao","paragraphs":["The Mid-Autumn Festival night banquet concluded without a hitch.","In keeping with tradition, the children would normally head out after the family dinner to admire lanterns, stroll around, and keep the streets as lively all night as they did during the Lantern Festival.","This year, however, no one in the Zhou family was in the mood.","After the Emperor and the Crown Prince departed from the Zhou residence, rumors began to quietly sprout.","Nanny Fan reported to Second Madam, \"The Secondary Wife got beaten, and it was a caning.\"","Second Madam was taken aback: \"Who gave the order?\"","\"The old dowager's maids did it,\" Nanny Fan said. \"It's not just about who gave the order. Think about it: which family canes their female dependents?\"","Caning was reserved for servants or unruly young masters of the household.","There were plenty of other ways to punish a woman: confinement to quarters, forced kneeling, or even being sent off to a country estate, cut off from everything—effectively imprisonment.","Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen had gone to the main gate to see off the Emperor and hadn't returned yet.","Second Madam waited at the crossroads.","Soon, the two of them came over.","\"...She offended His Majesty,\" Cheng Zhao told her mother-in-law. \"She had no sense of rank. In His Majesty's fury, it could have implicated the entire Duke's mansion. Grandmother had her beaten—a 'self-imposed penalty'—to appease His Majesty's anger.\"","\"Is Mu Jiang out of her mind? I said all along she'd cause disaster one day, and then it would be impossible to clean up,\" Second Madam fumed.","Cheng Zhao stood in the shadows, keeping quiet.","The sky was clear tonight, with fireworks going off from time to time, yet a dark shadow hung over the Duke's mansion. Cheng Zhao could feel it.","It was like water flowing: it was most dangerous at the narrowest point, but once it broke through, the road ahead opened wide.","The discord within the Zhou family had already grown severe, and a major shift was sure to follow.","What exactly would happen before things settled, however, was anyone's guess. But the storm was already brewing, and it could not be stopped.","Cheng Zhao felt a flicker of excitement. She was thoroughly weary of this lukewarm existence, slowly grinding on. Her situation needed a big leap forward, and she wanted to stir the waters while they were troubled.","But shadows would carry the stench of blood, and people would suffer for it. Her anticipation of upheaval made her feel rather uncharitable.","If her mother-in-law knew she was itching to stoke the fire, the dowager would likely think her terrifying and be disappointed in her.","Cheng Zhao cast a surreptitious glance at her kind-hearted mother-in-law and reined in her emotions.","\"Mother, she was raised by Grandmother. Grandmother will handle the rest,\" Zhou Yuanshen said flatly.","Second Madam remained vexed.","By the next morning, the matter had spread throughout the Duke's mansion. Everyone knew the Secondary Wife had been caned.","\"Should we change how we address her?\" some quick-witted servants ventured.","Were they still to call Mu Jiang the Secondary Wife?","Or should they now call her Concubine Mu?","After all, she was only a concubine.","\"The Dowager Madame is still in a temper. Now is not the time to poke the hornet's nest. And who would you be trying to please by changing her title? The Third Young Madam couldn't care less about these things.\"","The Dowager Madame wouldn't want to slap her own face either.","So the servants saw no benefit in deriding Mu Jiang by changing her title.","In the course of this talk, the conversation turned to Cheng Zhao.","The Mid-Autumn Festival banquet had gone off without a single flaw. It proved that Cheng Zhao's way of managing the household was more effective.","The stewards who had been given time off—perhaps they had never once in their lives experienced a holiday when they didn't have to work—and had gained plenty of face to boot. How could they not feel grateful to Cheng Zhao?","On the sixteenth of the eighth month, gossip circulated around the Duke's mansion.","On the seventeenth, the Eldest Young Madame, Huan Qingtang, felt unwell and rested for a day. While she was away, the Chenming Hall carried on its duties as usual—indeed, with greater efficiency than when she was there.","By the eighteenth, Huan Qingtang returned to Chenming Hall, her expression none too pleased.","But it was something else that happened inside the Duke's mansion that day.","It counted as a minor matter.","Yet a minor matter could still shock and unsettle people.","\"Nanny Qin, who manages the main kitchen, has been dismissed by the Dowager Madame. She's the younger sister of the head steward's wife. Her sister and brother-in-law are both confidants of the Dowager Madame, and she herself had always been highly favored.\"","It was Nanny Fan who again brought this to Second Madam's attention.","Second Madam of course knew Nanny Qin, who ran the main kitchen.","In the Duke's mansion, well-established senior stewards with strong backing often had more authority than a mistress like Second Madam herself. And Nanny Qin was known for her sharp capability.","\"She was a formidable one—hard to deal with. Why would she be dismissed?\" Second Madam asked.","The stone cast into the water on Mid-Autumn Festival night sent ripples that had yet to subside.","Second Madam sent word to the Nonghua Courtyard, telling Cheng Zhao to come for supper at Jiangyun Court once she was done with Chenming Hall.","Cheng Zhao didn't come, but Second Master returned from court.","Second Madam relayed the matter of Nanny Qin to him.","Second Master said, \"They're all household slaves born into the dowager's dowry household—the Sun family. Their roots run deep and tangled through the mansion. But if she's being dismissed, then she must have committed a grave offense.\"","She brought up the Sun family and asked him, \"Whatever came of that case where the man was beaten to death in the water channel? Has it been settled?\"","\"Not settled. The Emperor won't discuss it, so it sits in abeyance,\" Second Master said.","Second Madam: \"So Princess Qing'an's husband just stays locked up like that?\"","\"Mm.\"","Second Madam was mildly surprised.","Second Master's tone was indifferent, as if making small talk: \"That's how the court grinds people down. No conviction, no trial—just keep you locked up for a year or two.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","The old couple talked on like this until Zhou Yuanqi came home from his lessons.","Cheng Zhao hadn't come, but Zhou Yuanqi knew exactly why Nanny Qin had been dismissed.","\"...On the night of Mid-Autumn Festival, Third Sister-in-Law told me to run into Nanny Qin, find some excuse to accuse her of shoving me, and have her tied up,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Second Madam and Second Master snapped their heads toward their son.","They knew nothing of this, and both looked stunned.","\"Why tie her up?\" Second Madam asked.","\"We didn't hear any such rumor,\" said Second Master. \"You two were too reckless.\"","\"Father, Mother, it wasn't reckless at all. While she was locked away, her underlings kept searching for her, trying to swap the vegetarian broth made from mushrooms for the real chicken broth,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Second Madam: \"...\"","Second Master: \"...Sabotaging the vegetarian fast—what a bold, audacious wretch!\"","\"But Nanfeng, that mischievous page boy of mine, took the chance to interrogate Nanny Qin. He wanted to bluff some useful information out of her, so that when Third Brother came to ask, he'd have a bit of credit to show off. Father, don't you think Nanfeng is a sharp, scheming little fellow? At his age, he thinks three steps ahead—plans before he even moves.\" Zhou Yuanqi laughed in spite of himself.","Second Master: \"...\"","A nine-year-old boy, calling a thirteen-year-old page \"at his age,\" with the air of an old man.","Second Master doubted Zhou Yuanqi would ever find a wife—no mother-in-law or bride would take to such a \"little old man.\"","\"You tied up Nanny Qin for no reason, and you interrogated her?\" Second Madam asked.","Zhou Yuanqi: \"Yes.\"","Second Madam: \"Don't ever go into officialdom when you grow up. With such an addled mind, you'd only bring ruin to the court and to the people.\"","Zhou Yuanqi frowned, displeased: \"But we found out for sure—she really did intend to pour real chicken broth over the vegetarian meal. Later, when her people couldn't find her, they didn't dare act on their own, and when they heard the Emperor had arrived, they gave up the scheme.\"","Second Madam: \"...\"","Second Master said, \"Well, it worked out in the end. It's lucky you had her tied up—you saved us from a scandal.\"","\"And it saved Third Sister-in-Law from disaster too. If something had gone wrong on Mid-Autumn Festival night, the elder branch and Grandmother would surely have found a way to blame her,\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Second Master nodded with satisfaction."]},{"number":196,"title":"Chapter 196: All That Was Gained Was as Cheng Zhao Had Wished","paragraphs":["After night fell, Cheng Zhao and Zhou Yuanshen arrived together at Crimson Cloud Court for the evening meal.","The Second Madam could not wait to ask about Senior Kitchen Nanny Qin's matter.","\"...Nanfeng did interrogate her. The remaining minor stewards were handed over to Grandmother. It was only after Grandmother conducted her own investigation that she dismissed Nanny Qin.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","The Second Madam was equal parts exasperated and amused.","\"The old lady must be furious. The Mid-Autumn Festival vegetarian feast is a significant matter, and her own people sabotaged her.","Though it did not succeed, Nanfeng locked the woman up and interrogated her — the news might as well have gotten out. It is the same as if the deed had been done.\" The Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao laughed: \"Exactly, that is why Grandmother is angry.\"","She added, \"Grandmother only observes vegetarianism on the first of every month, plus the various Buddhist festival days; she is not strict about it otherwise. Mid-Autumn does not count. Precisely because she is not overly particular, Nanny Qin dared to use underhanded means, swapping in chicken broth with my people to dilute the vegetarian soup.\"","Cheng Zhao knew that trouble would inevitably arise in the Senior Kitchen.","Rather than analyze and guard against it, it was better to eliminate those who might cause chaos at the source.","Of course, doing so carried great risk.","If Nanny Qin had truly been innocent, Cheng Zhao's arbitrary act of detaining her would not only offend the Grand Dowager but also bring trouble upon herself.","After much deliberation, Cheng Zhao shifted the blame onto her young brother-in-law, letting him handle the matter in her stead.","If Nanny Qin were innocent, it would be dismissed as a child's ignorant mischief. Even if everyone knew Cheng Zhao was behind it, Grandmother could not do anything about it.","Cheng Zhao had learned this trick from Huan Qingtang.","Huan Qingtang used this tactic again and again. Not because it was particularly brilliant, but simply because it worked — it never failed.","\"The old lady has always loved to torment people, deliberately making others uncomfortable to display her authority. Now her own people are slapping her in the face.\" The Second Madam laughed.","She then covered her mouth: \"I must not say such unfilial things before the children. I am guilty.\"","Zhou Yuanqi said: \"Mother, your children are not fools. If elders are unkind, the younger generations need not be respectful. We will not follow that example.\"","The Second Madam patted his head: \"Yuanqi is truly good, coming to your sister-in-law's aid.\"","\"I am reliable.\" Zhou Yuanqi said proudly, casting a glance at Zhou Yuanshen. \"More reliable than the Duke of Chen.\"","\"Yuanqi gets the top credit this time.\" Zhou Yuanshen said. \"We owe you a favor. What do you want?\"","He spoke slowly and coolly, without any hint of gratitude in his demeanor.","Zhou Yuanqi rolled his eyes, thinking to himself — what business is it of yours?","Are you really tied to my celestial sister-in-law's boat now?","That remains to be seen.","Who knows whether you will still marry for two branches of the family, or whether you will take more concubines.","Just wait and see.","My sister-in-law and I are ties for a lifetime. We are family; there is no need to stand on ceremony.","\"What favor? Must we count everything so precisely from now on, Third Sister-in-law?\" Zhou Yuanqi asked.","Cheng Zhao laughed: \"Yuanqi is right. It is his duty to help me. Why call it a favor? I even made him a sachet.\"","She added, \"Next time we play cuju, you and I will be on the same team.\"","\"Fine. Practice well.\" Zhou Yuanqi said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","The Second Madam watched her capable, astute daughter-in-law playing with the children and becoming childlike herself, and could not help but laugh.","The room was full of laughter and joy.","Zhou Yuanshen glanced at Cheng Zhao.","After the meal, returning to Verdant Fragrance Court, Zhou Yuanshen reached out and took Cheng Zhao's hand as they walked.","Held within his grasp, her steps felt light, and her heart felt light too.","\"Cheng Zhao, you are someone with great foresight and a sense of the bigger picture.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","This was a good opportunity.","For the sake of the family enjoying a peaceful Mid-Autumn Festival, Cheng Zhao set aside everything, suppressed all matters, and sought only to avoid trouble.","She possessed the magnanimity befitting a principal daughter-in-law.","\"Not really — I am afraid of the Emperor.\" Cheng Zhao said in a low voice, laughing.","\"He is the Son of Heaven; it is proper to fear him.\" Zhou Yuanshen's tone grew three degrees colder.","Cheng Zhao: \"I fear him, not revere him. We are all one family here. If he were to start killing at will, I worry it would frighten Yuanqi.\"","Zhou Yuanshen turned his head back, a bit of warmth returning to his eyes.","\"Have no fear.\"","\"Mm.\"","\"Cheng Zhao, is my sachet finished?\" he asked.","\"Almost done.\" Cheng Zhao said, this time with full confidence.","She had put in effort over the past few nights; both his sachet and his pouch were complete. A few finishing stitches, and everything would be done tonight.","\"I will be waiting.\" he said.","When Cheng Zhao returned, she washed up first.","By the time Zhou Yuanshen came back from the bath, Cheng Zhao had already placed the pouch and sachet on the bedside table.","He picked them up with great solemnity.","Looked at them, then smelled them.","\"Your needlework is truly fine. You learn everything, and you do it all well — you put real effort into everything.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Her hard work and dedication, seen by him — Cheng Zhao smiled: \"I am best at superficial display.\"","\"Only when the foundation runs deep can the surface be so well done. You need not be modest.\" Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao smiled.","He leaned over and kissed her lips. \"Thank you.\"","Cheng Zhao was wrapped in his arms.","By the end, Cheng Zhao was limp and soft all over, yet Zhou Yuanshen still stubbornly insisted on kissing her.","The lamps were out, but the little lanterns under the eaves had not yet been taken down, their glow reflecting off the glass-bead curtain at the door — light and shadow shifting, the indoor night scene was also resplendent.","Cheng Zhao knew she had gained much.","\"Perhaps — let things take their natural course. Neither push away, nor desperately cling. Then I can keep the good things for a long, long time.\"","She thought drowsily.","When she rose in the morning for the Hall of Inherited Radiance, she knew the situation was more favorable to her, and her mood was bright. She fell into sleep and dreamed a sweet, beautiful dream.","Zhou Yuanshen did not sleep.","He was somewhat exhilarated, joy washing over him like tides. He picked up the sachet and pouch, looking at them again and again.","Then he looked at her quiet sleeping face, and gently stroked her cheek.","He kissed her lips once more.","In Long Life Court, the Grand Dowager — like Zhou Yuanshen — also had not slept.","At Mid-Autumn Festival, the Emperor had personally taken the Sun family matter upon himself, and the Grand Dowager should have been able to set aside that worry.","As for Mu Jiang, she had planned to speak plainly with her. Perhaps Mu Jiang would have another child.","The Grand Dowager knew Mu Jiang's character best — greedy for vanity, easily swayed, and the easiest to manipulate.","Yet Mu Jiang had dared to resist her, to try to escape.","Then she could not be kept.","Dealing with Mu Jiang was simple.","But every pit needs a stone, and who would fill this one, burying herself as fertile soil to nourish the Zhou family's prosperity and glory?","The Grand Dowager could not decide for the moment.","And just then, her confidante Nanny Qin made her look a fool.","The matter of Nanny Qin had already spread.","\"The Grand Dowager's own trusted servant plotted to ruin the Grand Dowager's vegetarian feast.\"","Nanny Qin had to be removed; such betrayal could never be pardoned.","Betrayal is like a cough among trusted retainers. Once one coughs too loudly, the others involuntarily start coughing too.","But with Nanny Qin gone, the entire Senior Kitchen fell into Cheng Zhao's hands.","From the moment Cheng Zhao married into the Duke's household, the first place she worked to gain influence was the Senior Kitchen; the first department she obtained managerial authority over was also the Senior Kitchen.","With Nanny Qin gone, the few remaining minor stewards of the Senior Kitchen were no match for Cheng Zhao.","This had opened a crack in the Grand Dowager's dominion.","\"All under heaven belongs to the king.\" And upon the king's land, a vassal state had suddenly appeared — no matter how tiny — it was an affront to imperial authority.","Such small cracks were but ants' nests that breach a thousand-li dike.","The old Madam watched helplessly as Cheng Zhao established his own separate kitchen in the main compound, and for the first time in her life, she lay awake all night, unable to sleep."]},{"number":197,"title":"Chapter 197: The Secret of the Huan Family Was Leaked","paragraphs":["The situation in the Chen Marquis Manor had quietly undergone many changes, with the Mid-Autumn Festival as the turning point.","\"...Third Young Madam, please try this tea. It was grown at my home. My wife is very skilled at roasting tea leaves,\" said a minor steward from the purchasing office.","Cheng Zhao recognized him. He was in charge of procuring incense and candles.","In any noble household, the purchasing office was a position rife with emoluments, and those appointed to it were always confidants of the matriarch who managed the household affairs.","In the Zhou family, all purchasing positions were uniformly staffed by the Grand Lady's people. Eight out of ten of them were surnamed Sun. The First Madam had managed the household for years, yet had never managed to place a single person of her own in that office; Huan Qingtang had not touched that area either.","Now, the purchasing office was also under Huan Qingtang's management.","The steward surnamed Sun who offered Cheng Zhao his private tea was around twenty-seven or twenty-eight years old.","He was a household-born servant. His parents were certainly the Grand Lady's dowry retainers, and he was a second-generation steward. His brothers, sisters, and in-laws were surely scattered throughout the ranks of the manor's stewards.","Cheng Zhao took a sip. Indeed, it was fragrant and slightly sweet. She smiled, \"That's very good tea.\"","\"It can't compare to what you usually drink, but we added osmanthus flowers when roasting it. It gives it a bit more flavor,\" Steward Sun said.","Cheng Zhao took another sip and smiled. \"There is indeed a hint of osmanthus sweetness.\"","The other stewards watched from the side as they observed.","Cheng Zhao had finished a cup of tea before Huan Qingtang arrived. However, no private tea had been prepared specially for her; a maid simply handed her a cup of hot tea.","When the meeting ended that day, the stewards each left with their own thoughts.","Cheng Zhao went to the Crimson Cloud Courtyard for the evening meal.","The Second Madam asked her how it went.","\"Everyone in the purchasing office came to curry favor with me,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"They're not trying to butter you up just to bring you down, are they?\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao smiled. \"Mother, your concern is quite reasonable. But even if they lift me up high, they may not be able to bring me down. If they want to flatter me, I'll accept it all.\"","Then she added, \"Mother, are there any people among your dowry retainers who could be of use? I want to slowly start placing people of our own in various positions.\"","The Second Madam was taken aback. \"Zhao Zhao, isn't that a bit rash? Your grandmother may be deliberately showing weakness to lull you into letting your guard down.\"","Cheng Zhao said, \"I understand. But we should start preparing anyway.\"","The Second Madam: \"...\"","For the first time, mother-in-law and daughter-in-law discussed their respective dowry retainers.","The Second Madam hardly ever concerned herself with such matters. Her dowry retainers were all stationed elsewhere, taking care of her farms and shops, not serving within the manor.","\"I only have seven families of dowry retainers. Two were sent away, and I have five left. Not that they're much use—each one of them is lazy and good for nothing.","My largest dowry estate is seven thousand mu of fertile farmland, all in one contiguous stretch. It's managed by a man who has served your father-in-law since he was young.","The rest are scattered plots of land—I let them muddle along, and I don't care whether they yield a harvest or not. As for my two shops and two residences, they're all rented out,\" the Second Madam said.","She quickly laid out the full details of her dowry for Cheng Zhao.","The Second Madam went on to say that her shops were in good commercial locations and had never lacked tenants; her houses were near the capital's prefectural office, so they were also easy to rent out.","\"Mother, were all these dowry properties gifts your mother added later?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","\"One can tell at a glance,\" the Second Madam said with a laugh. \"We got married on the frontier back then—how could we have afforded things like this?\"","The situation was so turbulent in those days. The Fan family and the Zhou family were both military clans without much influence. Never mind residences near the government offices—even contiguous plots of farmland couldn't be bought.","Large contiguous fields were more expensive and highly sought after; it wasn't just a matter of money alone.","\"They're all quite low-maintenance and easy to manage. Grandmother doted on you and thought everything through thoroughly for you,\" Cheng Zhao said, smiling.","\"I wish she'd just given me silver instead—that would have been even easier,\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao: \"...\"","The Second Madam then asked Cheng Zhao about her own dowry.","Cheng Zhao gave a helpless laugh. \"Haven't you seen my dowry list, Mother?\"","A daughter-in-law's dowry list was always kept in her mother-in-law's hands. Even if the original wasn't kept, a copy would be made and given to the mother-in-law.","\"...Why would I be bothered to read it? You can just tell me about it—it's the same thing,\" the Second Madam said.","So Cheng Zhao began to tell her.","It was much the same—farmland, shops, and residences.","Cheng Zhao's farmland wasn't particularly extensive—only three thousand mu—but she had eight shops and eight residences.","All eight residences were in the vicinity of the Six Ministries, which made them even easier to rent and fetch higher rents than the Second Madam's properties.","\"That's quite impressive,\" the Second Madam said, marveling.","Besides what her natal family had given her, there were also the empress's gifts and those prepared by the Ministry of Rites. Cheng Zhao's dowry was exceedingly generous.","\"How many families of dowry retainers do you have?\" the Second Madam asked.","Cheng Zhao said, \"Mother, this might come as a bit of a surprise to you.\"","\"Quite a lot, is it?\"","\"I have seventeen families of dowry retainers,\" Cheng Zhao said.","The Second Madam's eyes widened: !","Then she asked, \"Where have they been stationed?\"","\"Only four families are in the manor; the rest are all outside. Of the four inside, they serve in the kitchens, the stables and carriage house, and the gatehouse,\" Cheng Zhao said.","\"Then you certainly don't lack for people to use,\" the Second Madam said.","Then she added, \"Huan Qingtang definitely doesn't have as big a dowry as yours, nor that many retainers.\"","\"Ah, but we can't know that for sure.\"","The Second Madam thought for a moment. \"Your First Aunt is restless too. How about I go sit a while in the Clear Breeze Courtyard and pump her for information? At this critical juncture, she's just looking to sell us a favor cheap—she might actually be willing to tell me something.\"","Cheng Zhao laughed. \"Aren't you afraid she'll turn the tables and pump you instead?\"","\"I'll be on my guard against her,\" the Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao encouraged the Second Madam to try all sorts of things: \"Then go ahead and ask.\"","The Second Madam began to plan how she might go about it.","And she actually did go.","She went to pay her respects to the Grand Lady and offered up simmered beef soup; then she asked the Grand Lady, \"I'd like to send a bowl to Sister-in-Law as well. She's moved to the Clear Breeze Courtyard, and I haven't been to see her yet.\"","The Grand Lady gave her a slightly surprised look. \"That's a matter between you sisters-in-law. No need to ask me.\"","She knew the Second Madam had an ulterior motive, but she wasn't too concerned.","The Second Madam went to the Clear Breeze Courtyard.","The First Madam, née Song, was equally astonished.","The two sisters-in-law met, each harboring their own schemes, and chatted about this and that.","Then it suddenly struck the Second Madam that Song Shi was extremely eager to know what her real intention was in coming.","\"...What am I trying to find out? Huan Qingtang is no match for my daughter-in-law, period. I'm not saying a word. Let them all guess!\" A mischievous impulse rose in the Second Madam.","She didn't mention even half a word about dowries or retainers. Instead, she just talked about festivals and holidays; she also launched into a lengthy discussion about what flowers and plants to put in the courtyard.","The First Madam, née Song, was eager to pry something out of her, so she offered up a secret of her own: \"Huan Qingtang and Yuancheng never consummated the marriage. Even back then, Yuancheng's health wasn't too good.\"","The Second Madam: !","There was no need to tell her that, really.","She felt like her ears were going to fall off.","Who wanted to hear gossip like that?","So what if Huan Qingtang had kept her virtue? The Second Madam didn't believe that Zhou Yuanshen was so lacking in spine that he'd be swayed by such news.","Zhao Zhao had said that Yuanshen was trustworthy.","Zhao Zhao trusted him, so the Second Madam believed her son had some depth to him and was steady on his feet.","She felt a little disgusted hearing it, but she didn't let it show on her face.","The First Madam then brought up Princess Qing'an's residence.","\"What's been going on lately? Everywhere I turn, it's Princess Qing'an's residence?\" the Second Madam suddenly thought.","It was eerie.","The Second Madam had a vague premonition that Princess Qing'an's husband might end up imprisoned because of the Sun family affair. The Sun family was the Grand Lady's natal clan, and the Second Madam's younger brother was the Vice Minister of the Court of Judicial Review.","But that wasn't of immediate concern.","Right now, the Second Madam just wanted to hurry back and share the secret she'd heard with Cheng Zhao."]},{"number":198,"title":"Chapter 198: Stepping Stone","paragraphs":["Second Madam rarely took the initiative, but this time she did.","“......I said nothing at all, and that actually forced her to leak the secret to me.” Second Madam laughed.","When Cheng Zhao heard about Huan Qingtang, she was briefly surprised at first.","She praised Second Madam first, saying, “Mother, you were very quick-witted. You knew to go and inform Grandmother beforehand, and you also knew to change your probing words at the last moment.”","Second Madam: “It wasn’t that hard.”","Though her little game of scheming had been clumsy, the results were good, and it gave her tremendous confidence.","From now on, she could slowly explore and learn.","Second Madam suddenly found it quite interesting—once you taste the sweetness of success in something, it feels easy and you begin to enjoy it.","“Zhaozhao, do you think Madam Song deliberately deceived me, or was she telling the truth?” Second Madam asked again.","Cheng Zhao: “It might be true.”","“Then I don’t understand. Lady Huan married Yuan Cheng for two or three years before he passed away. I remember in the early days of their marriage, Yuan Cheng’s health was still fine.” Second Madam said.","The reason Second Madam remembered so clearly was because Zhou Yuancheng’s chambermaid had gotten pregnant, and in the middle of the night she miscarried with massive bleeding.","It was said that blood flooded the entire bed, and the chambermaid died that very night.","Second Madam had remarked at the time—the heir had been married for several months already, why wasn’t the chambermaid given medicine to prevent pregnancy?","Yuan Cheng married in spring, the maid bled to death in high summer, so more than one season of clothes had changed in between.","Cheng Zhao hesitated, as if about to speak.","Second Madam waited for Cheng Zhao’s analysis, and when she saw her expression flicker, she quickly asked, “Is there some inside story?”","Cheng Zhao’s information network was very well-connected.","Could it be that Second Madam was the only one who didn’t know again?","“Mother, do you remember Princess Qing’an?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Second Madam: “......”","What was the deal with this Princess Qing’an? She turned up everywhere.","“This matter was told to me by the Lord Duke, and I didn’t dare mention it to you. Back then, Princess Qing’an had been the matchmaker between the Lord Duke and Lady Huan.” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam: “I know that. That’s why for a while after Ah Shen inherited the title, the servants were always gossiping about Ah Shen and Lady Huan. They said Ah Shen had taken a liking to Lady Huan, but she didn’t fancy him back. It infuriated me.”","“That’s not true.”","“I didn’t dare ask either, for fear it would upset Ah Shen.” Second Madam said.","Cheng Zhao then explained: “Back then, Princess Qing’an’s daughter had her eye on the Duke of Chen’s household and wanted to marry the heir, to become the future mistress of the ducal mansion. The Princess sent two rounds of people to test the waters. The Dowager Madame put out word that she wanted a well-bred lady from a distinguished but modest family. At Princess Qing’an’s birthday banquet, the Huan family suddenly brought up that their own family was of a refined and noble lineage. At that time, didn’t you and the Lord Duke also attend? They had a quiet contest of wits behind the scenes. The Princess, fearing that Lady Huan might catch the Dowager Madame’s eye, hastily dragged Lady Huan into the scheme, offering to make a match between her and the Lord Duke.”","Second Madam was stunned.","She had no idea at all.","In the Zhou family’s social circles, there was almost no one Second Madam was close to, and no one had tipped her off.","At that birthday banquet, was she the only one left in the dark?","“A hastily arranged match naturally couldn’t succeed—the Zhou family didn’t owe Princess Qing’an that favor. The Princess was cornered and made a rash move, but the outcome was premeditated.” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam frowned deeply: “Premeditated?”","“Think about it—who did Zhou Yuancheng fear and despise the most?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Second Madam: “He was always afraid that Ah Shen would take his position. Ah Shen was superior to him in every way.”","“If I were Lady Huan, I would find a way to let Zhou Yuancheng know that I had rejected his cousin, yet I admired him greatly. Wouldn’t he think I was someone out of the ordinary and look at me differently?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Second Madam: “......A refined lady from a scholarly family would dare to engage in private assignations?”","“Private matters, kept quiet—who would know?” Cheng Zhao said.","Then she added, “She got acquainted with Zhou Yuancheng, refused the Lord Duke, and had the whole affair spread all over town. Even the Dowager Madame would have heard about Princess Qing’an’s matchmaking scheme. With the Princess and the Lord Duke as stepping stones, Lady Huan rose to prominence and caught the Dowager Madame’s eye, didn’t she?”","Second Madam’s expression darkened.","“Then the Dowager Madame went to inquire. The daughter of the Hanlin Academy’s head—not only widely read, but with a father who had countless disciples, all of them future connections in officialdom. A scholarly family, educated and courteous, with a hidden network of civil officials spread across the court. All it would take is a little support from the Zhou family, and the Huan family would take the top spot among the refined houses, perhaps even surpassing my grandfather’s. Such an easy-to-control and promising family—Mother, don’t you think the Dowager Madame would be tempted?” Cheng Zhao asked again.","Second Madam listened, and a chill ran down her spine.","Were Huan Qingtang and her natal family’s schemes truly that deep?","“As for Princess Qing’an and the Lord Duke, the Dowager Madame paid them no mind. In short, the Dowager Madame soon arranged for the match to be vetted, and had Lady Huan betrothed to Zhou Yuancheng.” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam felt as if she’d fallen into an ice pit: “Ah Shen was used by others all along, and had to bear the gossip and slander.”","“The Lord Duke knows full well what that matter was about.” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam was both furious and heartbroken for her son: “He never tells us anything!”","“Princess Qing’an realized she had been used, so she deliberately told Zhou Yuancheng about it, and also told the Lord Duke.” Cheng Zhao said.","“Her?”","Second Madam realized then, no one was a pushover.","Princess Qing’an was no simple soul either.","“I think Zhou Yuancheng may not have had much backbone, but to be schemed against like this, and to have his fear and jealousy toward his cousin exposed—wouldn’t he have flown into a rage from humiliation?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Second Madam: “Definitely!”","“Then that is why Zhou Yuancheng refused to consummate his marriage with his lawful wife, and Lady Huan remained a virgin by the time she was widowed.” Cheng Zhao said.","Second Madam sat in silence for a long time.","“Mother?” Cheng Zhao gently patted Second Madam’s arm.","Second Madam’s heart felt blocked with bitterness.","She felt as disgusted as if she had swallowed a fly. This sickening feeling gave rise to a kind of despair, and she suddenly lost interest in everything.","“Zhaozhao, how filthy the human heart can be!” Second Madam said gloomily.","Cheng Zhao: “Mother, there are filthy and dark hearts, but there are also bright and passionate ones.”","After returning from the Crimson Cloud Courtyard, Cheng Zhao sat alone.","She went into the side room and looked at her statue of the Bodhisattva of Sons.","She thought of what First Madam Song had said before the Mid-Autumn Festival, when she’d come to Crimson Cloud Courtyard and offered to copy sutras and make offerings to the Bodhisattva.","Cheng Zhao knew something would come of it.","But she refused to wait passively for trouble to land on her head before striking back. She wanted to take the initiative, to make the wind blow in the direction she desired.","This time, she had “used” her mother-in-law, goading her into going to First Madam’s residence.","She didn’t need her mother-in-law to do anything—just by going, Cheng Zhao’s plan could begin.","If First Madam Song had no ill intentions, the matter might just fizzle out. But if she did harbor murderous intent, she would do Cheng Zhao a big favor.","Cheng Zhao lit a stick of incense for the Bodhisattva.","Nanny Li stood beside her, and when she finished her prostrations, helped her to her feet: “You seem heavy-hearted today. Did the Hall of Resplendence give you grief?”","“No.” Cheng Zhao shook her head. “Nanny, it’s time we moved to the Hall of Resplendence. The moment is ripe.”","She no longer wanted to stay in the Fragrant Blossom Courtyard.","She wanted to take another step forward.","Nanny Li sighed helplessly: “How would the Dowager Madame ever agree?”","“She will come to a compromise.” Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Li held her arm.","She said nothing more, only urged Cheng Zhao to be even more cautious."]},{"number":199,"title":"Chapter 199: United as One","paragraphs":["Cheng Zhao sat under the lamp reading.","Half an hour passed without her turning a single page, her mind elsewhere.","“...Still waiting for the Duke?” Nanny Li asked her. “Perhaps you should go to bed first and have the night watch keep an eye on the door.”","Zhou Yuanshen had not yet gone to the Jingyi Camp.","That afternoon, he had sent someone back to pass along a message—he would be dining with Fan Xiao that evening and might return rather late.","He had only told Cheng Zhao not to wait dinner for him, yet instructed that the door be left unlatched for his return.","The hour was still early; Cheng Zhao was not sleepy. Nanny Li saw her staring blankly into space, uncomfortable just sitting idle, and thought it better for her to lie down.","“I’ll do some needlework,” Cheng Zhao said.","This way, she could keep thinking while her hands stayed busy.","Nanny Li fetched the sewing basket.","At the start of the hour of Hai (around 9 PM), Zhou Yuanshen returned.","He bore no smell of wine, though there was a faint sheen of sweat at his temples.","“What’s the matter?” Cheng Zhao asked.","Zhou Yuanshen: “Nothing major—my maternal uncle shared some Northwest intelligence reports with me.”","“I assumed you were in a hurry,” said Cheng Zhao.","Zhou Yuanshen wiped his brow: “I was afraid of being too late and delaying your sleep, so I rode back at full gallop.”","Cheng Zhao: “...If the night patrol caught you, they’d impeach you.”","Zhou Yuanshen rarely curved his lips in a smile: “I took the back alleys.”","He went off to the washroom.","After the couple lay down, Zhou Yuanshen let down the bed curtains and turned to find Cheng Zhao propped against a bolster, still not asleep.","“Something on your mind?”","“Mother uncovered a secret today,” Cheng Zhao said.","She then recounted how Second Madam had gone to the Qingfeng Courtyard and how First Madam had revealed Huan Qingtang’s hidden secret, telling Zhou Yuanshen everything.","She also shared her own analysis with him.","She believed that before Zhou Yuancheng passed away, he and Huan Qingtang had been deeply at odds, their marriage fractured in spirit.","“...You see, she schemed endlessly to step on you and marry into the Duke’s mansion, yet even she has no real glory on the surface. She has her own recompense,” Cheng Zhao said.","Cheng Zhao was not testing him. Zhou Yuanshen was a man of honor, and being used so calculatingly—even the gentle Zhou Yuancheng could not tolerate it, let alone a man like him.","He would never share a dual inheritance marriage with Huan Qingtang.","Zhou Yuanshen, however, frowned.","He shared Second Madam’s attitude toward this: what filthy gossip deserved to pass through his ears? He had even heard it twice—last time, the Emperor had told him.","Cheng Zhao glanced at him again.","She had brought this up to show him that “good and evil are each repaid,” to comfort him.","The irritation in Zhou Yuanshen’s heart instantly dissolved: “First Madam dragging out such filthy matters is truly excessive.”","“She’s at her wit’s end now—why would she care about appearances?” Cheng Zhao said.","“Mother wasn’t tricked into revealing anything, and even managed to pry out a secret?” Zhou Yuanshen asked.","Cheng Zhao: “I was pleasantly surprised as well.”","“Very well—Mother is increasingly showing the shrewdness of a seasoned matriarch,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","The couple talked of other things.","As they chatted, the night grew deep.","Cheng Zhao grew drowsy. She swept her hair over to the right side, and just as she was about to lie down, Zhou Yuanshen leaned in and kissed her earlobe.","Cheng Zhao didn’t shy away.","But the next moment he took her earlobe into his mouth, grazing it lightly with his teeth.","Cheng Zhao pressed against him: “It’s very late!”","“How about we take a day off tomorrow and sleep until the sun is high?” he whispered.","Cheng Zhao: “...”","“My uncle also said that the day after tomorrow we’re going to play polo, and he’s invited your fourth brother. Cheng Zhao, let’s go together.” He continued—covering his actions with words, distracting her attention, his hand slipping beneath her garment.","Cheng Zhao found his palm scorching hot.","That warmth melted her, and she softened in his arms, murmuring his name: “Zhou Yuanshen...”","“Mm?”","“If we never have children, don’t blame me,” she said.","Zhou Yuanshen paused: “Why would I blame you?”","“Because I’m about to offend the Bodhisattva of Fertility,” Cheng Zhao said.","Zhou Yuanshen didn’t understand.","Cheng Zhao wrapped her arms around his neck, pulling herself close to him, and silenced his question with a kiss.","The storm within the bed curtains did not subside for a long while.","After a simple rinse, Zhou Yuanshen held her, dressed in soft under robes, against his chest: “What’s this about the Fertility Bodhisattva?”","Cheng Zhao, sated like a cat basking in the sun, had no desire to exert her mind, only wanting to stretch and unwind.","She answered offhandedly: “Zhou Yuanshen, I want to move into the Chengming Hall.”","“...Let me do my part too,” Zhou Yuanshen said, tightening his arms.","Mother and Fifth Brother had both helped.","“Alright,” Cheng Zhao nodded. “With your help, it’ll be twice as effective.”","“But I’m leaving for the Jingyi Camp in a few days—will we have enough time?” he asked. “If it’s too rushed, we can find another way.”","Cheng Zhao thought it over.","She ran the entire plan through her mind once more, then nodded decisively: “These few days should suffice.”","Zhou Yuanshen asked whether she wanted to tell him everything now, or wait until after they woke.","Cheng Zhao let out a soft “Mmm,” wanting to doze off, and leaned against him to sleep.","She nestled obediently in his arms.","Zhou Yuanshen pressed a kiss to her brow. Thinking back to the Fertility Bodhisattva earlier, and linking it with her previous words, he vaguely grasped what Cheng Zhao planned to do.","The next morning, Nanny Li knocked at the appointed hour.","Cheng Zhao was already awake.","With something heavy on her mind, her sleep was light, and she woke at the usual time.","The maids came in to help them both dress.","“Cheng Zhao, you were born in the Year of the Pig,” Zhou Yuanshen said.","Cheng Zhao gave him a puzzled look.","He didn’t seem to be asking a question.","Ever since Zhou Yuanqi had given her a five-catty golden pig as a birthday gift, the entire Duke’s mansion could no longer forget that the Third Young Madam was born in the Year of the Pig.","“Grandmother was born in the Year of the Sheep,” Zhou Yuanshen added.","Cheng Zhao instantly understood what he meant.","He glanced at her.","Their eyes met and intertwined, and Cheng Zhao smiled: “Understood.”","“I’m off now. I’ll be back by noon,” he said. “If you’re not too hungry, you can wait for me to join you for lunch.”","Cheng Zhao said yes.","After Zhou Yuanshen left, Cheng Zhao slowly washed and applied her makeup, then went to the Jiangyun Courtyard to have breakfast with her mother-in-law.","After the meal, she headed to the Chengming Hall.","At the entrance, Cheng Zhao encountered a young maid.","The maid was trembling, peeking around furtively. Huan Qingtang and her personal maid had arrived first.","Seeing the young maid, Huan Qingtang’s maid barked: “Which quarters are you from, skulking about here? Do you want to die?”","The maid started in fright and hastily knelt: “Please spare me, sister.”","“Fool—this is First Young Madam,” Huan Qingtang’s maid snapped.","The maid turned to Huan Qingtang: “First Young Madam, please spare me. This servant is from the Yujin Courtyard, here to seek Third Young Madam.”","The Yujin Courtyard housed a newly installed concubine, one bestowed upon Zhou Yuanshen by the Emperor.","Huan Qingtang’s sidelong glance had already spotted Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao stepped down from her small carriage, Suyue following beside her.","Without asking anything, she simply said to Suyue: “Take her aside and find out what’s going on. If you can handle it, do so; if you can’t decide, go back and ask Nanny Li.”","That is—she didn’t need to be consulted for a verdict; Suyue and Nanny Li could handle it on their own.","Suyue acknowledged and said to the young maid: “Come with me.”","Cheng Zhao gave Huan Qingtang a smile: “Sister-in-law, after you.”","The two sisters-in-law stepped into the Chengming Hall together.","Huan Qingtang looked at the main residence of the Chengming Hall, bathed in the brilliant golden autumn dawn, and her heart stirred. She turned her gaze to Cheng Zhao.","And Cheng Zhao, at that same moment, was also looking at those few main rooms.","Their eyes met once. Neither spoke, but it seemed each saw straight through to the other’s heart."]},{"number":200,"title":"Chapter 200: Cheng Zhao Strikes the Mountain to Startle the Tiger","paragraphs":["The morning council proceeded as usual.","Huan Qingtang remained composed, and Cheng Zhao responded with equal ease.","During a brief break, the maidservants came to refresh their tea.","Huan Qingtang remarked, \"I heard Second Mother went to Qingfeng Courtyard yesterday? She wasn't given a hard time, was she? Mother's temper hasn't been good lately.\" She added, \"I had the main kitchen stew some pear syrup and sent it over to her—good for relieving the autumn dryness.\"","“My mother also felt the dryness of autumn, so she specifically had some clear beef soup stewed for Aunt. It’s nourishing and helps dispel heat as well,” said Cheng Zhao.","The fact that Second Madam had visited Qingfeng Courtyard—everyone knew. Everyone was probing her purpose. And there was considerable tension about it.","Cheng Zhao thought to herself that her mother-in-law was truly a lucky star. Even if she had blundered into it by accident, she had managed to muddy the waters.","\"...Aunt also told my mother a secret,\" Cheng Zhao said with a smile.","Huan Qingtang smiled too. \"A secret, is there?\"","\"However, my mother is reluctant to spread it around. It concerns the harmony of the household. Aunt really is reckless to have told anyone so casually,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Huan Qingtang paused.","Her expression shifted slowly, her smile seeming to freeze on her lips.","Cheng Zhao noticed her fingers tighten slightly.","She vaguely understood what secret Cheng Zhao was referring to. This matter was not merely confidential—it was her dignity. If First Madam were to lose all restraint and make it public, Huan Qingtang would be utterly humiliated.","When First Madam was in her right mind, she wouldn't casually divulge such a thing either—after all, it also concerned her deceased son.","Huan Qingtang seemed unable to believe that First Madam would have spoken of it.","Her expression flickered, but she still tested: \"What did she say?\"","\"Mother didn't tell me,\" Cheng Zhao said with a radiant smile. \"Only that it affects family harmony.\"","Huan Qingtang: \"...\".","They were speaking in private, with no stewards present—just the two sisters-in-law resting alone.","Then the stewards filed back in.","Cheng Zhao took another sip of tea and leafed through an account ledger, as if she'd suddenly noticed something. She said to Huan Qingtang, \"I need to go to Shou'an Courtyard. To consult Grandmother.\"","Huan Qingtang said, \"I'll accompany you. I was just about to go and have lunch with Grandmother anyway.\" Then she added with a smile, as if deliberately speaking for others to hear, \"Grandmother's appetite has been poor these past few days. It's easier for her to eat when someone keeps her company.\"","The Mid-Autumn Festival incident had apparently affected the Dowager Madame, who hadn't been eating or sleeping well.","The assembled stewards understood that the Eldest Young Madam was favored by the Dowager Madame.","But they still looked at Cheng Zhao.","Cheng Zhao remained expressionless, saying nothing.","Huan Qingtang's face stiffened slightly, but she quickly recovered and put on a calm, faint smile.","The two of them went to Shou'an Courtyard.","Cheng Zhao had no major business, so she raised a matter about the gatekeepers.","\"There are too many of them—wasted manpower. Better to transfer some to manage the rear garden,\" Cheng Zhao said.","She had only just gained control of the main kitchen, and now she was eyeing the gatehouse—trying to devour everything piece by piece.","The gatehouse was important. Even more important than the main kitchen.","The Dowager Madame immediately dismissed her suggestion: \"The gatehouse staff aren't for ordinary use—they're for festivals, holidays, and important occasions. What does daily socializing matter? If the staffing proves insufficient on significant days, people will laugh at us.\"","Cheng Zhao then said, \"Grandmother, since you see no harm in extra staff, may I place two of my dowry retainers there?\"","The Dowager Madame gave her a smile. \"Your retainers only arrived less than a year ago. They still have much to learn. We'll talk again after the New Year.\"","She refused without hesitation.","Cheng Zhao's true intention hadn't been the gatehouse in the first place, so she nodded submissively: \"Grandmother is right.\"","Huan Qingtang's restless heart, in contrast, calmed with the exchange.","She felt that Cheng Zhao had grown too pleased with herself.","After Cheng Zhao dealt with Nanny Qin in the main kitchen and had the whole kitchen under her control, she'd started getting cocky.","She was riding on momentum, stretching her hand toward the gatehouse.","That was fine.","Let her be smug.","The higher she climbed, the harder she'd fall. That would be Huan Qingtang's opportunity.","\"...Is Cheng Zhao always this shallow and lacking in patience? Or is it all an act?\" Huan Qingtang wondered again.","Her gaze rested on Cheng Zhao.","After Cheng Zhao finished with the gatehouse, she said to the Dowager Madame, \"Grandmother, I recall one of my retainers saying that this year's fortune doesn't favor me.\"","The Dowager Madame chuckled. \"Why is it about fortune again? You, child.\"","\"People born in the Year of the Goat are my lucky stars. In this household, I believe that's you,\" Cheng Zhao said. \"Grandmother, I've been married without bearing a child—it must be that fortune is working against me.\"","The Dowager Madame: \"...\"","\"Last time, Aunt said she'd copy sutras for me to offer to the Goddess of Fertility. Grandmother, could you gift me some of your own?\" Cheng Zhao asked.","Huan Qingtang looked at Cheng Zhao again.","Usually, it was the younger generation who copied sutras for their elders. For an elder to copy for the young—Cheng Zhao was showing a lack of filial piety.","\"Venerable Huining previously copied some sutras for me. Take them and offer them before the Bodhisattva,\" the Dowager Madame said after a pause, conceding.","Cheng Zhao beamed with joy: \"Thank you, Grandmother.\"","Then she turned to Huan Qingtang: \"Sister-in-law, will you also copy some for me? With Grandmother's sutras anchoring them, the more the better.\"","She trailed off after saying this, seemingly afraid Huan Qingtang would refuse.","Huan Qingtang, recalling what had been said earlier at Chengming Hall, glanced at the Dowager Madame, then smiled: \"Of course. I certainly hope you'll soon bear children for the Zhou family. I'll copy them tonight when I return.\"","Cheng Zhao then asked the Dowager Madame, \"May I also ask Aunt to copy some for me?\"","The Dowager Madame: \"That matter, you may discuss privately among yourselves.\"","\"I'll have my mother ask Aunt,\" Cheng Zhao said. And added, \"My mother and Aunt actually get along better now. Aunt even told her some private things.\"","The Dowager Madame showed little interest: \"So long as you're all harmonious, the family will prosper.\"","By this series of remarks, Cheng Zhao was simply conveying one thing: she was worried about heirs, hoping to borrow the luck of the women of the household.","Second Madam's visit to Qingfeng Courtyard seemed to have been over just such a matter. And Cheng Zhao bringing up the gatehouse here—its purpose was also this.","\"If she becomes pregnant, she'll propose moving into Chengming Hall? Is that her plan?\" Huan Qingtang thought to herself.","Or perhaps she was already pregnant, and this grand show was merely laying the groundwork?","Huan Qingtang glanced at the Dowager Madame again.","The Dowager Madame's countenance was kind and gentle, revealing nothing.","At noon, Cheng Zhao did not stay at Shou'an Courtyard for lunch.","She said, \"This morning, it seemed someone from Yujin Courtyard was looking for me. I had Nanny Li go handle it. I'll go check on the matter while I eat.\"","The Dowager Madame: \"Go on then, see to your duties. Don't overwork yourself.\"","\"Thank you for your concern, Grandmother,\" Cheng Zhao said.","Nanny Sun, one of the Dowager Madame's attendants, fetched a stack of sutras from the small prayer room and gave them to her. These had been copied by Venerable Huining and had been offered before the Bodhisattva for quite some time—said to be quite efficacious.","Carrying the sutras, Cheng Zhao walked slowly back.","She went to Chenhui Courtyard.","Suyue was waiting at the entrance, and said: \"The Lord Duke has returned, and Nanny Li is also waiting to report.\"","\"Bring in the meal,\" Cheng Zhao said.","With that, she stepped over the threshold.","Suyue and the young servant Nanfeng fetched the food box from the main kitchen.","Nanny Li had already summarized the affairs of Yujin Courtyard for Cheng Zhao.","Zhou Yuanshen sat quietly nearby, not interjecting, merely flipping through the sutras Cheng Zhao had brought back.","\"...Young Madam, what should we do?\" Nanny Li concluded, awaiting Cheng Zhao's decision."]}]);
